export const TEXTBOOK_CHUNKS: TextbookChunk[] = [
  {"id":0,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"The Series 65 opens with economics because an investment adviser must be able to read the economy the way a doctor reads vital signs. You are not expected to be a professional economist. You are expected to know the vocabulary, understand cause and effect (especially how the Federal Reserve and Congress push the economy around), and answer a set of questions that reward clear thinking over memorization. This subsection builds that foundation from the ground up.\n\n#### The Business Cycle"},
  {"id":1,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"An economy does not grow in a straight line. It moves through a repeating, wave-like pattern called the **business cycle**, measured primarily by changes in real Gross Domestic Product (GDP). The cycle has four phases:"},
  {"id":2,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"| Phase | What is happening | Typical signs |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Expansion** | The economy grows; real GDP rises | Rising output, falling unemployment, rising consumer spending, often rising inflation |\n| **Peak** | Growth tops out; the high point of the cycle | Full employment, capacity strained, inflation pressure highest |\n| **Contraction (recession)** | The economy shrinks; real GDP falls | Falling output, rising unemployment, weak spending and investment |\n| **Trough** | The low point; the economy bottoms out | Unemployment high but stabilizing, ready to recover into the next expansion |"},
  {"id":3,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"The order flows in a loop: **expansion → peak → contraction → trough → expansion** again. The phases go by other names — expansion may be called \"recovery\" or \"boom,\" and contraction may be called \"recession,\" \"downturn,\" or \"slowdown.\"\n\nTwo definitions are heavily tested:\n\n- **Recession:** the standard exam definition is **two consecutive quarters (6 months) of declining real GDP.**\n- **Depression:** a prolonged, severe contraction. The commonly tested benchmark is **six or more consecutive quarters (18 months) of declining real GDP**, often accompanied by a decline in real GDP of 10% or more."},
  {"id":4,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"**Exam tip:** If a question describes falling GDP for exactly two quarters, the answer is *recession*, not depression. A depression requires the longer, deeper decline.\n\n**Common trap:** Candidates confuse the peak (top, best conditions, highest inflation risk) with the trough (bottom, worst conditions). Remember: a *peak* is the top of a mountain; a *trough* is the low point where water collects.\n\n#### Economic Indicators: Measuring the Cycle\n\nEconomists track the cycle using **economic indicators** — published statistics that describe the health of the economy. Know the following core indicators cold."},
  {"id":5,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"**Gross Domestic Product (GDP)** is the total market value of all final goods and services produced within a country's borders in a given period (usually a year or a quarter). It is *the* broadest measure of economic output. GDP is built from four categories of spending:\n\n> GDP = C + I + G + (X − M)\n\nwhere **C** = consumer spending, **I** = business investment, **G** = government spending, **X** = exports, and **M** = imports.\n\n*Worked example:* Suppose a country reports consumer spending of $14 trillion, business investment of $4 trillion, government spending of $6 trillion, exports of $3 trillion, and imports of $4 trillion.\n\n> GDP = 14 + 4 + 6 + (3 − 4) = 14 + 4 + 6 − 1 = **$23 trillion**"},
  {"id":6,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"Notice that imports *subtract* from GDP because they represent output produced elsewhere.\n\n- **Nominal GDP** is measured in current dollars and is not adjusted for inflation.\n- **Real GDP** is adjusted for inflation, so it reflects actual changes in output. Real GDP is the number used to date the business cycle.\n\n> Real GDP = Nominal GDP ÷ (GDP Deflator ÷ 100)\n\n*Worked example:* If nominal GDP is $23 trillion and the GDP deflator is 115 (meaning prices are 15% higher than the base year), then:\n\n> Real GDP = 23 ÷ (115 ÷ 100) = 23 ÷ 1.15 = **$20 trillion**"},
  {"id":7,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"**Exam tip:** Growth is always judged by *real* GDP. Nominal GDP can rise purely because prices went up, even if the economy produced nothing more.\n\n**GDP vs. GNP:** GDP counts output produced *within a country's borders* regardless of who owns the resources. GNP (Gross National Product) counts output produced *by a country's residents and companies* regardless of where it occurs. GDP is the modern standard."},
  {"id":8,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"**The Consumer Price Index (CPI)** measures the average change over time in the prices paid by urban consumers for a fixed \"basket\" of goods and services (food, housing, transportation, medical care, etc.). CPI is the most-watched gauge of **inflation**. When CPI rises, the cost of living is rising.\n\n> Inflation rate = (CPI this year − CPI last year) ÷ CPI last year × 100\n\n*Worked example:* If CPI moved from 300 to 309 over one year:\n\n> Inflation rate = (309 − 300) ÷ 300 × 100 = 9 ÷ 300 × 100 = **3%**\n\n**Employment indicators** describe the labor market:"},
  {"id":9,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"- **Unemployment rate** — the percentage of the labor force actively seeking work but unable to find it. (Note: it is a *lagging* indicator; more on that below.)\n- **Nonfarm payrolls** — the monthly count of jobs added or lost.\n- **Initial jobless claims** — new filings for unemployment benefits; a timely, forward-looking signal.\n- **Average weekly hours in manufacturing** — employers cut hours before laying off, so this turns early.\n\n**The trade deficit (balance of trade)** compares the value of a country's exports to its imports:\n\n> Balance of Trade = Exports − Imports"},
  {"id":10,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"A **trade deficit** exists when a country imports more than it exports (a negative number); a **trade surplus** exists when it exports more than it imports. The U.S. typically runs a trade deficit. A persistent deficit tends to put downward pressure on the country's currency.\n\n*Worked example:* Exports of $3 trillion and imports of $4 trillion produce a balance of trade of 3 − 4 = **−$1 trillion**, a $1 trillion trade deficit.\n\n#### Leading, Coincident, and Lagging Indicators\n\nIndicators are also classified by their *timing* relative to the business cycle. This is one of the most frequently tested economics concepts on the exam."},
  {"id":11,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"| Class | Definition | Commonly tested examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Leading** | Change *before* the economy changes; used to *predict* turning points | Stock market prices (S&P 500), building permits and housing starts, new orders for durable goods, average weekly manufacturing hours, initial unemployment claims, money supply (M2), the yield-curve spread, consumer expectations |\n| **Coincident** | Change *at the same time* as the economy; confirm the *current* phase | Nonfarm payroll employment, industrial production, personal income, manufacturing and trade sales, GDP |\n| **Lagging** | Change *after* the economy has already turned; *confirm* trends | Unemployment rate and average duration of unemployment, corporate profits, the prime rate, CPI/inflation, business inventories, consumer installment debt |"},
  {"id":12,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"**Exam tip:** The stock market is the classic *leading* indicator — investors buy in anticipation of a recovery before it shows up in the data. Memorize this.\n\n**Common trap:** The **CPI/inflation** and the **unemployment rate** are *lagging* indicators, not leading. Inflation keeps rising after a peak and unemployment keeps rising after a trough, because employers are slow to rehire. Test writers love to see whether you will wrongly call unemployment a leading indicator.\n\n#### Inflation and Deflation\n\n**Inflation** is a sustained increase in the general price level, which means each dollar buys less — a loss of purchasing power. It is measured mainly by the CPI. Two causes are tested:"},
  {"id":13,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"- **Demand-pull inflation** — too much money chasing too few goods; demand outruns supply.\n- **Cost-push inflation** — rising input costs (wages, raw materials, energy) push producers to raise prices.\n\nRelated terms you must distinguish:"},
  {"id":14,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"- **Hyperinflation** — extremely rapid, out-of-control inflation.\n- **Disinflation** — a *slowing of the rate* of inflation (prices still rise, just more slowly). This is not the same as deflation.\n- **Deflation** — a sustained *decrease* in the general price level (a negative inflation rate). Purchasing power rises, but deflation is dangerous: consumers delay purchases expecting lower prices, demand falls, and the economy can spiral downward.\n- **Stagflation** — the unusual combination of stagnant growth (high unemployment) *plus* high inflation at the same time. It is difficult for policymakers because the usual cure for one problem worsens the other."},
  {"id":15,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"**Exam tip:** Inflation hurts lenders and bondholders (they are repaid in cheaper dollars) and helps borrowers (they repay with cheaper dollars). Deflation does the reverse.\n\nThe **real interest rate** strips inflation out of a stated (nominal) rate:\n\n> Real Interest Rate = Nominal Interest Rate − Inflation Rate\n\n*Worked example:* A bond yields a nominal 5% while inflation runs at 3%. The real return is 5% − 3% = **2%.** If inflation rose to 6%, the real return would be 5% − 6% = **−1%**, an actual loss of purchasing power despite a positive stated yield.\n\n#### Interest Rates, Yield Curves, and Credit Spreads"},
  {"id":16,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"**Interest rates** are the price of borrowing money. The single most important relationship in fixed income — and one the exam tests relentlessly — is the **inverse relationship between interest rates and bond prices:** when rates rise, existing bond prices fall; when rates fall, existing bond prices rise. Longer-maturity and lower-coupon bonds are the most sensitive to rate changes.\n\n**Common trap:** Rising rates sound \"good\" but they *lower* the market value of bonds an investor already owns. Do not equate higher yields with higher prices.\n\nKey benchmark rates to know:"},
  {"id":17,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"- **Federal funds rate** — the rate banks charge *each other* for overnight loans of reserves. This is the most volatile short-term rate and the Fed's primary policy target.\n- **Discount rate** — the rate the Federal Reserve charges banks that borrow reserves directly from it.\n- **Prime rate** — the rate banks charge their most creditworthy corporate customers; a *lagging* indicator.\n- **Broker call (call money) rate** — what banks charge broker-dealers on loans used to finance customer margin accounts."},
  {"id":18,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"**The yield curve** is a graph plotting the yields of bonds of the same credit quality (usually U.S. Treasuries) across different maturities, from short-term to long-term. Its shape signals expectations about growth and interest rates."},
  {"id":19,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"| Shape | Description | What it usually signals |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Normal (positive/upward-sloping)** | Long-term yields higher than short-term yields | Healthy expansion; investors demand more yield to lend longer |\n| **Inverted (negative/downward-sloping)** | Short-term yields higher than long-term yields | Widely watched *recession warning*; markets expect the Fed to cut rates as growth slows |\n| **Flat** | Short- and long-term yields nearly equal | Transition; often between expansion and contraction, or the reverse |\n| **Humped** | Intermediate yields higher than both short- and long-term | Uncertainty; relatively rare |"},
  {"id":20,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"**Exam tip:** A *normal* curve slopes up because investors require extra compensation (for inflation and uncertainty) to tie up money longer. An *inverted* curve is the notable one — it is the classic leading indicator of a coming recession.\n\n**Credit spreads** measure the extra yield investors demand to hold a riskier bond instead of a \"risk-free\" Treasury of the same maturity:\n\n> Credit Spread = Yield on Corporate (or lower-quality) Bond − Yield on Comparable Treasury\n\n*Worked example:* A 10-year corporate bond yields 6.2% while the 10-year Treasury yields 4.2%.\n\n> Credit Spread = 6.2% − 4.2% = 2.0%, or **200 basis points** (1% = 100 basis points)"},
  {"id":21,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"Spreads *widen* when investors grow fearful (recessions, financial stress) and demand more compensation for default risk; spreads *narrow* when confidence and risk appetite return. Widening credit spreads are therefore a sign of economic anxiety, and lower-rated (\"high-yield\" or \"junk\") bonds show the widest spreads.\n\n#### Monetary Policy — The Federal Reserve\n\n**Monetary policy** is the management of the money supply and interest rates to promote stable prices and maximum employment. It is conducted by the **Federal Reserve (the Fed)** — the U.S. central bank — with open market operations directed by its **Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC)**. The Fed has three classic tools:"},
  {"id":22,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"| Tool | What it is | To *loosen* (stimulate) | To *tighten* (restrain) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Open market operations** | Buying/selling U.S. government securities in the open market | **Buy** securities → adds reserves to banks → lowers rates | **Sell** securities → drains reserves → raises rates |\n| **Discount rate** | Rate the Fed charges banks that borrow reserves from it | **Lower** the rate | **Raise** the rate |\n| **Reserve requirement** | Percentage of deposits banks must hold in reserve | **Lower** it → banks can lend more | **Raise** it → banks can lend less |"},
  {"id":23,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"**Open market operations are the Fed's most frequently used and most flexible tool.** When the Fed *buys* securities, it injects money into the banking system, pushing interest rates (including the fed funds rate) down — an **easy-money / accommodative** stance meant to stimulate growth. When the Fed *sells* securities, it pulls money out, pushing rates up — a **tight-money** stance meant to cool inflation.\n\n**Exam tip:** Trace the chain of cause and effect. Fed *buys* securities → money supply *up* → interest rates *down* → borrowing and spending *up* → economy stimulated (and bond prices *up*). Reverse every arrow for a Fed *sale*."},
  {"id":24,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"**Common trap:** The **reserve requirement** is considered the Fed's *bluntest* tool because a small change affects every bank's lending capacity dramatically. Also note the **discount rate** is charged by the Fed to banks, whereas the **fed funds rate** is charged *between* banks — do not swap those definitions. (The reserve requirement was reduced to 0% in 2020, but the Series 65 still tests it as a conceptual tool.)\n\n#### Fiscal Policy — Congress and the President"},
  {"id":25,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"**Fiscal policy** is the use of **government spending and taxation** to influence the economy. Unlike monetary policy, fiscal policy is set by the **President and Congress** (the executive and legislative branches), not the Fed. This approach is rooted in **Keynesian economics**, which holds that government can smooth the business cycle by managing aggregate demand."},
  {"id":26,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"- **Expansionary (stimulative) fiscal policy:** *increase* government spending and/or *cut* taxes. This puts more money in the hands of businesses and consumers to boost demand — used to fight recessions. It tends to enlarge the budget deficit.\n- **Contractionary (restrictive) fiscal policy:** *decrease* government spending and/or *raise* taxes. This removes money from the economy to cool inflation.\n\nTwo terms to distinguish:\n\n- **Budget deficit** — the shortfall in a single year when the government spends more than it collects in revenue.\n- **National debt** — the accumulated total of all past deficits (minus surpluses); the cumulative amount owed."},
  {"id":27,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"**Exam tip:** Match the branch to the policy. *Monetary* = the Fed (money supply, interest rates). *Fiscal* = Congress and the President (spending, taxes). Questions often test whether you can attribute a given action to the correct body.\n\nAlso know the competing schools test writers reference:"},
  {"id":28,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"- **Keynesian theory** — emphasizes *demand-side* management; government spending and taxation are the key levers to steer the economy.\n- **Monetarist theory** (associated with Milton Friedman) — emphasizes controlling the *money supply*; argues that steady, predictable money growth matters most and that fiscal fine-tuning is less effective.\n- **Supply-side theory** — argues that lower taxes and lighter regulation stimulate production, investment, and ultimately growth.\n\n#### Global Factors\n\nThe U.S. economy is part of a connected world, and the exam expects you to understand several international influences."},
  {"id":29,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"**Currency valuation and exchange rates.** The value of the U.S. dollar relative to other currencies drives trade and investment flows. The key idea is the effect of a **strong** versus a **weak** dollar:\n\n| | Strong (appreciating) U.S. dollar | Weak (depreciating) U.S. dollar |\n|---|---|---|\n| U.S. exports | More expensive abroad → **hurts** U.S. exporters | Cheaper abroad → **helps** U.S. exporters |\n| Imports into the U.S. | Cheaper → **helps** importers and U.S. consumers | More expensive → **hurts** importers, raises import prices |\n| U.S. tourists abroad | Their dollars buy more → **benefit** | Their dollars buy less → **disadvantage** |"},
  {"id":30,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"*Worked example:* Suppose 1 euro = $1.10. A U.S. importer buying a €50,000 machine pays 50,000 × 1.10 = **$55,000**. If the dollar *weakens* so that 1 euro = $1.25, the same machine now costs 50,000 × 1.25 = **$62,500** — the weaker dollar made the import more expensive. Meanwhile, a European buyer finds U.S. goods cheaper, helping American exporters.\n\nThe **effective exchange rate** is a *trade-weighted* measure of a currency's value against a basket of the currencies of its trading partners, rather than against a single currency. It gives a more complete picture of whether a currency is broadly strengthening or weakening."},
  {"id":31,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"**Exam tip:** A weak domestic currency *helps exporters* and *hurts importers*; a strong currency does the reverse. Reason it through from \"which direction makes our goods cheaper for foreigners.\""},
  {"id":32,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"**Sovereign debt** is debt issued by national governments (for the U.S., Treasury securities). U.S. Treasuries are treated as the global benchmark for a \"risk-free\" rate because of the government's taxing power and its ability to print its own currency. Debt issued by other nations — especially emerging markets or countries borrowing in a foreign currency — carries **sovereign (default) risk**: the risk that a government cannot or will not repay. A **sovereign debt crisis** can trigger currency collapse, capital flight, and financial contagion that spreads across borders."},
  {"id":33,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"**Geopolitical factors** — wars, trade disputes and tariffs, sanctions, elections, regime changes, oil-supply shocks, and pandemics — create uncertainty that ripples through currencies, commodity prices, interest rates, and stock markets. Heightened geopolitical risk typically drives a **\"flight to quality\"**: investors sell riskier assets and move into perceived safe havens such as U.S. Treasuries and gold, pushing Treasury prices up and their yields down."},
  {"id":34,"part":"Part I","section":"A. Basic Economic Concepts","anchor":"a-basic-economic-concepts","text":"**Exam tip:** When a question describes fear, crisis, or global instability, expect money to flow *toward* safety (Treasuries, gold) and *away* from risk (stocks, high-yield bonds). That flight-to-quality reflex ties together yields, credit spreads, and currency movements into one coherent story — exactly the kind of integrated reasoning the Series 65 rewards."},
  {"id":35,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"Financial reporting is the language companies use to tell investors how they are doing. On the Series 65, this material shows up in two ways: (1) direct questions asking you to identify a financial statement, a line item, an SEC filing, or an accounting method, and (2) as the raw material for the ratio and analysis questions covered elsewhere in the exam. You do not need to be an accountant, but you must recognize each of the three core financial statements, know what each one measures, understand the accounting methods and audit levels behind them, and match the major SEC filings to their purpose and frequency. This subsection builds those skills in order: first the accounting fundamentals that underlie every statement, then the three statements themselves, then the auditor's role, and finally the public filings that package it all for investors."},
  {"id":36,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"#### Accounting Fundamentals: Cash vs. Accrual Accounting\n\nBefore you can read a financial statement, you have to know *when* a company records its revenues and expenses. There are two methods."},
  {"id":37,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"- **Cash-basis accounting** records revenue when cash is actually **received** and records expenses when cash is actually **paid**. It is simple and tracks the checkbook, but it can distort the picture of a business. A company could deliver $1 million of product in December, collect nothing until January, and show zero revenue for the year it did the work.\n- **Accrual accounting** records revenue when it is **earned** and expenses when they are **incurred**, regardless of when cash changes hands. Under the \"matching principle,\" expenses are matched to the revenues they help produce in the same period. If the company delivers that $1 million of product in December, it books $1 million of revenue in December even though the cash arrives later (the amount owed sits on the balance sheet as **accounts receivable**)."},
  {"id":38,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"**Exam tip:** Accrual accounting is required under **GAAP** (Generally Accepted Accounting Principles) and is the method used in the audited financial statements of public companies. When a question asks which method gives the most accurate picture of a company's true economic performance for a period, the answer is **accrual**. Cash basis is associated with very small businesses and simple tax situations.\n\n**Common trap:** Watch for questions that pair a timing fact with a method. \"A firm earns revenue in Q4 but is not paid until Q1 of the next year; under accrual accounting, when is the revenue recognized?\" The answer is Q4 (when earned), not Q1 (when collected)."},
  {"id":39,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"#### Accounting Fundamentals: Audited vs. Unaudited\n\nThe second fundamental distinction is the level of independent verification behind the numbers."},
  {"id":40,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"- **Audited** financial statements have been examined by an **independent public accounting firm** (a CPA firm) that tests the records and issues a formal opinion on whether the statements fairly present the company's financial position in conformity with GAAP. An audit provides the highest level of assurance.\n- **Unaudited** financial statements are prepared by the company's own management and have **not** been independently verified. They may still be accurate, but they carry no outside opinion. Interim (quarterly) figures are typically unaudited; they may be \"reviewed\" by accountants, which is a lower level of assurance than a full audit."},
  {"id":41,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"**Exam tip:** Tie this to the filings. The annual **10-K** contains **audited** statements; the quarterly **10-Q** contains **unaudited** (reviewed) statements. This audited/unaudited split is one of the most testable facts in this subsection.\n\n#### The Balance Sheet\n\nThe **balance sheet** is a snapshot of what a company **owns and owes at one specific point in time** (for example, \"as of December 31\"). It answers the question, \"What is the company's financial position on this date?\" It is built on the fundamental accounting equation:\n\nAssets = Liabilities + Shareholders' Equity"},
  {"id":42,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"Because it must always balance, shareholders' equity (also called **net worth** or **book value**) is simply what is left for owners after subtracting what the company owes from what it owns:\n\nShareholders' Equity = Total Assets − Total Liabilities\n\n**Major components:**"},
  {"id":43,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"| Section | Contains |\n|---|---|\n| **Current assets** | Cash, marketable securities, accounts receivable, inventory — items expected to be converted to cash within 12 months |\n| **Fixed / non-current assets** | Property, plant & equipment (PP&E), shown net of accumulated depreciation |\n| **Intangible assets** | Goodwill, patents, trademarks |\n| **Current liabilities** | Accounts payable, accrued expenses, and the portion of long-term debt due within 12 months |\n| **Long-term liabilities** | Bonds and notes payable due beyond 12 months |\n| **Shareholders' equity** | Par value of stock, capital in excess of par (paid-in surplus), and retained earnings; treasury stock is subtracted |"},
  {"id":44,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"**Key balance-sheet calculations.** These formulas frequently appear on the exam:"},
  {"id":45,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"- **Working Capital = Current Assets − Current Liabilities.** This measures short-term liquidity — the cushion available to meet obligations coming due within a year.\n  - *Worked example:* Current assets = $600,000; current liabilities = $350,000. Working Capital = $600,000 − $350,000 = **$250,000**.\n- **Current Ratio = Current Assets / Current Liabilities.** A ratio above 1.0 means current assets exceed current liabilities.\n  - *Worked example:* $600,000 / $350,000 = **1.71**.\n- **Quick Ratio (Acid-Test) = (Current Assets − Inventory) / Current Liabilities.** This is a stricter test because inventory is the hardest current asset to convert to cash quickly, so it is removed.\n  - *Worked example:* If inventory is $150,000, then ($600,000 − $150,000) / $350,000 = $450,000 / $350,000 = **1.29**."},
  {"id":46,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"**Common trap:** The quick ratio removes **inventory** (and sometimes prepaid expenses) from current assets — it does **not** remove cash or receivables. A distractor that subtracts receivables is wrong.\n\n**Exam tip:** The balance sheet is a \"point in time\" statement, while the income statement and statement of cash flows cover a \"period of time.\" Questions test that distinction directly.\n\n#### The Income Statement"},
  {"id":47,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"The **income statement** (also called the profit-and-loss or P&L statement) reports a company's **revenues and expenses over a period of time** (a quarter or a year) and shows whether the company earned a profit or a loss. It answers, \"Was the company profitable during this period?\" Its top-to-bottom structure builds down to the \"bottom line\":\n\nRevenue (Net Sales)\n− Cost of Goods Sold (COGS)\n= Gross Profit\n− Operating Expenses (including depreciation)\n= Operating Income (EBIT — earnings before interest and taxes)\n− Interest Expense\n= Pre-Tax Income (EBT)\n− Taxes\n= **Net Income**\n\n**Key income-statement calculation — Earnings Per Share (EPS):**"},
  {"id":48,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"EPS = (Net Income − Preferred Dividends) / Number of Common Shares Outstanding\n\n- *Worked example:* Net income = $5,000,000; preferred dividends = $500,000; common shares outstanding = 2,000,000. EPS = ($5,000,000 − $500,000) / 2,000,000 = $4,500,000 / 2,000,000 = **$2.25 per share**.\n\n**Common trap:** Preferred dividends are subtracted **before** dividing by common shares, because EPS measures earnings available to **common** stockholders. Forgetting to remove preferred dividends is the classic wrong answer."},
  {"id":49,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"**Exam tip:** Know the vocabulary layers — gross profit is after COGS only; operating income (EBIT) is after operating expenses but before interest and taxes; net income is the final figure after everything. Interest paid to bondholders is deducted **before** taxes (making debt financing tax-advantaged), whereas dividends to shareholders are paid **after** taxes from net income.\n\n#### The Statement of Cash Flows"},
  {"id":50,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"The **statement of cash flows** reconciles the accrual-based income statement back to actual cash. Because accrual accounting records revenues and expenses that have not yet been settled in cash, a company can report positive net income yet still run short of cash — this statement reveals where cash actually came from and went during the period. It is divided into three sections:"},
  {"id":51,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"| Section | What it captures | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Operating activities** | Cash generated by the core business | Cash from customers, cash paid to suppliers and employees; starts from net income and adds back non-cash charges like depreciation |\n| **Investing activities** | Cash used for or from long-term assets | Buying or selling PP&E; buying or selling securities or other companies |\n| **Financing activities** | Cash from or to capital providers | Issuing or repaying debt, issuing or repurchasing stock, paying cash dividends |"},
  {"id":52,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"**Exam tip:** The most testable idea is *why* this statement exists: because accrual accounting means reported earnings are not the same as cash. A profitable company can still fail if it runs out of cash. Also remember that **depreciation** is a non-cash expense that reduces net income on the income statement but is **added back** in the operating section, because no cash actually left the company.\n\n**Common trap:** Paying a cash dividend is a **financing** activity, not an operating one. Purchasing new equipment is an **investing** activity. Do not confuse the three buckets.\n\n#### Auditor Disclosures: Qualified vs. Unqualified Opinions"},
  {"id":53,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"When an independent CPA firm audits a company, it attaches an **auditor's report (opinion)** to the financial statements. The type of opinion tells investors how much to trust the numbers. There are four levels; the exam focuses heavily on the first two."},
  {"id":54,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"| Opinion | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Unqualified opinion** | The \"clean\" opinion — the **best** outcome. The auditor states that the financial statements fairly present the company's position **in conformity with GAAP**, with no reservations. |\n| **Qualified opinion** | The statements are fairly presented **\"except for\"** a specific issue — a particular item the auditor could not verify or that departs from GAAP. It is a caution flag, not an outright rejection. |\n| **Adverse opinion** | The statements do **not** fairly present the company's position and do not conform to GAAP. This is the most negative opinion. |\n| **Disclaimer of opinion** | The auditor cannot form an opinion at all (for example, due to insufficient information or a scope limitation). |"},
  {"id":55,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"**Exam tip:** Do not let the words mislead you. An **un**qualified opinion is the **good** one (nothing was disqualified — clean), while a **qualified** opinion signals a problem (\"except for...\"). This counterintuitive naming is a favorite trap. If a question asks which opinion an investor most wants to see, the answer is **unqualified**.\n\n**Common trap:** An audit opinion — even a clean, unqualified one — is **not** a guarantee against fraud, a prediction of future performance, or a statement that the company is a good investment. It only attests that the statements fairly present the financials in accordance with GAAP.\n\n#### Corporate SEC Filings: 10-K, 10-Q, and 8-K"},
  {"id":56,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"Public reporting companies must file periodic reports with the **SEC**, which makes them available to the public through the **EDGAR** system. You must match each filing to its purpose, frequency, and audit level."},
  {"id":57,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"| Filing | Frequency / Trigger | Content | Audited? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Form 10-K** | **Annually** (after fiscal year end) | Comprehensive annual report: full audited financial statements, MD&A (Management's Discussion and Analysis), business and risk-factor disclosures | **Yes — audited** |\n| **Form 10-Q** | **Quarterly** (for the first three fiscal quarters) | Condensed interim financial statements and MD&A | **No — unaudited** (reviewed) |\n| **Form 8-K** | **As needed**, upon a material event | \"Current report\" announcing significant events — e.g., a merger or acquisition, bankruptcy, change of auditors, resignation of a director, or a change in control | N/A |"},
  {"id":58,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"**Filing timelines.** The 10-Q covers only the first three quarters because the fourth quarter is folded into the annual 10-K. Filing deadlines vary by company size: a smaller (non-accelerated) filer's **10-K** is generally due within **90 days** of fiscal year-end and its **10-Q** within **45 days** of quarter-end, while large accelerated filers face shorter windows (60 and 40 days). An **8-K** must generally be filed within **4 business days** of the triggering event. For the Series 65, prioritize the concepts — which filing, how often, audited or not, and that material events go on the 8-K — over memorizing every deadline, but know that the **8-K = 4 business days** figure and the **annual/quarterly** cadence are commonly tested."},
  {"id":59,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"**Exam tip:** The cleanest distinctions to lock in: **10-K = annual and audited; 10-Q = quarterly and unaudited; 8-K = material current events, filed promptly (within 4 business days).** If a question describes a company announcing that it just fired its auditor or agreed to be acquired, the correct filing is the **8-K**.\n\n#### Annual Reports"},
  {"id":60,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"The **annual report** is the document a company sends to its **shareholders** each year. Do not confuse it with the 10-K. The annual report is typically a polished, narrative document — it includes the audited financial statements (income statement, balance sheet, statement of cash flows), the auditor's opinion, a letter from the CEO or chairman, and a discussion of the year's performance and outlook. The **10-K** is the more detailed, standardized, regulatory filing submitted directly to the SEC. Their financial statements overlap and are drawn from the same audited data, but the audiences and formats differ: the annual report speaks to **investors**, and the 10-K satisfies the **SEC**."},
  {"id":61,"part":"Part I","section":"B. Financial Reporting","anchor":"b-financial-reporting","text":"**Exam tip:** If asked where a retail investor would find a plain-language discussion of the company's year along with its audited financials, the answer is the **annual report**; if asked for the comprehensive statutory disclosure filed with the SEC, the answer is the **10-K**. Both contain audited financial statements, which is exactly why the exam likes to contrast them."},
  {"id":62,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"Analytical methods are the quantitative tools an investment adviser uses to value cash flows, measure risk and return, and judge the financial health of a company. On the Series 65 you will see a handful of calculation questions and a larger number of \"what does this number mean\" interpretation questions. You do not need a financial calculator's advanced functions, but you must know each formula, be able to plug in numbers, and — most importantly — know what a rising or falling result signals. This subsection walks through the four required tool sets: the time value of money, descriptive statistics, liquidity/leverage ratios, and valuation ratios.\n\n#### Time Value of Money (TVM)"},
  {"id":63,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"The core idea is that a dollar today is worth more than a dollar in the future, because a dollar today can be invested to earn a return. All four TVM concepts below are just different ways of moving money forward (compounding) or backward (discounting) through time.\n\n**Future Value (FV)** answers: \"What will a sum today grow into?\" It moves money *forward* by compounding.\n\nFormula (plain text): FV = PV x (1 + r)^n\n\n- PV = amount invested today\n- r = periodic interest/growth rate (as a decimal)\n- n = number of periods\n\nWorked example: You invest $10,000 today at 6% compounded annually for 3 years.\nFV = 10,000 x (1.06)^3 = 10,000 x 1.191016 = **$11,910.16**"},
  {"id":64,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"**Present Value (PV)** answers: \"What is a future sum worth in today's dollars?\" It moves money *backward* by discounting — the reverse of future value.\n\nFormula: PV = FV / (1 + r)^n\n\nWorked example: You need $11,910.16 in 3 years and can earn 6%.\nPV = 11,910.16 / (1.06)^3 = 11,910.16 / 1.191016 = **$10,000**\n\n**Exam tip:** The higher the discount rate (r) or the longer the time horizon (n), the *lower* the present value. This inverse relationship is why rising interest rates push bond prices down — future coupons and principal are discounted more heavily."},
  {"id":65,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"A useful shortcut the exam sometimes rewards is the **Rule of 72**: the approximate number of years to double your money = 72 / rate. At 6%, money doubles in about 72 / 6 = 12 years.\n\n**Net Present Value (NPV)** extends present value to an entire stream of cash flows. It is the sum of the present values of all expected inflows minus the initial cost (outflow), discounted at the investor's required rate of return.\n\nFormula: NPV = (Sum of the PVs of all future cash inflows) - Initial Investment\n\nDecision rule:\n- NPV > 0 -> the investment earns more than the required return -> accept\n- NPV = 0 -> earns exactly the required return\n- NPV < 0 -> earns less than the required return -> reject"},
  {"id":66,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"Worked example: An investment costs $1,000 today and is expected to pay $600 at the end of year 1 and $600 at the end of year 2. Required return = 10%.\n- PV of year-1 cash flow = 600 / 1.10 = 545.45\n- PV of year-2 cash flow = 600 / (1.10)^2 = 600 / 1.21 = 495.87\n- Total PV of inflows = 545.45 + 495.87 = 1,041.32\n- NPV = 1,041.32 - 1,000 = **+$41.32** (positive, so accept)\n\n**Internal Rate of Return (IRR)** is the discount rate that makes NPV equal exactly zero. In plain English, IRR is the *actual* compound annual return the investment is expected to deliver, taking the timing of every cash flow into account.\n\nKey relationship the exam loves:"},
  {"id":67,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"| If... | Then... | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| IRR > required return | NPV is positive | Accept |\n| IRR = required return | NPV is zero | Indifferent |\n| IRR < required return | NPV is negative | Reject |\n\n**Exam tip:** For a bond, IRR is the same thing as **yield to maturity (YTM)** — it is the single rate that discounts all coupons plus the return of principal back to the bond's current price. If a bond is bought at par, its IRR/YTM equals the coupon rate (which at par also equals the current yield). IRR is superior to a simple average return because it accounts for the *time value* and *timing* of each cash flow."},
  {"id":68,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"**Common trap:** IRR assumes interim cash flows are reinvested at the IRR itself. Do not confuse IRR (a percentage rate) with NPV (a dollar amount). A question that gives you a discount rate and asks for a dollar figure wants NPV; a question asking for the rate that zeroes out NPV wants IRR.\n\n#### Descriptive Statistics\n\nDescriptive statistics summarize a data set (usually a series of investment returns) so the adviser can describe its central tendency, its dispersion, and its risk-adjusted quality.\n\n**Mean, Median, and Mode** describe the \"center\" of the data."},
  {"id":69,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"- **Mean** = arithmetic average = sum of all values / number of values\n- **Median** = the middle value when the data is sorted (with an even count, average the two middle values)\n- **Mode** = the value that appears most frequently\n\nWorked example using annual returns of 3%, 5%, 5%, 7%, and 30%:\n- Mean = (3 + 5 + 5 + 7 + 30) / 5 = 50 / 5 = **10%**\n- Median = middle of the sorted list = **5%**\n- Mode = most frequent value = **5%**"},
  {"id":70,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"**Exam tip:** Notice how one outlier (the 30% year) pulls the *mean* up to 10% while the *median* stays at 5%. The exam tests this: the median is more representative of a \"typical\" outcome when data is skewed by extreme values, whereas the mean is distorted by outliers.\n\n**Range** is the simplest measure of dispersion: Range = Highest value - Lowest value. In the example above, 30% - 3% = **27%**. Range is crude because it ignores everything between the two extremes."},
  {"id":71,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"**Standard Deviation** is the exam's primary measure of an investment's **total risk** (also called total volatility). It quantifies how far, on average, returns deviate from their mean — a higher standard deviation means a wider spread of possible outcomes and thus greater risk.\n\nIts power comes from the **normal (bell-curve) distribution**, where returns cluster around the mean in a predictable way:\n- About **68%** of outcomes fall within ±1 standard deviation of the mean\n- About **95%** fall within ±2 standard deviations\n- About **99.7%** fall within ±3 standard deviations"},
  {"id":72,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"Worked example: A fund has a mean annual return of 10% and a standard deviation of 15%. Then about 68% of the time its annual return should land between 10% - 15% = **-5%** and 10% + 15% = **+25%**. About 95% of the time it lands between -20% and +40%.\n\n**Exam tip:** Standard deviation measures *total* risk — both systematic (market) and unsystematic (company-specific) risk. It is most meaningful when comparing two similar investments; the one with the higher standard deviation is the more volatile.\n\n**Beta** measures **systematic risk** only — an investment's volatility *relative to the overall market*. The market (e.g., the S&P 500) has a beta of exactly 1.0."},
  {"id":73,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"- Beta > 1.0 = more volatile than the market (aggressive)\n- Beta < 1.0 = less volatile than the market (defensive)\n- Beta = 1.0 = moves in line with the market\n- Negative beta = tends to move opposite the market\n\nWorked example: A stock with a beta of 1.5 is expected to rise about 15% when the market rises 10%, and fall about 15% when the market falls 10%.\n\n**Common trap:** Standard deviation vs. beta. Standard deviation captures *total* risk and can compare any two investments; beta captures only *market-related* (systematic) risk relative to a benchmark. A well-diversified portfolio can drive unsystematic risk toward zero, but beta/systematic risk cannot be diversified away."},
  {"id":74,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"**Alpha** measures **risk-adjusted excess return** — the amount by which an investment beat (or lagged) the return that would be expected given its beta. Conceptually, alpha = actual return - expected return (where the expected return is derived from the market's return and the investment's beta via CAPM).\n\n- Positive alpha = the portfolio outperformed what its risk level predicted (often attributed to manager skill)\n- Zero alpha = performed exactly as expected\n- Negative alpha = underperformed for the risk taken\n\nWorked example: If a stock's expected return based on its beta is 9%, and it actually returned 12%, its alpha is 12% - 9% = **+3%**."},
  {"id":75,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"**Sharpe Ratio** measures **risk-adjusted return using total risk (standard deviation)**. It tells you how much *excess* return an investor earned per unit of total risk.\n\nFormula: Sharpe Ratio = (Portfolio Return - Risk-Free Rate) / Standard Deviation of the Portfolio\n\nWorked example: A portfolio returned 12%, the risk-free rate (e.g., a T-bill) is 2%, and the portfolio's standard deviation is 8%.\nSharpe = (12 - 2) / 8 = 10 / 8 = **1.25**\n\nA higher Sharpe ratio is better — it means more return per unit of risk. When comparing two portfolios, the one with the higher Sharpe ratio delivered the superior risk-adjusted performance."},
  {"id":76,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"**Common trap:** The Sharpe ratio uses **standard deviation** (total risk) in the denominator, and it subtracts the **risk-free rate** in the numerator (not the market return, and not zero). Do not confuse it with alpha or beta. If a question asks for return per unit of *total* risk, it wants the Sharpe ratio.\n\n**Correlation and Covariance** describe how two investments move in relation to each other — the foundation of diversification."},
  {"id":77,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"- **Covariance** measures the *direction* of the relationship between two assets' returns. A positive covariance means they tend to move together; a negative covariance means they tend to move in opposite directions. Its size is unbounded and hard to interpret on its own.\n- **Correlation** standardizes covariance onto a fixed scale from **-1.0 to +1.0**, making it directly interpretable.\n\nFormula: Correlation (A,B) = Covariance (A,B) / (Standard Deviation of A x Standard Deviation of B)\n\nReading the correlation coefficient:"},
  {"id":78,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"| Correlation | Meaning | Diversification benefit |\n|---|---|---|\n| +1.0 | Perfectly positive — move in lockstep | None |\n| 0 | No linear relationship | Substantial |\n| -1.0 | Perfectly negative — mirror opposites | Maximum (risk can be greatly reduced) |\n\n**Exam tip:** Diversification reduces portfolio risk most when assets have **low or negative correlation**. Combining two assets with a correlation below +1.0 lowers the portfolio's overall standard deviation. This is the mathematical heart of Modern Portfolio Theory: you can reduce risk without necessarily sacrificing return by mixing assets that do not move together.\n\nQuick reference for the risk/return statistics:"},
  {"id":79,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"| Measure | What it captures | Key point |\n|---|---|---|\n| Standard deviation | Total risk (all volatility) | Higher = riskier; basis of the 68-95-99.7 rule |\n| Beta | Systematic (market) risk | Market = 1.0 |\n| Alpha | Risk-adjusted excess return vs. expectation | Positive = outperformance/skill |\n| Sharpe ratio | Return per unit of total risk | Uses (return - risk-free) / standard deviation; higher is better |\n| Correlation | How two assets move together | Ranges -1 to +1; low = better diversification |\n\n#### Financial Ratios: Liquidity and Leverage"},
  {"id":80,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"These ratios are drawn from a company's balance sheet and tell an analyst whether a firm can pay its short-term bills (liquidity) and how heavily it relies on borrowed money (leverage).\n\n**Current Ratio** measures short-term **liquidity** — whether current assets are sufficient to cover current liabilities (obligations due within one year).\n\nFormula: Current Ratio = Current Assets / Current Liabilities\n\nWorked example: Current assets = $600,000; current liabilities = $300,000.\nCurrent Ratio = 600,000 / 300,000 = **2.0 (or 2:1)**"},
  {"id":81,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"A current ratio of 2:1 is often cited as healthy — the company has twice the current assets needed to cover its near-term debts. A ratio below 1.0 signals potential liquidity problems.\n\n**Quick (Acid-Test) Ratio** is a stricter liquidity test. It removes **inventory** from current assets, because inventory is the current asset that is hardest to convert quickly into cash (and may have to be sold at a discount in a pinch).\n\nFormula: Quick Ratio = (Current Assets - Inventory) / Current Liabilities\n\nWorked example: Current assets = $600,000; inventory = $200,000; current liabilities = $300,000.\nQuick Ratio = (600,000 - 200,000) / 300,000 = 400,000 / 300,000 = **1.33 (or 1.33:1)**"},
  {"id":82,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"**Exam tip:** The only difference between the current ratio and the quick ratio is that the quick ratio subtracts inventory. If a company's current ratio looks strong but its quick ratio is weak, the firm is heavily dependent on selling inventory to meet its obligations.\n\n**Debt-to-Equity Ratio** measures **leverage** — how much of the company's capital structure is financed by debt (creditors) versus equity (owners). It signals financial risk: the more debt, the greater the fixed interest burden and the more vulnerable the company is in a downturn.\n\nFormula: Debt-to-Equity = Total Debt / Total Shareholders' Equity"},
  {"id":83,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"Worked example: Total debt = $4,000,000; total shareholders' equity = $8,000,000.\nDebt-to-Equity = 4,000,000 / 8,000,000 = **0.5 (or 0.5:1)**\n\nA higher debt-to-equity ratio means more leverage and more financial risk; a lower ratio means the firm is more conservatively financed. (Some analyses use only long-term debt in the numerator; the exam focuses on the interpretation — more debt relative to equity equals more risk.)\n\n#### Valuation Ratios\n\nValuation ratios compare a company's *market price* to a fundamental measure of value, helping the adviser judge whether a stock is relatively cheap or expensive."},
  {"id":84,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"**Price-to-Earnings (P/E) Ratio** is the single most-cited valuation metric. It tells you how many dollars investors are willing to pay for each dollar of a company's annual earnings.\n\nFormula: P/E Ratio = Market Price per Share / Earnings per Share (EPS)\n\nWhere EPS = (Net Income - Preferred Dividends) / Number of Common Shares Outstanding.\n\nWorked example: A stock trades at $40 and has EPS of $2.\nP/E = 40 / 2 = **20** (investors pay $20 for each $1 of current earnings)"},
  {"id":85,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"Interpretation:\n- A **high P/E** typically reflects a growth stock — the market expects rapid future earnings growth and is willing to pay a premium.\n- A **low P/E** typically reflects a value stock — priced modestly relative to current earnings.\n\n**Exam tip:** A \"trailing\" P/E uses the past 12 months of actual earnings; a \"forward\" P/E uses projected earnings. A high P/E is not automatically \"bad\" and a low P/E is not automatically \"good\" — the ratio must be compared to industry peers and the company's own growth prospects."},
  {"id":86,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"**Price-to-Book (P/B) Ratio** compares the market price to the company's **book value** (net worth) per share. Book value is essentially what common shareholders would theoretically receive if the company were liquidated at balance-sheet values.\n\nFormula: P/B Ratio = Market Price per Share / Book Value per Share\n\nWhere Book Value per Share = (Total Shareholders' Equity - Preferred Equity) / Common Shares Outstanding.\n\nWorked example: A stock trades at $30 and has a book value per share of $15.\nP/B = 30 / 15 = **2.0** (the market values the company at twice its accounting net worth)"},
  {"id":87,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"Interpretation:\n- **P/B < 1.0** means the stock trades below its book value, which value investors may view as potentially undervalued (though it can also signal trouble).\n- **P/B > 1.0** means the market prices the company above its accounting net worth, reflecting expected future profitability or intangible value."},
  {"id":88,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"**Common trap:** Do not mix up the two valuation ratios. P/E is priced against *earnings* (from the income statement); P/B is priced against *book value/net worth* (from the balance sheet). Book value relies on historical accounting figures and often understates firms whose value lies in intangibles (brands, patents, software), which is why high-growth technology companies frequently carry high P/B ratios.\n\nSummary of the ratios and what a higher number signals:"},
  {"id":89,"part":"Part I","section":"C. Analytical Methods","anchor":"c-analytical-methods","text":"| Ratio | Formula | Higher number signals |\n|---|---|---|\n| Current ratio | Current Assets / Current Liabilities | Stronger short-term liquidity |\n| Quick (acid-test) ratio | (Current Assets - Inventory) / Current Liabilities | Stronger liquidity excluding inventory |\n| Debt-to-equity | Total Debt / Shareholders' Equity | More leverage / higher financial risk |\n| Price-to-earnings (P/E) | Price per Share / EPS | More expensive per $1 of earnings (growth expectations) |\n| Price-to-book (P/B) | Price per Share / Book Value per Share | More expensive relative to net worth |"},
  {"id":90,"part":"Part I","section":"D. Types of Risk","anchor":"d-types-of-risk","text":"Every investment carries risk — the chance that its actual return differs from what the investor expected, including the chance of losing money. For the Series 65, you must be able to name a risk from a short scenario, know which securities each risk hits hardest, and understand which risks can be reduced through diversification and which cannot.\n\nThe single most important framework in this subsection is the split between **systematic** and **unsystematic** risk:\n\n> **Total risk = Systematic risk + Unsystematic risk**"},
  {"id":91,"part":"Part I","section":"D. Types of Risk","anchor":"d-types-of-risk","text":"- **Systematic risk** affects the entire market (or a broad segment of it) and **cannot be diversified away**. It is also called *market risk* or *non-diversifiable risk*.\n- **Unsystematic risk** is unique to a single company or industry and **can be reduced or eliminated through diversification**. It is also called *diversifiable*, *specific*, or *non-systematic risk*.\n\n**Exam tip:** Standard deviation measures an investment's *total* risk (both types combined). *Beta* measures only *systematic* (market) risk. A well-diversified portfolio can drive unsystematic risk toward zero, but its systematic risk (beta) remains."},
  {"id":92,"part":"Part I","section":"D. Types of Risk","anchor":"d-types-of-risk","text":"| | Systematic risk | Unsystematic risk |\n|---|---|---|\n| Also called | Market / non-diversifiable | Specific / diversifiable |\n| Source | The whole market or economy | One company or industry |\n| Reduced by diversification? | **No** | **Yes** |\n| Measured by | Beta | (Removed by diversification) |\n| Examples | Market, interest rate, inflation, sector, geopolitical | Business/financial, credit/default, legal/regulatory, issuer-specific |\n\n#### Systematic (Non-Diversifiable) Risks\n\nThese risks move whole markets at once, so holding more securities does not protect you."},
  {"id":93,"part":"Part I","section":"D. Types of Risk","anchor":"d-types-of-risk","text":"**Market risk / interest rate risk.** *Market risk* is the risk that a broad decline in the overall market drags down nearly all securities regardless of their individual merits; it is the systematic risk of equities and is measured by beta (beta of 1.0 moves with the market, above 1.0 is more volatile than the market, below 1.0 is less)."},
  {"id":94,"part":"Part I","section":"D. Types of Risk","anchor":"d-types-of-risk","text":"*Interest rate risk* is the risk that changing interest rates change a security's value. It is felt most sharply in **bonds and other fixed-income securities**, which have an **inverse relationship with interest rates**: when market rates rise, the prices of existing bonds fall (their fixed coupons become less attractive than newly issued bonds), and when rates fall, existing bond prices rise. The longer a bond's maturity (and duration) and the lower its coupon, the greater its price swing for a given change in rates. Fixed-rate **preferred stock** and rate-sensitive equities (utilities, REITs) also carry meaningful interest rate risk."},
  {"id":95,"part":"Part I","section":"D. Types of Risk","anchor":"d-types-of-risk","text":"**Exam tip:** \"Interest rates up, bond prices down.\" The bond with the **longest maturity and lowest coupon** has the **greatest** interest rate (price) risk; a short-term, high-coupon bond has the least. A closely related idea is *reinvestment risk* — the risk that coupon payments must be reinvested at lower rates when rates fall (zero-coupon bonds have no reinvestment risk because they pay no coupons to reinvest)."},
  {"id":96,"part":"Part I","section":"D. Types of Risk","anchor":"d-types-of-risk","text":"**Purchasing-power (inflation) risk.** This is the risk that inflation erodes the real value — the buying power — of an investment's future dollars. It is the classic enemy of **fixed-income investments and cash**: a bond's fixed coupon and a fixed annuity's level payment buy less and less as prices rise. Assets whose values tend to rise with inflation — **common stock, real estate, commodities, and TIPS** — are considered better inflation hedges.\n\nTo see how much inflation costs an investor, convert a *nominal* return into a *real* (inflation-adjusted) return:\n\n> **Real Return ≈ Nominal Return − Inflation Rate**"},
  {"id":97,"part":"Part I","section":"D. Types of Risk","anchor":"d-types-of-risk","text":"*Worked example:* A bond yields a nominal 5% while inflation runs at 3%. The approximate real return is 5% − 3% = **2%**. (The precise Fisher calculation is (1.05 ÷ 1.03) − 1 = 1.94%, but the exam accepts the simple subtraction.)\n\n**Exam tip:** When a question asks which security has the **greatest purchasing-power risk**, the answer is almost always a **long-term fixed-rate bond or a fixed annuity**. **Common trap:** Do not confuse inflation risk with interest rate risk. Interest rate risk is about a bond's *price* changing when *rates* move; inflation risk is about the *real value* of the returns being eroded even if the price never changes."},
  {"id":98,"part":"Part I","section":"D. Types of Risk","anchor":"d-types-of-risk","text":"**Sector risk.** This is the risk that a specific industry or sector — technology, energy, financials, biotech — declines as a group because of forces affecting the whole industry (regulation, commodity prices, technological shifts). The outline classifies it among the systematic risks because a portfolio concentrated in one sector bears the full brunt of an industry-wide downturn no matter which particular companies it holds; concentration in a single sector cannot be diversified away by adding more names within that same sector. Spreading holdings across *different* sectors is what reduces exposure to any one industry."},
  {"id":99,"part":"Part I","section":"D. Types of Risk","anchor":"d-types-of-risk","text":"**Geopolitical risk.** The risk that political or global events — wars, elections, changes in government, sanctions, trade policy, terrorism, or international instability — move markets broadly. Its close relatives include *political risk* (especially acute in emerging or foreign markets) and, for overseas investing, *currency/exchange-rate risk*.\n\n#### Unsystematic (Diversifiable) Risks\n\nThese risks strike individual companies or industries, so an investor can wash them out by holding many different, unrelated securities."},
  {"id":100,"part":"Part I","section":"D. Types of Risk","anchor":"d-types-of-risk","text":"**Business risk and financial risk.** These are two distinct company-level risks:\n- **Business risk** is the operating risk inherent to what the company does — competition, poor management, weak product demand, rising costs. Every operating company has business risk.\n- **Financial risk** is the risk created by the company's use of **debt (leverage)** in its capital structure. The more a firm borrows, the larger its fixed interest obligations, and the greater the chance it cannot meet them in a downturn. A highly leveraged company (high debt-to-equity) has high financial risk; an all-equity firm has **no financial risk** (though it still has business risk)."},
  {"id":101,"part":"Part I","section":"D. Types of Risk","anchor":"d-types-of-risk","text":"**Exam tip:** If a question ties the risk to the *amount of borrowing or leverage*, the answer is **financial risk**."},
  {"id":102,"part":"Part I","section":"D. Types of Risk","anchor":"d-types-of-risk","text":"**Credit (default) risk.** The risk that a bond issuer fails to make timely interest or principal payments (defaults), or that its creditworthiness deteriorates and the bond is **downgraded** (downgrade risk). Credit quality is graded by the rating agencies — **Moody's, Standard & Poor's, and Fitch**. Bonds rated **BBB−/Baa3 and above are \"investment grade\"**; below that they are **high-yield or \"junk\" bonds** carrying high default risk and paying higher yields to compensate. **U.S. Treasury securities are considered free of credit/default risk** because they are backed by the full faith and credit of the U.S. government."},
  {"id":103,"part":"Part I","section":"D. Types of Risk","anchor":"d-types-of-risk","text":"**Common trap:** Treasuries have essentially **no** credit risk, but they still carry **full interest rate risk and purchasing-power risk** — a long Treasury bond can still lose value when rates rise or fail to keep pace with inflation.\n\n**Legal/regulatory risk.** The risk that a change in laws, regulations, or tax policy — or an adverse court or regulatory action — hurts an investment or an entire industry. Also called *legislative risk*. Example: new environmental rules that raise costs across a mining sector, or a tax-law change that removes a benefit an investment relied on."},
  {"id":104,"part":"Part I","section":"D. Types of Risk","anchor":"d-types-of-risk","text":"**Issuer-specific risk.** The risk unique to a single issuer — a product recall, accounting scandal, lawsuit, fraud, or loss of a key executive. This is the textbook example of diversifiable risk: spreading money across many unrelated issuers means one company's bad news barely dents the portfolio.\n\n#### Opportunity Cost\n\n**Opportunity cost** is the return an investor gives up by choosing one investment (or holding cash) instead of the next-best alternative. It is not money paid out of pocket — it is a *forgone* return, which is exactly why it is easy to overlook.\n\n> **Opportunity Cost = Return on Best Forgone Alternative − Return on Chosen Investment**"},
  {"id":105,"part":"Part I","section":"D. Types of Risk","anchor":"d-types-of-risk","text":"*Worked example:* An investor keeps cash in a savings account paying 1% when a comparable, essentially risk-free 91-day Treasury bill yields 4.5%. The opportunity cost of staying in savings is 4.5% − 1% = **3.5% per year** in forgone return.\n\nThe **risk-free rate**, typically proxied by the **91-day (13-week) U.S. Treasury bill**, is the baseline opportunity cost of investing. Because investors could always earn that rate with virtually no risk, they demand a **risk premium** above it before accepting a riskier investment."},
  {"id":106,"part":"Part I","section":"D. Types of Risk","anchor":"d-types-of-risk","text":"**Exam tip:** Opportunity cost underlies required-return and discount-rate questions — the rate used to discount future cash flows in a present-value calculation reflects the opportunity cost of capital. **Common trap:** Opportunity cost is a *forgone* return, not a cash expense; a \"free\" decision to sit in cash still has a real cost.\n\n#### Capital Structure and Liquidation Priority"},
  {"id":107,"part":"Part I","section":"D. Types of Risk","anchor":"d-types-of-risk","text":"A firm's **capital structure** is the mix of debt and equity it uses to finance itself. That mix determines the **liquidation priority** — the order in which claimants are paid if the company is dissolved or goes bankrupt. Claims are settled from most senior (paid first, lowest risk) to most junior (paid last, highest risk):"},
  {"id":108,"part":"Part I","section":"D. Types of Risk","anchor":"d-types-of-risk","text":"| Priority | Claimant | What it is |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 (paid first) | **Secured creditors** | Bondholders backed by specific collateral — mortgage bonds, equipment trust certificates, collateral trust bonds |\n| 2 | **Unsecured creditors / general creditors** | Debenture holders (backed only by the issuer's full faith and credit) and trade/general creditors |\n| 3 | **Subordinated debt holders** | Junior debt that ranks behind general creditors |\n| 4 | **Preferred stockholders** | Equity, but paid ahead of common |\n| 5 (paid last) | **Common stockholders** | Residual claim — whatever is left, if anything |"},
  {"id":109,"part":"Part I","section":"D. Types of Risk","anchor":"d-types-of-risk","text":"The rule of thumb: **all debt is paid before any equity, secured debt before unsecured debt, and preferred stock before common stock. Common stock is always last.**\n\nBecause junior claimants absorb losses first, they demand higher expected returns — which is why **common stock has the highest risk (and highest potential return), preferred stock is in between, and secured bonds have the lowest**. This links back to *financial risk* (heavy debt increases the fixed claims ahead of stockholders) and to *capital risk* (the risk of losing invested principal, greatest for common shareholders)."},
  {"id":110,"part":"Part I","section":"D. Types of Risk","anchor":"d-types-of-risk","text":"**Common trap:** Preferred stock is **equity**, not debt. Despite its fixed dividend and \"senior\" name, it ranks **below every bond and general creditor** — it is senior only to common stock. **Exam tip:** For the Series 65, apply the securities-holder order shown above — secured debt → unsecured/general creditors → subordinated debt → preferred → common."},
  {"id":111,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"\"Cash and cash equivalents\" is the most conservative rung of the asset ladder. It covers the actual money in a bank account plus a family of very short-term, very safe debt instruments that can be converted into cash quickly and with little or no loss of principal. On the Series 65 these instruments matter for two reasons: first, they are the parking place investors use for money they cannot afford to put at risk (emergency reserves, funds needed soon, the \"safe\" sleeve of an allocation), and second, they are the classic answer whenever a question asks which security carries the **least** market risk, the **most** liquidity, or the **shortest** maturity. Master the definitions and the comparisons and this subsection becomes easy points."},
  {"id":112,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"#### General Characteristics of Cash Equivalents\n\nEvery cash equivalent shares three defining traits. Memorize these as a package, because exam questions frequently describe an instrument by these characteristics and ask you to identify it as a cash equivalent (or vice versa)."},
  {"id":113,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"- **High liquidity.** The instrument can be turned into cash quickly at a price at or very close to its full value. There is either an active secondary market (as with Treasury bills) or an on-demand claim against a bank (as with a checking account).\n- **Short maturity.** Cash equivalents are short-term debt. The general benchmark is a maturity of **one year or less**; for accounting (\"cash equivalent\") purposes the stricter definition is an **original maturity of 90 days (three months) or less**. Short maturity is the key to safety: because the money comes back so soon, the price barely moves when interest rates change.\n- **Low risk.** Two kinds of risk are minimal. **Default (credit) risk** is low because issuers are the U.S. government, insured banks, or the strongest corporations. **Interest-rate (market) risk** is low because of the short maturity — there is very little duration for rising rates to erode."},
  {"id":114,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"**Exam tip:** The reason cash equivalents have almost no interest-rate risk is their short maturity. A longer-maturity bond has more interest-rate risk; a 90-day T-bill has almost none. If a question links \"long maturity\" to \"cash equivalent,\" that answer is wrong.\n\n**Common trap:** Low risk is not **no** risk. The biggest genuine risks for cash equivalents are **purchasing-power (inflation) risk** and **reinvestment risk** — the yields are so low that inflation can erode real value, and when the instrument matures the investor may only be able to reinvest at a lower rate. Watch for questions where the \"conservative\" money-market investor is actually most exposed to inflation risk."},
  {"id":115,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"#### Insured Deposits: Demand Deposits\n\nA **demand deposit** is money held at a bank that the depositor can withdraw \"on demand\" — at any time, without notice and without penalty. The classic example is a **checking account**; savings and NOW accounts offer similarly ready access to funds. Because the balance is payable immediately, a demand deposit is among the most liquid assets there is. In exchange for that instant access, demand deposits pay little or no interest.\n\nKey points for the exam:\n\n- No maturity date — the funds are available at all times.\n- Highest liquidity, lowest (often zero) yield.\n- Covered by **FDIC insurance** when held at an FDIC-member bank (see below)."},
  {"id":116,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"#### Insured Deposits: Certificates of Deposit (CDs)\n\nA **certificate of deposit** is a **time deposit**: the depositor agrees to leave a fixed sum with the bank for a fixed term (for example, 6 months, 1 year, 5 years) in return for a stated interest rate. Because the bank has use of the money for a set period, a CD generally pays more than a demand deposit. The critical distinction on the Series 65 is between **non-negotiable** and **negotiable** CDs.\n\n##### Non-negotiable CDs"},
  {"id":117,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"This is the ordinary retail bank CD. Its defining feature is that it **cannot be sold or transferred** to another investor — there is no secondary market. If the depositor needs the money before maturity, the only way out is to redeem it early with the issuing bank and pay an **early-withdrawal penalty** (typically a forfeiture of some months of interest). Non-negotiable CDs are usually smaller retail amounts and are **FDIC-insured up to the coverage limit**.\n\n##### Negotiable CDs (Jumbo CDs)"},
  {"id":118,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"A **negotiable CD** is a large-denomination CD that **can be traded in the secondary market** before maturity. Because it is negotiable, a holder who needs cash early does not pay an early-withdrawal penalty — instead, the holder **sells** the CD to another investor at the prevailing market price. Key features:"},
  {"id":119,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"- Large minimum denomination — commonly **$100,000 or more**, frequently issued in $1,000,000 amounts (hence \"jumbo\").\n- Actively traded, so it is a **money market instrument**.\n- Maturities are usually one year or less (though they can be longer).\n- Because it trades, its market value fluctuates with interest rates, so a seller before maturity bears **interest-rate/market risk** and may receive more or less than face value.\n\nThe table below summarizes the distinction — a favorite exam comparison."},
  {"id":120,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"| Feature | Non-negotiable CD | Negotiable (jumbo) CD |\n|---|---|---|\n| Secondary market? | No | Yes — can be traded |\n| Typical size | Retail (small) | $100,000+, often $1 million |\n| Early access to cash | Redeem early with penalty | Sell in the market (no penalty) |\n| Price risk before maturity | None (fixed redemption) | Yes — value moves with rates |\n| Money market instrument? | No | Yes |\n| FDIC insured | Yes, up to the limit | Yes, up to the limit; excess uninsured |"},
  {"id":121,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"**Common trap:** Investors sometimes assume a jumbo negotiable CD is fully insured. FDIC coverage is capped (currently $250,000 per depositor, per bank, per ownership category), so on a $1,000,000 negotiable CD only $250,000 is FDIC-insured — the remaining $750,000 is exposed to the bank's credit. Its safety beyond the cap depends on the issuing bank, not the FDIC.\n\n#### FDIC Insurance"},
  {"id":122,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"The **Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC)** insures **deposits** at member banks against bank failure. The standard, commonly tested coverage limit is **$250,000 per depositor, per insured bank, per ownership category**. Because the limit is applied per ownership category (individual, joint, certain retirement, revocable trust, etc.), a household can lawfully obtain coverage above $250,000 at a single bank by titling accounts in different categories.\n\nWhat FDIC covers vs. what it does not is a frequent exam point:"},
  {"id":123,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"- **Covered:** demand deposits (checking), savings accounts, and CDs (both negotiable and non-negotiable) held at an FDIC-member bank — up to the limit.\n- **NOT covered:** securities of any kind, including **stocks, bonds, mutual funds, and money market mutual funds**, even when purchased through a bank. Commercial paper and Treasury bills are also **not** FDIC-insured (T-bills instead carry the direct guarantee of the U.S. government)."},
  {"id":124,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"**Exam tip:** FDIC insures **deposits**, not **investments**. If an item is a security, FDIC does not stand behind it. A money market **mutual fund** (an investment company product) is not the same as a money market **deposit account** — the fund is not FDIC-insured.\n\n#### Money Market Instruments: Commercial Paper"},
  {"id":125,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"**Commercial paper** is short-term, **unsecured** debt issued by large, creditworthy **corporations** (and some other institutions) to finance short-term needs such as payroll, inventory, and accounts receivable. Because it is unsecured, it is backed only by the general credit and reputation of the issuer, so only financially strong companies can issue it cheaply. Key features tested on the Series 65:"},
  {"id":126,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"- **Maximum maturity of 270 days (nine months).** Issuers keep it at or under 270 days so it qualifies for an exemption from registration under the Securities Act of 1933; most commercial paper matures in a matter of days to a few months.\n- Issued at a **discount** to face value (like a zero-coupon instrument): the investor pays less than par and receives par at maturity, and the difference is the interest earned. (Some paper is interest-bearing, but the discount form is standard.)\n- Large denominations, typically **$100,000 or more**, so it is largely an institutional instrument.\n- Rated by the rating agencies; the highest short-term tiers (e.g., A-1/P-1) signal top quality."},
  {"id":127,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"**Exam tip — the Uniform Securities Act exemption.** Commercial paper is an **exempt security** under the USA when it meets all three commonly tested conditions: a maturity of **nine months (270 days) or less**, issued in denominations of **$50,000 or more**, and **rated in one of the top three rating categories** by a nationally recognized rating agency. Being \"exempt\" means it is exempt from state **registration and advertising-filing** requirements — it is **not** exempt from the antifraud provisions.\n\n#### Money Market Instruments: Treasury Bills (T-bills)"},
  {"id":128,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"**Treasury bills** are short-term debt obligations of the **U.S. federal government**. They are the benchmark \"risk-free\" asset because they are backed by the **full faith and credit of the United States**, so their default risk is treated as effectively zero. Key features:"},
  {"id":129,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"- **Maturities of one year or less** — commonly issued in terms of 4, 13, 26, and 52 weeks (the Treasury also issues certain other short terms).\n- Sold at a **discount** and mature at **par (face value)**; T-bills pay **no periodic coupon**. The investor's return is the difference between the discounted purchase price and the face value received at maturity. This is why T-bills are quoted on a **discount basis**.\n- Purchased at auction; highly liquid with a deep, active secondary market.\n- **Taxation:** the interest (the discount earned) is **subject to federal income tax but exempt from state and local income tax** — the standard treatment for all direct U.S. Treasury obligations. This state-tax exemption is a frequent exam point."},
  {"id":130,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"**Common trap:** T-bills are essentially free of **default** risk, but they are **not** free of all risk — they still carry **inflation/purchasing-power risk** and **reinvestment risk**. \"Risk-free\" on the exam specifically means free of credit/default risk, not free of every risk.\n\n#### Calculating the Yield on a Discount Instrument\n\nBecause T-bills (and discount commercial paper) have no coupon, their yield comes entirely from buying below par and being redeemed at par. Two calculations appear in test-prep material.\n\n**1. Bank discount yield** — the conventional quoting method, which uses face value and a 360-day year:"},
  {"id":131,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"Bank Discount Yield = (Discount / Face Value) x (360 / Days to Maturity)\n\nwhere Discount = Face Value − Purchase Price.\n\nWorked example: A $10,000 face-value T-bill is purchased for $9,800 with 180 days to maturity.\n- Discount = $10,000 − $9,800 = $200\n- Bank Discount Yield = ($200 / $10,000) x (360 / 180) = 0.02 x 2 = **4.00%**\n\n**2. Investment (bond-equivalent) yield** — a truer measure of the investor's return, because it uses the actual amount invested (the purchase price) and a 365-day year:\n\nInvestment Yield = (Discount / Purchase Price) x (365 / Days to Maturity)\n\nUsing the same numbers:\n- Investment Yield = ($200 / $9,800) x (365 / 180) = 0.020408 x 2.0278 = **4.14%**"},
  {"id":132,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"Notice the investment yield (4.14%) is higher than the quoted bank discount yield (4.00%). That is always the case, for two reasons: the investment yield divides the gain by the **smaller** amount actually paid ($9,800, not $10,000), and it uses a **365-day** year instead of 360.\n\n**Exam tip:** You are far more likely to be tested on the **concepts** — that T-bills are sold at a discount, pay no coupon, are quoted on a discount basis, and that their price rises toward par as maturity approaches — than to grind through a full bank-discount calculation. Know that a discount instrument's price is **below par** and that its return is the discount, and you will handle most questions."},
  {"id":133,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"#### Putting It Together: Cash Equivalents Compared\n\n| Instrument | Issuer | Maturity | Return form | Insured/Guaranteed | Tradable (money market)? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Demand deposit (checking) | Bank | None (on demand) | Little/no interest | FDIC to limit | No |\n| Non-negotiable CD | Bank | Fixed term | Stated interest | FDIC to limit | No |\n| Negotiable (jumbo) CD | Bank | Usually ≤ 1 yr | Stated interest | FDIC to limit; excess uninsured | Yes |\n| Commercial paper | Corporation | ≤ 270 days | Discount (usually) | None — issuer credit | Yes |\n| Treasury bill | U.S. government | ≤ 1 year | Discount | U.S. full faith & credit | Yes |"},
  {"id":134,"part":"Part II","section":"A. Cash and Cash Equivalents","anchor":"a-cash-and-cash-equivalents","text":"**Exam tip:** When a question asks for the **safest** or **most liquid** security overall, the answer is almost always the **Treasury bill** — it combines a short maturity, the deepest market, and the direct backing of the U.S. government. When it asks which cash instrument lets an investor sell before maturity without a penalty, look for the **negotiable CD** or a **Treasury bill**, not the ordinary bank CD."},
  {"id":135,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"Fixed income securities are debt instruments. When you buy a bond, you are lending money to the issuer in exchange for a promise to pay periodic interest (the coupon) and to return the face amount (par, typically $1,000) at maturity. This subsection covers the major issuer categories the Series 65 tests: U.S. government securities, asset-backed securities, corporate bonds, municipal bonds, and foreign-issued bonds. Because every bond type shares the same pricing and yield mechanics, we start with the fundamentals that recur throughout the exam.\n\n#### Fixed Income Basics: Price, Yield, and Quotes"},
  {"id":136,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"A bond's **par value** (face value) is usually $1,000 and is the amount repaid at maturity. The **coupon rate** (also called the nominal yield) is the fixed annual interest as a percentage of par. A 5% bond pays $50 per year per $1,000 bond, almost always in two semiannual installments of $25.\n\nBond prices move inversely to interest rates: when market rates rise, existing bond prices fall, and vice versa. A bond trading below par is at a **discount**; above par, a **premium**.\n\n**Current Yield** measures annual coupon income relative to what you pay for the bond:\n\n- Current Yield = Annual Coupon Income / Current Market Price"},
  {"id":137,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"Worked example: A bond has a 5% coupon ($50 annual income) and trades at $950.\nCurrent Yield = 50 / 950 = 0.0526 = **5.26%**.\n\nThe current yield (5.26%) exceeds the coupon (5%) because the bond was bought at a discount. This produces one of the most heavily tested relationships on the exam:\n\n| Bond trading at | Order of yields (lowest to highest) |\n|---|---|\n| Discount (below par) | Nominal (coupon) < Current Yield < Yield to Maturity < Yield to Call |\n| Premium (above par) | Yield to Call < Yield to Maturity < Current Yield < Nominal (coupon) |"},
  {"id":138,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** Yield to maturity (YTM) accounts for the coupon *plus* the gain or loss from buying below or above par and holding to maturity. Yield to call (YTC) assumes the issuer redeems the bond early at the call date. For a premium bond, YTC is the *lowest* yield an investor should expect (\"yield to worst\").\n\nQuote conventions differ by issuer and are frequently tested:"},
  {"id":139,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"- **Treasury notes and bonds** are quoted as a percentage of par in **32nds**. A quote of \"99-16\" (sometimes written 99:16) means 99 and 16/32 = 99.50% of par = **$995.00** per $1,000 bond.\n- **Treasury bills** are quoted on a **discount yield** basis, not as a price.\n- **Corporate bonds** are quoted as a percentage of par; **municipal bonds** are often quoted on a yield (basis) basis.\n\nAccrued interest day-count conventions also appear: **Treasuries accrue on an actual/actual basis; corporate and municipal bonds use 30/360.**\n\n#### U.S. Government Securities: Treasury Bills, Notes, and Bonds"},
  {"id":140,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"Marketable U.S. Treasury securities are direct obligations backed by the **full faith and credit of the U.S. government**. They are considered effectively free of **credit (default) risk**, which makes them the benchmark \"risk-free\" investment. They are *not* free of all risk—longer maturities still carry **interest rate risk** and **purchasing power (inflation) risk**."},
  {"id":141,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"| Security | Maturity at issue | Pays a coupon? | How sold |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Treasury bill (T-bill) | 1 year or less (4, 13, 26, 52 weeks) | No — issued at a discount | Auctioned at a discount to par; return = par minus purchase price |\n| Treasury note (T-note) | 2 to 10 years | Yes — semiannual | At or near par |\n| Treasury bond (T-bond) | More than 10 years (20 and 30 years) | Yes — semiannual | At or near par |\n\nA **T-bill** pays no coupon. You buy it below par and receive full par at maturity; the difference is your interest. **T-notes and T-bonds** pay semiannual interest and return par at maturity."},
  {"id":142,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**STRIPS** (Separate Trading of Registered Interest and Principal Securities) are zero-coupon Treasuries created by \"stripping\" the coupon and principal payments and selling them separately. Like all zero-coupon bonds, they are bought at a deep discount and generate **imputed (\"phantom\") interest**—the annual accretion is taxed each year even though no cash is received until maturity.\n\nTaxation is a critical, high-yield exam point: **interest on all U.S. Treasury securities is subject to federal income tax but is exempt from state and local income tax.**"},
  {"id":143,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**Common trap:** Students reverse the tax treatment of Treasuries and municipals. Treasury interest is **federally taxable, state/local exempt**. Municipal interest is the mirror image—**federally exempt, potentially state taxable**. Memorize the two as opposites.\n\n#### Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities (TIPS)"},
  {"id":144,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"TIPS are Treasury securities designed to protect investors from **inflation (purchasing power) risk**. The coupon *rate* is fixed, but the **principal is adjusted for inflation** based on changes in the Consumer Price Index (CPI). Because the fixed rate is applied to a rising principal balance, the *dollar amount* of each semiannual interest payment rises with inflation (and can fall during deflation).\n\nWorked example: An investor buys $1,000 of TIPS with a 4% coupon. Over the first year, inflation is 5%.\n\n- Adjusted principal = $1,000 × 1.05 = **$1,050**.\n- Annual interest = 4% × $1,050 = **$42** (versus $40 on the un-adjusted principal)."},
  {"id":145,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"In practice the CPI adjustment and the 2% (half of 4%) semiannual coupon are applied every six months, but the concept is the same: principal grows with inflation, and interest is calculated on the higher balance.\n\nAt maturity, the investor receives the **greater of the inflation-adjusted principal or the original par**—a built-in **deflation floor** guaranteeing you never receive less than your original principal.\n\n**Common trap:** The annual upward adjustment to principal is **taxable in the year it occurs**, even though the investor does not receive that principal until maturity. This is another form of **phantom income**, which is why TIPS are often held in tax-deferred accounts."},
  {"id":146,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** If a question asks which security best protects a client worried that rising prices will erode their fixed income, the answer is **TIPS**. If the concern is a bond with *no* reinvestment risk and a locked-in return, that points to zero-coupon bonds/STRIPS—but those offer no inflation protection.\n\n#### Asset-Backed Securities (ABS)"},
  {"id":147,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"An asset-backed security is created by pooling many individual income-producing assets and selling investors a claim on the cash flows those assets generate. The pooled collateral can be auto loans, credit card receivables, student loans, equipment leases, or—most commonly tested—home mortgages. Turning illiquid loans into tradable securities is called **securitization**.\n\n**Mortgage-backed securities (MBS)** are the classic ABS. In a **pass-through** security, homeowners' monthly payments of principal and interest flow through to investors monthly. The main agency issuers are frequently tested on their differing government backing:"},
  {"id":148,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"| Issuer | Nickname | Government backing |\n|---|---|---|\n| GNMA (Government National Mortgage Association) | Ginnie Mae | Directly backed by the **full faith and credit of the U.S. government** |\n| FNMA (Federal National Mortgage Association) | Fannie Mae | Government-sponsored enterprise (GSE); **not** directly backed by the U.S. government |\n| FHLMC (Federal Home Loan Mortgage Corp.) | Freddie Mac | Government-sponsored enterprise (GSE); **not** directly backed by the U.S. government |\n\n**Collateralized mortgage obligations (CMOs)** repackage mortgage cash flows into different maturity classes called **tranches**, allowing investors to choose their preferred risk and maturity profile."},
  {"id":149,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"Two risks unique to mortgage-backed products are essential exam knowledge:\n\n- **Prepayment risk:** When interest rates *fall*, homeowners refinance and pay off their mortgages early. Investors get their principal back sooner and must reinvest it at *lower* prevailing rates.\n- **Extension risk:** When interest rates *rise*, homeowners pay off mortgages more slowly, so principal is returned later than expected—leaving the investor locked into a below-market yield for longer."},
  {"id":150,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** Only **GNMA** carries the direct full-faith-and-credit guarantee of the U.S. government. A common distractor treats Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac as government-guaranteed—they are not. Also remember that CMO cash flows are paid **monthly**, and that interest on agency MBS is generally **fully taxable at both the federal and state levels**, unlike direct Treasuries.\n\n#### Corporate Bonds"},
  {"id":151,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"Corporate bonds are debt obligations issued by companies to raise capital. They carry higher yields than Treasuries because they expose investors to **credit (default) risk**—the risk that the issuer cannot make interest or principal payments. Corporate bond interest is **fully taxable** at the federal, state, and local levels.\n\nCorporate debt is classified by whether it is backed by specific collateral:"},
  {"id":152,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"- **Secured bonds** are backed by specific assets. Examples: **mortgage bonds** (backed by real property), **equipment trust certificates** (backed by equipment such as railcars or aircraft), and **collateral trust bonds** (backed by securities the issuer owns).\n- **Unsecured bonds** are backed only by the issuer's general creditworthiness. A **debenture** is an unsecured corporate bond; a **subordinated debenture** ranks *below* other debt in a liquidation and therefore pays a higher yield."},
  {"id":153,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"Bonds may also be **convertible** (exchangeable for the issuer's common stock), **callable** (the issuer can redeem them early, usually when rates fall), or **puttable** (the investor can require early redemption).\n\nCreditworthiness is measured by ratings agencies (Moody's, Standard & Poor's, Fitch). The dividing line the exam tests is **investment grade versus high-yield (\"junk\")**:\n\n| Category | Standard & Poor's / Fitch | Moody's |\n|---|---|---|\n| Investment grade (top four tiers) | AAA, AA, A, BBB | Aaa, Aa, A, Baa |\n| Below investment grade / high-yield | BB and below | Ba and below |"},
  {"id":154,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** The lowest investment-grade rating is **BBB (S&P/Fitch) / Baa (Moody's)**. Anything below that is speculative \"junk,\" offering higher yield to compensate for higher default risk.\n\n**Common trap:** In a corporate liquidation, the priority of claims is tested: secured creditors, then unsecured creditors (general creditors, including debenture holders), then subordinated debt holders, then preferred stockholders, and finally common stockholders. Bondholders always rank ahead of stockholders.\n\n#### Municipal Bonds: General Obligation, Revenue, and Insured"},
  {"id":155,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"Municipal bonds (\"munis\") are issued by states, cities, counties, and other local government entities to fund public projects. Their defining feature is **federal tax-exempt interest** (covered in detail below). Munis fall into three exam-critical categories."},
  {"id":156,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**General Obligation (GO) bonds** are backed by the **full faith, credit, and taxing power** of the issuer. Repayment comes from the issuer's general tax revenues—**ad valorem (property) taxes** for local issuers, and income or sales taxes for states. Because GO bonds obligate the taxpayers, they typically require **voter approval** and are subject to statutory **debt limits**. They are generally considered safer than revenue bonds of the same issuer."},
  {"id":157,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**Revenue bonds** are backed **only by the revenue generated by a specific project or facility**—a toll road, airport, hospital, water/sewer system, or stadium. They are **not** backed by taxing power, do **not** require voter approval, and are **not** subject to debt limits. Before issuance, a **feasibility study** estimates whether the project's revenue will be sufficient. Because repayment depends entirely on the project's success, revenue bonds generally carry **more credit risk and higher yields** than GO bonds."},
  {"id":158,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"| Feature | General Obligation (GO) | Revenue |\n|---|---|---|\n| Backed by | Full faith, credit, and taxing power | Revenue from a specific project |\n| Repayment source | General taxes (e.g., property taxes) | User fees/tolls from the facility |\n| Voter approval | Usually required | Not required |\n| Subject to debt limits | Yes | No |\n| Analysis focuses on | Issuer's tax base and finances | Project feasibility/revenue |\n| Relative risk | Generally lower | Generally higher |"},
  {"id":159,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**Insured municipal bonds** carry a guarantee from a private bond insurer (historically firms such as MBIA and AMBAC) that interest and principal will be paid even if the issuer defaults. Insurance typically **raises the bond's credit rating** (often to the insurer's higher rating) and therefore **lowers its yield**—investors accept less income in exchange for the added safety.\n\nShort-term municipal financing is done with **municipal notes** such as TANs, RANs, BANs, and TRANs (Tax, Revenue, Bond, and Tax & Revenue Anticipation Notes), which are repaid from expected future taxes, revenues, or a permanent bond issue.\n\n#### Municipal Bond Tax Implications and Tax-Equivalent Yield"},
  {"id":160,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"The central appeal of municipal bonds is that their **interest is exempt from federal income tax**. If an investor buys a bond issued *within their own state of residence*, the interest is usually also exempt from that **state's** income tax (\"double tax-exempt\"). Bonds from a different state are federally exempt but generally **state-taxable**.\n\n**Common trap:** Only the *interest* is tax-exempt. Any **capital gain** from selling a municipal bond at a profit (or having it redeemed above your cost) **is taxable**. A question describing an investor who buys a muni at a discount and sells at a gain is testing whether you know the gain is taxable even though the coupon interest is not."},
  {"id":161,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**Common trap:** Interest on certain **private activity bonds** (municipal bonds financing private projects) may be a preference item subject to the **alternative minimum tax (AMT)**. Not all municipal interest is entirely tax-free for every investor.\n\nBecause their interest escapes federal tax, munis pay lower nominal yields than comparable taxable bonds. To compare a tax-free muni against a taxable bond, use the **tax-equivalent yield (TEY)**:\n\n- Tax-Equivalent Yield = Municipal (Tax-Free) Yield / (1 − Investor's Marginal Tax Rate)"},
  {"id":162,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"Worked example: A municipal bond yields 4%. The investor is in the 32% federal marginal tax bracket.\nTEY = 0.04 / (1 − 0.32) = 0.04 / 0.68 = 0.0588 = **5.88%**.\n\nInterpretation: This investor would need a **fully taxable** bond yielding 5.88% to match the after-tax return of the 4% muni. If a comparable corporate bond yields only 5.5%, the muni is the better choice for this investor.\n\nThe reverse calculation converts a taxable yield into its after-tax equivalent:\n\n- After-Tax Yield = Taxable Yield × (1 − Marginal Tax Rate)"},
  {"id":163,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"Worked example: A corporate bond yields 5.5% for an investor in the 32% bracket.\nAfter-Tax Yield = 0.055 × (1 − 0.32) = 0.055 × 0.68 = 0.0374 = **3.74%**, which is *less* than the muni's 4% tax-free yield—confirming the muni wins for this high-bracket investor.\n\n**Exam tip:** The higher an investor's tax bracket, the more valuable tax-free municipal interest becomes. Municipal bonds are most suitable for **high-tax-bracket investors** and are generally **inappropriate inside tax-deferred accounts** (like IRAs), where the tax exemption is wasted and the investor gives up higher taxable yields for no benefit.\n\n#### Foreign-Issued Bonds: Sovereign and Corporate Debt"},
  {"id":164,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"Foreign bonds are debt securities issued by governments or corporations outside the United States. They can enhance yield and provide diversification, but they add risks not present in domestic bonds—most importantly **currency (exchange rate) risk** (the value of foreign-currency interest and principal changes as exchange rates move) and **political risk** (instability, expropriation, or changes in law in the issuer's country)."},
  {"id":165,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**Sovereign (government) debt** is issued by national governments. Examples include UK **gilts**, German **bunds**, and Japanese government bonds (**JGBs**). Credit quality varies enormously: debt of stable, developed economies may be very safe, while **emerging-market** sovereign debt can carry substantial default risk. A distinctive feature is that there is **no bankruptcy court for a nation**—if a sovereign defaults or restructures its debt, investors have limited legal recourse to compel repayment."},
  {"id":166,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**Foreign corporate debt** is issued by companies domiciled outside the U.S. It carries the same credit risk as domestic corporate debt *plus* currency and political risk, and issuers may report under **different accounting standards** and lighter regulation than U.S. companies.\n\nTwo related terms are commonly tested for how bonds are denominated and where they are sold:"},
  {"id":167,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"- **Yankee bonds:** issued by a **foreign** entity but denominated in **U.S. dollars**, registered with the SEC, and sold in the U.S. market. A U.S. investor buying a Yankee bond bears the *issuer's* credit risk but **no direct currency risk**, because payments are in dollars.\n- **Eurodollar bonds:** denominated in **U.S. dollars** but issued and sold **outside** the United States. More broadly, a **Eurobond** is denominated in a currency different from that of the country where it is sold."},
  {"id":168,"part":"Part II","section":"B. Types of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"b-types-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** The single most-tested risk for foreign bonds (other than Yankee bonds) is **currency/exchange rate risk**. If the foreign currency weakens against the dollar, a U.S. investor's interest and principal decline in dollar terms even if the issuer pays in full. The key exception is the Yankee bond, which removes currency risk for the U.S. investor by paying in dollars."},
  {"id":169,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"Fixed income securities (bonds) are debt instruments: the investor lends money to an issuer, and the issuer promises to pay periodic interest and to return the principal at maturity. On the Series 65, this topic is tested less through complicated math and more through your command of vocabulary, relationships, and a handful of formulas. If you understand how price, yield, coupon, and maturity fit together, most questions answer themselves. This subsection builds that framework piece by piece.\n\n#### The Building Blocks: Par, Coupon, and Maturity\n\nEvery bond has three defining features:"},
  {"id":170,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"- **Par value (face value):** the amount repaid at maturity, standardized at **$1,000** for most corporate and municipal bonds (Treasuries are also based on $1,000 par per bond). Par is the reference point for everything else.\n- **Coupon rate (nominal yield):** the fixed annual interest rate stated on the bond, expressed as a percentage of par. A 5% coupon on a $1,000 bond pays **$50 per year**, almost always split into two semiannual payments of $25.\n- **Maturity:** the date the issuer repays par and the bond ceases to exist.\n\nBonds are quoted as a **percentage of par**, where each \"point\" equals $10 (1% of $1,000). A bond quoted at 98 is priced at $980; a bond at 101.5 is priced at $1,015."},
  {"id":171,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** The coupon rate never changes over the life of a standard bond. What changes is the bond's market price, and therefore its yield. Keep \"coupon\" (fixed) and \"yield\" (moves with price) mentally separated.\n\n#### Coupon Bonds vs. Zero-Coupon Bonds\n\nA **coupon bond** makes periodic interest payments (typically semiannual) and returns par at maturity.\n\nA **zero-coupon bond** makes no periodic payments. It is issued at a deep **discount** to par and \"accretes\" up to par at maturity; the investor's entire return comes from the difference between the discounted purchase price and the $1,000 received at maturity. Treasury STRIPS are the classic example."},
  {"id":172,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"Key features of zeros the exam loves:\n\n- **No reinvestment risk on the coupons** (there are no coupons to reinvest), which makes them useful for funding a known future liability like a college tuition payment.\n- **Highest volatility for a given maturity.** Because all the cash flow arrives at the end, a zero has the **longest duration** of any bond of the same maturity, so its price swings the most when interest rates change.\n- **Phantom income.** The annual accretion (the imputed interest) on a taxable zero is taxed each year as ordinary income even though the investor receives no cash. This is a heavily tested trap (see Tax Implications below)."},
  {"id":173,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"#### Pricing: Par, Premium, and Discount, and the Price–Yield Seesaw\n\nThe single most important relationship in fixed income: **bond prices and yields move in opposite directions.** When market interest rates rise, existing bond prices fall; when rates fall, existing bond prices rise. Picture a seesaw with price on one end and yield on the other.\n\nWhere a bond trades relative to par is determined entirely by comparing its fixed coupon to current market yields:"},
  {"id":174,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"| Situation | Bond trades at | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Coupon = current market yield | **Par** ($1,000) | The bond is competitive as-is |\n| Coupon < current market yield | **Discount** (below $1,000) | A below-market coupon must be offset by a lower price |\n| Coupon > current market yield | **Premium** (above $1,000) | An above-market coupon is worth paying extra for |\n\n**Common trap:** Students assume a \"good\" bond trades at a premium because something is wrong with a discount bond. Not so. A bond trades at a discount simply because its coupon is lower than what newly issued bonds offer. The premium/discount is a pricing adjustment, not a quality judgment."},
  {"id":175,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"#### Yield: The Four Yields and the Yield Hierarchy\n\nThere are four yields tested on the Series 65. Learning them in order is the whole game.\n\n**1. Nominal yield (coupon yield).** Just the coupon rate — annual coupon divided by par. A 5% bond has a 5% nominal yield, always.\n\n**2. Current yield.** Annual coupon income as a percentage of the bond's current market price. This reflects the cash return you actually earn at today's price.\n\n> **Current Yield = Annual Coupon Income / Current Market Price**\n\n*Worked example:* A bond has a 6% coupon (so it pays $60 per year) and is currently trading at $800 (a discount).\n\nCurrent Yield = $60 / $800 = **0.075 = 7.5%**"},
  {"id":176,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"Notice the current yield (7.5%) is higher than the nominal yield (6%) because the bond is bought at a discount. If the same bond instead traded at a premium of $1,200: Current Yield = $60 / $1,200 = 5.0%, below the 6% coupon."},
  {"id":177,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**3. Yield to maturity (YTM).** The total annualized return if the bond is held to maturity, accounting for coupon income *plus* the gain (for a discount bond) or loss (for a premium bond) equal to the difference between purchase price and par, and assuming coupons are reinvested at the YTM. YTM is the bond's true \"market rate of return\" and is what people mean when they quote \"the yield\" on a bond. A discount bond has a built-in gain that pulls YTM above the current yield; a premium bond has a built-in loss that pulls YTM below the current yield."},
  {"id":178,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**4. Yield to call (YTC).** The annualized return if the bond is called (redeemed early) at the first call date rather than held to maturity. Calculated the same way as YTM but using the call date and the call price in place of the maturity date and par.\n\n**The yield hierarchy.** For a bond bought at a **discount**, the four yields line up in ascending order; for a bond bought at a **premium**, the exact reverse:\n\n| Bond bought at a... | Yield ordering (lowest to highest) |\n|---|---|\n| **Discount** | Nominal < Current < YTM < YTC |\n| **Premium** | YTC < YTM < Current < Nominal |\n| **Par** | Nominal = Current = YTM (≈ YTC) |"},
  {"id":179,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**Exam tip — how to remember it:** List the four yields in this fixed order — Nominal, Current, YTM, YTC. For a **discount** bond they rank from lowest to highest in exactly that order; for a **premium** bond the ranking is the mirror image (Nominal highest, YTC lowest). The logic behind the endpoints: for a discount bond, a call lets you capture the gain up to par *sooner*, which raises the annualized return, so **YTC is the highest**. For a premium bond, a call forces you to absorb the loss down to par sooner, which lowers the return, so **YTC is the lowest** — this is why YTC is the **worst-case yield** on a premium callable bond, disclosed as the \"yield to worst.\""},
  {"id":180,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**Common trap:** Expect a question asking \"which yield is highest (or lowest) on a premium bond bought at 105?\" The answer for *highest* is nominal yield; for *lowest* it is yield to call.\n\n#### Call Features\n\nA **callable bond** gives the issuer the right to redeem the bond before maturity, usually at par or at a small **call premium** (e.g., 102). Issuers call bonds when interest rates have fallen, so they can refinance at a lower coupon — which is exactly when the bondholder least wants to give the bond up.\n\nConsequences for the investor:"},
  {"id":181,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"- **Call risk / reinvestment risk:** the bondholder gets cash back early and must reinvest at lower prevailing rates.\n- **Capped upside:** a callable bond's price appreciation is limited near the call price, because buyers know it may be redeemed.\n- **Call protection:** many bonds have a period (e.g., the first 5–10 years) during which they cannot be called; this is valuable to investors.\n- Because of call risk, **yield to call** becomes the relevant valuation yield, especially for premium bonds."},
  {"id":182,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** Callable bonds carry a higher coupon than otherwise-identical non-callable bonds to compensate investors for accepting call risk. Contrast with a **put feature**, which benefits the investor (the holder can force early redemption) and therefore carries a *lower* coupon.\n\n#### Duration\n\n**Duration** is the weighted-average time (in years) to receive a bond's cash flows, and, more usefully, it is the standard measure of a bond's **interest rate sensitivity** — how much its price will move when yields change. Higher duration means greater price volatility.\n\nThe drivers of duration:"},
  {"id":183,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"- **Longer maturity → higher duration** (more sensitivity to rate changes).\n- **Lower coupon → higher duration** (less cash arrives early, so more of the value sits far in the future). A **zero-coupon bond's duration equals its maturity** — the maximum for any bond of that length.\n- **Higher market yields → slightly lower duration.**\n\n**Modified duration** translates this into an estimate: a bond with a modified duration of 7 will fall roughly **7% in price for a 1% (100 basis point) rise in yields**, and rise about 7% for a 1% drop."},
  {"id":184,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"*Quick illustration:* If interest rates rise 1%, a bond with a duration of 4 loses about 4% of its value, while a bond with a duration of 9 loses about 9%. Same rate move, very different pain.\n\n**Exam tip:** If a client fears rising interest rates, recommend **short-duration** (short-maturity, higher-coupon) bonds to reduce price risk. If a client wants to maximize price gains from an expected *drop* in rates, favor **long-duration** bonds (long maturity, low or zero coupon).\n\n#### Bond Ratings: Investment Grade vs. High Yield"},
  {"id":185,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"Independent agencies — **Moody's, Standard & Poor's, and Fitch** — assess an issuer's creditworthiness (its ability to pay interest and principal). Ratings sort bonds into two broad camps:\n\n| Grade | Moody's | S&P / Fitch |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Investment grade** (highest to lowest) | Aaa, Aa, A, **Baa** | AAA, AA, A, **BBB** |\n| **High yield / \"junk\"** (speculative) | Ba, B, Caa, Ca, C | BB, B, CCC, CC, C, D |\n\nThe dividing line is critical: the **lowest investment-grade rating is Baa3 (Moody's) / BBB– (S&P and Fitch)**. Anything below that is **high yield** (also called junk or speculative-grade), which pays a higher yield to compensate for higher default risk."},
  {"id":186,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** Memorize the cutoff. A bond rated **BB** or **Ba** is *below* investment grade. Many fiduciaries and regulated institutions (pension funds, insurers) are restricted to investment-grade holdings, so a downgrade from BBB– to BB+ (\"falling out of investment grade,\" a \"fallen angel\") can force selling and is a commonly tested consequence.\n\n#### Credit Spread\n\nThe **credit spread** is the difference in yield between a bond and a comparable-maturity U.S. Treasury (the risk-free benchmark). It isolates the extra yield investors demand for taking on credit (default) risk.\n\n> **Credit Spread = Yield on the bond − Yield on a same-maturity Treasury**"},
  {"id":187,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"If a 10-year corporate bond yields 6.0% and the 10-year Treasury yields 4.0%, the credit spread is **2.0% (200 basis points)**. **Widening spreads** signal rising perceived risk or economic stress (a \"flight to quality\" into Treasuries); **narrowing spreads** signal improving confidence. Lower-rated (high-yield) bonds carry wider spreads than investment-grade bonds.\n\n#### Market Liquidity"},
  {"id":188,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**Liquidity** is how quickly and cheaply a bond can be sold near its fair value. **U.S. Treasuries are the most liquid** securities in the world; large, highly rated corporate issues are reasonably liquid; small municipal issues and lower-rated corporates can be **thinly traded**, forcing a seller to accept a lower price (a wider bid-ask spread). This is **liquidity risk**, and it is one reason less-liquid bonds must offer higher yields to attract buyers.\n\n#### Liquidation Preference\n\nIf an issuer goes bankrupt, claims are paid in a fixed priority order. Bondholders (creditors) always rank **ahead of stockholders (owners)**. From highest to lowest priority:"},
  {"id":189,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"1. **Secured creditors** (e.g., mortgage bonds, equipment trust certificates — backed by specific collateral)\n2. **Unsecured creditors** — **debentures** (backed only by the issuer's general credit) and general creditors\n3. **Subordinated debentures** (junior debt, paid after senior debt)\n4. **Preferred stockholders**\n5. **Common stockholders** (last, residual claim)\n\n**Exam tip:** A **debenture** is an *unsecured* bond backed only by the issuer's full faith and credit — a frequent definitional question. Within debt, \"senior\" and \"subordinated\" describe rank; secured bonds outrank all unsecured debt.\n\n#### Conversion Valuation (Convertible Bonds)"},
  {"id":190,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"A **convertible bond** can be exchanged by the holder for a fixed number of shares of the issuer's common stock. This gives the investor upside participation in the stock while retaining a bond's interest and priority — so convertibles pay a **lower coupon** than comparable non-convertible bonds.\n\nTwo formulas drive the math:\n\n> **Conversion Ratio = Par Value ($1,000) / Conversion Price**\n>\n> **Parity (Conversion Value) = Conversion Ratio × Current Stock Price**\n\n*Worked example:* A $1,000 par convertible bond has a conversion price of $40."},
  {"id":191,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"- Conversion Ratio = $1,000 / $40 = **25 shares** per bond.\n- If the common stock trades at **$45**, the parity (conversion) value = 25 × $45 = **$1,125**. Converting yields $1,125 of stock, so the bond should trade at *at least* $1,125.\n- Working backward, if the bond trades at **$1,080**, the **parity price of the stock** = $1,080 / 25 = **$43.20**. The stock must reach $43.20 for the stock and bond to be at parity."},
  {"id":192,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** The bond and stock are \"at parity\" when converting produces neither a gain nor a loss. If the bond's market price is *above* parity, it is trading at a **conversion premium**. Watch for questions that give you the conversion price and stock price and ask for shares, parity value, or parity price.\n\n#### Tax Implications\n\nTaxation of interest income is one of the most tested fixed-income concepts:\n\n| Issuer | Federal tax on interest | State/local tax on interest |\n|---|---|---|\n| **U.S. Treasuries** | Taxable | **Exempt** |\n| **Corporate bonds** | Taxable | Taxable |\n| **Municipal bonds** | **Exempt** | Exempt in the issuer's home state; taxable if out-of-state |"},
  {"id":193,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"An in-state municipal bond can be **\"triple tax-free\"** (exempt from federal, state, and local tax) for a resident. Because muni interest is federally tax-exempt, it appeals most to investors in **high tax brackets**, and munis carry lower stated yields than comparable taxable bonds. To compare fairly, use the **taxable-equivalent yield (TEY)**:\n\n> **Taxable-Equivalent Yield = Municipal Yield / (1 − Investor's Marginal Tax Rate)**\n\n*Worked example:* A muni yields **4%** and the investor is in the **32%** federal bracket.\n\nTEY = 0.04 / (1 − 0.32) = 0.04 / 0.68 = **0.0588 = 5.88%**"},
  {"id":194,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"The investor would need a **5.88%** yield on a taxable corporate bond to net the same after-tax return as the 4% muni. If comparable corporates yield less than 5.88%, the muni is the better after-tax choice.\n\n**Common trap:** Municipal bond *interest* is federally tax-exempt, but a **capital gain** from selling a muni above your cost basis is **still taxable**. Tax-exempt applies to the interest, not to price appreciation."},
  {"id":195,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**Phantom income on zeros:** The annual accretion on a **taxable** zero-coupon bond (e.g., a corporate zero or Treasury STRIP) is taxed each year as it accrues, even though no cash is received — hence \"phantom income.\" A **municipal** zero, by contrast, accretes tax-free at the federal level. This makes tax-deferred accounts (IRAs) a natural home for taxable zeros.\n\n#### Discounted Cash Flow: How a Bond Is Actually Valued\n\nAt its core, a bond's fair price is the **present value of all its future cash flows** — every coupon plus the return of par at maturity — discounted at the investor's required rate of return (the market yield):"},
  {"id":196,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"> **Bond Price = Sum of the present values of each future coupon + present value of par at maturity**\n\nWhen the required yield (discount rate) **rises**, each future cash flow is discounted more heavily, so the present value — the price — **falls**; when the required yield falls, the price rises. This is the mathematical engine behind the price–yield seesaw, and it explains why longer-maturity and lower-coupon bonds (whose cash flows sit further in the future) are more sensitive to yield changes.\n\n#### Summary of Fixed-Income Risks\n\nPull the risk vocabulary together, because questions often ask you to match a client's concern to a risk or a solution:"},
  {"id":197,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"| Risk | What it means | Who it hurts most |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Interest rate (market) risk** | Prices fall when rates rise | Long-duration bonds |\n| **Reinvestment risk** | Coupons/principal must be reinvested at lower rates | High-coupon and callable bonds |\n| **Credit/default risk** | Issuer fails to pay interest or principal | Low-rated / high-yield bonds |\n| **Call risk** | Bond redeemed early, capping gains and forcing reinvestment | Callable premium bonds |\n| **Liquidity risk** | Hard to sell near fair value | Small/thinly traded issues |\n| **Purchasing power (inflation) risk** | Fixed payments lose real value | Long-term, fixed-rate bonds |"},
  {"id":198,"part":"Part II","section":"C. Characteristics and Valuation Factors of Fixed Income Securities","anchor":"c-characteristics-and-valuation-factors-of-fixed-income-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** Note that duration/interest-rate risk and reinvestment risk pull in **opposite directions** — a bond held to maturity trades off one against the other. Zero-coupon bonds eliminate reinvestment risk on coupons but maximize interest-rate risk, a favorite contrast on the exam."},
  {"id":199,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"Equity securities represent **ownership** in a corporation. Unlike a bond, which is a loan to the issuer, a share of stock makes the holder a part-owner with a claim on the company's earnings and, ultimately, its assets. The two broad classes of equity are **common stock** and **preferred stock**. On the Series 65 you must know the rights attached to each, how foreign ownership is packaged for U.S. investors through ADRs, and the several varieties of preferred stock — especially convertible and floating-rate preferred. This subsection walks through each in turn and then contrasts common and preferred directly.\n\n#### Common Stock: The Basics of Equity Ownership"},
  {"id":200,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"Common stock is the most basic ownership unit of a corporation. A common shareholder participates in the company's success primarily two ways: through **dividends** (a share of profits, paid only when the board of directors declares them) and through **capital appreciation** (a rise in the market price of the shares). Together these make up an investor's **total return**."},
  {"id":201,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"Common stock is attractive because it offers the greatest potential for long-term growth and historically serves as a **hedge against inflation** — as prices and corporate earnings rise, share values tend to rise as well. The trade-off is that common stock carries the most **market risk** and **business risk** of any equity position and sits **last in line** if the company fails.\n\nKey features every candidate must know:"},
  {"id":202,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"- **Limited liability.** A shareholder can lose no more than the amount invested; personal assets are not exposed to the corporation's debts.\n- **Residual claim.** In a liquidation, common stockholders are paid **last**, after all creditors and preferred stockholders. The liquidation priority ladder is: (1) secured creditors, (2) unsecured (general) creditors, including debenture holders and other unsecured bondholders, (3) subordinated (junior) debt holders, (4) preferred stockholders, and (5) common stockholders.\n- **Transferability.** Shares can generally be bought and sold freely.\n- **Right of inspection.** Shareholders may review the corporate books and the list of shareholders."},
  {"id":203,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** Remember the phrase \"common is *last* but has the *most* upside.\" Bondholders and preferred holders have priority in bad times, but common shareholders capture unlimited growth in good times.\n\n#### Common Stockholder Rights: Voting and Preemptive Rights\n\nCommon shareholders have two rights that preferred shareholders usually lack: the right to vote and (sometimes) the preemptive right.\n\n**Voting rights.** Common shareholders elect the board of directors and vote on major corporate matters (mergers, issuance of additional shares, and other fundamental changes). There are two voting methods:"},
  {"id":204,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"| Method | How votes are allocated | Who it favors |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Statutory voting** | One vote per share *for each* open board seat, cast separately | Large/majority shareholders |\n| **Cumulative voting** | Shares × open seats = total votes, which may be **concentrated** on one candidate | Small/minority shareholders |\n\nExample: an investor owns 100 shares, and 3 board seats are open. Under **statutory** voting she casts up to 100 votes for each seat (100/100/100). Under **cumulative** voting she has 300 total votes and may pile all 300 on a single candidate."},
  {"id":205,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** Cumulative voting **benefits the small (minority) shareholder** because it lets them concentrate their voting power to elect at least one favorable director. This is a classic tested distinction.\n\n**Preemptive rights.** A preemptive right lets existing shareholders maintain their proportional ownership by subscribing to newly issued shares *before* they are offered to the public. If a company issues new stock, a shareholder who owns 2% of the company can buy enough new shares to stay at 2%, avoiding **dilution** of their ownership percentage.\n\n#### Domestic vs. Foreign Stock"},
  {"id":206,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"**Domestic stock** is issued by a company organized and headquartered in the United States. **Foreign stock** is issued by a company domiciled outside the U.S. Investing directly in foreign shares gives U.S. investors diversification and access to growth abroad, but it introduces two risks the exam emphasizes:\n\n- **Currency (exchange-rate) risk** — the value of the investment and its dividends, when converted back to dollars, changes as exchange rates move.\n- **Political risk** — instability, expropriation, or changes in law in the foreign country can impair the investment."},
  {"id":207,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"Buying foreign shares directly can also be cumbersome: the shares trade on a foreign exchange, in a foreign currency, during foreign market hours, and dividends arrive in foreign currency. To solve this, U.S. investors typically use **American Depositary Receipts**.\n\n#### American Depositary Receipts (ADRs)"},
  {"id":208,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"An **American Depositary Receipt (ADR)** is a negotiable certificate issued by a **U.S. depositary bank** that represents a specific number of shares (or a fraction of a share) of a **foreign** company. The actual foreign shares are held in custody by a bank in the issuer's home country; the U.S. bank issues receipts against them. This lets Americans invest in foreign companies conveniently — the ADR **trades on U.S. exchanges (or OTC), in U.S. dollars, during U.S. market hours**, and settles like any domestic security.\n\nHow ADRs work in practice:"},
  {"id":209,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"- **Dividends** are paid by the foreign company in its local currency, then **converted into U.S. dollars** by the depositary bank and passed through to the ADR holder.\n- ADRs are a tool to facilitate **foreign investment**, so they still carry **currency risk** and **political risk** — the ADR's price reflects both the underlying stock's price and the exchange rate.\n- ADR holders generally have **limited or no voting rights** and typically **no preemptive rights**. The depositary bank, as record holder of the foreign shares, usually votes on the holder's behalf or forwards proxy materials."},
  {"id":210,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"**Common trap:** Candidates sometimes think an ADR eliminates currency risk because it trades in dollars. It does **not**. The dollar price of an ADR still rises and falls with the foreign exchange rate, and dividends shrink when the foreign currency weakens against the dollar.\n\n**Exam tip:** Know the one-line definition cold — \"an ADR is a receipt issued by a U.S. bank representing shares of a foreign company, trading in U.S. dollars on U.S. markets, subject to currency and political risk.\"\n\n#### Preferred Stock: Equity That Behaves Like a Bond"},
  {"id":211,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"**Preferred stock** is an equity security, but it behaves in many ways like a fixed-income (debt) security. Its defining feature is a **fixed dividend**, stated either as a percentage of par or as a fixed dollar amount. Par value for preferred stock is conventionally **$100** (unlike the largely nominal $0.01 par often assigned to common).\n\nBecause the dividend is fixed, preferred stock is prized mainly for **income and safety**, not growth. Its two priorities over common stock are:"},
  {"id":212,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"1. **Dividend priority** — preferred dividends must be paid before any common dividend.\n2. **Liquidation priority** — in a bankruptcy, preferred is paid *before* common (but still *after* all creditors/bondholders).\n\nIn exchange for that priority and steadier income, preferred shareholders typically **give up voting rights and preemptive rights**, and they usually **do not participate** in the company's growth — the fixed dividend caps their income."},
  {"id":213,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"**Interest-rate sensitivity.** Because the dividend is fixed, straight preferred stock prices move **inversely to interest rates**, just like bonds. When market interest rates rise, existing preferred (with its now-below-market fixed rate) falls in price; when rates fall, preferred rises. This makes **interest-rate risk** the primary risk of most preferred stock.\n\n**Common trap:** Preferred stock is often labeled a \"fixed-income\" or \"senior\" security, which can trick students into calling it debt. It is still **equity** — dividends can be *skipped* (they are not a legal obligation the way bond interest is), and preferred holders rank *below* every creditor in liquidation."},
  {"id":214,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"#### Calculating Preferred Dividends and Current Yield\n\n**Stated (fixed) dividend.** The annual dividend equals the stated rate times par:\n\nAnnual Dividend = Stated Rate × Par Value\n\nWorked example: A **5% preferred** stock with a **$100 par** value pays:\n\n0.05 × $100 = **$5.00 per share per year.**\n\nA preferred stock may instead be quoted directly in dollars — e.g., a \"$6 preferred\" simply pays $6 per year.\n\n**Current yield.** Because preferred trades in the market at prices above or below par, its actual yield to a buyer is the **current yield**:\n\nCurrent Yield = Annual Dividend / Current Market Price"},
  {"id":215,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"Worked example: The $5 preferred above is trading at **$80** (below par because rates rose):\n\n$5.00 / $80 = **6.25%.**\n\nIf instead it traded at **$125** (rates fell), the current yield would be $5 / $125 = **4.0%.** Notice the inverse relationship: as the price rises, the yield falls.\n\n#### Types of Preferred Stock\n\nPreferred stock comes in several varieties, and a single issue can combine features (e.g., \"cumulative convertible preferred\"). The commonly tested types:"},
  {"id":216,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"- **Straight (noncumulative) preferred.** Pays the fixed dividend when declared. If the board **skips** a dividend, it is gone forever — the holder has no claim to the missed payment.\n- **Cumulative preferred.** If the issuer skips (omits) a dividend, it accumulates as **dividends in arrears**. All arrears *plus* the current dividend must be paid to cumulative preferred holders **before any common dividend** can be paid. This makes cumulative preferred **safer** than straight preferred, so it typically carries a **lower** stated rate.\n- **Participating preferred.** In addition to its fixed dividend, it may receive **extra** dividends when the company has strong earnings, effectively sharing"},
  {"id":217,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"in growth alongside common.\n- **Callable (redeemable) preferred.** The **issuer** can buy the shares back at a preset **call price** (often at or slightly above par), usually after a set date. Callability benefits the issuer — companies call when rates fall so they can reissue at a lower rate — so callable preferred pays a **higher** stated rate to compensate the holder for that call risk.\n- **Convertible preferred** and **floating-rate preferred** — covered in detail below."},
  {"id":218,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"**Cumulative preferred worked example.** A **6% cumulative preferred** ($100 par, so $6/year) paid no dividend for **2 years**. Before common shareholders may receive anything, the company must pay the two years of arrears plus the current year:\n\n($6 × 2 years in arrears) + $6 current = **$18 per share** to cumulative preferred holders first.\n\n**Exam tip:** With cumulative preferred, arrears do **not** earn interest — you simply add up the missed fixed dividends plus the current one. And skipped preferred dividends never trigger default the way skipped bond interest would.\n\n#### Convertible Preferred Stock"},
  {"id":219,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"**Convertible preferred** gives the holder the right to exchange each preferred share for a fixed number of **common shares**. This lets the investor collect a steady preferred dividend *and* participate in the upside of the common stock. Because that conversion feature is valuable, convertible preferred pays a **lower** stated dividend than comparable nonconvertible preferred.\n\nTwo numbers drive the math:\n\n**Conversion ratio** = number of common shares received per preferred share. It is set by a **conversion price**:\n\nConversion Ratio = Par Value / Conversion Price\n\nWorked example: A $100 par convertible preferred with a **conversion price of $25**:"},
  {"id":220,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"$100 / $25 = **4 common shares** per preferred share.\n\n**Conversion (parity) value** = what the preferred is worth if converted right now:\n\nConversion Value = Conversion Ratio × Current Common Price\n\nIf the common trades at **$30**:\n\n4 × $30 = **$120.** The preferred is worth at least $120 in conversion value, so it should trade at or above $120.\n\n**Parity price of the common** answers the reverse question — at what common price does the common equal the preferred's market price:\n\nParity Price of Common = Market Price of Preferred / Conversion Ratio\n\nIf the preferred trades at **$110**:\n\n$110 / 4 = **$27.50.** The two are at parity when the common is at $27.50."},
  {"id":221,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"**Common trap:** If the common price is above parity (e.g., common at $30 while the preferred still trades at $110), an **arbitrage** opportunity exists — buy the underpriced preferred, convert, and sell the common. Exam questions test whether you can compare conversion value to the preferred's market price and spot which is cheaper.\n\n#### Floating-Rate (Adjustable-Rate) Preferred Stock"},
  {"id":222,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"**Floating-rate preferred** (also called **adjustable-rate preferred**) does **not** pay a fixed dividend. Instead, its dividend **resets periodically** based on a benchmark interest rate (for example, a reference short-term rate). When market rates rise, the dividend rises; when rates fall, the dividend falls."},
  {"id":223,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"The key consequence — and the whole reason it's tested — is **price stability**. Because the dividend adjusts toward market rates, the security stays attractive at roughly its par value regardless of rate moves, so its **market price is far more stable** than that of straight (fixed-rate) preferred. In other words, floating-rate preferred has **much lower interest-rate (price) risk**.\n\nThe trade-off: the investor gives up **income predictability**. The dollar amount of the dividend is uncertain and will fall if rates decline."},
  {"id":224,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** Contrast the two directly. **Fixed-rate preferred → stable income, volatile price.** **Floating-rate preferred → volatile income, stable price.** If a question asks which preferred best protects *principal value* when interest rates are expected to change, the answer is **floating-rate (adjustable-rate) preferred**.\n\n#### Key Differences Between Common and Preferred Stock\n\nThe exam frequently asks candidates to distinguish the two directly. Use this comparison:"},
  {"id":225,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"| Feature | Common Stock | Preferred Stock |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Ownership** | Yes — full equity owner | Yes — equity, but with debt-like traits |\n| **Dividend** | Variable; only if board declares | **Fixed** (stated % of par or $ amount) |\n| **Dividend priority** | Paid **last**, after preferred | Paid **before** common |\n| **Growth potential** | High — participates in appreciation | Limited — capped by fixed dividend |\n| **Voting rights** | **Yes** | Usually **no** |\n| **Preemptive rights** | Often yes | Usually **no** |\n| **Liquidation priority** | **Last** (residual claim) | Ahead of common, **behind all creditors** |\n| **Primary risk** | Market/business risk | **Interest-rate risk** (like a bond) |\n| **Primary appeal** | Growth + inflation hedge | Income + relative safety |\n| **Par value** | Nominal (often $0.01) | Conventionally **$100** |"},
  {"id":226,"part":"Part II","section":"D. Types of Equity Securities","anchor":"d-types-of-equity-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** A reliable memory anchor: **preferred = safer, steadier income, priority, but no vote and no growth; common = riskier, no priority, but votes and unlimited growth.** Most Series 65 equity questions turn on one of these contrasts, on the ADR definition and its currency risk, or on the preferred-stock calculations shown above."},
  {"id":227,"part":"Part II","section":"E. Characteristics of Equity Securities","anchor":"e-characteristics-of-equity-securities","text":"Equity securities represent **ownership** in a corporation. The two broad classes are **common stock** (the residual ownership interest, carrying voting control and the greatest upside) and **preferred stock** (a senior equity that trades more like a fixed-income instrument). This subsection covers the rights that attach to equity ownership, the special rules governing restricted and control stock, how dividends are paid and measured, and the two types of employee stock options. On the Series 65 these topics are tested mostly conceptually — you must know *who has which right*, *the order of priority*, and *how each item is treated* — with a few quick calculations (dividend yield and cumulative voting) mixed in."},
  {"id":228,"part":"Part II","section":"E. Characteristics of Equity Securities","anchor":"e-characteristics-of-equity-securities","text":"#### Shareholder Voting Rights: Statutory vs. Cumulative\n\nCommon shareholders elect the board of directors and vote on major corporate matters (mergers, charter amendments, authorization of additional shares, etc.). Preferred shareholders normally do **not** vote. There are two systems for counting votes in a board election, and the difference determines whether a small shareholder can influence the outcome."},
  {"id":229,"part":"Part II","section":"E. Characteristics of Equity Securities","anchor":"e-characteristics-of-equity-securities","text":"- **Statutory (regular) voting.** A shareholder may cast up to the number of shares owned **for each open seat**, but votes cannot be combined across seats. This favors **large/majority** shareholders, because a holder controlling a majority can fill every seat.\n- **Cumulative voting.** A shareholder's total votes equal **shares owned × number of open seats**, and those votes may be concentrated on one or a few candidates. This favors **small/minority** shareholders, because it lets them pool votes behind a single director.\n\n**Worked example — same investor, both systems.** An investor owns **200 shares** and **4 board seats** are up for election."},
  {"id":230,"part":"Part II","section":"E. Characteristics of Equity Securities","anchor":"e-characteristics-of-equity-securities","text":"- *Statutory:* the maximum for any one candidate = **200** (200 per seat, not transferable). The investor could vote 200-200-200-200 across the four seats but no more than 200 for any single candidate.\n- *Cumulative:* total votes = 200 × 4 = **800**, all of which may be placed on one candidate (e.g., 800-0-0-0).\n\nFormula to remember: **Cumulative votes = Shares Owned × Number of Directors Being Elected.**\n\n**Exam tip:** If a question stresses protecting the \"little guy\" or a minority shareholder's ability to win a board seat, the answer is **cumulative voting**. Statutory voting benefits those who already hold the most shares.\n\n#### Preemptive Rights and Antidilution Protection"},
  {"id":231,"part":"Part II","section":"E. Characteristics of Equity Securities","anchor":"e-characteristics-of-equity-securities","text":"A **preemptive right** gives existing common shareholders the right — but not the obligation — to buy newly issued shares **before** they are offered to the public, in an amount sufficient to **maintain their proportional (percentage) ownership**. This protects them against **dilution** of both their ownership percentage and their voting power when the company raises capital by selling more stock."},
  {"id":232,"part":"Part II","section":"E. Characteristics of Equity Securities","anchor":"e-characteristics-of-equity-securities","text":"- Preemptive rights are exercised through a **rights offering**. Each shareholder receives one **right** per share held; a stated number of rights plus a **subscription price** (usually below the current market price) buys one new share.\n- Rights are short-term (typically a few weeks) and transferable — a shareholder who does not wish to subscribe may sell the rights in the market.\n- \"Antidilution provisions\" is the broader term for contractual protections (common in preferred stock, convertible securities, and warrants) that adjust conversion terms or grant additional shares when new stock is issued, again to preserve value/percentage."},
  {"id":233,"part":"Part II","section":"E. Characteristics of Equity Securities","anchor":"e-characteristics-of-equity-securities","text":"**Common trap:** Preemptive rights protect *percentage ownership*, not a guaranteed price. Also, a **right** (short-term, issued to existing holders, usually below market) differs from a **warrant** (long-term, a \"sweetener\" attached to a bond or preferred offering, usually with an exercise price *above* market at issuance). The exam frequently contrasts the two.\n\n#### Liquidation Preferences and Priority of Claims\n\nIf a corporation is liquidated (dissolved or in bankruptcy), its assets are distributed in a fixed **order of priority**. Equity holders are last in line because they are **owners**, not creditors. The commonly tested ladder, from first paid to last paid, is:"},
  {"id":234,"part":"Part II","section":"E. Characteristics of Equity Securities","anchor":"e-characteristics-of-equity-securities","text":"| Priority | Claimant |\n|---|---|\n| 1 (paid first) | **Secured creditors** (e.g., mortgage bondholders, equipment trust certificate holders) |\n| 2 | Unpaid **wages** and **taxes** owed |\n| 3 | **General/unsecured creditors** (debenture holders, trade creditors) |\n| 4 | **Subordinated debt** holders |\n| 5 | **Preferred stockholders** |\n| 6 (paid last) | **Common stockholders** |\n\nThe key concept is that **debt is always senior to equity**, and within equity, **preferred is senior to common**. A \"liquidation preference\" is exactly this: preferred stock has a stated par value (often $100) that must be satisfied ahead of any distribution to common shareholders."},
  {"id":235,"part":"Part II","section":"E. Characteristics of Equity Securities","anchor":"e-characteristics-of-equity-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** Two memory anchors. (1) \"**DEBT before EQUITY, PREFERRED before COMMON.**\" (2) Common stockholders have **limited liability** — the most they can lose is their investment; they are never personally liable for the corporation's debts.\n\n#### Restricted and Control Stock; Resale Restrictions (Rule 144)\n\nNot all shares can be freely resold. Two categories are restricted, and **SEC Rule 144** governs how they may re-enter the public market."},
  {"id":236,"part":"Part II","section":"E. Characteristics of Equity Securities","anchor":"e-characteristics-of-equity-securities","text":"- **Restricted (unregistered) stock** is acquired in a transaction that was *not* registered — most often a **private placement** under Regulation D. It bears a legend and cannot be sold publicly until Rule 144 conditions are met.\n- **Control stock** is stock held by an **affiliate** — an officer, director, or large shareholder (the figure usually tested is a holder of **more than 10%**), i.e., someone in a control relationship with the issuer. Control stock may have been bought in the open market (so it is \"registered\"), but the *affiliate's* resale is still restricted because of the insider's influence.\n\n**Rule 144 conditions (the commonly tested points):**"},
  {"id":237,"part":"Part II","section":"E. Characteristics of Equity Securities","anchor":"e-characteristics-of-equity-securities","text":"1. **Holding period (applies to restricted stock).** The securities must be held, fully paid, for at least **6 months** if the issuer is a **reporting company** (files with the SEC), or **1 year** if it is a **non-reporting** company. The holding period does **not** apply to control stock purchased in the open market.\n2. **Volume limits (apply to affiliates).** In any **90-day** period, an affiliate may sell no more than the **greater of**:\n   - **1% of the total outstanding shares**, or\n   - the **average weekly trading volume** over the **preceding 4 weeks**.\n3. **Form 144 (notice of sale).** An affiliate must file **Form 144** with the SEC at the time the sell order is placed if the sale within a 90-day period will exceed **5,000 shares OR $50,000**. Form 144 is effective for **90 days**.\n4. **Non-affiliates get relief.** A non-affiliate who has satisfied the holding period may sell **without volume limits**; once the securities have been held for the full period (and public-information requirements are met), the shares essentially become freely tradable."},
  {"id":238,"part":"Part II","section":"E. Characteristics of Equity Securities","anchor":"e-characteristics-of-equity-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** Remember the \"**greater of 1% or average weekly volume**\" test and the \"**5,000 shares or $50,000**\" Form 144 threshold — both are frequent exam facts. **Common trap:** Volume limits and Form 144 apply to **affiliates (control persons)**; a long-term non-affiliate holder is largely freed from these restrictions.\n\n#### Dividends: Key Dates and Dividend Yield\n\nA **dividend** is a distribution of a portion of a corporation's earnings to shareholders, declared at the discretion of the **board of directors** (there is no legal obligation to pay a common dividend). Four dates define the process:"},
  {"id":239,"part":"Part II","section":"E. Characteristics of Equity Securities","anchor":"e-characteristics-of-equity-securities","text":"| Date | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| **Declaration date** | The board announces (declares) the dividend, its amount, the record date, and the payment date. |\n| **Ex-dividend date** | The first day the stock trades **without** the right to the upcoming dividend. A buyer on or after this date does **not** receive it; the seller keeps it. The stock price typically **drops by roughly the dividend amount** on this day. |\n| **Record date** | The date on which you must be the **holder of record** to receive the dividend. |\n| **Payment date** | The date the dividend is actually paid to shareholders of record. |"},
  {"id":240,"part":"Part II","section":"E. Characteristics of Equity Securities","anchor":"e-characteristics-of-equity-securities","text":"The ex-dividend date is tied to the securities **settlement cycle**. Under current **T+1** settlement (effective May 2024), a trade must settle by the record date to make the buyer a holder of record, so the **ex-dividend date falls on the same business day as the record date**.\n\n**Exam tip:** Many older question banks were written under **T+2** settlement, where the ex-date was **one business day before** the record date. If a question clearly assumes T+2, apply \"one business day before record\"; otherwise, under current T+1 rules, ex-date = record date. The tested *concept* — that you must own the stock **before** the ex-dividend date to receive the dividend — never changes."},
  {"id":241,"part":"Part II","section":"E. Characteristics of Equity Securities","anchor":"e-characteristics-of-equity-securities","text":"**Dividend Yield.** This measures the income return a stock pays relative to its price. The formula (plain text) is:\n\n**Dividend Yield = Annual Dividends per Share / Current Market Price per Share**\n\n**Worked example.** A stock pays a quarterly dividend of **$0.50** and currently trades at **$40**.\n\n- Annualize the dividend: $0.50 × 4 = **$2.00 per share** per year.\n- Dividend Yield = $2.00 / $40.00 = **0.05 = 5%**."},
  {"id":242,"part":"Part II","section":"E. Characteristics of Equity Securities","anchor":"e-characteristics-of-equity-securities","text":"**Common trap:** Use the **annual** dividend, not the quarterly figure — a very common miss. Also note the **inverse relationship**: if the dividend is unchanged but the **share price falls**, the dividend yield **rises** (and vice versa), exactly parallel to the bond price/yield relationship.\n\n#### Employee Stock Options: ISOs vs. NQSOs\n\nCompanies grant employees the right to buy company stock at a fixed **exercise (strike) price**. The two types differ almost entirely in **taxation**, which is what the Series 65 tests."},
  {"id":243,"part":"Part II","section":"E. Characteristics of Equity Securities","anchor":"e-characteristics-of-equity-securities","text":"- **Incentive Stock Options (ISOs)** — also called *statutory* options.\n  - **No regular income tax at grant or at exercise.** However, the **bargain element** (the spread between market value and the strike price at exercise) is an **AMT preference item**, so exercising can trigger **Alternative Minimum Tax**.\n  - **Qualifying disposition:** if the shares are held **at least 2 years from the grant date AND at least 1 year from the exercise date**, the **entire gain** (sale price minus strike price) is taxed as **long-term capital gain** — the most favorable outcome.\n  - If those holding periods are **not** met (a **disqualifying disposition**), the spread is taxed as **ordinary income**, like an NQSO.\n  - ISOs can be granted **only to employees**, are subject to a **$100,000** annual limit on the value first exercisable, and generally must be exercised within **10 years** of grant."},
  {"id":244,"part":"Part II","section":"E. Characteristics of Equity Securities","anchor":"e-characteristics-of-equity-securities","text":"- **Nonqualified Stock Options (NQSOs / NSOs).**\n  - **Taxed as ordinary income at exercise** on the spread (market value at exercise minus strike price); this amount is also subject to payroll (FICA) taxes, and the **employer receives a tax deduction** for it.\n  - Any further gain or loss from exercise to sale is a **capital gain or loss**.\n  - NQSOs are **flexible** — they may be granted to employees, directors, consultants, and others."},
  {"id":245,"part":"Part II","section":"E. Characteristics of Equity Securities","anchor":"e-characteristics-of-equity-securities","text":"| Feature | ISO (Incentive) | NQSO (Nonqualified) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Tax at grant | None | None |\n| Tax at exercise | No regular tax; **spread is an AMT item** | **Ordinary income** on the spread |\n| Best-case tax on sale | **Long-term capital gain** on entire gain (if holding periods met) | Capital gain only on post-exercise appreciation |\n| Required holding for favorable tax | ≥2 yrs from grant **and** ≥1 yr from exercise | None (already taxed at exercise) |\n| Who may receive | **Employees only** | Employees and non-employees |\n| Employer deduction | Generally none (if qualifying) | **Yes**, at exercise |"},
  {"id":246,"part":"Part II","section":"E. Characteristics of Equity Securities","anchor":"e-characteristics-of-equity-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** The single most tested fact is *when the spread is taxed*: an **NQSO is taxed as ordinary income at exercise**, while an **ISO is not taxed for regular purposes at exercise but can create AMT** and, if the holding periods are satisfied, produces **all long-term capital gain**. **Common trap:** Watch for a \"disqualifying disposition\" — selling ISO shares too soon converts the favorable capital-gain treatment into **ordinary income**, erasing the ISO's main advantage."},
  {"id":247,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"Analysts use two broad approaches to judge what a stock is worth and when to buy or sell it: **fundamental analysis**, which asks *\"What is this company actually worth?\"*, and **technical analysis**, which asks *\"What are supply and demand doing to the price right now?\"* Within fundamental analysis sit two specific valuation models the exam tests directly: the **dividend discount model (DDM)** and **discounted cash flow (DCF)**. This section walks through each, shows the math you must be able to do by hand, and then contrasts the two schools of thought.\n\n#### Fundamental Analysis: Valuing the Business"},
  {"id":248,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"**What it is.** Fundamental analysis estimates a security's **intrinsic value** — what the stock is really worth — by studying the company itself and the economy around it. A fundamentalist reads the firm's **financial statements**, examines its industry, and looks at macroeconomic conditions (interest rates, GDP, inflation). If the intrinsic value they calculate is *higher* than the current market price, the stock is considered **undervalued** (a buy); if intrinsic value is *lower* than market price, it is **overvalued** (a sell or avoid)."},
  {"id":249,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"**Top-down vs. bottom-up.** A *top-down* analyst starts with the broad economy, narrows to promising industries, and finally picks companies. A *bottom-up* analyst starts with an individual company's financials regardless of the macro picture. Both are forms of fundamental analysis.\n\n**The three core financial statements:**"},
  {"id":250,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"| Statement | What it shows | Key idea |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Balance sheet** | Assets, liabilities, and shareholders' equity at a single point in time | Assets = Liabilities + Shareholders' Equity |\n| **Income statement** | Revenues, expenses, and net income over a period | Shows profitability |\n| **Cash flow statement** | Cash generated and used in operating, investing, and financing activities | Cash is not the same as reported earnings |\n\n**Key financial ratios.** The exam expects you to recognize what each ratio measures and to compute the simple ones."},
  {"id":251,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"- **Working capital = Current Assets − Current Liabilities.** A dollar measure of short-term liquidity.\n- **Current ratio = Current Assets / Current Liabilities.** Measures liquidity. A ratio of 2:1 means the firm has twice the current assets it needs to cover current liabilities.\n- **Quick ratio (acid-test) = (Current Assets − Inventory) / Current Liabilities.** A stricter liquidity test because inventory is the current asset hardest to convert to cash.\n- **Debt-to-equity ratio = Total Long-Term Debt / Total Shareholders' Equity.** Measures leverage; higher means more financial risk.\n- **Earnings per share (EPS) = (Net Income − Preferred Dividends) / Common Shares Outstanding.**\n- **Price-t"},
  {"id":252,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"o-earnings (P/E) ratio = Market Price per Share / EPS.** How much investors pay per dollar of earnings; a \"growth\" stock typically carries a high P/E.\n- **Dividend payout ratio = Common Dividends per Share / EPS.** The percentage of earnings paid out to common shareholders.\n- **Book value per share = (Tangible Assets − Liabilities − Preferred Stock) / Common Shares Outstanding.** A rough \"liquidation\" value per share.\n- **Return on equity (ROE) = (Net Income − Preferred Dividends) / Common Shareholders' Equity.** Profitability relative to owners' capital."},
  {"id":253,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"**Worked example — EPS and P/E.** A company reports net income of $10,000,000, pays $1,000,000 in preferred dividends, and has 3,000,000 common shares outstanding, trading at $60.\n\n- EPS = ($10,000,000 − $1,000,000) / 3,000,000 = $9,000,000 / 3,000,000 = **$3.00**\n- P/E = $60 / $3.00 = **20** (investors pay $20 for each $1 of earnings)\n\nIf it also pays a $1.20 common dividend, the payout ratio = $1.20 / $3.00 = **40%**.\n\n**Worked example — current and quick ratios.** Current assets = $2,000,000 (of which inventory = $500,000); current liabilities = $1,000,000.\n\n- Current ratio = $2,000,000 / $1,000,000 = **2:1**\n- Quick ratio = ($2,000,000 − $500,000) / $1,000,000 = **1.5:1**"},
  {"id":254,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** Remember which asset the quick ratio strips out — **inventory** (and any prepaid expenses). If a question hands you inventory, it usually wants the quick ratio, not the current ratio.\n\n**Common trap:** EPS subtracts the **preferred** dividend from net income (preferred holders get paid before common), but it divides by **common** shares only. Don't subtract common dividends when computing EPS — common dividends are paid *out of* the earnings that EPS measures."},
  {"id":255,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"**How it's tested.** Expect questions that: (1) identify fundamental analysis as the study of a company's financial statements, industry, and the economy; (2) ask you to label a stock over- or undervalued by comparing intrinsic value to market price; (3) require a one-step ratio calculation; and (4) match a ratio to what it measures (liquidity, leverage, profitability, valuation).\n\n#### The Dividend Discount Model (DDM)"},
  {"id":256,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"**What it is.** The DDM is a fundamental-analysis tool that says a share of stock is worth the **present value of all the future dividends** it will pay. Because a dollar received years from now is worth less than a dollar today, each expected dividend is discounted back to the present using the investor's required rate of return.\n\n**Constant-growth DDM (Gordon Growth Model).** When dividends are assumed to grow at a steady rate forever, the formula collapses to:\n\n    Price = D1 / (r − g)"},
  {"id":257,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"where:\n- **D1** = the dividend expected next year = D0 × (1 + g)\n- **D0** = the most recent dividend just paid\n- **r** = required rate of return (the discount rate)\n- **g** = constant annual dividend growth rate\n\n**Worked example — constant growth.** A stock just paid a dividend (D0) of $2.00. Dividends are expected to grow 5% per year forever, and an investor requires a 10% return.\n\n- D1 = $2.00 × (1 + 0.05) = $2.10\n- Price = $2.10 / (0.10 − 0.05) = $2.10 / 0.05 = **$42.00**\n\nIf the stock currently trades below $42, a fundamentalist calls it undervalued."},
  {"id":258,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"**Zero-growth DDM.** If dividends never change (the classic case is **fixed-rate preferred stock**), growth g = 0 and the formula simplifies to:\n\n    Price = D / r\n\n**Worked example — zero growth.** A preferred stock pays a fixed $5.00 annual dividend and investors require an 8% return.\n\n- Price = $5.00 / 0.08 = **$62.50**\n\n**Exam tip:** In the constant-growth formula you must \"grow\" the dividend by one year first (use **D1**, not D0). Multiply the given dividend by (1 + g) before dividing."},
  {"id":259,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"**Common trap:** The denominator is **r − g**. The model only works when the required return exceeds the growth rate (r > g). It also cannot value a company that pays **no dividends** — a frequently tested limitation. For non-dividend payers, analysts turn to discounted cash flow instead.\n\n**How it's tested.** Questions supply D0 (or D1), g, and r and ask for the theoretical price, or they describe the model conceptually (\"the value of a stock equals the present value of its expected future dividends\"). Know that DDM is a fundamental method and that its weaknesses are its dependence on dividends and its sensitivity to the growth and discount-rate assumptions.\n\n#### Discounted Cash Flow (DCF)"},
  {"id":260,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"**What it is.** DCF is the broader present-value technique behind the DDM. Instead of discounting only dividends, DCF discounts a stream of expected **cash flows** (or free cash flows) back to today at a chosen discount rate. It rests entirely on the **time value of money**: money available sooner is worth more than the same amount later.\n\n**Core present-value formula:**\n\n    Present Value = Cash Flow / (1 + r)^n\n\nwhere r = the discount rate and n = the number of years until the cash flow is received. For a multi-year stream you discount each year's cash flow separately and add them up."},
  {"id":261,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"**Worked example — single cash flow.** An investment is expected to return $110 one year from now, and the appropriate discount rate is 10%.\n\n- PV = $110 / (1 + 0.10)^1 = $110 / 1.10 = **$100.00**\n\nSo $100 today is a fair price for that future $110. If the asset were offered for $95, its cash flows are worth more than its price (attractive); at $105 it is overpriced.\n\n**Worked example — a multi-year cash-flow stream.** Suppose a stock is expected to generate cash flows of $50 in year 1 and $50 in year 2, discounted at 10%.\n\n- Year 1 PV = $50 / 1.10 = $45.45\n- Year 2 PV = $50 / (1.10)^2 = $50 / 1.21 = $41.32\n- Total intrinsic value = $45.45 + $41.32 = **$86.77**"},
  {"id":262,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"If the market price is below $86.77, DCF flags the stock as undervalued.\n\n**Discount rate.** The discount rate reflects the investor's **required return**, which rises with risk and with prevailing interest rates. Note the inverse relationship the exam likes: **as the discount rate rises, present value falls** (and vice versa) — the same reason bond prices fall when rates rise.\n\n**Exam tip:** DDM is simply a *special case* of DCF where the cash flows being discounted are dividends. If a question contrasts them, DCF is the more general model and can value firms that pay no dividends by discounting free cash flow."},
  {"id":263,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"**Common trap:** Present value moves **inversely** to the discount rate. A higher required return does not make a security worth more — it makes its discounted value **smaller**.\n\n**How it's tested.** Look for one-step present-value calculations (PV = future amount ÷ (1 + r)^n), the inverse relationship between discount rate and present value, and the conceptual point that DCF/DDM value a security as the present value of its expected future cash flows.\n\n#### Technical Analysis: Reading the Market"},
  {"id":264,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"**What it is.** Technical analysis ignores the company's fundamentals and instead studies **market data — price and trading volume — plotted on charts** to forecast future price movements. The technician assumes that all known information is already reflected in the price, that prices move in **trends**, and that historical patterns tend to repeat because investor psychology repeats. Technicians are concerned with *timing* (when to buy or sell), while fundamentalists are concerned with *selection* (what to buy)."},
  {"id":265,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"**Trends.** A **trendline** connects a series of price points to reveal direction: an **uptrend** shows successively higher highs and higher lows; a **downtrend** shows lower highs and lower lows; a **sideways (horizontal) trend** stays within a range.\n\n**Support and resistance.** These are the two most-tested technical terms:\n\n- **Support** is a price level (a \"floor\") where buying interest has historically been strong enough to stop a price decline. Demand emerges here.\n- **Resistance** is a price level (a \"ceiling\") where selling pressure has historically stopped a price advance. Supply emerges here."},
  {"id":266,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"A **breakout** occurs when the price pushes decisively **above resistance** (bullish signal) or **below support** (bearish signal). Technicians often act on breakouts. When a stock breaks above former resistance, that old resistance level frequently becomes new support.\n\n**Other tools technicians use** (recognize, don't memorize deeply):"},
  {"id":267,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"- **Moving averages** smooth out day-to-day noise to reveal the underlying trend.\n- **Chart patterns** such as the head-and-shoulders (a reversal pattern) or consolidations.\n- **Market sentiment / contrarian indicators:** the **short interest theory** (high short interest is bullish because those shorts must eventually buy to cover), the **odd-lot theory** (small investors are presumed to be wrong at market turning points), and put/call ratios.\n- **Breadth indicators** such as the advance/decline line."},
  {"id":268,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** Fix the direction in your mind — **support is below** the current price (buyers step in), **resistance is above** (sellers step in). A break *below support* is bearish; a break *above resistance* is bullish.\n\n**Common trap:** Technical analysis uses **charts of price and volume**, not financial statements, earnings, or economic data. Any answer choice that has a \"technician\" studying P/E ratios, balance sheets, or GDP is describing a *fundamentalist* and is wrong."},
  {"id":269,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"**How it's tested.** Expect definitions of support, resistance, trendlines, and breakouts; recognition that technicians chart price and volume to time trades; and identification of tools (moving averages, short interest, odd-lot theory) as technical rather than fundamental.\n\n#### Contrasting Technical vs. Fundamental Analysis\n\nThe single most important distinction: **fundamental analysis tells you *what* to buy; technical analysis tells you *when* to buy.** Use this comparison to answer the \"which analyst would…\" questions that appear on the exam."},
  {"id":270,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"| Feature | Fundamental Analysis | Technical Analysis |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Central question** | What is the stock really worth? | Which way is the price headed, and when? |\n| **Data used** | Financial statements, ratios, industry data, the economy (interest rates, GDP, inflation) | Charts of price and trading volume |\n| **Goal** | Estimate **intrinsic value**; find over-/undervalued stocks | Identify **trends** and time entry/exit points |\n| **Key tools** | P/E and other ratios, EPS, DDM, DCF | Support/resistance, trendlines, breakouts, moving averages, sentiment indicators |\n| **Primary use** | Security **selection** (what to buy) | Market **timing** (when to buy or sell) |\n| **View of price** | Price may differ from true value — that's the opportunity | Price already reflects all information; study its behavior |\n| **Typical practitioner** | Long-term investor, portfolio manager | Trader, market timer |"},
  {"id":271,"part":"Part II","section":"F. Methods Used to Determine the Value of Equity Securities","anchor":"f-methods-used-to-determine-the-value-of-equity-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** Many practitioners use these two approaches together — pick a fundamentally sound company (fundamental), then use support/resistance to time the purchase (technical). The exam may reward recognizing that the methods are complementary, not mutually exclusive.\n\n**Common trap:** Don't let a question blur the two. \"An analyst reviewing a company's cash flow statement to estimate intrinsic value\" is **fundamental**. \"An analyst noting the stock bounced off its $40 support level three times\" is **technical**. Match the *data source* — statements/economy versus charts — and you will place the analyst correctly every time."},
  {"id":272,"part":"Part II","section":"G. Equity Public Offering","anchor":"g-equity-public-offering","text":"When a corporation raises money by selling ownership (equity) to the public, it conducts an **equity public offering**. This subsection covers how new stock reaches investors, the difference between a company raising capital and an insider cashing out, and the special-purpose vehicles (SPACs) that have become a heavily tested topic. The organizing idea that ties everything together is the distinction between the **primary market** and the **secondary market** — master that first and the rest falls into place.\n\n#### Primary vs. Secondary Market\n\nThe two \"markets\" describe *who receives the money* when a security changes hands."},
  {"id":273,"part":"Part II","section":"G. Equity Public Offering","anchor":"g-equity-public-offering","text":"- **Primary market** — where an **issuer** sells **newly created** securities to investors for the first time. The cash flows *into* the company (the issuer). This is capital formation. Every IPO and every follow-on issuance of new shares is a primary-market transaction. Sales in the primary market are made by **prospectus** and are regulated under the **Securities Act of 1933** (the \"paper act\" governing new issues).\n- **Secondary market** — where investors trade **already-issued** securities among themselves. The issuer is not a party and receives none of the proceeds; money flows from one investor to another. Trading on the NYSE, Nasdaq, and the OTC markets is secondary-market activity, regulated primarily under the **Securities Exchange Act of 1934**."},
  {"id":274,"part":"Part II","section":"G. Equity Public Offering","anchor":"g-equity-public-offering","text":"| Feature | Primary Market | Secondary Market |\n|---|---|---|\n| Securities sold | Newly issued | Already outstanding |\n| Seller | The issuer (corporation) | An existing investor |\n| Who gets the proceeds | The issuer | The selling investor |\n| Purpose | Raise capital for the company | Provide liquidity / price discovery |\n| Governing act | Securities Act of 1933 | Securities Exchange Act of 1934 |\n| Document delivered | Prospectus | Trade confirmation |\n\n**Exam tip:** Ask \"Does the issuing company receive the cash?\" If yes, it is a **primary-market** transaction. If the money goes to another investor, it is **secondary-market** activity."},
  {"id":275,"part":"Part II","section":"G. Equity Public Offering","anchor":"g-equity-public-offering","text":"**Common trap:** Do not confuse the **secondary market** (any trading of outstanding shares) with a **secondary offering** (defined below — a specific type of registered distribution by insiders). They share a word but are different concepts, and the exam deliberately plays on the overlap.\n\n#### Initial Public Offering (IPO)\n\nAn **initial public offering** is the **first** time a company sells its stock to the general public — the event where a private company \"goes public.\" Because brand-new shares are being issued and the company receives the proceeds, an IPO is a **primary-market, primary offering**.\n\nHow an IPO works, in the sequence tested:"},
  {"id":276,"part":"Part II","section":"G. Equity Public Offering","anchor":"g-equity-public-offering","text":"1. **Underwriting.** The issuer hires an **investment bank (underwriter)**, often forming a **syndicate** of several firms to spread risk and distribution. Under a **firm-commitment** underwriting the syndicate buys the entire issue and resells it, bearing the risk of unsold shares; under a **best-efforts** underwriting it merely acts as agent and returns unsold shares to the issuer.\n2. **Registration.** The issuer files a **registration statement** with the SEC under the Securities Act of 1933, followed by a **cooling-off period** of at least **20 days**.\n3. **Preliminary prospectus (red herring).** During the cooling-off period the underwriter may circulate a **preliminary prospectus** to gauge interest and take *indications of interest* — but may **not** sell shares or accept money yet.\n4. **Effective date and final prospectus.** Once the SEC clears the registration, sales occur at a **fixed public offering price (POP)**, and every buyer must receive a **final (statutory) prospectus**."},
  {"id":277,"part":"Part II","section":"G. Equity Public Offering","anchor":"g-equity-public-offering","text":"**Exam tip:** SEC clearance (\"effectiveness\") is **never** an endorsement of the security's merit or accuracy. The SEC only reviews for **full and fair disclosure**; claiming otherwise is a prohibited representation.\n\n**Common trap:** IPO shares are frequently **speculative and illiquid at first**, and allocations are often limited. On the Series 65, an adviser recommending an IPO must still apply a **suitability/fiduciary** analysis — \"it's a hot IPO\" is never a substitute for matching the security to the client's objectives and risk tolerance.\n\n#### Secondary Offering (and Follow-On Offerings)"},
  {"id":278,"part":"Part II","section":"G. Equity Public Offering","anchor":"g-equity-public-offering","text":"After a company is public, it can bring more shares to market. Here the terminology is precise and heavily tested."},
  {"id":279,"part":"Part II","section":"G. Equity Public Offering","anchor":"g-equity-public-offering","text":"- **Primary (follow-on / subsequent) offering:** The already-public company issues **additional new shares**. This raises new capital, so proceeds go to the **issuer**. It is still a *primary* offering — just not the *initial* one. A downside for existing owners is **dilution**: more shares outstanding shrinks each holder's percentage ownership and can reduce earnings per share.\n- **Secondary offering:** A registered sale of **large blocks of already-outstanding shares by existing shareholders** — typically founders, early venture investors, or other insiders cashing out. The shares are not new; the company issues nothing and receives **no proceeds**. All cash goes to the **selling shareholders**.\n- **Combined (split) offering:** Some registrations include **both** new shares (primary) and insider shares (secondary) in the same deal."},
  {"id":280,"part":"Part II","section":"G. Equity Public Offering","anchor":"g-equity-public-offering","text":"| | Primary Offering | Secondary Offering |\n|---|---|---|\n| Source of shares | Newly issued by the company | Previously issued, held by insiders |\n| Who sells | The issuer | Existing large shareholders |\n| Who receives proceeds | The issuer | The selling shareholders |\n| Effect on shares outstanding | Increases (dilution) | No change |\n| Examples | IPO, follow-on issuance | Founder/insider block sale |"},
  {"id":281,"part":"Part II","section":"G. Equity Public Offering","anchor":"g-equity-public-offering","text":"**Worked example — following the money.** A public company files a combined offering: it issues **5,000,000 new shares** (primary) at a **$20** public offering price with a **$1.20** per-share underwriting spread, while insiders sell **2,000,000 existing shares** (secondary) at the same price and spread."},
  {"id":282,"part":"Part II","section":"G. Equity Public Offering","anchor":"g-equity-public-offering","text":"- Net proceeds to the **issuer** (primary portion) = new shares × (offer price − spread)\n  = 5,000,000 × ($20.00 − $1.20) = 5,000,000 × $18.80 = **$94,000,000**.\n- Proceeds to the **issuer from the secondary portion** = **$0**. The secondary shares gross 2,000,000 × $20.00 = $40,000,000, and after the spread the selling insiders net 2,000,000 × $18.80 = **$37,600,000** — none of which reaches the company.\n\n**Exam tip:** The defining test question is *who gets the proceeds*. In a **secondary offering the issuer receives nothing** — the money goes to the selling shareholders even though the sale is registered and made by prospectus."},
  {"id":283,"part":"Part II","section":"G. Equity Public Offering","anchor":"g-equity-public-offering","text":"**Common trap:** \"Secondary offering\" does **not** mean \"the second time a company sells stock.\" A company's *second* sale of **new** shares is a **follow-on primary** offering. A **secondary** offering specifically means **outstanding shares being sold by insiders**.\n\n#### Special Purpose Acquisition Companies (SPACs) / Blind Pools / Blank-Check Companies"},
  {"id":284,"part":"Part II","section":"G. Equity Public Offering","anchor":"g-equity-public-offering","text":"A **special purpose acquisition company (SPAC)** is a shell company with **no commercial operations** that conducts an **IPO for the sole purpose of raising cash to later acquire or merge with an existing private company**, thereby taking that target public. Because investors buy in *before* any target is identified, a SPAC is the classic example of two overlapping exam terms:"},
  {"id":285,"part":"Part II","section":"G. Equity Public Offering","anchor":"g-equity-public-offering","text":"- **Blank-check company** — a development-stage company with **no specific business plan**, or whose only plan is to merge with or acquire an unspecified company.\n- **Blind pool** — an offering in which investors commit money **without knowing the specific investments** the sponsor will make; they are trusting the sponsor's judgment and track record.\n\nEvery SPAC is both a blank-check company and a blind pool. The typical lifecycle:"},
  {"id":286,"part":"Part II","section":"G. Equity Public Offering","anchor":"g-equity-public-offering","text":"1. **IPO.** The SPAC sells **units** to the public, commonly one common share plus a fraction of a **warrant**, usually priced around **$10 per unit**. The offering is registered under the Securities Act of 1933.\n2. **Trust account.** IPO proceeds are placed in a **trust**, generally invested in short-term U.S. **government securities**, and held while the sponsor searches for a target.\n3. **Search window.** The sponsor typically has a **set deadline (commonly about 18–24 months)** to identify a target and complete a business combination (the **\"de-SPAC\"** transaction).\n4. **Vote and redemption.** Shareholders vote on the proposed merger and generally have the right to **redeem** their shares for their pro-rata share of the trust (roughly the IPO price plus interest) if they disapprove.\n5. **Liquidation if no deal.** If no acquisition is completed by the deadline, the SPAC is **dissolved and the trust is returned** to shareholders."},
  {"id":287,"part":"Part II","section":"G. Equity Public Offering","anchor":"g-equity-public-offering","text":"Key risks the Series 65 emphasizes:\n\n- **Speculative / blind-pool uncertainty** — investors cannot analyze a target that does not yet exist; they rely entirely on the **sponsor**.\n- **Conflicts of interest** — the sponsor's **\"promote\"** (often about **20%** in founder shares) rewards *completing a deal*, creating pressure to close even a mediocre acquisition.\n- **Dilution** from founder shares and warrants.\n- **Illiquidity and volatility**, especially around the de-SPAC vote."},
  {"id":288,"part":"Part II","section":"G. Equity Public Offering","anchor":"g-equity-public-offering","text":"**Exam tip:** When a question describes a company that \"raises money through an IPO before identifying what it will buy,\" that is a **SPAC = blank-check company = blind pool** — all three labels point to the same speculative structure.\n\n**Common trap:** Do not treat the trust account as a guarantee of profit. It protects the **return of principal** to investors who redeem or if the SPAC liquidates, **not** the success of any eventual acquisition. Recommending a SPAC still demands a full **suitability/fiduciary** analysis, because the underlying investment is unknown and high-risk."},
  {"id":289,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"A **pooled investment** collects money from many investors into a single portfolio managed toward a stated objective. Pooling gives the small investor three things they could not get alone: professional management, diversification, and economies of scale. The Series 65 expects you to know how each pooled vehicle is structured, how it is priced, how it is bought and sold, whether it is redeemable or traded, and — critically — which are registered \"investment companies\" and which are exempt private funds.\n\nStart with the legal backbone. The **Investment Company Act of 1940** defines and regulates *investment companies* and sorts them into three classifications:"},
  {"id":290,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"| 1940 Act classification | Examples | Managed? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Face-amount certificate company** | Issues certificates promising a fixed sum at maturity | N/A (nearly obsolete) |\n| **Unit investment trust (UIT)** | Fixed portfolio sold in \"units\" | No — supervised, not managed |\n| **Management company** | **Open-end** (mutual fund) and **closed-end** funds | Yes — has an adviser and board |\n\nEverything in this subsection is either one of these registered types (mutual funds, closed-end funds, UITs, most ETFs) or a vehicle deliberately structured to *avoid* investment-company registration (private funds; REITs are exempt on different grounds)."},
  {"id":291,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Exam tip:** Memorize the three 1940 Act classifications and that \"management company\" splits into open-end and closed-end. A very common question simply asks you to identify which of these categories a UIT, mutual fund, or hedge fund falls into (a hedge fund falls into *none* — it is exempt).\n\n#### 1. Mutual Funds: Open-End vs. Closed-End\n\nBoth open-end and closed-end funds are **management companies**: each has a board of directors, a registered investment adviser, and an actively (or index-) managed portfolio. What separates them is capitalization — how shares are created and priced.\n\n##### Open-End Funds (the traditional \"mutual fund\")"},
  {"id":292,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"An open-end fund has a *continuous* offering. It stands ready to **issue new shares** to any buyer and to **redeem** existing shares from any seller, every business day. Because supply is elastic, an open-end share never trades in a secondary market; you buy from and sell back to the fund itself.\n\nKey structural features tested on the exam:"},
  {"id":293,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"- **Redeemable securities.** Investors sell shares back to the fund at net asset value (NAV). The fund must pay redemption proceeds within **seven days**.\n- **One class of security — common stock only.** An open-end fund may *not* issue senior securities (no bonds, no preferred stock) and is sharply limited in borrowing. It may, however, offer multiple *share classes* (A, B, C) that differ only in how sales charges are levied.\n- **Forward pricing (Rule 22c-1).** Orders are executed at the **next** computed NAV, not the last one. NAV is calculated at least once per business day (typically 4:00 p.m. ET market close). You cannot know your exact price at the moment you place the order."},
  {"id":294,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Net asset value (NAV) per share.** This is the fund's \"wholesale\" value.\n\nNAV per share = (Total Assets − Total Liabilities) / Shares Outstanding\n\n*Worked example:* A fund holds $500,000,000 in assets and owes $20,000,000 in liabilities, with 24,000,000 shares outstanding.\n\nNAV = ($500,000,000 − $20,000,000) / 24,000,000 = $480,000,000 / 24,000,000 = **$20.00 per share**.\n\n**Sales charges (loads) and the Public Offering Price (POP).** Many mutual funds add a front-end sales charge. The price the investor pays is the POP:\n\nPOP = NAV + Sales Charge\nPOP = NAV / (100% − Sales Charge %)\nSales Charge % = (POP − NAV) / POP\n\n*Worked example:* NAV is $20.00 and the fund carries a 5% front-end load."},
  {"id":295,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"POP = $20.00 / (1 − 0.05) = $20.00 / 0.95 = **$21.05**.\nDollar sales charge = $21.05 − $20.00 = $1.05.\nSales charge % = ($21.05 − $20.00) / $21.05 ≈ **5%** — note that the load is always figured as a percentage of the **POP**, not of NAV.\n\n**The 8.5% ceiling.** FINRA caps the maximum sales charge on a mutual fund at **8.5% of the POP**, and a fund may charge the full 8.5% only if it offers investors all three of: breakpoints, rights of accumulation, and reinvestment of dividends at NAV. Otherwise the maximum is lower."},
  {"id":296,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Breakpoints and related concepts** (frequent suitability questions):\n- **Breakpoints** — discounts on the sales charge at stated investment levels (e.g., a lower load at $25,000, $50,000, $100,000).\n- **Letter of Intent (LOI)** — a non-binding letter to invest enough to reach a breakpoint within **13 months**; it may be **backdated up to 90 days**.\n- **Rights of accumulation (ROA)** — lets prior holdings count toward reaching a breakpoint on new purchases.\n- **Breakpoint selling** — deliberately keeping an order just *below* a breakpoint (e.g., recommending $24,000 instead of $25,000) to earn a higher commission. This is a **prohibited practice** and a favorite exam trap."},
  {"id":297,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"**12b-1 fees.** An annual, asset-based fee charged out of fund assets to cover distribution and marketing. It is reviewed by the board (at least quarterly). A fund may not call itself **\"no-load\"** if its 12b-1 fee exceeds **0.25%**.\n\n**Share classes** (know the suitability logic):"},
  {"id":298,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"| Class | Charge structure | Best suited for |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A** | Front-end load; lowest 12b-1; gets breakpoints | Large and/or long-term investors |\n| **B** | Back-end load (CDSC) that declines to zero over years; higher 12b-1; converts to A | Smaller long-term investors (largely phased out) |\n| **C** | Level load (~1% 12b-1); little/no back-end charge after ~1 year | Shorter horizons; **expensive if held long term** |\n\n**Common trap:** Because Class C has no front-end load, students assume it is \"cheapest.\" For a long holding period, the perpetual ~1% 12b-1 makes Class C the *most* expensive. Match the share class to the time horizon and amount invested.\n\n##### Closed-End Funds"},
  {"id":299,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"A closed-end fund raises a **fixed** amount of capital in a one-time IPO, then its shares **trade on an exchange or OTC** like any stock. The fund does **not** continuously issue or redeem shares."},
  {"id":300,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"- **Not redeemable.** To exit, you sell your shares to another investor in the secondary market through a broker (paying a commission or spread), not back to the fund.\n- **Priced by supply and demand.** The market price can be **above NAV (a premium)** or **below NAV (a discount)** — it is not tied to NAV.\n- **Priced continuously** throughout the trading day, not once at 4:00 p.m.\n- **May issue senior securities.** Closed-end funds can issue preferred stock and bonds and use **leverage**, which open-end funds cannot.\n\n**Premium/discount to NAV:**\n\nPremium/Discount % = (Market Price − NAV) / NAV"},
  {"id":301,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"*Worked example:* A closed-end fund has an NAV of $20.00 and trades at $18.00.\n(18 − 20) / 20 = −0.10 = **trading at a 10% discount**. If instead it traded at $22.00: (22 − 20) / 20 = +10% **premium**.\n\n**Open-end vs. closed-end — the master comparison (memorize this):**"},
  {"id":302,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"| Feature | Open-end (mutual fund) | Closed-end fund |\n|---|---|---|\n| Shares outstanding | Variable (continuous issue/redeem) | Fixed (IPO) |\n| How you exit | **Redeem** with the fund at NAV | **Sell** in the secondary market |\n| Pricing | NAV-based (POP), forward priced daily | Market price; continuous; premium/discount to NAV |\n| Secondary trading | No | Yes (exchange/OTC) |\n| Senior securities / leverage | Not permitted | Permitted |\n| Cost to buy | Sales charge/load | Commission/markup |"},
  {"id":303,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Common trap:** \"Closed-end fund shares are redeemable\" is **false** — that is an open-end feature. Conversely, \"a mutual fund can trade at a discount to NAV\" is **false**; only closed-end (and other exchange-traded) vehicles do.\n\n#### 2. Unit Investment Trusts (UITs)\n\nA **UIT** is the second 1940 Act classification. It buys a **fixed, unmanaged portfolio** of securities and holds it, then sells redeemable **\"units\"** (units of beneficial interest) to investors."},
  {"id":304,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"Defining characteristics:\n- **No active management.** There is **no board of directors and no investment adviser**, so there are **no management fees**. The portfolio is *supervised* (may be swapped only under narrow rules) but not traded around. A trustee holds the assets.\n- **Fixed portfolio with a termination date.** A UIT has a defined maturity/termination point — for example, a bond UIT holds a set of bonds until they mature, then dissolves and returns principal.\n- **Redeemable units.** Like a mutual fund, units are **redeemable** with the trust at NAV; the sponsor often maintains a secondary market as a convenience.\n- Two broad forms: a **fixed (debt) trust** holding bonds to maturity, and an **equity trust** holding a fixed basket of stocks for a set term."},
  {"id":305,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Exam tip:** The single most-tested UIT fact is that it is **not managed** — no adviser, no board, no ongoing management fee. If an answer choice says a UIT is \"actively managed\" or \"has a portfolio manager who trades to beat an index,\" it is wrong.\n\n**Common trap:** Do not confuse a UIT with a *closed-end* fund. Both may be traded through a sponsor's secondary market, but UIT units are **redeemable** and the portfolio is **fixed/unmanaged**, whereas closed-end fund shares are **not redeemable** and the portfolio **is actively managed**. Note also that many **ETFs and insurance-company separate accounts** are legally organized as UITs.\n\n#### 3. Exchange-Traded Funds (ETFs)"},
  {"id":306,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"An **ETF** is a registered investment company — usually an **open-end management company**, sometimes a **UIT** — whose shares **trade on an exchange throughout the day** like a stock. Most ETFs are **passively managed to track an index** (e.g., the S&P 500), though actively managed ETFs exist."},
  {"id":307,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"What to know:\n- **Intraday trading and pricing.** Unlike a mutual fund (one daily NAV), an ETF trades continuously at a **market price** set by supply and demand, and can trade at a small premium or discount to NAV.\n- **Arbitrage keeps price near NAV.** Large institutions called **authorized participants** create and redeem shares in giant \"creation units\" (in kind) whenever price diverges from NAV, arbitraging the gap away. Retail investors **cannot redeem** individual shares with the fund — they must **sell in the market**.\n- **Trades like a stock.** ETFs can be **bought on margin, sold short, and traded with limit/stop orders and options**. Investors pay a **commission/spread**, not a traditional sales load.\n- **Low expense ratios and tax efficiency.** The in-kind creation/redemption mechanism minimizes taxable capital-gain distributions, and index ETFs have low costs."},
  {"id":308,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"**ETF vs. open-end mutual fund (frequent comparison):**\n\n| Feature | Mutual fund (open-end) | ETF |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pricing frequency | Once/day at NAV (forward priced) | Continuous, intraday market price |\n| How bought/sold | With the fund; sales charge | On an exchange; commission/spread |\n| Margin / short selling / options | No | Yes |\n| Retail redemption | Yes, at NAV | No (only APs redeem creation units) |\n| Typical management style | Active or index | Mostly index (some active) |"},
  {"id":309,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Exam tip:** The distinguishing ETF features are **intraday exchange trading, marginability/shortability, and low cost/tax efficiency**. **Common trap:** an ETF is *not* \"redeemable\" for an ordinary investor the way a mutual fund is; the retail investor **sells** the ETF in the market.\n\n#### 4. Real Estate Investment Trusts (REITs)\n\nA **REIT** is a company that **owns, operates, or finances income-producing real estate** and pools investor capital to do so. A REIT is **not** an investment company under the 1940 Act — it is a distinct tax entity — but the exam treats it as a pooled real-estate vehicle."},
  {"id":310,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Types by what they hold:**\n- **Equity REIT** — owns and operates properties (apartments, malls, offices); income is mostly rent. Most common.\n- **Mortgage REIT** — holds real-estate debt (mortgages/mortgage-backed securities); income is mostly interest; sensitive to interest rates.\n- **Hybrid REIT** — combination of both."},
  {"id":311,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Tax structure (heavily tested):** To qualify for special \"conduit\" tax treatment, a REIT must generally distribute **at least 90% of its taxable income** to shareholders; it then avoids corporate tax on the distributed portion. Qualification also requires roughly **75% of assets** in real estate and about **75% of gross income** from real estate. REIT dividends are generally taxed to the investor as **ordinary income** (they are usually *not* \"qualified dividends\"), though a portion may be eligible for the 20% qualified business income deduction."},
  {"id":312,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Common trap — the DPP distinction:** A REIT **passes through income but NOT losses**. It is *not* a direct participation program (limited partnership). If a question says a REIT lets investors \"deduct the property's operating losses/depreciation on their own returns,\" that describes a real estate **limited partnership/DPP**, not a REIT.\n\n**Liquid (listed) vs. non-liquid (non-traded) REITs** — an explicit outline point:"},
  {"id":313,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"| | **Listed (liquid) REIT** | **Non-traded (non-liquid) REIT** |\n|---|---|---|\n| SEC registration | Registered | **Registered** (SEC-registered, but not listed) |\n| Trading venue | Trades on a national exchange | **No public exchange** listing |\n| Liquidity | High — sell any trading day | **Low** — redemption programs are limited, suspendable, and often penalized |\n| Pricing | Transparent market price | Opaque; NAV estimated periodically |\n| Fees | Modest | **High upfront** commissions/offering costs |\n| Suitability concern | Standard | Illiquidity, valuation uncertainty, high costs |"},
  {"id":314,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Exam tip:** Distinguish these three: **listed/public** REITs (registered + exchange-traded = liquid), **non-traded/public** REITs (registered but *not* listed = illiquid, high fees, valuation risk), and **private** REITs (not registered, sold to accredited investors only). The exam's biggest concern with **non-traded REITs is illiquidity and lack of transparent pricing**, which drive suitability problems.\n\n#### 5. Private Funds: Hedge Funds, Private Equity, and Venture Capital\n\n**Private funds** are pooled vehicles deliberately structured to **avoid registration as investment companies** under the 1940 Act. They rely on one of two exclusions:"},
  {"id":315,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"- **Section 3(c)(1)** — no more than **100 investors**.\n- **Section 3(c)(7)** — an unlimited number of investors, but **all must be \"qualified purchasers\"** (generally individuals with **$5 million** in investments).\n\nBecause they are private, their interests are sold by **private placement (Regulation D)** to **accredited investors** — generally income over **$200,000** (individual) / **$300,000** (joint) for two years, **or** net worth over **$1 million excluding the primary residence**. They are illiquid, lightly regulated, and carry high minimums."},
  {"id":316,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Performance-based (incentive) fees.** Private funds famously charge **\"2 and 20\"** — about a **2% management fee** plus a **20% performance fee (carried interest)** on profits. Under **Investment Advisers Act Rule 205-3**, an adviser may charge such performance fees only to a **\"qualified client,\"** defined (current thresholds) as a client with at least **$1,100,000** managed by the adviser **or** a net worth over **$2,200,000**.\n\n**The three private-fund types:**"},
  {"id":317,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"- **Hedge funds** — aggressively managed pools that pursue absolute returns using **leverage, short selling, derivatives, and concentrated bets**. They often impose **lock-up periods** and redemption gates, so liquidity is limited. Investments in the underlying securities are generally liquid, but *investor access* is restricted.\n- **Private equity (PE) funds** — invest in the **equity of private (or taken-private) operating companies**, often via **buyouts**, aiming to improve and later sell them. Structured as **limited partnerships** (investors are LPs; the fund manager is the GP), with **capital calls** over a multi-year horizon and long lock-ups (often 7–10+ years). Highly illiquid.\n- **Venture capital (VC) funds** — a form of private equity focused on **early-stage, high-growth startups**. Very high risk, long horizons, and expected high failure rates offset by occasional large winners."},
  {"id":318,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Private funds vs. registered funds (the key contrasts):**\n\n| | Registered fund (mutual/closed-end) | Private fund (hedge/PE/VC) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1940 Act registration | Registered | **Exempt** — 3(c)(1) or 3(c)(7) |\n| Who may invest | General public | **Accredited investors / qualified purchasers** |\n| Offering | Public offering (prospectus) | **Private placement (Reg D)** |\n| Liquidity | High (daily) | **Low** (lock-ups, gates, capital calls) |\n| Performance fees | Restricted | **Common (2 and 20)** — only to qualified clients |"},
  {"id":319,"part":"Part II","section":"H. Types of Pooled Investments","anchor":"h-types-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Exam tip:** The defining traits of a private fund are that it is **unregistered under the 1940 Act, sold privately to accredited/qualified investors, illiquid, and charges performance fees**. **Common trap:** hedge funds are *not* registered investment companies and are *not* liquid to the investor — an answer describing a hedge fund as \"SEC-registered and redeemable daily like a mutual fund\" is wrong. Also remember the distinction in emphasis: **VC = startups/early stage; PE (buyouts) = mature private companies; hedge funds = tradable securities with aggressive strategies.**"},
  {"id":320,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"A **pooled investment** collects money from many investors and buys a single, professionally managed portfolio of securities. Each investor owns a proportional share of that portfolio. The four types you must know are:"},
  {"id":321,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"| Vehicle | Legal form | How shares are priced | Where you buy/sell |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Open-end fund** (mutual fund) | Continuously issues/redeems shares | At NAV (forward priced) once per day | Bought from and redeemed by the fund itself |\n| **Closed-end fund (CEF)** | Fixed number of shares, one-time IPO | Market price set by supply/demand | Traded on an exchange all day |\n| **Exchange-traded fund (ETF)** | Usually open-end structure with a creation/redemption mechanism | Market price, kept near NAV by arbitrage | Traded on an exchange all day |\n| **Unit investment trust (UIT)** | Fixed, unmanaged portfolio, set termination date | At NAV | Redeemable with the trust |"},
  {"id":322,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"Most Series 65 questions center on **open-end mutual funds**, so start there and treat CEFs, ETFs, and UITs as contrasts.\n\n**Exam tip:** The single most important structural fact is *redeemability*. Open-end fund shares and UIT units are **redeemable securities** — you sell them back to the issuer at NAV. Closed-end fund shares are **not** redeemable at all; ETF shares are redeemable only by authorized participants in large blocks. An ordinary investor exits a CEF or ETF by selling to another investor on an exchange at the prevailing market price.\n\n#### Pricing: Net Asset Value (NAV) and Forward Pricing"},
  {"id":323,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"**NAV** is the per-share value of the fund's portfolio. It is the foundation for everything else in this subsection.\n\n> **NAV = (Total Assets − Total Liabilities) / Number of Shares Outstanding**\n\n**Worked example.** A fund holds securities and cash worth $500,000,000, owes $20,000,000 in liabilities, and has 24,000,000 shares outstanding.\n\n- NAV = ($500,000,000 − $20,000,000) / 24,000,000\n- NAV = $480,000,000 / 24,000,000 = **$20.00 per share**"},
  {"id":324,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Forward pricing** is the rule (Rule 22c-1 under the Investment Company Act of 1940) that mutual fund orders are executed at the **next** NAV calculated after the order is received — not the last posted price. NAV must be computed at least once every business day, and in practice this happens at the close of the market (4:00 p.m. ET). An order to buy or redeem placed at 2:00 p.m. gets that day's 4:00 p.m. NAV; an order placed at 4:30 p.m. gets the *next* business day's NAV."},
  {"id":325,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Common trap:** Forward pricing exists to prevent **late trading** and stale-price arbitrage. Answer choices suggesting an investor can lock in the \"current\" or \"known\" price of a mutual fund are wrong — the exact NAV is unknown at the moment the order is entered.\n\n#### Pricing: Discount and Premium (ETFs and Closed-End Funds)\n\nBecause closed-end funds and ETFs trade on an exchange, their **market price is set by supply and demand** and can differ from NAV.\n\n- **Premium:** market price is *above* NAV.\n- **Discount:** market price is *below* NAV.\n\n> **Premium/Discount % = (Market Price − NAV) / NAV**\n\n**Worked example (closed-end fund).** A CEF has an NAV of $22.00 but trades at $19.80."},
  {"id":326,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"- (19.80 − 22.00) / 22.00 = −2.20 / 22.00 = −0.10 = **trading at a 10% discount**\n\n**Why CEFs can drift from NAV:** A closed-end fund has a fixed share count and no redemption mechanism, so nothing forces price toward NAV. Many CEFs trade at persistent discounts (sometimes premiums), driven purely by market demand."},
  {"id":327,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Why ETFs stay close to NAV:** ETFs have a **creation/redemption** mechanism. Large institutions (\"authorized participants\") can exchange baskets of the underlying securities for ETF shares and vice versa. If the ETF trades above NAV, arbitrageurs create new shares and sell them (pushing price down); if below NAV, they buy shares and redeem them (pushing price up). This **arbitrage** keeps the ETF's market price tightly aligned with NAV.\n\n**Exam tip:** Only exchange-traded pooled products (CEFs and ETFs) can trade at a premium or discount. An **open-end mutual fund never trades at a discount or premium** — it is always transacted at NAV (plus any sales charge on the buy side)."},
  {"id":328,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"#### Fee Structures and Other Costs\n\nThere are two very different kinds of cost, and the exam tests whether you can tell them apart:\n\n1. **Sales charges (loads)** — one-time charges tied to buying or selling, paid to compensate the selling broker-dealer.\n2. **Ongoing operating expenses (the expense ratio)** — annual costs deducted from fund assets every year you hold the fund.\n\n**Loads and the Public Offering Price (POP).** For a front-end-load mutual fund, the price you pay (the POP) equals NAV plus the sales charge.\n\n> **POP = NAV + Sales Charge**, and equivalently **POP = NAV / (1 − Sales Charge %)**\n> **Sales Charge % = (POP − NAV) / POP**"},
  {"id":329,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Worked example.** A Class A fund has an NAV of $20.00 and a 5% sales charge.\n\n- POP = $20.00 / (1 − 0.05) = $20.00 / 0.95 = **$21.05**\n- Dollar sales charge = $21.05 − $20.00 = $1.05\n- Check: $1.05 / $21.05 = 4.99% ≈ 5%\n\n**Common trap:** The sales charge percentage is figured **on the POP, not the NAV**. Do *not* compute POP as $20.00 × 1.05 = $21.00 — that understates the load. Always divide NAV by (1 − load %)."},
  {"id":330,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Maximum sales charge:** Under FINRA rules, a mutual fund's total sales charge may not exceed **8.5% of the POP**. To charge the full 8.5%, the fund must offer investors **breakpoints, rights of accumulation, and reinvestment of dividends at NAV**; missing any of these lowers the cap."},
  {"id":331,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Breakpoints** are volume discounts — the sales charge percentage drops at stated investment thresholds. Two ways to reach a breakpoint:\n- **Letter of Intent (LOI):** the investor pledges to invest enough to reach a breakpoint within **13 months**; it may be **backdated up to 90 days**. Shares are held in escrow to cover the difference if the investor falls short.\n- **Rights of accumulation (ROA):** existing holdings count toward the next breakpoint on new purchases (no time limit).\n\n**Common trap: breakpoint sales.** It is a prohibited practice to recommend a purchase just *below* a breakpoint (e.g., $24,000 when the discount begins at $25,000) so as to earn a higher commission."},
  {"id":332,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Expense ratio.** This is the fund's **annual operating cost** as a percentage of average net assets. It captures the management (advisory) fee, 12b-1 fees, and other administrative costs.\n\n> **Expense Ratio = Annual Fund Operating Expenses / Average Net Assets**\n\n**Worked example.** A fund incurs $15,000,000 of operating expenses on average net assets of $1,000,000,000.\n\n- Expense Ratio = $15,000,000 / $1,000,000,000 = 0.015 = **1.5%**\n\n**Exam tip:** The expense ratio **includes** the management fee and 12b-1 fees but **excludes sales loads** (loads are a one-time charge, not an annual operating expense) and **excludes portfolio brokerage/transaction costs**."},
  {"id":333,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"**12b-1 fees.** Named after the 1940 Act rule that permits them, these are annual fees charged against fund assets to pay for **distribution and marketing** (advertising, selling, and shareholder-service costs). Because they are deducted every year, they raise the expense ratio and are a form of ongoing cost that ultimately compensates for distribution.\n\n- The maximum 12b-1 fee is **0.75%** for distribution/marketing, plus up to **0.25%** for shareholder services — **1.00% total**.\n- A fund may call itself **\"no-load\"** only if it charges no sales load and its 12b-1/service fee is **0.25% or less**."},
  {"id":334,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Exam tip:** A \"no-load\" fund still has an expense ratio — it just has no sales charge and a low (≤0.25%) 12b-1 fee. \"No-load\" does **not** mean \"no cost.\"\n\n#### Share Classes (A, B, and C)\n\nMany funds offer the *same* portfolio through different share classes that repackage *when and how* the investor pays. The portfolio is identical; only the cost structure differs."},
  {"id":335,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"| Feature | **Class A** | **Class B** | **Class C** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Front-end load | Yes (paid at purchase) | No | No |\n| Back-end load (CDSC) | Usually none | Yes, **declining** over time | Small (often 1% for the first year), then none |\n| 12b-1 / ongoing expenses | **Lowest** | High | High (level ~1%) |\n| Breakpoints | Yes | No | No |\n| Converts to Class A? | — | Yes, after several years | Historically no (level load continues) |\n| Best suited for | **Large amounts / long horizons** | Rarely offered today | **Smaller amounts / shorter-to-intermediate horizons** |"},
  {"id":336,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"- **Class A** — pay a front-end load up front, but enjoy the lowest annual expenses and qualify for breakpoints. Cheapest for a large investment held a long time.\n- **Class B** — no front-end load, but a **contingent deferred sales charge (CDSC)** applies if you redeem early; the CDSC declines each year to zero (e.g., 5% year 1 down to 0% by year 6), after which the shares typically convert to lower-cost Class A. B shares carry high 12b-1 fees while held and have largely been phased out in the industry.\n- **Class C** — no front-end load and usually a small 1% CDSC only in the first year, but a **level load** — persistently high (~1%) annual 12b-1 expenses that never step down. Cheapest for short holding periods; most expensive over long periods."},
  {"id":337,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Exam tip:** Match the class to the **investment amount and time horizon**. Large + long-term → **Class A** (breakpoints + low ongoing cost). Short-term → **Class C** (no front load, small CDSC). Recommending **Class B or C for a very large, long-term purchase** is a classic *unsuitable* recommendation because the investor loses A-share breakpoints and pays higher annual fees.\n\n#### Liquidity"},
  {"id":338,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"- **Open-end mutual funds:** Highly liquid — shares are redeemable with the fund at NAV. By law the fund must send redemption proceeds within **7 days**. The limitation is that you can transact **only once per day** at the forward-priced NAV; you cannot trade intraday.\n- **Closed-end funds and ETFs:** Trade **intraday** on an exchange, so you can buy or sell any time the market is open at the prevailing market price. ETFs are generally very liquid; a thinly traded closed-end fund may have less trading volume and wider spreads.\n- **UITs:** Units are redeemable with the trust, and many sponsors maintain a secondary market."},
  {"id":339,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Exam tip:** Both structures are \"liquid,\" but in different ways — mutual funds offer **once-daily redemption at NAV**, while exchange-traded products offer **continuous intraday trading at market price**.\n\n#### Tax Implications\n\nUnder **Subchapter M** of the Internal Revenue Code, a fund that distributes at least **90% of its net investment income** to shareholders qualifies as a **regulated investment company (RIC)** and is taxed only on the amount it *retains*. This is the **conduit (pipeline) theory** — income \"flows through\" to shareholders, so distributed income is taxed once, at the shareholder level, rather than twice.\n\nKey rules the exam tests:"},
  {"id":340,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"- **Distributions are taxable to the shareholder even if reinvested.** Automatic reinvestment does not defer the tax.\n- **Reinvested distributions increase the shareholder's cost basis**, which reduces the taxable gain when the shares are eventually sold.\n- **Dividend distributions:** taxed as ordinary income unless they are **qualified dividends** (taxed at lower long-term capital-gains rates when holding-period requirements are met).\n- **Capital gains distributions:** when the fund sells appreciated holdings, it passes the gain to shareholders. A capital gains **distribution** is treated as **long-term** to the shareholder if the *fund* held the asset more than a year — regardless of how long the shareholder has owned the fund.\n- **Selling your fund shares:** any gain or loss is the shareholder's own capital gain/loss, and *its* long- vs. short-term character depends on how long the **shareholder** held the shares."},
  {"id":341,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Common trap — \"buying a dividend\":** Purchasing fund shares just before a distribution's record date is generally unwise. The NAV drops by the distribution amount, and the investor simply receives back part of their own principal as a **taxable** distribution.\n\n**ETF tax efficiency:** Because ETFs use an **in-kind creation/redemption** process, they can dispose of appreciated securities without triggering taxable capital gains inside the fund. This typically makes ETFs **more tax-efficient** than comparable open-end mutual funds, which must sell securities for cash to meet redemptions.\n\n#### Benefits and Risks"},
  {"id":342,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Benefits of pooled investing:**\n- **Diversification** — spread risk across many securities that a small investor could not buy individually.\n- **Professional management** — a dedicated adviser selects and monitors the portfolio.\n- **Economies of scale** — lower per-share transaction costs and access to institutional pricing.\n- **Liquidity** — daily (mutual funds) or intraday (ETFs/CEFs) access to your money.\n- **Convenience** — automatic dividend reinvestment, systematic investing, recordkeeping, and a range of objectives to choose from."},
  {"id":343,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Risks and drawbacks:**\n- **Market risk** — diversification reduces company-specific (unsystematic) risk but **cannot eliminate systematic (market) risk**; the fund still falls when its market falls.\n- **Costs** — loads and annual expenses drag on returns; high-fee funds must outperform just to keep pace.\n- **No control over the timing of capital gains** — a shareholder can receive (and owe tax on) a capital gains distribution even in a year the fund's price *declined*.\n- **Manager / strategy risk** — performance depends on the manager's skill, and the strategy may underperform its benchmark.\n- **Closed-end/ETF-specific** — a CEF can trade at a persistent discount; thinly traded products carry liquidity and pricing risk."},
  {"id":344,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Exam tip:** A favorite point is that a mutual fund **cannot diversify away market (systematic) risk**. Answer choices claiming a diversified fund is \"safe\" or \"removes all risk\" are wrong.\n\n#### Relative Comparisons: Evaluating and Comparing Funds\n\nWhen an adviser compares funds (a suitability skill the exam rewards), several relative factors matter beyond raw past return:"},
  {"id":345,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"- **Benchmarks.** Compare a fund's performance to an **appropriate index** and to peer funds with the same objective. A large-cap U.S. stock fund should be measured against the **S&P 500**; a small-cap fund against a small-cap index; a bond fund against a bond index. Beating the wrong benchmark tells you nothing. Also weigh return **relative to the risk** taken, not return alone.\n- **Manager tenure.** A track record is only meaningful if the **same manager** produced it. If the star manager who generated the fund's history recently left, the historical numbers may not reflect what the new manager will do. Always ask *how long the current manager has run the fund.*\n- **Change in investment po"},
  {"id":346,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"licy.** A fund's **fundamental investment objective or policy cannot be changed without a majority vote of the outstanding shares** (Investment Company Act of 1940). A change signals that past results were achieved under a different mandate and may not carry forward — evaluate the fund under its current policy.\n- **Investment style.** Classify the fund by **style** — e.g., **value vs. growth** and **large-, mid-, or small-cap** (the familiar \"style box\"). Comparisons are only fair between funds of the same style. Watch for **style drift** — a manager straying from the stated style (a growth manager buying value names), which can throw off diversification and benchmark comparisons and make the fund behave differently than the client expects."},
  {"id":347,"part":"Part II","section":"I. Characteristics of Pooled Investments","anchor":"i-characteristics-of-pooled-investments","text":"**Exam tip:** Past performance is disclosed with the reminder that it **does not guarantee future results**, and it is most relevant only when the **same manager, same policy, and same style** remain in place. Comparing a fund only to itself, to an inappropriate benchmark, or ignoring risk and expenses are all flawed approaches the exam wants you to reject."},
  {"id":348,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"A **derivative** is a financial instrument whose value is *derived from* — that is, depends on — the value of some other asset. That other asset is called the **underlying**. The underlying can be a stock, a bond, a stock index, a commodity (oil, wheat, gold), a currency, or an interest rate. A derivative is not the underlying asset itself; it is a separate contract whose price moves in response to changes in the underlying's price."},
  {"id":349,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"On the Series 65, derivatives are tested at a **conceptual level**, not at the level of complex trading strategies found on a securities-representative exam. You are expected to know the **definitions**, who is obligated versus who has a choice, the direction of the bet (bullish or bearish), where each instrument comes from (issuer versus exchange), and the standard vocabulary. Heavy multi-leg options math is generally *not* tested, though a small amount of intrinsic-value or breakeven arithmetic can appear."},
  {"id":350,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** The single most useful mental model is *right versus obligation*. **Options** give the buyer a **right** (a choice); **futures** and **forwards** create an **obligation** (a binding commitment) for both parties. Nail this distinction and most derivative questions become easy.\n\n#### Basic Terminology (underlying, strike, expiration)\n\nLearn this vocabulary first, because every other topic in this subsection uses it."},
  {"id":351,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"- **Underlying (underlying asset):** The asset the derivative is based on. If you own a call option on ABC stock, ABC stock is the underlying. A standard listed equity option contract covers **100 shares** of the underlying stock.\n- **Strike price (exercise price):** The fixed, predetermined price at which the holder of an option (or warrant/right) may buy or sell the underlying if the contract is exercised. It does **not** change as the market price moves.\n- **Expiration date:** The date on which the contract ends. After expiration, the right is gone and the contract is worthless if not exercised. Options have relatively short lives (traditionally up to about nine months for standard listed"},
  {"id":352,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"options; **LEAPS** are longer-dated options that can run a year or more). Rights are very short-lived (weeks); warrants are long-lived (often years).\n- **Premium:** The market price a buyer pays to acquire an option — the cost of obtaining the right. The option *seller* (writer) receives the premium.\n- **Holder (long) vs. writer (short):** The **holder** buys and owns the option and has the right to exercise. The **writer** sells (creates) the option and takes on the obligation to perform if the holder exercises.\n- **Exercise:** Invoking the contractual right — e.g., a call holder buying the stock at the strike.\n- **In-the-money / at-the-money / out-of-the-money:** Describes whether exercising the option would currently produce value (defined precisely below)."},
  {"id":353,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"**Intrinsic value** is the amount by which an option is in-the-money — the value it would have if exercised right now.\n\n- For a **call**: Intrinsic Value = Market Price of Underlying − Strike Price (never less than 0)\n- For a **put**: Intrinsic Value = Strike Price − Market Price of Underlying (never less than 0)\n\n*Worked example (call):* An investor holds a call with a **$50 strike** while the stock trades at **$58**. Intrinsic Value = $58 − $50 = **$8 per share**. Because a standard contract covers 100 shares, that is $800 of intrinsic value for one contract. If the stock instead traded at $47, the call would be out-of-the-money and its intrinsic value would be **$0** (never negative)."},
  {"id":354,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"Any premium above intrinsic value is **time value**. If that $50 call trades at a $10 premium while intrinsic value is $8, the extra **$2** is time value. At expiration, time value is gone and an option is worth only its intrinsic value.\n\n#### Options: Calls and Puts"},
  {"id":355,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"An **option** is a contract that gives its **buyer/holder the right, but not the obligation**, to buy or sell the underlying at the strike price on or before expiration. The **seller/writer** has the *obligation* to perform if the holder exercises. Listed options trade on exchanges and are standardized; the **Options Clearing Corporation (OCC)** issues and guarantees listed options, which is why an option holder does not rely on the specific writer's creditworthiness.\n\n**Call option — the right to BUY.**\nA call gives the holder the right to *buy* the underlying at the strike price."},
  {"id":356,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"- **Call buyer (long call):** **Bullish** — profits if the underlying rises. Maximum loss is the premium paid; potential gain is theoretically unlimited (the stock can keep rising). Motivation: to profit from a price increase with limited capital at risk, or to lock in a future purchase price.\n- **Call writer (short call):** **Bearish to neutral** — wants the stock to stay flat or fall so the option expires worthless and the writer keeps the premium. A writer who does not own the underlying (a **naked/uncovered call**) faces theoretically unlimited loss.\n\n**Put option — the right to SELL.**\nA put gives the holder the right to *sell* the underlying at the strike price."},
  {"id":357,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"- **Put buyer (long put):** **Bearish** — profits if the underlying falls. Maximum loss is the premium paid; maximum gain is large but limited — at most the strike price minus the premium, reached if the stock falls to $0. A put is also commonly bought as **portfolio protection** (a \"protective put\"): an investor who owns a stock buys a put to guarantee a minimum selling price, similar to insurance.\n- **Put writer (short put):** **Bullish to neutral** — wants the stock to stay flat or rise so the option expires worthless and the writer keeps the premium. Maximum loss (strike price minus premium) occurs if the stock falls to $0."},
  {"id":358,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"The table below summarizes the four basic positions (max loss and max gain are stated per share, as profit/loss figures).\n\n| Position | Right or Obligation | Market View | Max Loss | Max Gain |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Long call (buy call) | Right to buy | Bullish | Premium paid | Unlimited |\n| Short call (write call) | Obligation to sell | Bearish/neutral | Unlimited (if naked) | Premium received |\n| Long put (buy put) | Right to sell | Bearish | Premium paid | Strike − Premium |\n| Short put (write put) | Obligation to buy | Bullish/neutral | Strike − Premium | Premium received |"},
  {"id":359,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"**Breakeven** (a commonly tested calculation): the underlying price at which the option buyer neither makes nor loses money at expiration.\n\n- Call breakeven = Strike Price + Premium\n- Put breakeven = Strike Price − Premium\n\n*Worked example (put):* An investor buys one put with a **$40 strike** for a **$3 premium**. Put breakeven = $40 − $3 = **$37**. The put buyer starts to profit only if the stock falls below $37. If the stock is at $35 at expiration, intrinsic value is $40 − $35 = $5; subtract the $3 premium for a **net profit of $2 per share** ($200 per contract)."},
  {"id":360,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** Remember the mnemonic — **calls give the right to *call away* (buy)**; **puts give the right to *put to* (sell)** the underlying. Buyers of options are optimistic about their chosen direction (call buyers bullish, put buyers bearish); writers take the opposite view.\n\n**Common trap:** Do not confuse the *writer's* obligation with the *buyer's* right. The exam loves to ask what happens if the option is exercised: the **buyer chooses** whether to exercise; the **writer must perform** and has no choice. Also, the *maximum loss for any option buyer is the premium paid* — a frequent, easy-to-miss answer.\n\n#### Warrants"},
  {"id":361,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"A **warrant** is a certificate, **issued by a corporation**, that gives the holder the right to buy the issuer's stock at a fixed price for a **long period of time** — often **several years, and occasionally with no expiration (perpetual)**. Functionally it behaves like a long-term call option, but it is issued by the company itself rather than created by market participants, and exercising a warrant causes the company to **issue new shares** (diluting existing shareholders).\n\nKey features:"},
  {"id":362,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"- **Long-lived:** Warrants may run for years, far longer than typical options.\n- **Issued at a premium:** When first issued, the exercise price is typically set *above* the current market price of the stock, so warrants are usually out-of-the-money at issuance and have value only if the stock rises over time.\n- **A \"sweetener\":** Warrants are frequently attached to a bond or preferred stock offering to make it more attractive, allowing the issuer to offer a lower coupon. They are often **detachable** and can then trade separately.\n- **No dividends, no voting rights:** A warrant holder is not yet a shareholder.\n\n#### Rights (Subscription Rights / Preemptive Rights)"},
  {"id":363,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"A **right** (subscription right) is also **issued by the corporation**, but it exists to protect existing shareholders from dilution when the company issues new shares. Under a **preemptive right**, existing common shareholders are given the opportunity to buy newly issued shares **before** they are offered to the public, so they can maintain their proportionate ownership.\n\nKey features:"},
  {"id":364,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"- **Short-lived:** Rights expire quickly — typically a few **weeks** (often 30–45 days).\n- **Issued below market:** The subscription price is set *below* the current market price, giving existing holders an incentive to subscribe. This makes rights immediately valuable (in-the-money) when issued.\n- **One right per share:** Shareholders receive one right for each share they own; it usually takes a specified number of rights (plus cash) to buy one new share.\n- **Tradable:** Rights can typically be exercised, sold, or allowed to expire.\n\nThe following table contrasts the three instruments most often confused on the exam."},
  {"id":365,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"| Feature | Right (subscription) | Warrant | Listed Call Option |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Issued by | The corporation | The corporation | Created by investors; guaranteed by the OCC |\n| Gives right to | Buy new shares | Buy shares from issuer | Buy shares from the writer |\n| Life span | Very short (weeks) | Long (years / sometimes perpetual) | Short (traditionally ≤ ~9 months; LEAPS longer) |\n| Price vs. market at issue | Below current market | Above current market | Varies |\n| Primary purpose | Prevent dilution of current holders | \"Sweetener\" to make an offering attractive | Speculation / hedging |\n| New shares created on exercise? | Yes | Yes | No |"},
  {"id":366,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** The cleanest way to separate **rights** from **warrants**: **R**ights are **R**apid/short-term and priced **below** market; **W**arrants are **W**ay long-term and priced **above** market at issue. Both are issued *by the company* and both are ways to buy the issuer's stock, which is why they are contrasted with options (created by third parties)."},
  {"id":367,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"**Common trap:** A question may describe \"a long-term instrument, issued by the company, letting the holder buy stock, sometimes attached to a bond to lower its interest cost.\" That is a **warrant**, not a right and not an option. Conversely, \"a short-term instrument protecting existing shareholders' proportionate ownership\" is a **right**.\n\n#### Futures"},
  {"id":368,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"A **futures contract** is a standardized, **exchange-traded, legally binding agreement to buy or sell a specific quantity of an underlying asset at a set price on a set future date**. The crucial difference from an option is obligation: **both parties are obligated** to perform. There is no premium and no \"choice\" to walk away — the buyer must buy and the seller must sell (unless the position is closed out before delivery, which is what happens the vast majority of the time).\n\nTerminology mirrors options in some ways but differs in spirit:"},
  {"id":369,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"- **Long position (buyer):** Obligated to *take delivery* (buy) at the agreed price. Bullish — profits if prices rise.\n- **Short position (seller):** Obligated to *make delivery* (sell) at the agreed price. Bearish — profits if prices fall.\n- **Underlying:** Historically agricultural and other **commodities** (grain, livestock, metals, energy), and now also **financial futures** on interest rates, currencies, and stock indexes.\n- **Settlement:** Most contracts are closed out before expiration by taking an offsetting position; a minority go to actual physical delivery or cash settlement.\n\nTwo common uses on the exam:"},
  {"id":370,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"- **Hedging:** A farmer worried that wheat prices will fall before harvest sells wheat futures to lock in a selling price; a manufacturer worried about rising input costs buys futures to lock in a purchase price. Hedgers use futures to *reduce* risk.\n- **Speculation:** Traders take futures positions purely to profit from expected price moves, accepting risk in pursuit of gain."},
  {"id":371,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"A closely related instrument is a **forward contract**: like a futures contract, it obligates both parties to a future transaction at a set price, but a **forward is a private, customized, non-standardized agreement** (negotiated directly between two parties and traded over-the-counter) rather than a standardized exchange-listed product. Forwards carry more **counterparty (default) risk** because there is no exchange clearinghouse guaranteeing performance, and they are less liquid because their custom terms make them hard to trade to a third party."},
  {"id":372,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"| Feature | Futures | Forward | Option |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Obligation | Both parties obligated | Both parties obligated | Only the *writer* is obligated; buyer has a *choice* |\n| Standardized / where traded | Standardized; exchange-traded | Customized; over-the-counter (private) | Standardized (listed); exchange-traded |\n| Counterparty risk | Low (clearinghouse guarantees) | Higher (no clearinghouse) | Low for listed (OCC guarantees) |\n| Upfront cost | No premium (margin/good-faith deposit) | No premium | Buyer pays a premium |\n| Bullish party | Long (buyer) | Long (buyer) | Call buyer / put writer |"},
  {"id":373,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** If a question hinges on \"who *must* perform,\" think **futures/forwards = obligation for both sides; options = right for the buyer, obligation only for the writer.** If it hinges on \"standardized and exchange-traded versus customized and private,\" that is **futures versus forwards.**"},
  {"id":374,"part":"Part II","section":"J. Types of Derivative Securities","anchor":"j-types-of-derivative-securities","text":"**Common trap:** Students assume \"buying\" a futures contract works like buying an option — that the buyer can simply choose not to complete the trade and lose only a premium. That is wrong. A futures **buyer is obligated** and has real exposure well beyond any small good-faith margin deposit; there is no premium and no walk-away right. This obligation-versus-right contrast is the most frequently tested idea in this entire subsection."},
  {"id":375,"part":"Part II","section":"K. Characteristics of Derivative Securities","anchor":"k-characteristics-of-derivative-securities","text":"A **derivative** is a security or contract whose value is *derived from*—that is, based on—the price of some other underlying asset. That underlying asset can be a stock, a stock index, a bond, a commodity, a currency, or an interest rate. The derivative has no independent value of its own; it is simply a claim tied to, or a bet on, the movement of the underlying."},
  {"id":376,"part":"Part II","section":"K. Characteristics of Derivative Securities","anchor":"k-characteristics-of-derivative-securities","text":"The main derivatives tested on the Series 65 are **options** (calls and puts), **forwards and futures**, and the two equity derivatives issued by corporations, **rights** and **warrants**. You are not expected to trade complex multi-leg options strategies the way a Series 7 candidate is. Instead, the exam focuses on the *characteristics*: why investors use derivatives, how leverage magnifies both gains and losses, the difference between hedging and speculating, who the counterparty is and whether that party can fail to pay, and which positions expose an investor to potentially unlimited loss.\n\n#### Overview of the Main Derivative Types"},
  {"id":377,"part":"Part II","section":"K. Characteristics of Derivative Securities","anchor":"k-characteristics-of-derivative-securities","text":"Before analyzing the risks, know the vehicles at a high level."},
  {"id":378,"part":"Part II","section":"K. Characteristics of Derivative Securities","anchor":"k-characteristics-of-derivative-securities","text":"| Derivative | What it is | Underlying | Where it trades |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Call option** | Right to *buy* 100 shares at a set price (strike) before expiration | Stock/index | Listed exchange (standardized) |\n| **Put option** | Right to *sell* 100 shares at a set price before expiration | Stock/index | Listed exchange (standardized) |\n| **Forward** | Private contract to buy/sell an asset at a set price on a future date | Commodity, currency, etc. | Over-the-counter (customized) |\n| **Future** | Standardized, exchange-traded version of a forward | Commodity, index, rate | Futures exchange |\n| **Right** | Short-term privilege to buy new shares at a discount (from a rights offering) | Issuer's own stock | Issued to existing shareholders; short-lived (weeks) |\n| **Warrant** | Long-term option to buy the issuer's stock at a fixed price, often above market at issuance | Issuer's own stock | Often attached to bonds/stock as a \"sweetener\"; lasts years |"},
  {"id":379,"part":"Part II","section":"K. Characteristics of Derivative Securities","anchor":"k-characteristics-of-derivative-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** The classic rights-versus-warrants distinction is *time and price*. **Rights** are short-term (weeks) and let existing shareholders buy new shares *below* the current market price. **Warrants** are long-term (often years) and are typically issued with a subscription price *above* the market price at the time of issue.\n\n#### Options Vocabulary You Must Know\n\nEvery option has two sides:\n\n- The **buyer (long/holder)** pays the **premium** and owns the *right* to exercise. The buyer's maximum loss is limited to the premium paid.\n- The **seller (short/writer)** receives the premium and takes on the *obligation* to perform if exercised."},
  {"id":380,"part":"Part II","section":"K. Characteristics of Derivative Securities","anchor":"k-characteristics-of-derivative-securities","text":"For a **call**, the buyer profits when the underlying rises; for a **put**, the buyer profits when the underlying falls. One listed equity option contract covers **100 shares**, so a premium quoted as \"$3\" costs $3 x 100 = **$300**.\n\n**Breakeven formulas (plain text):**\n\n- **Call** breakeven = Strike Price + Premium\n- **Put** breakeven = Strike Price − Premium\n\n*Worked example:* An investor buys 1 XYZ Oct 50 call at a premium of 3.\nBreakeven = 50 + 3 = **$53**. The stock must climb above $53 by expiration for the buyer to profit. Between $50 and $53 the call has intrinsic value, so the holder recovers part—but not all—of the premium and still shows a net loss."},
  {"id":381,"part":"Part II","section":"K. Characteristics of Derivative Securities","anchor":"k-characteristics-of-derivative-securities","text":"#### Benefit and Risk #1: Leverage\n\n**Leverage** is the single most important characteristic of derivatives. It means controlling a large amount of the underlying asset with a small amount of money. A $300 call can control $5,000 worth of stock (100 shares at $50). This magnifies **percentage** gains and losses far beyond what owning the stock outright would produce.\n\n*Worked leverage example:*\nStock trades at $50/share. An investor has $300 to commit.\n\n- **Option route:** Buy 1 call with a $50 strike for a $3 premium ($300 total). This controls 100 shares.\n- **Stock route:** $300 buys 6 shares outright.\n\nNow assume the stock rises to $55."},
  {"id":382,"part":"Part II","section":"K. Characteristics of Derivative Securities","anchor":"k-characteristics-of-derivative-securities","text":"- Option intrinsic value = 55 − 50 = $5, so the contract is worth $5 x 100 = $500.\n  Gain = $500 − $300 = $200. Return = 200 / 300 = **+66.7%**.\n- Stock: gain = ($55 − $50) x 6 shares = $30. Return = 30 / 300 = **+10%**.\n\nThe same $5 move produces a 66.7% return with the option versus 10% with the stock—that is leverage. But leverage cuts both ways. If instead the stock closes at $50 or below at expiration, the option expires worthless and the buyer loses **100%** of the $300, while the stock owner is only down (or flat) a few dollars."},
  {"id":383,"part":"Part II","section":"K. Characteristics of Derivative Securities","anchor":"k-characteristics-of-derivative-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** When a question asks *why* an investor would buy an option instead of the stock, the answer is usually **leverage**—more exposure per dollar and a defined, limited maximum loss (the premium) for the buyer.\n\n**Common trap:** Leverage magnifies **percentage** gains and losses, not just the dollars at risk. A leveraged position can lose 100% of the premium quickly, whereas a diversified stock position rarely goes to zero overnight.\n\n#### Benefit #2: Hedging vs. Speculation\n\nDerivatives are used for two fundamentally different purposes, and the exam wants you to tell them apart."},
  {"id":384,"part":"Part II","section":"K. Characteristics of Derivative Securities","anchor":"k-characteristics-of-derivative-securities","text":"- **Hedging** = using a derivative to *reduce* risk in an existing position, like insurance. The investor already owns (or is short) the underlying and wants protection.\n- **Speculation** = using a derivative to *seek profit* from a price move, taking on risk in hopes of a leveraged gain. The speculator often has no underlying position at all.\n\nThe two most-tested hedges:"},
  {"id":385,"part":"Part II","section":"K. Characteristics of Derivative Securities","anchor":"k-characteristics-of-derivative-securities","text":"| Strategy | Position | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Protective put** | Own the stock **+** buy a put | Insures a *long* stock position against a price drop; the put sets a floor selling price. |\n| **Covered call** | Own the stock **+** sell a call | Generates premium income on a long position; the premium cushions a modest decline but caps the upside. |"},
  {"id":386,"part":"Part II","section":"K. Characteristics of Derivative Securities","anchor":"k-characteristics-of-derivative-securities","text":"*Protective put logic:* An investor owns 100 shares of a stock bought at $50 and fears a decline but doesn't want to sell. She buys a 45 put for $1. No matter how far the stock falls, she can exercise the put and sell at $45. Her maximum loss is limited to ($50 − $45) + $1 premium = **$6 per share**, in exchange for giving up $1 of return in every scenario. This is textbook risk reduction.\n\n**Exam tip:** A **protective put** is frequently described as \"buying insurance\" on a stock you own. The put's strike is the floor price; the premium is the insurance cost."},
  {"id":387,"part":"Part II","section":"K. Characteristics of Derivative Securities","anchor":"k-characteristics-of-derivative-securities","text":"**Common trap:** A **covered** call is a conservative income strategy—the risk is limited because you own the stock you might have to deliver. An **uncovered (naked)** call is the opposite: high-risk with unlimited loss potential (see below). Watch for questions that swap \"covered\" and \"uncovered.\"\n\n#### Risk #3: Unlimited Loss Potential on Some Positions\n\nThe most heavily tested risk concept for derivatives is that **certain positions carry unlimited loss potential, while others have loss capped at the premium.** The key is whether the position is \"naked\" (uncovered) and whether the underlying can move against it without limit."},
  {"id":388,"part":"Part II","section":"K. Characteristics of Derivative Securities","anchor":"k-characteristics-of-derivative-securities","text":"| Position | Maximum Gain | Maximum Loss | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Long call** (buyer) | Unlimited | Premium paid | Stock can rise without limit; buyer only risks premium. |\n| **Long put** (buyer) | Strike − Premium (stock to $0) | Premium paid | Stock can only fall to $0; buyer only risks premium. |\n| **Short (naked) call** (writer) | Premium received | **Unlimited** | Stock can rise infinitely; writer must deliver at strike. |\n| **Short put** (writer) | Premium received | Strike − Premium | Stock can fall only to $0; large but *limited* loss. |\n| **Short stock** | Short-sale price (stock to $0) | **Unlimited** | Must buy back; price can rise without limit. |"},
  {"id":389,"part":"Part II","section":"K. Characteristics of Derivative Securities","anchor":"k-characteristics-of-derivative-securities","text":"*Worked max-loss example (naked call):* A speculator writes (sells) 1 uncovered call with a $50 strike and collects a $3 premium. If the stock rises to $90, the writer must deliver 100 shares he does not own—buying them at $90 and selling at $50, a $40 loss, offset by the $3 premium = **$37 loss per share ($3,700)**. Because there is no ceiling on the stock price, the theoretical loss is unlimited.\n\n**Exam tip:** Memorize this rule of thumb—**the seller of a naked call and the short seller of stock both face unlimited loss** because the underlying price has no upper bound. Everyone else's downside is finite (buyers lose only the premium; put writers lose down to a zero underlying)."},
  {"id":390,"part":"Part II","section":"K. Characteristics of Derivative Securities","anchor":"k-characteristics-of-derivative-securities","text":"**Common trap:** A long put's maximum gain is *not* unlimited. Because the stock can fall no lower than $0, the put buyer's max gain = Strike − Premium (times 100 shares). Candidates often mislabel a long put as having \"unlimited\" profit.\n\n#### Risk #4: Counterparty Risk\n\n**Counterparty risk** (also called default or credit risk) is the danger that the *other side of the contract fails to honor its obligation*. This risk depends almost entirely on **where** the derivative trades."},
  {"id":391,"part":"Part II","section":"K. Characteristics of Derivative Securities","anchor":"k-characteristics-of-derivative-securities","text":"- **Exchange-traded/standardized derivatives** (listed options, futures) carry **little counterparty risk**. A central clearinghouse stands between buyer and seller and guarantees performance. For listed options that clearinghouse is the **Options Clearing Corporation (OCC)**; for futures it is the exchange's clearinghouse, supported by daily **margin** and **mark-to-market** settlement. You are effectively facing the clearinghouse, not an individual trader.\n- **Over-the-counter (OTC) derivatives** (forwards, swaps, and other privately negotiated contracts) carry **significant counterparty risk** because there is no central guarantor. If the party owing you money defaults, you may collect nothing. This is exactly the risk that surfaced with OTC derivatives in the 2008 financial crisis."},
  {"id":392,"part":"Part II","section":"K. Characteristics of Derivative Securities","anchor":"k-characteristics-of-derivative-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** Remember the trade-off. **Forwards** are customized and private, so they carry counterparty risk but offer flexibility. **Futures** are standardized and cleared, so they sacrifice customization but nearly eliminate counterparty risk. If a question emphasizes \"customized\" or \"private negotiation,\" think forward/OTC and heightened counterparty risk.\n\n**Common trap:** Do not assume \"derivative = high counterparty risk.\" Listed, exchange-cleared options and futures actually have *low* counterparty risk thanks to the clearinghouse. The high counterparty risk lives in the **OTC/forward/swap** world.\n\n#### Costs of Owning Derivatives"},
  {"id":393,"part":"Part II","section":"K. Characteristics of Derivative Securities","anchor":"k-characteristics-of-derivative-securities","text":"Beyond the market risks, derivatives carry specific *costs* the exam may reference:"},
  {"id":394,"part":"Part II","section":"K. Characteristics of Derivative Securities","anchor":"k-characteristics-of-derivative-securities","text":"- **Premium (options):** The non-refundable price the buyer pays; it is a real cost that must be recovered before the position is profitable (hence the breakeven formulas above).\n- **Time decay (theta):** An option is a *wasting asset*. All else equal, its value erodes as expiration approaches, and it becomes **worthless** if it expires out-of-the-money. The buyer can lose 100% simply by the passage of time even if the stock never moves against them.\n- **No income or ownership rights:** Option holders and warrant/right holders receive **no dividends** and have **no voting rights** on the underlying shares unless and until they exercise. Derivatives give price exposure only.\n- **Margin and mark-to-market (futures/short options):** Writers and futures traders must post margin and meet daily variation margin calls as positions are marked to market, which ties up capital and can force liquidation at a loss.\n- **Commissions and bid-ask spreads:** Transaction costs, plus the frictional cost of illiquid or wide-spread contracts."},
  {"id":395,"part":"Part II","section":"K. Characteristics of Derivative Securities","anchor":"k-characteristics-of-derivative-securities","text":"#### Putting It Together: Benefits vs. Risks Summary\n\n| Benefits of derivatives | Risks of derivatives |\n|---|---|\n| **Leverage**—large exposure for a small outlay | Leverage magnifies losses; buyers can lose 100% of premium |\n| **Hedging**—transfer or reduce risk (protective put, covered call) | **Unlimited loss** on naked calls and short stock |\n| **Income**—premium collection (covered calls) | **Time decay**—options are wasting assets |\n| **Flexibility**—tailor exposure to a view | **Counterparty risk** on OTC forwards/swaps |\n| Low counterparty risk on **listed/cleared** contracts | No dividends or voting rights; margin and transaction costs |"},
  {"id":396,"part":"Part II","section":"K. Characteristics of Derivative Securities","anchor":"k-characteristics-of-derivative-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** For a suitability question, derivatives are generally appropriate only for investors with the **sophistication, risk tolerance, and financial capacity** to absorb potentially rapid, total, or (in the case of naked writing) unlimited losses. A protective put or covered call can be *conservative*, but naked option writing and speculative futures trading are aggressive strategies suitable only for a small, knowledgeable subset of investors."},
  {"id":397,"part":"Part II","section":"L. Alternative Investments","anchor":"l-alternative-investments","text":"\"Alternative investments\" is the umbrella term for products that fall outside the traditional trio of stocks, bonds, and plain mutual funds. On the Series 65 they are grouped together because they share three recurring themes the exam loves to test: **complexity**, **illiquidity or hidden risk**, and **narrow suitability**. For almost every product below, the correct answer to a suitability question is some version of \"only for a sophisticated investor who understands the structure, can tolerate the risk, and does not need liquidity.\" Learn the mechanics, but also learn to recognize when a product is simply inappropriate.\n\n#### Limited Partnerships and Direct Participation Programs (DPPs)"},
  {"id":398,"part":"Part II","section":"L. Alternative Investments","anchor":"l-alternative-investments","text":"A **Direct Participation Program (DPP)** is a pooled investment that lets income, gains, losses, deductions, and credits flow **directly through** to the investors, who are taxed as if they owned the underlying assets personally. The most common legal form is the **limited partnership (LP)**. Common DPP categories tested on the exam are **real estate**, **oil and gas** (exploratory/wildcatting, developmental, and income programs), and **equipment leasing**.\n\n**Two classes of participant:**"},
  {"id":399,"part":"Part II","section":"L. Alternative Investments","anchor":"l-alternative-investments","text":"| Role | Management | Liability | Tax treatment |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **General Partner (GP)** | Runs day-to-day operations; makes all decisions | **Unlimited** personal liability | Flow-through |\n| **Limited Partner (LP)** | **Passive**; may NOT manage | Limited to amount invested (plus any signed obligations) | Flow-through |\n\nKey features the exam expects you to know:"},
  {"id":400,"part":"Part II","section":"L. Alternative Investments","anchor":"l-alternative-investments","text":"- **Flow-through (pass-through) taxation.** The partnership itself pays no tax; there is **no double taxation** as there is with a C corporation. Each partner reports their share of income and loss on their own return.\n- **Passive income and passive losses.** DPP losses are **passive losses**, and passive losses may only offset **passive income** — not earned income (salary) and not portfolio income (interest, dividends, capital gains).\n- **Illiquidity.** There is little or no active secondary market, and interests are not freely transferable. This is a defining suitability concern.\n- **Limited liability is conditional.** A limited partner who begins to actively manage the business can lose LP status and be treated as a general partner with unlimited liability.\n- **Documents.** A **certificate of limited partnership** is filed with the state; the investor completes a **subscription agreement** (the application), and the interest is not effective until the **GP signs and accepts** it. The subscription agreement is where the investor attests to meeting net-worth/income suitability standards."},
  {"id":401,"part":"Part II","section":"L. Alternative Investments","anchor":"l-alternative-investments","text":"**Dissolution priority (order in which claims are paid if the partnership liquidates):** secured lenders → general (unsecured) creditors → limited partners → general partners.\n\n**Exam tip:** The single most important suitability principle for DPPs is that the investment must make **economic sense on its own**. Tax benefits should be a secondary consideration, not the primary reason to invest. An answer choice that recommends a DPP mainly to generate a tax write-off is a trap.\n\n**Common trap:** Confusing which losses can be used. A DPP passive loss **cannot** shelter an investor's wages or their dividend/interest income — only passive income from that or another passive activity."},
  {"id":402,"part":"Part II","section":"L. Alternative Investments","anchor":"l-alternative-investments","text":"#### Exchange-Traded Notes (ETNs)\n\nAn **Exchange-Traded Note (ETN)** is a **senior unsecured debt obligation**, usually issued by a bank, that promises to pay a return tied to a specified index or benchmark — an equity index, a commodity, a currency, or a volatility measure — minus fees, at maturity. ETNs trade on an exchange throughout the day like a stock.\n\nThe critical distinction the exam draws is **ETN versus ETF**:"},
  {"id":403,"part":"Part II","section":"L. Alternative Investments","anchor":"l-alternative-investments","text":"| Feature | Exchange-Traded Note (ETN) | Exchange-Traded Fund (ETF) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Legal nature | Unsecured **debt** of the issuer | **Equity/ownership** in a portfolio |\n| Underlying holdings | **None** — just a promise to pay | Owns actual securities/assets |\n| Primary risk | **Issuer credit (default) risk** | Market risk of the holdings |\n| Tracking | Promises index return (minus fee) | Approximates index; may have tracking error |\n| Maturity | Yes (often 10–30 years) | No maturity |"},
  {"id":404,"part":"Part II","section":"L. Alternative Investments","anchor":"l-alternative-investments","text":"Because an ETN holder owns **only the issuer's promise to pay**, the dominant risk is **credit risk**: if the issuing bank becomes insolvent, the investor can lose most or all of their money **even if the tracked index performed well**. Additional risks include **liquidity/market-price risk** (the issuer can stop issuing new notes, causing the market price to trade at a premium or discount to indicative value) and **call risk** (many ETNs can be called by the issuer).\n\n**Exam tip:** If a question asks for the feature that distinguishes an ETN from an ETF, the answer is that the ETN is **unsecured debt carrying issuer credit risk with no underlying portfolio**.\n\n#### Leveraged Funds"},
  {"id":405,"part":"Part II","section":"L. Alternative Investments","anchor":"l-alternative-investments","text":"A **leveraged fund** (typically a leveraged ETF) uses derivatives — swaps, futures, and options — to seek a **multiple** of the **daily** return of an index, commonly **2x** or **3x**. If the index rises 1% on a given day, a 3x fund seeks +3% **that day**."},
  {"id":406,"part":"Part II","section":"L. Alternative Investments","anchor":"l-alternative-investments","text":"The concept the exam insists on is **daily reset**. The fund rebalances its exposure at the end of **each trading day** so the multiple applies to a single day's return. Because returns **compound**, the fund's performance over any period longer than one day will **not** equal the multiple times the index's cumulative return. In volatile, choppy (up-and-down) markets this drift is generally negative — a phenomenon called **volatility decay** (or \"beta slippage\").\n\n**Worked example (daily-reset compounding):**\n\nFormula for a leveraged fund's cumulative value: **Ending Value = Start × (1 + m×r₁) × (1 + m×r₂) × …**, where *m* is the leverage multiple and *rₜ* is the index's return on day *t*."},
  {"id":407,"part":"Part II","section":"L. Alternative Investments","anchor":"l-alternative-investments","text":"Assume a 3x leveraged fund (m = 3) and an index that starts at 100.\n\n- **Day 1:** index +10% → index goes 100 → 110. Fund: $100 × (1 + 3×0.10) = $100 × 1.30 = **$130**.\n- **Day 2:** index −10% (from 110) → index goes 110 → 99. Fund: $130 × (1 + 3×(−0.10)) = $130 × 0.70 = **$91**.\n\nOver the two days the **index fell only about 1%** (100 → 99). A naive investor would expect a 3x fund to be down about 3% (to ~$97). Instead the fund is down **9%** (to $91). The daily reset, combined with volatility, produced a loss far worse than 3× the index's cumulative move."},
  {"id":408,"part":"Part II","section":"L. Alternative Investments","anchor":"l-alternative-investments","text":"**Exam tip:** Leveraged funds are designed as **short-term (often single-day) trading tools**, not buy-and-hold investments. Recommending a leveraged ETF as a long-term core holding for a retiree is a classic **wrong answer**.\n\n#### Inverse Funds\n\nAn **inverse fund** seeks to deliver the **opposite** of the daily return of an index — **−1x**, or in leveraged-inverse form **−2x** or **−3x**. They let an investor profit from, or hedge against, a market decline **without** selling short or buying puts. If the index falls 1% on a day, a −1x fund seeks +1% that day."},
  {"id":409,"part":"Part II","section":"L. Alternative Investments","anchor":"l-alternative-investments","text":"Inverse funds have the **same daily-reset and compounding problem** as leveraged funds, and the drift can be even more pronounced with leveraged-inverse products. Held over long or volatile periods, an inverse fund can lose money **even if the index ends lower over that period**, because each day's move is compounded off a reset base.\n\n**Common trap:** Assuming that holding a −1x inverse fund for a year gives you the exact opposite of the index's yearly return. It does not — only the **daily** return is inverted, and compounding causes multi-day results to diverge, usually to the investor's disadvantage in choppy markets."},
  {"id":410,"part":"Part II","section":"L. Alternative Investments","anchor":"l-alternative-investments","text":"**Exam tip:** For **both** leveraged and inverse funds, remember the tested phrase: because of **daily reset and compounding**, performance over periods **longer than one day** can differ **substantially** from the stated multiple of the index. These funds require **daily monitoring** and are unsuitable for unsophisticated, long-term investors.\n\n#### Structured Products"},
  {"id":411,"part":"Part II","section":"L. Alternative Investments","anchor":"l-alternative-investments","text":"A **structured product** is a security whose payoff is **derived from** an underlying asset — a single stock, a basket, an index, a commodity, or a currency — by combining a **debt instrument** with an embedded **derivative**. Common examples include **structured notes**, **principal-protected notes (PPNs)**, **market-linked (equity-linked) CDs**, and **reverse convertible notes**."},
  {"id":412,"part":"Part II","section":"L. Alternative Investments","anchor":"l-alternative-investments","text":"Most structured products are issued by a bank or brokerage firm as its own **unsecured debt**, so — like an ETN — they carry the **credit risk of the issuer**. (Market-linked CDs are an exception on principal: the CD itself is a bank deposit that is FDIC-insured up to applicable limits, though the market-linked return is not guaranteed.) Issuer credit risk is the heart of the \"principal protection\" trap:"},
  {"id":413,"part":"Part II","section":"L. Alternative Investments","anchor":"l-alternative-investments","text":"- A **principal-protected note** promises return of principal at maturity **plus** upside linked to an index. But that protection is only as good as the **issuer's solvency**. If the issuer defaults, the \"guarantee\" is worthless. (The historical teaching example is investors in Lehman Brothers structured notes, whose \"protected\" principal was lost when Lehman failed in 2008.)\n- A **reverse convertible** pays an **above-market coupon**, but if the linked stock falls below a set level, the investor can be repaid in **shares of the depreciated stock** rather than cash — exposing them to the full downside."},
  {"id":414,"part":"Part II","section":"L. Alternative Investments","anchor":"l-alternative-investments","text":"Other suitability concerns the exam raises about structured products: **complexity and opacity** (hard for a retail investor to value or understand), **illiquidity** (thin or nonexistent secondary market — often designed to be held to maturity), **high embedded costs/fees**, and frequently **capped upside** in exchange for only partial downside protection."},
  {"id":415,"part":"Part II","section":"L. Alternative Investments","anchor":"l-alternative-investments","text":"**Exam tip:** For structured products the recurring correct themes are (1) **issuer credit risk** — \"principal protection\" is a promise, not a guarantee; (2) **complexity** demanding a sophisticated investor; and (3) **illiquidity**. If a question offers a structured product to an unsophisticated client seeking safety and liquidity, it is unsuitable.\n\n#### Putting It Together: Suitability Summary"},
  {"id":416,"part":"Part II","section":"L. Alternative Investments","anchor":"l-alternative-investments","text":"| Product | What it really is | Signature risk | Suitable for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Limited partnership / DPP | Pass-through pooled venture | Illiquidity; passive-loss limits; GP unlimited liability | Sophisticated investors who don't need liquidity and can use passive losses |\n| ETN | Unsecured debt tracking an index | **Issuer credit risk** (no underlying holdings) | Investors who understand it is a bank's promise, not a portfolio |\n| Leveraged fund | Daily multiple (2x/3x) of an index | **Daily reset / volatility decay** | Short-term traders who monitor actively |\n| Inverse fund | Daily opposite (−1x/−2x/−3x) of an index | **Daily reset / compounding** | Short-term hedgers/traders, not buy-and-hold |\n| Structured product | Bond + embedded derivative | Issuer credit risk; complexity; illiquidity | Sophisticated investors who understand the payoff |"},
  {"id":417,"part":"Part II","section":"L. Alternative Investments","anchor":"l-alternative-investments","text":"**Exam tip:** Across this entire category, when in doubt the exam rewards caution: these are **complex, higher-risk, often illiquid** products, and the adviser's duty is to confirm the client truly understands the structure and that the risk and liquidity profile fits the client's objectives before recommending any of them."},
  {"id":418,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"Insurance-based products are contracts issued by insurance companies, but some of them are also **securities**. That dual nature is the single most tested idea in this subsection. A product becomes a security when the *purchaser* takes on investment risk through a **separate account** whose value rises and falls with the markets. When the *insurer* guarantees the outcome and invests in its own **general account**, the product is pure insurance and is regulated only by the state insurance commissioner.\n\nKeep this master distinction in mind as you read every product below:"},
  {"id":419,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"| Product | Security? | Who bears investment risk | Investment held in |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Fixed annuity | No | Insurance company | General account |\n| Indexed annuity | No (federally exempt) | Shared — insurer guarantees a floor; owner gives up full upside | General account |\n| **Variable annuity** | **Yes** | **Contract owner** | **Separate account** |\n| Term life | No | N/A (no cash value) | General account |\n| Whole life | No | Insurance company | General account |\n| Universal life | No | Insurance company (guaranteed minimum rate) | General account |\n| **Variable life / VUL** | **Yes** | **Policyholder** | **Separate account** |"},
  {"id":420,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"**Exam tip:** To sell any *variable* product (variable annuity, variable life, or variable universal life) an agent needs **both** a securities registration (Series 6 or 7, plus state securities registration) **and** a life insurance license. Fixed and indexed products require only the insurance license.\n\n#### Annuities: Purpose and the Two Phases"},
  {"id":421,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"An **annuity** is a contract between an individual and an insurance company designed to provide income, most often for retirement. Its defining feature is that it can pay income **for the life of the annuitant** — it insures against *outliving your money* (longevity risk). Non-qualified annuities have **no contribution limits** and no required starting age for contributions.\n\nEvery annuity has two phases:"},
  {"id":422,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"- **Accumulation phase** — the period when money is paid in and grows **tax-deferred**. Contributions can be a single lump sum (single-premium) or a series of payments (periodic-premium). No taxes are due while the money grows.\n- **Annuitization phase (payout phase)** — the contract is converted into a stream of income payments. This is a **one-way, irrevocable** decision once elected in a traditional annuity.\n\n**Common trap:** *Annuitizing* is not the same as simply *withdrawing*. You can take withdrawals without annuitizing, but true annuitization converts the accumulated value into a guaranteed income stream and is generally irreversible."},
  {"id":423,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"Common payout options (tested by their trade-off between payment size and survivor protection):\n\n| Payout option | Description | Relative payment |\n|---|---|---|\n| Straight life (life only) | Pays for the annuitant's life; **stops at death**, nothing to heirs | **Largest** payment |\n| Life with period certain | Pays for life, but guarantees a minimum number of years (e.g., 10) to a beneficiary if the annuitant dies early | Smaller |\n| Joint and last survivor | Pays until the **second** of two people dies | Smallest |\n| Unit/cash refund | Guarantees the remaining principal is refunded to a beneficiary | Smaller |"},
  {"id":424,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"**Exam tip:** Straight life pays the most because the insurer keeps the balance if the annuitant dies early; it delivers the **largest lifetime income** but **no protection for heirs**.\n\n#### Fixed Annuities\n\nA **fixed annuity** guarantees a fixed rate of return during accumulation and a fixed dollar payment during payout. Because the insurer promises the outcome, the **insurance company bears the investment risk**, and the money is held in the insurer's **general account**. A fixed annuity is an **insurance product, not a security**, and is regulated by the state insurance department."},
  {"id":425,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"- Guaranteed minimum interest rate during accumulation.\n- Predictable, level payments in retirement.\n- The annuitant's main danger is **purchasing-power (inflation) risk** — fixed dollars buy less over time.\n\n**Common trap:** Because a fixed annuity is not a security, it may be sold by someone holding **only** an insurance license. Don't require a securities registration for it.\n\n#### Variable Annuities"},
  {"id":426,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"A **variable annuity (VA)** lets the owner allocate premiums among **subaccounts** (similar to mutual funds — stock, bond, money-market portfolios) held in the insurer's **separate account**. Values fluctuate with the markets, so the **contract owner bears the investment risk**. There is **no guaranteed return and no guaranteed principal**. A VA is a **security**: the separate account is registered as an investment company under the Investment Company Act of 1940, the contract is registered under the Securities Act of 1933, and sales are supervised by the SEC/FINRA and state securities regulators. (This flows from *SEC v. VALIC*, the case establishing that variable annuities are securities.)"},
  {"id":427,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"**Units — the classic exam distinction:**\n\n- During the **accumulation phase**, premiums buy **accumulation units**. The **number of units you own varies** (you buy more or fewer each period depending on price), and the value per unit fluctuates.\n- At **annuitization**, accumulation units are converted into a **fixed number of annuity units**. From then on the **number of annuity units is fixed**, but the **value of each annuity unit still fluctuates**, which is why the monthly income varies."},
  {"id":428,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"**Assumed Interest Rate (AIR):** The AIR is a benchmark used to calculate variable annuity payments during the payout phase. Each period the **actual** separate-account performance is compared to the AIR:\n\n- Actual return **greater than** AIR → next payment **rises**.\n- Actual return **equal to** AIR → next payment **unchanged**.\n- Actual return **less than** AIR → next payment **falls**."},
  {"id":429,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"**Worked AIR example:** A variable annuity has an AIR of **4%**.\n- If the separate account earns **6%** this period, it beat the AIR, so the next payment is **larger** than the previous one.\n- If it earns exactly **4%**, the payment stays the **same**.\n- If it earns **2%**, the payment **decreases**, even though the account still made money — because 2% is below the 4% AIR.\n\n**Common trap:** The comparison is *actual performance vs. the AIR*, not actual performance vs. the prior period. A positive return can still produce a *smaller* payment if it falls short of the AIR."},
  {"id":430,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"Variable annuities also carry **mortality and expense (M&E) risk charges**: the insurer charges a fee to cover its guarantee to pay for the annuitant's lifetime (mortality guarantee) and to cap administrative expenses (expense guarantee). Many VAs add optional **living benefit** or **death benefit** riders (e.g., a guaranteed minimum death benefit returning at least premiums paid).\n\n#### Indexed Annuities"},
  {"id":431,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"An **indexed annuity** (also called an equity-indexed or fixed-indexed annuity) credits interest based on the performance of a securities index such as the **S&P 500**, subject to limits, while **guaranteeing a minimum return** so the owner cannot lose principal to market declines. Because principal is protected by the insurer, an indexed annuity is treated as an **insurance product, not a security**, under federal law (the SEC's attempt to regulate them via Rule 151A was vacated). Risk is **shared**: the insurer bears downside risk, and the owner gives up part of the upside.\n\nThree features control how much index gain the owner actually receives:"},
  {"id":432,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"- **Participation rate** — the percentage of the index's gain that is credited (e.g., 80%).\n- **Cap rate** — a ceiling on the credited return in a period (e.g., 6%).\n- **Floor / guaranteed minimum** — the least you can be credited, typically **0%** (no loss) or a small positive rate.\n\n**Worked example:** An indexed annuity has an **80% participation rate**, a **6% cap**, and a **0% floor**. The S&P 500 index gains **10%** this year.\n- Apply the participation rate: 10% × 80% = **8%**.\n- Apply the cap: 8% exceeds the 6% cap, so the credited rate is capped at **6%**.\n- Result: **6% is credited** for the year."},
  {"id":433,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"Now suppose the following year the index **falls 12%**. Because of the **0% floor**, the owner is credited **0%** — no gain, but **no loss of principal**.\n\n**Common trap:** Owners of indexed annuities do **not** receive index **dividends**, and their gains are limited by caps and participation rates. In strong bull markets an indexed annuity typically underperforms a direct index investment — that limited upside is the price of downside protection.\n\n#### Taxation of Annuities\n\nThese rules apply to **non-qualified** annuities (bought with after-tax dollars):"},
  {"id":434,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"- **No deduction** for contributions; earnings grow **tax-deferred**.\n- Withdrawn earnings are taxed as **ordinary income** — **never** as capital gains, even in a variable annuity holding stock subaccounts.\n- Non-qualified annuity withdrawals are **LIFO** (last-in, first-out): the **taxable earnings** are deemed withdrawn **first**, then the tax-free return of your cost basis.\n- Withdrawals of the taxable portion before age **59½** incur a **10% federal penalty** (on top of ordinary income tax).\n- **No step-up in basis at death** — heirs owe ordinary income tax on the deferred gain."},
  {"id":435,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"**Exclusion ratio (during annuitization):** Once annuitized, each payment is part tax-free return of principal and part taxable earnings.\n\n> Exclusion Ratio = Investment in the Contract / Expected Return"},
  {"id":436,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"**Worked example:** An investor contributed **$100,000** (the investment in the contract). Based on the payout option and life expectancy, the **expected return** is **$200,000**. The exclusion ratio = 100,000 / 200,000 = **50%**. If the annuitant receives **$10,000** per year, then **$5,000 (50%) is tax-free** return of principal and **$5,000 is taxable** as ordinary income. Once the entire $100,000 basis has been recovered (i.e., if the annuitant lives beyond life expectancy), **all** subsequent payments become **fully taxable**."},
  {"id":437,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"**1035 exchange:** Section 1035 permits a **tax-free exchange** of one annuity for another, or of a **life insurance policy into an annuity**. You **cannot** exchange an annuity **into** a life insurance policy tax-free.\n\n**Exam tip:** Placing an annuity **inside an IRA or other qualified plan is usually a red flag for unsuitability** — you are paying extra annuity fees (like M&E charges) for tax deferral the IRA **already** provides. Expect a question where the \"wrong\" recommendation is a variable annuity in an IRA sold to a young or low-tax client, or one who needs liquidity.\n\n#### Surrender Charges"},
  {"id":438,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"A **surrender charge** is a fee the insurer deducts if the owner withdraws money (beyond a small free-withdrawal amount) during the early years of the contract. Charges typically **decline each year over a surrender period** (often 6–8 years) until they disappear.\n\n**Example schedule:** 7% in year 1, 6% in year 2, 5% in year 3, and so on to 0% after year 7. A $50,000 withdrawal in year 2 (6% charge) would cost **$3,000** in surrender charges.\n\n**Common trap:** Surrender charges make annuities **illiquid** early on. Recommending an annuity to a client with a short time horizon or an anticipated near-term need for the funds is a classic **suitability failure**.\n\n#### Life Insurance: Overview"},
  {"id":439,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"Life insurance pays a **death benefit** to beneficiaries. **Permanent** policies (whole, universal, variable) also build **cash value** that grows tax-deferred and can be borrowed against. Death benefits are generally **income-tax-free** to beneficiaries. Only **variable** life products are securities.\n\n#### Term Life"},
  {"id":440,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"**Term life** is **pure insurance**: a death benefit only, for a specified period (the \"term\"), with **no cash value**. It is the **cheapest** way to buy the largest death benefit and provides only **temporary** protection. Common forms include **level term** (constant premium and death benefit), **annual renewable term** (renews yearly at rising premiums), and **decreasing term** (declining death benefit, often used to cover a mortgage). Term life is **not a security**.\n\n#### Whole Life"},
  {"id":441,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"**Whole life** (also called *ordinary* or *straight life*) is **permanent** insurance with three guarantees: a **fixed, level premium**, a **guaranteed death benefit**, and **guaranteed cash value** that grows on a set schedule. The **insurer bears all investment risk** and holds premiums in its **general account**. Cash value grows tax-deferred. Whole life is **not a security**. Its trade-off is higher, less flexible premiums in exchange for guarantees.\n\n#### Universal Life"},
  {"id":442,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"**Universal life (UL)** is **permanent** insurance built for **flexibility**: the policyholder can adjust the **premium amount and timing** and the **death benefit** (subject to limits and evidence of insurability). Cash value earns a **current interest rate with a guaranteed minimum floor**, so the **insurer still bears the core investment risk**. UL is \"unbundled\" — the cost of insurance and the savings element are shown separately. UL offers two death-benefit designs: **Option A** (level death benefit) and **Option B** (death benefit increases with cash value). Universal life is **not a security**."},
  {"id":443,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"**Common trap:** *Flexible premiums* in UL can backfire — if the owner underfunds the policy and the cash value can't cover the cost of insurance, the policy can **lapse**. Flexibility is not the same as a guarantee.\n\n#### Variable Life (and Variable Universal Life)\n\n**Variable life (VLI)** is **permanent** insurance whose **cash value and (in part) death benefit vary** with the performance of investments the policyholder selects in a **separate account**. Key features:"},
  {"id":444,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"- The **policyholder bears the investment risk**.\n- Premiums are typically **fixed/scheduled**, but the **cash value is not guaranteed**.\n- There is usually a **guaranteed minimum death benefit**, while amounts above it depend on investment performance.\n- Because it uses a separate account, **variable life IS a security** — the agent needs a securities registration plus an insurance license."},
  {"id":445,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"**Variable universal life (VUL)** combines the **separate-account investing of variable life** with the **flexible premiums and adjustable death benefit of universal life**. It offers the most flexibility and the most investment risk to the owner, and it is likewise a **security**. VUL often has **no guaranteed minimum cash value**, and depending on how the death benefit is structured, may offer weaker death-benefit guarantees than traditional variable life.\n\n**Life insurance comparison — memorize this grid:**"},
  {"id":446,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"| Feature | Term | Whole Life | Universal Life | Variable Life | VUL |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cash value | None | Guaranteed | Current rate w/ min. floor | Varies (separate acct.) | Varies (separate acct.) |\n| Premium | Level for term | Fixed & level | Flexible | Usually fixed | Flexible |\n| Death benefit | Level (temporary) | Guaranteed | Adjustable | Min. guaranteed + variable | Adjustable + variable |\n| Who bears investment risk | N/A | Insurer | Insurer (guar. min.) | **Policyholder** | **Policyholder** |\n| Security? | No | No | No | **Yes** | **Yes** |"},
  {"id":447,"part":"Part II","section":"M. Insurance-Based Products","anchor":"m-insurance-based-products","text":"**Exam tip:** The fastest way to answer most insurance questions on the Series 65 is to ask two things: (1) *Is there a separate account / does the customer bear investment risk?* — if yes, it's a **variable product and a security** requiring a securities registration; and (2) *Who is taxed and how?* — annuity and permanent-policy growth is **tax-deferred**, and annuity earnings come out as **ordinary income under LIFO** with a **10% penalty before 59½**."},
  {"id":448,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"Beyond stocks, bonds, packaged products, and derivatives, the Series 65 expects you to understand a small set of \"other\" assets that clients increasingly ask about: physical **commodities**, **precious metals**, and **digital assets** (cryptocurrencies and related tokens). They are grouped together because they share key traits — most are *not* traditional securities, they generally produce *no* cash flow, and their return depends almost entirely on price movement. The exam tests whether you understand their characteristics, how they are regulated, and how an investment adviser applies suitability and fiduciary judgment to them.\n\n#### Commodities"},
  {"id":449,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"A **commodity** is a raw physical good that is interchangeable with any other unit of the same grade — one producer's bushel of wheat or barrel of oil is essentially identical to another's. This interchangeability is called **fungibility**. Commodities fall into a few broad groups:\n\n| Category | Examples |\n|---|---|\n| Agricultural (\"softs\" and grains) | Wheat, corn, soybeans, coffee, sugar, cotton |\n| Livestock | Cattle, hogs |\n| Energy | Crude oil, natural gas, heating oil |\n| Industrial metals | Copper, aluminum, zinc |\n| Precious metals | Gold, silver, platinum, palladium |"},
  {"id":450,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"**How investors gain exposure.** A client rarely buys and stores physical barrels of oil. Instead, commodity exposure typically comes through:\n\n- **Spot market** — buying the physical good for immediate delivery.\n- **Futures and forwards** — contracts to buy or sell a set quantity at a set price on a future date (covered in the derivatives section).\n- **Options on futures.**\n- **Commodity ETFs / exchange-traded products (ETPs)** — funds that hold either the physical commodity (common for metals) or a portfolio of futures contracts.\n- **Commodity pools / managed futures funds** — pooled vehicles run by professional managers.\n\n**Key characteristics to remember:**"},
  {"id":451,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"- **No income.** A commodity pays no dividend or interest. The *only* source of return is price appreciation, and the investor also bears **carrying costs** (storage, insurance, and financing). This is a frequently tested distinction.\n- **Inflation hedge.** Commodity prices tend to rise with inflation, so commodities are often used to protect purchasing power.\n- **Low correlation** with stocks and bonds, which makes them useful for **diversification**.\n- **High volatility**, driven by weather, geopolitics, supply/demand shocks, and currency moves.\n- **Leverage.** Futures require only a small margin deposit relative to contract value, magnifying both gains and losses."},
  {"id":452,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"**Regulation — heavily tested.** Physical commodities and commodity futures are **not securities**. They are regulated by the **Commodity Futures Trading Commission (CFTC)** under the Commodity Exchange Act, with the **National Futures Association (NFA)** acting as the industry self-regulatory organization. Professionals in this space register differently from securities professionals:\n\n- A **Commodity Pool Operator (CPO)** operates a pooled commodity/futures fund.\n- A **Commodity Trading Advisor (CTA)** advises others on futures.\n\nBoth register with the **CFTC/NFA**, not the SEC or the states, when they deal purely in commodities and futures."},
  {"id":453,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"**Common trap:** A candidate sees \"commodity\" and assumes SEC/state securities regulation. The commodity or futures contract itself is *not* a security and is regulated by the CFTC. However, an *investment vehicle* that packages commodities — such as a **commodity ETF or a commodity pool sold as a fund interest** — can itself be a security, and advice about that packaged product can fall under securities law.\n\n**Worked example — leverage in a futures position.** Suppose crude oil futures trade at $80 per barrel and one contract is 1,000 barrels."},
  {"id":454,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"- Contract value = 1,000 barrels × $80 = **$80,000**.\n- Assume the exchange requires initial margin of $5,000 (6.25% of contract value).\n- If oil rises to $85, the gain = 1,000 × $5 = **$5,000**.\n\nReturn on margin = Gain / Margin Deposited = $5,000 / $5,000 = **100%**.\n\nA 6.25% move in the underlying produced a 100% return on the amount posted — and a 6.25% move the other way would wipe out the entire deposit. This is why the exam labels commodity/futures strategies as high-risk and suitable only for investors who understand and can absorb leveraged losses.\n\n#### Precious Metals"},
  {"id":455,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"Precious metals — chiefly **gold, silver, platinum, and palladium** — are a specialized commodity category tested on their own, with gold the classic example.\n\n**Ways to own precious metals:**"},
  {"id":456,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"| Method | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| Physical bullion and coins | Direct ownership; investor bears **storage, insurance, and security** costs and faces dealer markups/spreads. |\n| Precious-metal ETFs | Fund holds physical metal in a vault; investor holds a security representing that metal. Convenient, liquid. |\n| Mining company stocks / mining funds | Equity in producers — these *are* securities and behave partly like stocks (operational risk, leverage to the metal price). |\n| Futures and options | Leveraged, exchange-traded, CFTC-regulated. |\n\n**Key characteristics:**"},
  {"id":457,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"- **Store of value / safe haven.** Investors move into gold during economic stress, geopolitical crisis, or currency debasement.\n- **Inflation hedge**, similar to broader commodities.\n- **No income.** Like other commodities, bullion pays nothing; all return is price change, minus carrying costs.\n- **Inverse tendencies.** Gold often moves inversely to the **U.S. dollar** and to **real (inflation-adjusted) interest rates** — when rates rise, non-yielding gold becomes relatively less attractive.\n- **Volatility** and wide dealer spreads on physical metal.\n\n**Worked example — total return on physical gold.** Use:\n\nTotal Return = (Ending Value − Beginning Value − Holding Costs) / Beginning Value"},
  {"id":458,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"An investor buys gold at $1,800 per ounce, sells at $2,070, and pays $30 per ounce in combined storage and insurance over the holding period.\n\nTotal Return = ($2,070 − $1,800 − $30) / $1,800 = $240 / $1,800 = **13.3%**.\n\nNotice that the carrying cost *reduces* the return — a point the exam uses to contrast metals with income-producing assets.\n\n**Exam tip:** If a question stresses \"protection against inflation and currency risk, but no current income and carrying costs,\" precious metals (and commodities generally) are the intended answer, not bonds or dividend stocks.\n\n#### Digital Assets — Definition and Distinctions"},
  {"id":459,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"A **digital asset** is a representation of value or rights that exists in purely electronic form, typically recorded on a **distributed ledger** (a **blockchain**) and secured by **cryptography**. There is no paper certificate and, for most, no central issuer or intermediary — ownership is verified across a decentralized network rather than by a single institution.\n\nThe NASAA outline asks you to distinguish digital assets **across securities, currencies, and assets**. The crucial idea is that \"digital asset\" is an umbrella term, and *what a particular digital asset legally is* depends on how it functions and how it is offered:"},
  {"id":460,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"| Functional type | Description | Likely classification |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Cryptocurrency** (e.g., Bitcoin) | Designed as a medium of exchange / store of value; decentralized. | Often treated as a **commodity/currency** — the CFTC has called Bitcoin and Ether commodities. |\n| **Security token** | A token representing an investment stake, sold to raise capital with profit expectations (e.g., many ICO tokens). | A **security** if it meets the Howey test. |\n| **Utility token** | Grants access to a product or service on a network. | Depends on the facts; may still be a security if sold as an investment. |\n| **Stablecoin** | Pegged to a fiat currency or asset to reduce volatility. | Regulatory status still evolving; may implicate securities, money-transmission, or banking law. |"},
  {"id":461,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"**The Howey test determines whether a digital asset is a security.** Under *SEC v. W.J. Howey Co.*, an arrangement is an **investment contract** (and therefore a security) when there is:\n\n1. an **investment of money**,\n2. in a **common enterprise**,\n3. with a **reasonable expectation of profits**,\n4. derived **from the efforts of others** (a promoter or third party)."},
  {"id":462,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"If all four prongs are met, the digital asset is a security subject to the Securities Act, the Uniform Securities Act, and state registration and anti-fraud rules — even though it is \"digital.\" A token marketed to investors as a way to profit from a promoter's development efforts (a typical **ICO — initial coin offering**) generally satisfies Howey and is treated as a security. Bitcoin, by contrast, has no central promoter whose efforts drive an investor's profit and is generally *not* treated as a security."},
  {"id":463,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"**Exam tip:** The single most testable digital-asset concept is that **the Howey test — not the label \"coin\" or \"token\" — decides whether it is a security.** Do not assume all crypto is a security, and do not assume none of it is.\n\n#### Characteristics of Digital Assets"},
  {"id":464,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"- **Decentralization.** Many operate peer-to-peer with no central bank or clearing authority; transactions are validated by the network.\n- **Blockchain ledger.** Transactions are recorded on a shared, tamper-resistant, generally public ledger.\n- **Cryptographic security and self-custody.** Ownership is controlled by a **private key**. Whoever holds the key controls the asset — and losing the key means losing the asset permanently.\n- **Divisibility and portability.** Assets can be split into tiny fractions and transferred globally, quickly, at any hour (**24/7/365** trading).\n- **Pseudonymity.** Addresses are not overtly tied to names, though the ledger itself is transparent.\n- **No inherent cash flow.** Like commodities, most digital assets pay no dividend or interest; value rests on supply/demand and adoption expectations.\n- **No standard investor protection.** Digital assets are **not FDIC-insured** (they are not bank deposits) and generally **not SIPC-protected**."},
  {"id":465,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"#### Regulatory Uncertainty\n\nThe Series 65 explicitly wants you to acknowledge **regulatory uncertainty**. There is no single, comprehensive federal framework for digital assets; instead, overlapping regulators each claim a slice:"},
  {"id":466,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"| Regulator | Angle |\n|---|---|\n| **SEC** | Digital assets that are securities (applies Howey; polices ICOs and exchanges). |\n| **CFTC** | Digital assets deemed **commodities** (e.g., Bitcoin, Ether) and crypto derivatives. |\n| **FinCEN** | Anti-money-laundering and money-transmission rules. |\n| **IRS** | Treats virtual currency as **property**, so sales trigger capital-gain/loss reporting — *not* like spending cash. |\n| **State securities regulators (NASAA members)** | Enforce state registration and **anti-fraud** provisions and issue investor alerts. Digital-asset fraud is a recurring NASAA enforcement priority. |"},
  {"id":467,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"Because classification can shift with the facts and the rules are still developing, an adviser cannot assume a stable legal treatment. **NASAA has repeatedly flagged digital assets among its top investor-threat concerns**, emphasizing fraud, unregistered offerings, and unsuitable recommendations.\n\n**Common trap:** Thinking a token is \"unregulated.\" Even where no tailored digital-asset statute applies, the **anti-fraud provisions of state and federal securities law still reach any digital asset that is a security**, and an IA's fiduciary duty always applies to the advice given.\n\n#### Risks of Digital Assets (Including Volatility)"},
  {"id":468,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"The outline calls out **volatility** and **regulatory uncertainty** by name, but the full risk picture is testable:"},
  {"id":469,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"- **Extreme volatility.** Prices can swing double-digit percentages in a single day. On the exam, digital assets are the archetype of a **speculative, high-risk** holding — inappropriate for conservative, income-oriented, or short-horizon investors.\n- **Regulatory uncertainty.** Changing rules or enforcement can suddenly impair value or access.\n- **Custody and security risk.** Exchange hacks, failures, and **lost private keys** can cause total, irreversible loss. There is no bank to reverse a fraudulent or mistaken transfer.\n- **No safety net.** No FDIC or SIPC coverage, and limited recourse if a platform collapses.\n- **Fraud and manipulation.** Pump-and-dump schemes, fake ICOs, and Ponzi structures are common — a core NASAA enforcement theme.\n- **Liquidity risk.** Major coins are liquid, but thinly traded tokens can be difficult to sell at a fair price.\n- **Valuation difficulty.** With no cash flows, there is no cash-flow-based intrinsic value; pricing rests on sentiment and adoption."},
  {"id":470,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"**How this ties to the adviser's duties (the exam's real target).** An investment adviser recommending or holding digital assets must still meet the **suitability** and **fiduciary** standards:\n\n- Recommend digital assets only where consistent with the client's **risk tolerance, time horizon, objectives, and financial capacity** — typically a small, \"loss-you-can-afford\" allocation, if any.\n- Provide **full and fair disclosure** of the volatility, custody, and regulatory risks.\n- Recognize that **holding client crypto keys may constitute custody**, triggering the adviser's custody safeguards."},
  {"id":471,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"**Exam tip:** When a question describes a risk-averse retiree or someone needing stable income and asks whether digital assets are appropriate, the answer is generally **no** — the recommendation would violate suitability and fiduciary duty. Conversely, for a wealthy, risk-tolerant investor, a *small* speculative allocation with clear risk disclosure can be defensible.\n\n#### How \"Other Assets\" Is Tested on the Series 65\n\nFocus your recall on these high-yield points:"},
  {"id":472,"part":"Part II","section":"N. Other Assets","anchor":"n-other-assets","text":"- **Commodities and precious metals pay no income**; their return is price change minus carrying costs, and they serve as **inflation hedges and diversifiers**.\n- **Commodities and futures are regulated by the CFTC/NFA, not the SEC** — the commodity itself is *not* a security, though a commodity ETF or pool can be.\n- **Precious metals** (gold especially) act as a **safe haven** and tend to move **inversely to the dollar and real interest rates**.\n- For **digital assets**, apply the **Howey test** to decide whether the asset is a security; know that Bitcoin and Ether are generally treated as **commodities**, and the IRS treats crypto as **property**.\n- Emphasize **regulatory uncertainty**, **extreme volatility**, the lack of **FDIC/SIPC** protection, and **custody/fraud** risk.\n- Frame every digital-asset recommendation through the adviser's **suitability and fiduciary** obligations — speculative, disclose fully, size appropriately."},
  {"id":473,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"Before you can recommend a single security, you must know *who* the client is. On the Series 65, \"type of client\" is not trivia — the legal form of the account controls three things the exam tests over and over:\n\n- **Taxation** — who pays tax on the account's income and gains (the entity itself, or the people behind it).\n- **Liability** — whether the owners' personal assets are exposed to the entity's debts and lawsuits.\n- **Account authority** — who is legally allowed to give instructions on the account, and what document proves it."},
  {"id":474,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"Keep those three lenses — **tax, liability, authority** — in mind for every entity below. Most \"type of client\" questions are really asking about one of the three."},
  {"id":475,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"A useful starting distinction: a **natural person** is a living human being; a **legal person** (also called an artificial or juridical person) is an entity — a corporation, LLC, partnership, trust, or estate — that the law treats as capable of owning property and entering contracts. Some entities (a C- or S-corporation, an LLC, an irrevocable trust, an estate) are **separate legal persons** from their owners; others (a sole proprietorship, a revocable trust) are legally *the same* as the individual behind them. That separateness is the root of almost every taxation, liability, and authority answer.\n\n#### Individual / Natural Person"},
  {"id":476,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"An **individual account** is owned by one natural person who has full control and is the sole beneficial owner. It is the simplest client type."},
  {"id":477,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"- **Taxation:** All income, dividends, interest, and capital gains are reported on the individual's own Form 1040 and taxed at personal rates.\n- **Liability:** Not applicable in the entity sense — the person simply owns the assets directly.\n- **Authority:** Only the account owner may transact, unless the owner grants **trading authorization**. A **limited power of attorney** lets a third party trade but not withdraw funds; a **full power of attorney** also permits withdrawals. Authority ends at the owner's death, at which point the assets pass to the estate."},
  {"id":478,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"**Exam tip:** Watch for **legal capacity**. To open an account in one's own name, a person must be of legal age (the age of majority, generally 18) and mentally competent. Accounts for minors are handled under **UTMA/UGMA** custodial rules, not as individual accounts.\n\n#### Sole Proprietorship\n\nA **sole proprietorship** is an unincorporated business owned by one individual. The single most important idea to lock in: **a sole proprietorship is not a separate legal entity from its owner.** The business and the person are one and the same in the eyes of the law."},
  {"id":479,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"- **Taxation:** Fully \"pass-through.\" Business income is reported on **Schedule C** and flows onto the owner's personal Form 1040. There is no entity-level tax.\n- **Liability:** **Unlimited personal liability.** Because there is no legal separation, business creditors can reach the owner's personal assets (home, savings, personal investments). This is the proprietorship's biggest weakness.\n- **Authority:** The proprietor has complete authority over the account, exactly like an individual."},
  {"id":480,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"**Common trap:** A question may describe \"a business owner\" and tempt you toward corporate rules. If the business is a sole proprietorship, treat it like the individual — one taxpayer, unlimited liability, one decision-maker. The business assets and personal assets are legally indistinguishable.\n\n#### Business Entities Overview\n\nOnce more than one owner is involved, or the owners want liability protection, a formal entity is used. The exam expects you to distinguish five forms: **general partnership, limited partnership, LLC, C-corporation, and S-corporation.** The table below is the high-yield summary; the sections that follow explain each."},
  {"id":481,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"| Entity | Liability of owners | Taxation | Who has account authority |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| General partnership | Unlimited, joint & several — all partners | Pass-through (Form 1065 → K-1s) | Any general partner (per partnership agreement) |\n| Limited partnership | GP: unlimited; LPs: limited to investment | Pass-through (Form 1065 → K-1s) | The general partner(s) only |\n| LLC | Limited to investment (members) | Flexible — \"check-the-box\" (default pass-through) | Member(s) or manager per operating agreement |\n| C-corporation | Limited to investment (shareholders) | **Double taxation** (Form 1120) | Officers named in a corporate resolution |\n| S-corporation | Limited to investment (shareholders) | Pass-through (Form 1120-S → K-1s) | Officers named in a corporate resolution |"},
  {"id":482,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"#### General Partnership\n\nA **general partnership (GP)** is an association of two or more people carrying on a business for profit. It is easy to form (it can arise without any filing) but offers no liability shield."},
  {"id":483,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"- **Taxation:** Pass-through. The partnership files an **informational return, Form 1065**, and issues each partner a **Schedule K-1** reporting their share of income, deductions, and credits. The partnership itself pays no income tax; partners report their shares on their personal returns.\n- **Liability:** Each general partner has **unlimited personal liability**, and it is **joint and several** — a creditor can pursue any one partner for the *entire* obligation, not just that partner's fractional share. Partners are also generally liable for acts of the other partners taken in the ordinary course of business.\n- **Authority:** Any general partner can typically bind the partnership. To open a securities account, the firm requires a **partnership agreement** (and often a **partnership resolution**) identifying who is authorized to transact."},
  {"id":484,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"**Common trap:** \"Joint and several liability\" means a single partner can be made to pay 100% of a claim even if they own only, say, 25% of the partnership. Don't limit a partner's exposure to their ownership percentage.\n\n#### Limited Partnership\n\nA **limited partnership (LP)** has two classes of owner and must have at least one of each:\n\n- **General partner (GP):** Manages the business and carries **unlimited liability**.\n- **Limited partner (LP):** A passive investor whose liability is **limited to the amount invested**. In exchange for that protection, limited partners generally may **not participate in management**."},
  {"id":485,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"- **Taxation:** Pass-through, with mechanics identical to a general partnership — Form 1065 and K-1s to all partners.\n- **Liability:** As above. The critical rule: a **limited partner who takes an active role in management can lose limited-liability protection** and be treated like a general partner.\n- **Authority:** Only the **general partner** has authority to open and direct the account. A limited partner cannot bind the partnership."},
  {"id":486,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"**Exam tip:** LPs are the classic structure for pooled real-estate and oil-and-gas programs. Remember the trade-off: limited partners get liability protection *because* they give up control. Every LP must have at least one general partner who bears unlimited liability.\n\n#### Limited Liability Company (LLC)\n\nAn **LLC** is a hybrid that combines the **limited liability of a corporation** with the **tax flexibility of a partnership.** Owners are called **members**, and the entity is governed by an **operating agreement.**"},
  {"id":487,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"- **Taxation — the \"check-the-box\" rule:** An LLC can elect how it is taxed.\n  - A **single-member LLC** is by default a \"disregarded entity,\" taxed like a sole proprietorship.\n  - A **multi-member LLC** is by default taxed as a partnership (pass-through).\n  - Any LLC may instead **elect** to be taxed as a **C-corporation or S-corporation.**\n- **Liability:** Members enjoy **limited liability** — personal assets are protected from business debts (barring fraud or personal guarantees).\n- **Authority:** Governed by the operating agreement. An LLC may be **member-managed** (members transact directly) or **manager-managed** (a designated manager transacts). The custodian requires the **operating agreement** to confirm who is authorized."},
  {"id":488,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"**Exam tip:** If a question stresses that an entity wants *both* liability protection *and* pass-through taxation with maximum flexibility, the answer is an **LLC.** Its defining feature is flexibility of tax treatment.\n\n#### C-Corporation\n\nA **C-corporation** is a corporation taxed under Subchapter C — the default corporate form. It is a fully **separate legal person** from its owners (shareholders)."},
  {"id":489,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"- **Taxation — double taxation:** This is the C-corp's signature exam point. Corporate profits are taxed **once at the corporate level** (Form 1120), and then again **at the shareholder level** when after-tax profits are distributed as **dividends.** The same dollars are taxed twice.\n- **Liability:** Shareholders have **limited liability** — the most they can lose is their investment. Personal assets are shielded.\n- **Authority:** A **corporate resolution** (board authorization) must name the officers empowered to open and transact in the account.\n\n**Worked example — the cost of double taxation:**"},
  {"id":490,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"> A C-corporation earns **$1,000,000** in pre-tax profit and distributes all after-tax profit as dividends.\n>\n> Formula: Total tax = Corporate tax + Shareholder tax on the distributed amount\n>\n> - Corporate tax at 21% = 0.21 × $1,000,000 = **$210,000**\n> - After-tax profit available to distribute = $1,000,000 − $210,000 = **$790,000**\n> - Shareholder tax on qualified dividends at 15% = 0.15 × $790,000 = **$118,500**\n> - **Total tax paid = $210,000 + $118,500 = $328,500**\n> - Effective combined rate = $328,500 / $1,000,000 = **32.85%**\n>\n> (The 21% federal corporate rate and 15% qualified-dividend rate are used here as standard illustrative figures; the exam tests the *concept* of two layers of tax, not a memorized combined rate.)"},
  {"id":491,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"**Common trap:** Double taxation applies to **distributed** earnings. Profits the corporation retains and reinvests are taxed at the corporate level but not (yet) at the shareholder level — the second layer hits only when a dividend is paid.\n\n#### S-Corporation\n\nAn **S-corporation** is a corporation that has **elected** Subchapter S status to avoid double taxation while keeping the corporate liability shield. It behaves like a corporation for liability and like a partnership for taxes."},
  {"id":492,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"- **Taxation:** **Pass-through.** The S-corp files **Form 1120-S** (informational) and issues **K-1s**; income is taxed once, on the shareholders' personal returns. This eliminates the corporate-level layer — in the example above, the $210,000 corporate tax would never be assessed.\n- **Liability:** **Limited liability**, same as a C-corporation.\n- **Authority:** Same as any corporation — a **corporate resolution** names authorized officers.\n- **Eligibility restrictions (frequently tested):** To qualify for S status, a corporation must:\n  - Have **no more than 100 shareholders**;\n  - Have **only one class of stock**;\n  - Have shareholders who are generally **individuals** who are **U.S. citizens or resident aliens** (certain estates and specific trusts also qualify); and\n  - **Not** have shareholders that are **corporations, partnerships, or non-resident aliens.**"},
  {"id":493,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"**Exam tip:** The clean way to remember it — an **S-corp = corporate liability protection + partnership-style single taxation.** If a question wants to avoid double taxation *and* keep the corporate shield, the answer is S-corporation (or an LLC electing S status).\n\n**Common trap:** Don't confuse *number of shareholders* with *classes of stock.* An S-corp may have up to **100 shareholders** but only **one class of stock** (differences in voting rights are allowed; differences in distribution or liquidation rights are not).\n\n#### Trusts"},
  {"id":494,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"A **trust** is a legal arrangement in which one party (the **grantor**, also called settlor or trustor) transfers assets to a **trustee**, who manages them for the benefit of one or more **beneficiaries.** The document that creates and governs it is the **trust agreement (trust instrument).**\n\nTwo key classifications the exam tests:\n\n**1. Revocable vs. irrevocable**"},
  {"id":495,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"| Feature | Revocable (living/inter vivos) trust | Irrevocable trust |\n|---|---|---|\n| Can grantor change/cancel? | Yes | No (generally) |\n| Separate taxpayer? | No — grantor is taxed on the income | Yes — the trust is its own taxpayer |\n| Assets in grantor's estate? | Yes | Generally removed from estate |\n| Creditor / liability protection | Little | Stronger |"},
  {"id":496,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"- A **revocable trust** is a \"grantor trust\": the grantor keeps control, so the trust's income is taxed to the **grantor** personally. Its main benefit is **avoiding probate**, not saving tax.\n- An **irrevocable trust** is a separate legal and taxable entity. It files **Form 1041** and can shift assets out of the grantor's taxable estate, offering estate-tax and asset-protection advantages — at the cost of the grantor giving up control.\n\n**2. Living vs. testamentary**\n\n- A **living (inter vivos) trust** is created during the grantor's lifetime.\n- A **testamentary trust** is created by the grantor's **will** and comes into existence only **at death.**\n\nApplying the three lenses:"},
  {"id":497,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"- **Taxation:** Depends on type (see table). Irrevocable and testamentary trusts are separate taxpayers. **Exam-relevant point:** trusts reach the **highest marginal income-tax bracket at a very low income threshold** (the brackets are \"compressed\"), which pushes trustees toward distributing income to beneficiaries or investing for tax efficiency.\n- **Liability:** Trust assets are generally distinct from both the trustee's and the beneficiaries' personal assets.\n- **Authority:** The **trustee** — not the beneficiary — has authority to transact. The custodian requires the **trust agreement** or a **certificate of trust** identifying the trustee and their powers. The trustee is a **fiduciary** and, when investing, is bound by the **prudent investor rule** (the Uniform Prudent Investor Act): invest prudently, diversify, and act in the beneficiaries' best interest, judging the portfolio as a whole."},
  {"id":498,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"**Common trap:** The **beneficiary cannot direct the account** — only the trustee can. And in a **revocable** trust, remember the income is still taxed to the **grantor**, not the trust.\n\n#### Estates\n\nAn **estate** is the legal entity that holds a deceased person's assets from the moment of death until those assets are distributed to heirs. It is a **temporary, separate taxable entity.**"},
  {"id":499,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"- **Who runs it:** The **executor** (named in a valid will) or the **administrator** (appointed by the court when there is no will) — collectively the **personal representative.**\n- **Taxation — two different taxes, don't confuse them:**\n  - **Estate tax:** A transfer tax on the value of the estate itself, owed before assets pass to heirs (only very large estates exceeding the federal exemption are affected).\n  - **Estate income tax:** Income the estate *earns during administration* (interest, dividends, gains) is reported on **Form 1041**, just like a trust.\n- **Liability:** The estate's debts are paid from estate assets; heirs are not personally liable for the decedent's debts beyond what they inherit.\n- **Authority:** Only the personal representative may transact. The custodian requires **letters testamentary** (for an executor) or **letters of administration** (for a court-appointed administrator) as proof of authority."},
  {"id":500,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"**Exam tip:** Distinguish **estate tax** (a tax on the *transfer* of wealth at death) from **income tax on the estate** (a tax on money the estate *earns* while being settled). A question mentioning Form 1041 is pointing at the income-tax side.\n\n#### Foundations and Charities\n\n**Foundations and charities** are typically organized as **tax-exempt organizations under IRC Section 501(c)(3).** They exist for charitable, educational, religious, or scientific purposes rather than for private profit."},
  {"id":501,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"- **Two broad categories:**\n  - **Public charities** receive broad public support (churches, hospitals, universities, community organizations).\n  - **Private foundations** are usually funded by a single source — an individual, family, or corporation — and often make grants to other charities (e.g., a family foundation with an endowment).\n- **Taxation:** Generally **tax-exempt** on income related to their charitable mission, and donors can take a tax deduction for contributions. **Important exception:** income from a regularly carried-on trade or business unrelated to the charitable purpose is **unrelated business taxable income (UBTI)** and *is* taxable. Private foundations are also subject"},
  {"id":502,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"to certain excise taxes and minimum annual distribution requirements.\n- **Liability:** As formal entities (usually nonprofit corporations or trusts), they provide limited liability to their trustees/directors acting in good faith.\n- **Authority:** A **board of directors/trustees** or a designated **investment committee** holds authority. The custodian requires the organizing documents and a **resolution** naming authorized signers. Investment decisions for endowment funds are governed by **UPMIFA** (the Uniform Prudent Management of Institutional Funds Act), which — like the prudent investor rule — requires prudent, diversified management that balances current spending needs against preserving the fund for the future."},
  {"id":503,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"**Exam tip:** Tax-exempt status is not unlimited. If a question describes a charity running a business activity unrelated to its mission, that income is **UBTI** and taxable. Also remember these clients emphasize **capital preservation, income, and a long or perpetual time horizon** when suitability is tested.\n\n**Putting it together — the three lenses recap:**"},
  {"id":504,"part":"Part III","section":"A. Type of Client","anchor":"a-type-of-client","text":"| Client type | Liability | Taxation | Authority document |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Individual | N/A (owns directly) | Personal (1040) | Owner; POA if delegated |\n| Sole proprietorship | Unlimited | Pass-through (Schedule C) | Owner |\n| General partnership | Unlimited, joint & several | Pass-through (1065/K-1) | Partnership agreement |\n| Limited partnership | GP unlimited; LP limited | Pass-through (1065/K-1) | General partner |\n| LLC | Limited | Flexible (default pass-through) | Operating agreement |\n| C-corporation | Limited | Double taxation (1120) | Corporate resolution |\n| S-corporation | Limited | Pass-through (1120-S/K-1) | Corporate resolution |\n| Revocable trust | Weak | Grantor taxed | Trust agreement (trustee) |\n| Irrevocable trust | Stronger | Separate taxpayer (1041) | Trust agreement (trustee) |\n| Estate | Estate assets only | Estate income tax (1041) | Letters testamentary/administration |\n| Foundation/charity | Limited | Tax-exempt (UBTI taxable) | Board/committee resolution |"},
  {"id":505,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"Before an investment adviser representative (IAR) can recommend a single security or strategy, the adviser's fiduciary duty and professional standards require that the recommendation be **suitable** for — and in the **best interest** of — the specific client. To meet that standard you must first *know the client*. The organized collection of everything relevant about a client is called the **client profile** (sometimes \"customer profile\" or \"investment profile\"). It is the foundation of the suitability analysis: no valid recommendation exists without it."},
  {"id":506,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"A useful way to remember the required elements is that the profile combines **financial** facts (goals, cash flow, net worth, tax status, time horizon) with **nonfinancial** facts (risk attitude, values, experience, life stage, behavioral tendencies). Regulators expect the adviser to gather this information *before* advising, to keep it current, and to document it.\n\n**Exam tip:** On the Series 65, the phrase \"before making any recommendation, the IAR must…\" almost always resolves to \"**determine suitability / gather the client's financial situation, objectives, and risk tolerance**.\" Suitability is not a one-time event — it must be updated when the client's circumstances change."},
  {"id":507,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"**Common trap:** Suitability is not judged only by the client's *stated desire*. A client can *want* an aggressive growth fund, but if their situation, time horizon, or risk capacity does not support it, the recommendation is unsuitable. Wanting a product is not the same as it being appropriate.\n\n#### 1. Gathering Client Data (Identification, Questionnaires, Interviews)\n\nThe profile is built through a **data-gathering process**. The three tested methods are:"},
  {"id":508,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"| Method | What it is | Strengths / limits |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Identification** | Verifying who the client legally is — legal name, date of birth, physical residential or business address, and Social Security number or Tax ID | Required to open the account; anti-money-laundering (AML) compliance, not just suitability |\n| **Questionnaire / fact-finder** | A structured written form the client completes covering assets, liabilities, income, objectives, risk tolerance, and time horizon | Efficient, consistent, creates a documented record; but relies on the client's self-reporting and can miss nuance |\n| **Interview** | A face-to-face or verbal conversation with the client | Captures nuance, emotion, values, and follow-up detail a form cannot; but is less standardized and harder to document |"},
  {"id":509,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"Best practice combines methods: the written fact-finder captures the hard data, and the interview probes attitudes, values, and inconsistencies. This is often summarized as **\"Know Your Customer\" (KYC)**.\n\n**Identification** ties into two overlapping obligations you should recognize:\n- A **Customer Identification Program (CIP)** under the USA PATRIOT Act / AML rules — the firm must verify the client's identity (name, DOB, physical address, and an identification number such as an SSN or TIN) and check the client against government lists.\n- **Suitability/KYC**, which goes beyond identity to the client's financial and personal circumstances."},
  {"id":510,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"**Exam tip:** If a client *refuses* to provide financial information, the adviser may not simply guess. The recommendation must rest on the information reasonably available; if essential suitability information (objectives, financial situation, risk tolerance) is missing, the prudent answer is generally to **decline to make a specific recommendation** or limit advice to what can be justified.\n\n**Common trap:** Collecting the information once at account opening is not enough. Profiles must be **periodically updated** — especially after major life events — because suitability is measured against the client's *current* situation.\n\n#### 2. Financial Goals and Objectives"},
  {"id":511,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"**Goals** are what the client wants their money to accomplish; **objectives** translate those goals into investment terms. Common objectives, in the language the exam uses:"},
  {"id":512,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"- **Preservation of capital / safety** — protect principal; accept low return (money market, T-bills, CDs).\n- **Income** — generate current cash flow (bonds, dividend stocks, annuities).\n- **Growth (capital appreciation)** — increase asset value over time (equities); often paired as \"growth and income.\"\n- **Speculation / aggressive growth** — pursue high return, accept high risk of loss (options, small-cap/emerging growth).\n- **Liquidity** — ability to convert to cash quickly without loss of principal (note: this is a constraint/need, not a return objective).\n- **Tax minimization / tax advantages** — reduce the tax drag (municipal bonds, tax-deferred accounts)."},
  {"id":513,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"Goals should be **specific and prioritized**, because they often conflict. The classic point is that objectives cannot all be maximized at once — for example, an investment cannot be simultaneously highest **safety**, highest **return**, and highest **liquidity**. Something must be traded off.\n\n**Exam tip:** Watch for the **conflicting-objectives** question — e.g., a client who wants \"maximum growth **and** complete safety of principal.\" That combination is internally inconsistent; the correct answer identifies the conflict or picks the objective the client's situation supports."},
  {"id":514,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"**Common trap:** \"Liquidity\" is not a return goal. An investment being liquid (easily sold) is different from it being safe or high-yielding. Real estate is an illiquid asset even when it grows in value.\n\n#### 3. Time Horizon\n\n**Time horizon** is the length of time until the client needs the money for its intended goal. It is one of the most important inputs because it drives how much risk the portfolio can prudently take: a longer horizon allows the portfolio to ride out short-term volatility, so more equity exposure is appropriate; a short horizon calls for stable, liquid, lower-volatility holdings."},
  {"id":515,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"| Horizon | Typical range | Appropriate emphasis |\n|---|---|---|\n| Short-term | 0–3 years | Preservation, liquidity — money markets, short CDs, T-bills |\n| Intermediate | 3–10 years | Balanced — mix of bonds and equities |\n| Long-term | 10+ years | Growth — heavier equity allocation, can absorb volatility |\n\nTime horizon also **shortens as a goal approaches** — a retirement goal that was 25 years away at age 40 is a short horizon by age 64. This is the rationale behind target-date (\"glide path\") funds, which grow more conservative as the target year nears."},
  {"id":516,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"**Exam tip:** Longer time horizon → **greater capacity for risk/volatility**. A young investor saving for retirement can hold volatile growth assets *because* time smooths out short-term losses. Conversely, money needed in 18 months for a house down payment should **not** be in equities.\n\n**Common trap:** Age alone does not dictate the horizon. A 75-year-old investing for grandchildren or a bequest may have a *long* horizon; a 30-year-old needing tuition next year has a *short* one. Match horizon to the **goal**, not just the client's age.\n\n#### 4. Current and Future Financial Situation"},
  {"id":517,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"The adviser must quantify where the client stands financially today and where they are heading. Two core financial statements are tested:\n\n**a) Balance sheet (net worth statement).**\nThis is a snapshot of what the client owns versus what they owe at a point in time.\n\nFormula (plain text):\n\nNet Worth = Total Assets − Total Liabilities\n\nWorked example:"},
  {"id":518,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"| Assets |  | Liabilities |  |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Home (market value) | $400,000 | Mortgage | $280,000 |\n| Retirement accounts | $250,000 | Auto loan | $12,000 |\n| Brokerage account | $100,000 | Credit card balances | $8,000 |\n| Cash / savings | $30,000 |  |  |\n| Automobile | $20,000 |  |  |\n| **Total assets** | **$800,000** | **Total liabilities** | **$300,000** |\n\nNet Worth = $800,000 − $300,000 = **$500,000**"},
  {"id":519,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"Related liquidity concept — not every asset can be turned into cash quickly. The home ($400,000) and automobile ($20,000) are **illiquid**. The client's liquid holdings total $250,000 retirement + $100,000 brokerage + $30,000 cash = **$380,000**, but retirement accounts generally carry a 10% early-withdrawal penalty before age 59½, so truly accessible emergency reserves are closer to the **$130,000** in the brokerage account plus cash.\n\n**b) Cash flow statement (income vs. expenses).**\nThis measures the client's ability to *save and invest* out of ongoing income.\n\nFormula (plain text):\n\nNet Cash Flow = Total Income − Total Expenses\n\nWorked example:"},
  {"id":520,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"- Monthly gross income: $9,000\n- Monthly expenses (housing, debt service, living costs, taxes): $7,200\n- Net monthly cash flow = $9,000 − $7,200 = **$1,800 surplus**\n- Annualized investable surplus = $1,800 × 12 = **$21,600 per year**\n\nA **positive** (surplus) cash flow means the client has **discretionary income** to invest or build reserves; a **negative** (deficit) cash flow signals that new investment is inappropriate until spending or debt is addressed. A high-net-worth client with poor cash flow may still be constrained."},
  {"id":521,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"**Emergency fund / liquidity need.** A standard planning benchmark is to hold **3 to 6 months of living expenses** in liquid, safe reserves before committing to longer-term or volatile investments.\n\n**c) Existing investments.**\nThe adviser must review what the client already holds to assess **diversification, concentration, and overlap**. A recommendation is judged in the context of the *whole* portfolio, not in isolation. A classic red flag is **concentration** — e.g., a large position in the client's employer stock — which creates unsystematic (diversifiable) risk that a broader allocation could reduce.\n\n**d) Tax situation.**\nTwo rates are commonly distinguished:"},
  {"id":522,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"- **Marginal tax rate** — the rate on the *next* (highest) dollar of income; used to evaluate the tax benefit of an additional investment.\n- **Effective (average) tax rate** — total tax paid divided by total income.\n\nFor evaluating a *new* investment's tax impact, use the **marginal** rate.\n\nTwo tested formulas:\n\nAfter-Tax Yield = Taxable Yield × (1 − Marginal Tax Rate)\n\nTax-Equivalent Yield = Tax-Free (Municipal) Yield / (1 − Marginal Tax Rate)\n\nWorked example — should a client in the **32%** bracket choose a **3.5%** municipal bond or a **5.0%** corporate bond?"},
  {"id":523,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"- After-tax yield of the corporate = 5.0% × (1 − 0.32) = 5.0% × 0.68 = **3.40%**\n- Tax-equivalent yield of the muni = 3.5% / (1 − 0.32) = 3.5% / 0.68 = **5.15%**\n\nThe municipal's after-tax return (3.5%, since its interest is federally tax-free) beats the corporate's after-tax return (3.40%), and its tax-equivalent yield (5.15%) exceeds the corporate's 5.0%. For a **high-bracket** client, the muni is superior. For a low-bracket client the taxable corporate would usually win — the break-even here is a 30% bracket — so **the client's tax bracket determines whether tax-exempt bonds make sense.**"},
  {"id":524,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"**Exam tip:** Municipal bonds and other tax-favored products are most valuable to clients in **high** marginal brackets. Never put a tax-free muni inside a tax-deferred account like an IRA — you would waste the tax exemption (and, in a traditional account, convert tax-free interest into ordinary income at withdrawal).\n\n**e) Social Security and pensions (future / retirement income).**\nThe profile must account for **guaranteed or expected future income streams**, because they reduce how much the client's own portfolio must generate and may increase the client's *capacity* to take investment risk."},
  {"id":525,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"| Feature | Defined benefit (pension) | Defined contribution (401(k)/403(b)) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Who bears investment risk | The employer | The employee |\n| Payout | Guaranteed formula-based lifetime income | Depends on contributions and market performance |\n| Predictability for planning | High (a reliable income floor) | Variable |"},
  {"id":526,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"**Social Security** provides an inflation-adjusted (COLA), government-backed income floor; a traditional **defined-benefit pension** provides another. A client with a solid pension plus Social Security has a dependable income \"floor\" and may be able to take **more** risk with the discretionary portfolio, since day-to-day living expenses are already covered. A client relying entirely on a **defined-contribution** plan bears the market risk themselves and typically needs a more conservative, income-aware approach as retirement nears."},
  {"id":527,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"**Common trap:** More total wealth does not automatically mean more investment risk is appropriate. Capacity depends on the *reliability* of income sources and the *stability* of cash flow, not just the size of the balance sheet.\n\n#### 5. Risk Tolerance\n\n**Risk tolerance** is how much loss or volatility the client can and will accept. The exam splits it into two distinct components that must **both** be satisfied:"},
  {"id":528,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"| Component | Also called | Nature | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Ability to take risk** | Risk capacity | Objective / financial | Time horizon, net worth, cash flow, income stability, liquidity needs |\n| **Willingness to take risk** | Risk attitude | Subjective / emotional | The client's psychological comfort with loss |\n\nThe **binding constraint is the lower of the two.** If a client has the financial *ability* to take risk but is emotionally *unwilling* (they will panic-sell in a downturn), the portfolio should respect the lower willingness. Conversely, a client eager to gamble but with no financial cushion lacks the *ability*, and the adviser should not accommodate the appetite."},
  {"id":529,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"**Exam tip:** When ability and willingness **conflict**, the generally accepted approach is to educate the client and then invest at the **more conservative** level — usually the lower of the two — and document the discussion. Never solve a conflict by ignoring the client's emotional limits.\n\n**Common trap:** Risk tolerance is not the same as time horizon or net worth, though they influence it. A wealthy client can still have low willingness; a young client can still be risk-averse. Test each element separately.\n\n#### 6. Nonfinancial Considerations\n\nNumbers alone do not define a client. The exam explicitly tests several **nonfinancial factors** that shape a suitable recommendation."},
  {"id":530,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"**a) Values-based investing — ESG and religious values.**\nSome clients want their portfolio to reflect their beliefs. This is generally captured under **socially responsible / ESG (Environmental, Social, Governance) investing** and **faith-based (religious) investing**. Such clients may **screen out** industries they object to (e.g., tobacco, firearms, alcohol, gambling, fossil fuels) or **screen in** companies meeting certain standards. The adviser must honor these constraints even when a screened-out security would otherwise be suitable. These preferences act as **investment constraints**, similar to a legal or liquidity constraint."},
  {"id":531,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"**b) Investment experience and knowledge.**\nA client's familiarity with markets and complex products affects what is appropriate. A sophisticated client may understand options and margin; an inexperienced client needs simpler products and more explanation. Experience influences both the products recommended and the adviser's disclosure obligations.\n\n**c) Life events.**\nMarriage, divorce, birth or adoption of a child, a new job or job loss, a large inheritance, disability, or the death of a spouse each materially change goals, cash flow, risk capacity, and tax status. Life events are the primary trigger to **update the profile** and re-test suitability."},
  {"id":532,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"**d) Life stage (life-cycle investing).**\nFinancial needs evolve predictably across the life cycle:\n\n| Stage | Emphasis |\n|---|---|\n| Accumulation (younger, working) | Growth; long horizon; higher equity |\n| Consolidation (mid-career, peak earning) | Growth + income; moderate risk |\n| Spending / gifting (retirement) | Income and preservation; lower risk; liquidity |\n\nA general (not rigid) guideline is that **equity exposure declines as the client ages** and the horizon shortens, because there is less time to recover from losses."},
  {"id":533,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"**e) Behavioral finance biases.**\nBehavioral finance recognizes that clients do not act as perfectly rational investors. The exam distinguishes **two families** of bias, and the distinction drives the \"correct\" adviser response:\n\n- **Cognitive biases** — errors of *faulty reasoning or information processing*. Because they are informational errors, they can usually be **corrected through education and better data.**\n- **Emotional biases** — reactions rooted in *feeling and impulse*. They are harder to fix with facts, so the adviser more often **adapts or moderates the portfolio** to accommodate them."},
  {"id":534,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"| Bias | Family | What it looks like |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Anchoring** | Cognitive | Fixating on a reference price (e.g., \"I'll sell when it gets back to what I paid\") |\n| **Confirmation bias** | Cognitive | Seeking only information that supports an existing view |\n| **Availability bias** | Cognitive | Overweighting easily recalled or recent, vivid events |\n| **Recency bias** | Cognitive | Assuming recent performance will continue |\n| **Framing** | Cognitive | Deciding differently based on how a choice is worded (gain vs. loss) |\n| **Mental accounting** | Cognitive | Treating money differently by \"bucket\" (spending a bonus recklessly while hoarding salary) |\n| **Overconfidence** | Emotional |"},
  {"id":535,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"Overestimating one's own knowledge/skill; excessive trading |\n| **Loss aversion** | Emotional | Feeling losses far more than equivalent gains; refusing to sell losers |\n| **Regret aversion** | Emotional | Avoiding action for fear of a later-regretted decision; inertia |\n| **Status quo bias** | Emotional | Preferring to leave the portfolio unchanged |\n| **Endowment effect** | Emotional | Overvaluing something simply because you own it (e.g., inherited stock) |\n| **Herd mentality (herding)** | Emotional | Following the crowd into or out of the market |"},
  {"id":536,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"**Exam tip:** Memorize the standard remedies. For a **cognitive** bias, the best answer is usually to **educate/inform** the client. For an **emotional** bias, the best answer is usually to **accommodate/moderate** within a suitable portfolio rather than fight the emotion head-on.\n\n**Common trap:** Do not confuse **loss aversion** (hating losses more than valuing equal gains, leading clients to *hold* losers too long) with simple risk aversion. And distinguish the **disposition effect** — selling winners too early while holding losers too long — which is a downstream consequence of loss aversion and mental accounting."},
  {"id":537,"part":"Part III","section":"B. Client Profile","anchor":"b-client-profile","text":"Together, these financial and nonfinancial elements form the complete client profile. Every recommendation you make on the exam must be defensible against this profile: right objective, right time horizon, right risk level, right tax treatment, and consistent with the client's values, experience, and behavioral tendencies."},
  {"id":538,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"Capital market theory is the body of academic finance that answers three linked questions: *What return should I expect for taking on risk? How should I combine securities to get the best trade-off between risk and return? And how efficiently do prices already reflect information?* On the Series 65 these ideas are tested conceptually and with a handful of very predictable calculations. You will not derive anything, but you will plug numbers into the CAPM formula, identify what the efficient frontier represents, and match each form of the Efficient Market Hypothesis to the type of analysis it renders useless. Master those three tasks and this subsection is easy points."},
  {"id":539,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"First fix one distinction, because everything below depends on it:\n\n- **Systematic risk** (also called *market risk* or *non-diversifiable risk*) affects the entire market — interest rates, recession, inflation, war. You cannot diversify it away. It is measured by **beta**.\n- **Unsystematic risk** (also called *specific*, *business*, *diversifiable*, or *non-systematic risk*) is unique to one company or industry — a lawsuit, a product recall, a bad CEO. You *can* diversify it away by holding many securities.\n\nCapital market theory rewards investors only for bearing *systematic* risk, because unsystematic risk is something the investor could have removed for free through diversification."},
  {"id":540,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"#### The Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM)\n\nThe **Capital Asset Pricing Model** answers the question: given how much systematic risk a security carries, what return should an investor reasonably expect? CAPM says the expected return equals the risk-free return plus an extra reward for the systematic risk taken on.\n\nThe formula, in plain text, is:\n\n**Expected Return = Risk-Free Rate + Beta × (Market Return − Risk-Free Rate)**\n\nOr in the shorthand you may see on the exam:\n\n**E(R) = Rf + β × (Rm − Rf)**"},
  {"id":541,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"Where:\n- **Rf** = the risk-free rate, almost always represented by short-term U.S. Treasury bills.\n- **Rm** = the expected return of the overall market (e.g., the S&P 500).\n- **β (beta)** = the security's sensitivity to market movements — its measure of systematic risk.\n- **(Rm − Rf)** = the **market risk premium**, the extra return investors demand for holding the market instead of a risk-free T-bill.\n- **β × (Rm − Rf)** = the security's own risk premium.\n\n**Understanding beta.** Beta is the engine of CAPM. It measures how much a security moves relative to the market:"},
  {"id":542,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"| Beta | Meaning |\n|------|---------|\n| β = 1.0 | Moves in line with the market (same volatility) |\n| β > 1.0 | More volatile than the market (aggressive; e.g., a tech stock at 1.5 tends to move 15% when the market moves 10%) |\n| β < 1.0 | Less volatile than the market (defensive; e.g., a utility at 0.6) |\n| β = 0 | No systematic risk (a risk-free asset has a beta of 0) |\n| β < 0 | Moves opposite to the market (rare; e.g., certain hedging instruments) |\n\n**Worked example.** A stock has a beta of 1.5. Treasury bills yield 3%, and the overall market is expected to return 9%. What is the stock's expected return under CAPM?"},
  {"id":543,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"1. Market risk premium = Rm − Rf = 9% − 3% = **6%**\n2. Stock's risk premium = β × market risk premium = 1.5 × 6% = **9%**\n3. Expected Return = Rf + stock's risk premium = 3% + 9% = **12%**\n\nSo an investor should expect a 12% return to be fairly compensated for this stock's systematic risk. If the analyst believes the stock will actually return *more* than 12%, it is attractive (undervalued); if less, it is unattractive (overvalued).\n\n**A second example to cement the mechanics.** Beta = 0.8, Rf = 2%, Rm = 7%: E(R) = 2% + 0.8 × (7% − 2%) = 2% + 0.8 × 5% = 2% + 4% = **6%**."},
  {"id":544,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"**Alpha — the natural follow-up concept.** CAPM tells you the return a security *should* earn. **Alpha** measures how much a security's *actual* (or forecast) return exceeds or falls short of that CAPM benchmark.\n\n**Alpha = Actual Return − CAPM Expected Return**\n\nIf our first stock actually returned 14% against a CAPM expected return of 12%, its alpha is +2% — it outperformed on a risk-adjusted basis, a sign of manager skill or an undervalued security. A negative alpha means underperformance for the risk taken."},
  {"id":545,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"**Exam tip:** The most common CAPM question hands you Rf, Rm, and beta and asks for the expected return. Work it in the order above — find the market risk premium first, multiply by beta, then add the risk-free rate. A frequent wrong-answer trap multiplies beta by the *full market return* (1.5 × 9%) instead of by the *market risk premium* (1.5 × 6%). Beta scales only the **premium**, never the whole market return.\n\n**Common trap:** CAPM uses **beta** (systematic risk), *not* standard deviation (total risk). Any answer implying CAPM compensates investors for total risk or for company-specific risk is wrong. CAPM rewards only non-diversifiable, systematic risk."},
  {"id":546,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"**The Security Market Line (SML).** Graphing the CAPM expected return (vertical axis) against beta (horizontal axis) produces a straight line called the **Security Market Line**. Every fairly priced security plots *on* the SML. A security plotting *above* the line offers more return than its beta warrants (undervalued, positive alpha); one *below* it is overvalued (negative alpha). Do not confuse the SML with the **Capital Market Line (CML)**, discussed below — the SML uses **beta** on its horizontal axis, while the CML uses **standard deviation**.\n\n#### Modern Portfolio Theory (MPT)"},
  {"id":547,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"**Modern Portfolio Theory**, developed by Nobel laureate **Harry Markowitz**, made a revolutionary claim: a security should be evaluated not in isolation but by what it contributes to the risk and return of the *entire portfolio*. A volatile, risky stock can actually *lower* overall portfolio risk if it zigs when the rest of the portfolio zags. MPT is a theory about building portfolios, and its two big deliverables are **the role of correlation in diversification** and **the efficient frontier**."},
  {"id":548,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"**Risk aversion is the starting assumption.** MPT assumes investors are rational and risk-averse: for any given expected return they prefer the least risk, and for any given level of risk they prefer the highest expected return. Here \"risk\" is measured by **standard deviation**, the dispersion of a portfolio's returns around its average — a measure of *total* risk (systematic plus unsystematic).\n\n**Correlation is the key to diversification.** Combining securities reduces risk because securities do not move in perfect lockstep. This relationship is captured by the **correlation coefficient**, which always ranges from **−1 to +1**:"},
  {"id":549,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"| Correlation | Meaning | Diversification benefit |\n|-------------|---------|-------------------------|\n| +1.0 | Perfectly positive — securities move exactly together | **None** — no risk reduction |\n| Between 0 and +1 | Move together imperfectly (most real-world pairs) | Some risk reduction |\n| 0 | Uncorrelated — no relationship in movements | Meaningful risk reduction |\n| Between −1 and 0 | Tend to move opposite imperfectly | Strong risk reduction |\n| −1.0 | Perfectly negative — move exactly opposite | **Maximum** — risk can theoretically be eliminated |"},
  {"id":550,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"The lesson: **the lower (more negative) the correlation between holdings, the greater the diversification benefit.** Adding a security highly positively correlated with what you already own does almost nothing to reduce risk. This is why advisers combine asset classes that behave differently (e.g., stocks and bonds) rather than ten stocks in the same industry."},
  {"id":551,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"**Diversification eliminates only unsystematic risk.** As you add more, less-correlated securities, the portfolio's unsystematic risk shrinks toward zero. But no amount of diversification removes systematic (market) risk — that floor remains. This ties back to CAPM: because diversification can wipe out unsystematic risk for free, the market pays you (via beta) only for the systematic risk you cannot escape."},
  {"id":552,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"**The efficient frontier.** Plot every possible portfolio on a graph with **risk (standard deviation) on the horizontal axis** and **expected return on the vertical axis.** The cloud of portfolios has an upper-left boundary. The **efficient frontier** is that curved boundary: the set of portfolios offering the **highest expected return for each level of risk** (equivalently, the lowest risk for each level of return)."},
  {"id":553,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"- A portfolio **on** the efficient frontier is **efficient** — you cannot get more return without accepting more risk.\n- A portfolio **below/inside** the frontier is **inefficient** — another portfolio exists with the same risk but higher return, or the same return with lower risk. A rational investor would never hold it.\n- Nothing can exist **above** the frontier; it represents the best achievable trade-offs.\n\nWhich point *on* the frontier an investor chooses depends on personal risk tolerance: a conservative investor picks a spot toward the lower-left (less risk, less return), an aggressive investor a spot toward the upper-right."},
  {"id":554,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"**The Capital Market Line (CML).** As an extension of MPT, introducing a risk-free asset turns the optimal set of portfolios into a straight line running from the risk-free rate up through the \"market portfolio\" on the efficient frontier. This line is the **Capital Market Line**, and it plots expected return against **standard deviation (total risk)**. Remember the pairing: **CML uses standard deviation; SML uses beta.**"},
  {"id":555,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"**Exam tip:** Know the one-sentence definition cold — the efficient frontier is \"the set of portfolios offering the highest expected return for a given level of risk (standard deviation).\" Questions often describe a portfolio with the same return as another but higher risk and ask you to identify it as *inefficient* / *suboptimal*.\n\n**Common trap:** Wrong answers reverse the correlation extremes. Maximum diversification benefit comes from **−1** (negative correlation), *not* from +1; a correlation of +1 gives **zero** benefit. And diversification reduces **unsystematic** risk only — an answer claiming it eliminates market/systematic risk is wrong.\n\n#### The Efficient Market Hypothesis (EMH)"},
  {"id":556,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"The **Efficient Market Hypothesis**, associated with economist **Eugene Fama**, argues that security prices already reflect available information, adjusting rapidly and accurately as new information arrives. The practical punchline: if markets are efficient, it is very difficult (or impossible) to consistently \"beat the market\" through analysis or stock-picking — the intellectual case for **passive, low-cost index investing.** EMH comes in three forms, distinguished by *how much* information is assumed to be baked into prices. The higher the form, the more information is reflected, and the more types of analysis become useless."},
  {"id":557,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"Keep the two forms of analysis straight:\n- **Technical analysis** studies past price and volume data, charts, and trends to predict future prices.\n- **Fundamental analysis** studies a company's financials, management, industry, and the economy to estimate intrinsic value from *public* information."},
  {"id":558,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"| Form | Information already in the price | Which analysis becomes useless | What (if anything) could still give an edge |\n|------|-------------------------------|-------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|\n| **Weak** | All *past price and trading* data | **Technical analysis** | Fundamental analysis and inside information |\n| **Semi-strong** | All *publicly available* information (past prices + financials, news, filings) | **Technical *and* fundamental analysis** | Only material non-public (inside) information |\n| **Strong** | *All* information — public *and* private (including insider) | **Technical, fundamental, *and* inside information** | Nothing — no one can consistently beat the market |"},
  {"id":559,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"Walking up the ladder:\n\n- **Weak form.** Prices already reflect all historical price and volume data, so studying past charts and trends (technical analysis) cannot produce excess returns — past prices tell you nothing about future prices. However, digging into fundamentals or possessing inside information could still, in theory, beat the market."},
  {"id":560,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"- **Semi-strong form.** Prices reflect all *publicly available* information — not just past prices but earnings reports, news, analyst research, and regulatory filings — and adjust almost instantly when new public information is released. Therefore **both technical and fundamental analysis are useless**, because by the time you read the 10-K, the price already reflects it. Only someone acting on **material non-public (inside) information** could gain an edge. The semi-strong form is often considered the most widely accepted version."},
  {"id":561,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"- **Strong form.** Prices reflect *absolutely all* information, public and private — even corporate insiders trading on secret information cannot consistently earn excess returns, because prices already embed that information too. Under the strong form, no analysis of any kind beats the market. (This form is the hardest to defend in reality, since insider trading demonstrably *can* be profitable — which is precisely why it is illegal.)"},
  {"id":562,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"**How to reason about EMH questions.** Read the form named in the question, then ask \"what does this form say I *cannot* profit from?\" Each higher form adds another tool to the useless pile: weak kills technical, semi-strong adds fundamental, strong adds inside information.\n\n**Exam tip:** The classic question is a matching exercise: \"Under which form of the EMH is *fundamental analysis* unable to produce excess returns?\" Answer: **semi-strong (and strong)** — those forms assume all public information is already in the price. If the question asks which form makes *technical* analysis useless, the answer is **all three**, since even the weak form reflects past prices."},
  {"id":563,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"**Common trap:** Do not mix up which analysis each form defeats. A common wrong answer pairs the *weak* form with the defeat of *fundamental* analysis. The weak form defeats **technical** analysis only; you need the **semi-strong** form to defeat **fundamental** analysis. And only the **strong** form claims that even **inside information** provides no advantage."},
  {"id":564,"part":"Part III","section":"C. Capital Market Theory","anchor":"c-capital-market-theory","text":"**Tying it together.** These three theories form one coherent worldview the Series 65 expects you to recognize: MPT says diversify so you bear only systematic risk; CAPM prices that systematic risk through beta to tell you the return you deserve; and EMH argues that because prices already reflect information, the low-cost, well-diversified, passively managed portfolio is a rational default. An adviser who accepts EMH leans toward **index funds and strategic asset allocation**; one who rejects it believes skilled active management can generate positive **alpha**."},
  {"id":565,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"Once you understand a client's goals, risk tolerance, and time horizon, you have to actually build and run a portfolio. NASAA tests this on three levels, and it is worth keeping the vocabulary straight from the start:\n\n- **Strategies** answer *how the overall mix is set and adjusted* (strategic vs. tactical asset allocation).\n- **Styles** answer *what kind of manager or securities you use* (active/passive; growth/value/income/capital appreciation).\n- **Techniques** are the specific *tools and tactics* applied inside the portfolio (diversification, sector rotation, dollar-cost averaging, options, leverage, volatility management, inverse strategies, high-frequency trading)."},
  {"id":566,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"Most questions in this subsection are conceptual \"which best describes…\" or \"which is most suitable for this client\" items. A handful are calculations — dollar-cost averaging, option breakevens, and leverage math — so those are worked below.\n\n#### Strategic vs. Tactical Asset Allocation\n\n**Asset allocation** is the decision of how to split a portfolio among asset classes (stocks, bonds, cash, and sometimes alternatives/real estate). It is generally considered the single most important driver of a portfolio's long-run risk and return — more than individual security selection."},
  {"id":567,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"**Strategic asset allocation (SAA)** sets a *long-term target mix* based on the client's objectives, risk tolerance, and time horizon — for example, 60% equity / 35% fixed income / 5% cash. This target is the \"policy portfolio.\" The manager then **rebalances** periodically back to those targets. Rebalancing is inherently a *sell-high/buy-low* discipline: if stocks rally to 68% of the portfolio, you sell some stock and buy bonds to return to 60/35/5. Strategic allocation is essentially a **long-term, relatively passive** approach."},
  {"id":568,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"**Tactical asset allocation (TAA)** allows *short-term, deliberate deviations* from the strategic targets to exploit perceived market opportunities or mispricings — a controlled form of market timing. If a manager believes equities are temporarily undervalued, TAA might move the mix to 70% equity for a while, then return to the 60% strategic target. Tactical allocation is **shorter-term and active**; it is layered on top of the strategic policy, not a replacement for it."},
  {"id":569,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"| Feature | Strategic | Tactical |\n|---|---|---|\n| Time horizon | Long-term (\"policy\" mix) | Short-term deviations |\n| Nature | Passive; rebalance to targets | Active; market timing |\n| Goal | Match client's long-run objectives | Add return by exploiting opportunities |\n| Discipline | Rebalance back to target weights | Overweight/underweight, then revert |\n\n**Exam tip:** Strategic = **set the long-term target and rebalance to it**. Tactical = **temporary tilts to time the market**. If the question stresses \"long-term policy,\" it is strategic; if it stresses \"capitalize on current conditions\" or \"short-term,\" it is tactical."},
  {"id":570,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"**Common trap:** Rebalancing is part of *strategic* allocation, not a form of market timing. Selling appreciated assets to return to target weights is discipline, not a tactical bet.\n\n#### Portfolio Management Styles: Active vs. Passive\n\nThis choice is about *how a manager tries to add value*.\n\n**Active management** seeks to *outperform* a benchmark (to generate \"alpha\") through security selection, sector bets, and/or timing. It relies on the manager's skill and research. Trade-offs: **higher expense ratios**, **higher portfolio turnover** (more trading costs), and greater **tax inefficiency** (more realized capital gains passed through to investors)."},
  {"id":571,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"**Passive management (indexing)** seeks to *match* a benchmark, typically by holding an index (e.g., an S&P 500 index fund or ETF). Advantages: **low cost**, **low turnover**, and **tax efficiency**. The intellectual case for passive investing rests on the **Efficient Market Hypothesis (EMH)** — in its semi-strong and strong forms, prices already reflect available information, so consistently beating the market *after costs* is very difficult."},
  {"id":572,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"| Feature | Active | Passive |\n|---|---|---|\n| Objective | Beat the benchmark (seek alpha) | Match the benchmark |\n| Cost / expense ratio | Higher | Lower |\n| Turnover / trading | Higher | Lower |\n| Tax efficiency | Lower (more realized gains) | Higher |\n| Rationale | Markets are inefficient; skill pays | EMH; can't reliably beat market net of costs |\n\n**Exam tip:** When a question asks which style has lower costs and better tax efficiency, the answer is **passive/indexing**. When it emphasizes trying to add value through research and selection, it is **active**.\n\n#### Equity Styles: Growth, Value, Income, and Capital Appreciation"},
  {"id":573,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"These describe *what kinds of securities* a manager emphasizes."},
  {"id":574,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"- **Growth style** — Buys companies with above-average earnings/revenue growth. These stocks usually have **high P/E and high price-to-book ratios**, **reinvest earnings** (so they pay little or no dividend), and are **more volatile (higher beta)**. Return comes mainly from **price appreciation**.\n- **Value style** — Buys stocks that appear **underpriced relative to fundamentals**: **low P/E, low price-to-book, higher dividend yield**. These are often mature or temporarily out-of-favor companies. Value investing emphasizes a **\"margin of safety\"** and is somewhat **contrarian** (associated with Graham and Buffett).\n- **Income style** — Emphasizes **current income** over growth: dividend-paying stocks, bonds, preferred stock, REITs. Suits investors who need cash flow, such as **retirees**.\n- **Capital appreciation** — The objective is **growth in the principal value** of the assets over time, with little concern for current income. In practice it overlaps heavily with the growth style and is the *opposite* of an income objective."},
  {"id":575,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"| Style | Typical P/E | Dividend yield | Return source | Suits |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Growth / Capital appreciation | High | Low or none | Price appreciation | Younger, aggressive, long horizon |\n| Value | Low | Higher | Undervaluation + dividends | Patient, contrarian |\n| Income | Varies | High | Current income | Retirees, income-needers |\n\n**Common trap:** Do not mix up the ratios. **Growth = high P/E, low/no dividend.** **Value = low P/E, higher dividend yield.** Exam questions love to describe a \"low P/E, low price-to-book, out-of-favor\" stock and ask for the style — that is **value**, not growth."},
  {"id":576,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"**Exam tip:** \"Capital appreciation\" and \"income\" are usually presented as *opposite objectives*. A client who needs cash flow now is an **income** investor; a client who wants the portfolio's value to grow and does not need income is a **capital appreciation/growth** investor.\n\n#### Diversification\n\n**Diversification** means spreading investments across many securities (and asset classes, sectors, and geographies) so that no single holding can sink the portfolio. Its purpose is to reduce risk without necessarily reducing expected return.\n\nTotal risk has two parts:"},
  {"id":577,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"- **Unsystematic risk** (also called *diversifiable, nonsystematic,* or *company/industry-specific* risk) — this is what diversification **can eliminate**.\n- **Systematic risk** (market risk, measured by **beta**) — this affects the whole market and **cannot be diversified away**."},
  {"id":578,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"Diversification works best when the assets have **low or negative correlation**. The correlation coefficient ranges from **-1.0 (perfectly negatively correlated)** to **+1.0 (perfectly positively correlated)**. Combining assets whose returns do not move together lowers the portfolio's overall standard deviation (volatility). The marginal benefit shrinks as you add names — most diversifiable risk is removed by holding a moderate number of well-chosen, low-correlation securities, after which only systematic risk remains. This is a core idea behind **Modern Portfolio Theory (MPT)**."},
  {"id":579,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"**Exam tip:** Diversification reduces **unsystematic** risk only; **systematic (market) risk remains** no matter how many stocks you hold. Buying 50 different technology stocks is *poor* diversification because they are highly correlated.\n\n#### Sector Rotation\n\n**Sector rotation** is a tactical technique of shifting money among industry sectors based on where the economy sits in the **business cycle**. It rests on the idea that different sectors lead at different stages."},
  {"id":580,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"- **Cyclical** stocks (consumer discretionary, technology, financials, industrials, materials, energy) rise and fall *with* the economy.\n- **Defensive / non-cyclical** stocks (**consumer staples, utilities, healthcare**) hold up relatively well in downturns because demand for their products is stable.\n\n| Business-cycle phase | Sectors that tend to lead |\n|---|---|\n| Early expansion / recovery | Consumer discretionary, technology, financials |\n| Late expansion / peak | Energy, materials, industrials |\n| Contraction / recession | Defensive: consumer staples, utilities, healthcare |"},
  {"id":581,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"**Exam tip:** In a slowing economy or recession, a sector-rotation manager moves toward **defensive sectors (utilities, staples, healthcare)**. Sector rotation is an **active/tactical** technique — recognize it as a form of market timing across industries.\n\n#### Dollar-Cost Averaging (DCA)\n\n**Dollar-cost averaging** means investing a **fixed dollar amount at regular intervals** (e.g., $300 every month) regardless of price. Because the dollar amount is fixed, you automatically buy **more shares when prices are low and fewer shares when prices are high**. The mathematical result is that your **average cost per share is lower than the average of the prices** you paid.\n\nTwo formulas are tested:"},
  {"id":582,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"- Average price per share = (sum of the purchase prices) / (number of purchase periods)\n- Average cost per share = (total dollars invested) / (total shares purchased)\n\n**Worked example:** An investor puts in **$300 per month** for four months at these prices:\n\n| Month | Price | Shares bought ($300 / price) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | $10 | 30 |\n| 2 | $15 | 20 |\n| 3 | $20 | 15 |\n| 4 | $12 | 25 |\n\n- Total invested = 4 × $300 = **$1,200**\n- Total shares = 30 + 20 + 15 + 25 = **90 shares**\n- **Average cost per share** = $1,200 / 90 = **$13.33**\n- **Average price per share** = ($10 + $15 + $20 + $12) / 4 = $57 / 4 = **$14.25**"},
  {"id":583,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"The average **cost** ($13.33) is lower than the average **price** ($14.25) — the signature result of DCA.\n\n**Exam tip:** If a question asks for **average cost**, divide **total dollars by total shares**. If it asks for **average price**, take the **simple mean of the prices**. Average cost is always **less than** average price when prices fluctuate (the two are equal only if the price never changes).\n\n**Common trap:** DCA does **not** guarantee a profit and does **not** protect against loss in a steadily declining market. Its discipline also depends on the investor **continuing to buy through down periods** — which is exactly when investors are tempted to stop."},
  {"id":584,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"#### Buying and Selling Options\n\nOptions let a manager adjust risk and return in ways stock alone cannot. Remember that **one listed option contract covers 100 shares**, and the **premium** is the price of the option. The most-tested positions are:"},
  {"id":585,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"- **Protective put (long stock + long put)** — Buying a put on stock you own acts like **insurance**: the put sets a floor selling price. Cost is the premium paid.\n- **Covered call (long stock + short call)** — Writing a call against stock you own **generates income** (the premium) and provides a small downside cushion, but **caps your upside** because the stock can be called away at the strike.\n- **Collar (long stock + long put + short call)** — Combine the two: the sold call's premium helps pay for the protective put, limiting **both** downside and upside — a low-cost hedge.\n- **Buying calls** (bullish, leveraged upside) and **buying puts** (bearish or hedging) are the basic long positions."},
  {"id":586,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"**Worked example — covered call:** You own stock bought at **$50** and write one call with a **$55 strike** for a **$3 premium**.\n- Breakeven = purchase price − premium = $50 − $3 = **$47**\n- Maximum gain = (strike − purchase price) + premium = ($55 − $50) + $3 = **$8 per share ($800 per contract)**, realized if the stock is called away at $55.\n- The premium cushions losses down to $47, but you give up any gain above $55."},
  {"id":587,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"**Worked example — protective put:** You own stock bought at **$50** and buy a put with a **$45 strike** for a **$2 premium**.\n- Breakeven = purchase price + premium = $50 + $2 = **$52** (the stock must recover the cost of the insurance)\n- Maximum loss = (purchase price − strike) + premium = ($50 − $45) + $2 = **$7 per share**, no matter how far the stock falls.\n\n**Exam tip:** Match the goal to the strategy: **income → covered call**; **downside protection/insurance on a long position → protective put**; **cheap two-sided hedge → collar**.\n\n#### Leveraging"},
  {"id":588,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"**Leverage** means using **borrowed money** (or leveraged products) to increase market exposure beyond the cash invested. The classic vehicle is a **margin account**; under **Regulation T the initial margin requirement is 50%**, so an investor can buy up to twice the value of the cash deposited. Leverage **magnifies both gains and losses**, adds **interest cost**, and creates **margin-call risk**."},
  {"id":589,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"**Worked example:** An investor has **$10,000** in cash and buys **$20,000** of stock using 50% margin (borrowing $10,000). Ignore interest for simplicity.\n- If the stock **rises 10%** to $22,000: repay the $10,000 loan → **$12,000 equity**. Gain of $2,000 on $10,000 invested = **+20%** (double the 10% unleveraged move).\n- If the stock **falls 10%** to $18,000: repay the $10,000 loan → **$8,000 equity**. Loss of $2,000 = **−20%**.\n\nSo 2-to-1 leverage roughly **doubles the percentage return in both directions**. **Leveraged ETFs (2x, 3x)** achieve a similar effect without a margin account, but — like inverse funds below — they reset daily and are meant for short holding periods."},
  {"id":590,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"**Exam tip:** The key phrase is that leverage **amplifies returns and losses** and is therefore only suitable for investors who can tolerate higher risk. A margin call occurs when equity falls below the maintenance requirement.\n\n#### Volatility Management\n\n**Volatility management** refers to strategies aimed at **controlling the ups and downs (standard deviation) of portfolio returns** to produce smoother results and smaller drawdowns. Common tools include:"},
  {"id":591,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"- **Diversification** and adding **low-correlation** or uncorrelated assets.\n- **Low-volatility / minimum-variance** equity strategies (favoring stocks with historically smaller swings).\n- **Options overlays** — protective puts and collars to cap downside.\n- **Managed-volatility (target-volatility) funds** that **reduce equity exposure when market volatility rises and increase it when volatility falls**.\n- Monitoring the **VIX** (the \"fear index\"), which measures expected S&P 500 volatility.\n\nThe trade-off is that damping volatility usually means **giving up some upside** in strong bull markets."},
  {"id":592,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"**Exam tip:** Volatility management targets **risk (standard deviation)**, not maximizing return. It suits **risk-averse clients** who value stability and smaller losses over maximum growth.\n\n#### Inverse Strategies\n\n**Inverse strategies** are designed to **profit when the underlying falls in value**. The main forms are **short selling**, **buying puts**, and **inverse ETFs** (−1x, −2x, −3x) that are built to deliver the *opposite* of an index's return."},
  {"id":593,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"A critical, frequently tested feature: **leveraged and inverse ETFs reset daily**, so over multiple days their results **do not simply mirror the index** — compounding and path-dependency cause **tracking error (\"decay\")**. They are intended for **short-term (often single-day) use**, not buy-and-hold.\n\n**Worked example — inverse ETF decay:** An index starts at **100**; a −1x inverse ETF starts at **$100**.\n- Day 1: index rises **+10%** to 110. The inverse ETF falls **10%** → $100 × 0.90 = **$90**.\n- Day 2: index falls back from 110 to 100, a change of **−9.09%**. The inverse ETF rises **+9.09%** → $90 × 1.0909 = **$98.18**."},
  {"id":594,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"The **index is flat over the two days (back to 100)**, yet the inverse ETF is at **$98.18, down about 1.82%**. You might have expected it to be unchanged too — the gap is daily-reset decay.\n\n**Exam tip:** Leveraged/inverse ETFs are **short-term trading tools** and are generally **unsuitable for long-term retail investors** — a favorite suitability/regulatory point. Because of daily reset, their long-horizon return can differ sharply from the stated multiple of the index's return.\n\n#### High-Frequency Trading (HFT)"},
  {"id":595,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"**High-frequency trading** is a form of **algorithmic trading** in which powerful computers execute enormous numbers of orders in **fractions of a second**, profiting from tiny, fleeting price discrepancies. HFT firms often use **co-location** (placing servers physically near the exchange to shave microseconds) and can act as **liquidity providers**. It is also **controversial**, associated with concerns about market fairness, front-running, and events like \"flash crashes.\"\n\nOn the Series 65, HFT appears as a **definitional/conceptual** item, not something an individual adviser or retail client would implement. Recognize it as **institutional, ultra-fast, computer-driven trading**."},
  {"id":596,"part":"Part III","section":"D. Portfolio Management Strategies, Styles and Techniques","anchor":"d-portfolio-management-strategies-styles-and-techniques","text":"**Exam tip:** If a choice describes **millisecond, algorithm-driven institutional trading using co-located servers**, that is **high-frequency trading** — distinct from the longer-horizon strategies (strategic/tactical allocation, DCA, buy-and-hold) that individual advisers actually use for clients."},
  {"id":597,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"Taxes are one of the few \"certain\" drags on an investor's return, and a suitable recommendation is always measured on an **after-tax** basis. The Series 65 does not turn you into a CPA, but it expects you to understand how the major tax rules affect a client's investments, retirement income, and estate — and to spot the traps advisers walk clients into. This subsection builds from the individual income tax, up through entity taxation, and finishes with the transfer (estate and gift) taxes."},
  {"id":598,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"**Exam tip:** The exam almost never asks you to compute an exact tax bill from a full bracket schedule. It asks you to *classify* items (ordinary vs. capital, qualified vs. non-qualified, marginal vs. effective) and to know the rules and thresholds. Learn the concepts and the commonly tested numbers, not a tax return.\n\n#### Individual Income Tax: Ordinary Income, Marginal Bracket, and Effective Rate\n\nThe U.S. individual income tax is **progressive**: income is sliced into brackets, and each additional slice is taxed at a higher rate. Two rate concepts are constantly confused, and the exam loves the distinction."},
  {"id":599,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"- **Marginal tax bracket (marginal rate):** the rate applied to the client's *next* dollar of income — the top bracket their income reaches.\n- **Effective (average) tax rate:** total tax paid divided by total taxable income.\n\nFormula (plain text):\n\n- Effective Tax Rate = Total Tax Paid / Taxable Income\n\nWorked example (using simplified illustrative single-filer brackets: 10% on the first $11,000, 12% on $11,000–$44,000, 22% on $44,000–$95,000):\n\nA client has $60,000 of taxable income.\n- 10% × $11,000 = $1,100\n- 12% × ($44,000 − $11,000) = 12% × $33,000 = $3,960\n- 22% × ($60,000 − $44,000) = 22% × $16,000 = $3,520\n- Total tax = $1,100 + $3,960 + $3,520 = **$8,580**"},
  {"id":600,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"This client's **marginal rate is 22%** (the last dollar is taxed there), but the **effective rate is $8,580 / $60,000 = 14.3%**.\n\n**Exam tip:** When a question asks \"what rate should you use to evaluate whether a municipal bond beats a corporate bond?\" the answer is the client's **marginal** rate, because the muni's tax-free advantage applies to the *next* dollars of income the client would otherwise be taxed on.\n\n**Common trap:** Choosing the effective rate when the question is about the value of a deduction or a tax-free investment. The benefit of a deduction or exclusion is always valued at the marginal rate.\n\n#### Capital Gains and Losses: Short-Term vs. Long-Term"},
  {"id":601,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"A **capital gain (or loss)** arises when a capital asset (stock, bond, fund, real estate) is *sold*. It is a **realized** event — unrealized appreciation (\"paper gains\") is not taxed until sale.\n\nThe single most tested distinction is the **holding period**:\n\n| Holding period | Classification | Taxed at |\n|---|---|---|\n| **One year or less** | Short-term | Ordinary income rates (up to 37%) |\n| **More than one year** (\"a year and a day\") | Long-term | Preferential rates: 0%, 15%, or 20% |\n\nLong-term rates are deliberately lower to reward longer holding. The applicable long-term rate (0/15/20%) depends on the taxpayer's income; most clients land at 15%."},
  {"id":602,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"**Netting rules.** Gains and losses are netted at year-end: short-term against short-term, long-term against long-term, then the two categories against each other. Key limits the exam expects you to know:\n\n- Net capital losses can offset ordinary income only up to **$3,000 per year**.\n- Any excess loss is **carried forward indefinitely** to future years (never lost, never carried back for individuals).\n\n**Wash sale rule.** If a client sells a security at a loss and buys the *same or a substantially identical* security within **30 days before or after** the sale (a 61-day window), the loss is **disallowed**. The disallowed loss is added to the basis of the replacement shares."},
  {"id":603,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"**Common trap:** Buying the identical security 25 days after harvesting a loss triggers the wash sale — it applies to purchases on *either side* of the sale, not just after.\n\n**Exam tip:** Long-term status requires **more than** 12 months. Exactly 12 months is still short-term. \"A year and a day\" is the memory hook.\n\n#### Tax Basis (Cost Basis)\n\n**Basis** is the taxpayer's investment in an asset for tax purposes — the amount that is *not* taxed again on sale. Gain or loss equals sale proceeds minus adjusted basis.\n\n- Capital Gain/Loss = Net Sale Proceeds − Adjusted Cost Basis"},
  {"id":604,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"Basis includes the purchase price **plus commissions/acquisition costs**, and it is **increased by reinvested dividends and capital gains distributions** (because those were already taxed when reinvested).\n\nWorked example: A client buys 100 shares at $40 ($4,000) and pays a $50 commission → initial basis $4,050. Over the years she reinvests $600 of dividends (already taxed). Adjusted basis = $4,050 + $600 = **$4,650**. She later sells all shares for $7,000 (net of commission). Capital gain = $7,000 − $4,650 = **$2,350**."},
  {"id":605,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"**Common trap:** Forgetting to add reinvested dividends to basis. If the client is taxed on the dividend when reinvested and again treats it as $0 basis at sale, the gain is double-taxed. Reinvested distributions raise basis.\n\n**Two special basis rules the exam tests:**"},
  {"id":606,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"- **Inherited property — step-up (or step-down) in basis:** Assets passed at death receive a basis equal to **fair market value on the date of death**. Years of appreciation escape income tax entirely. Example: father bought stock at $10/share; at his death it is worth $90; the heir's basis is $90. If the heir sells at $92, the taxable gain is only $2.\n- **Gifted property — carryover basis:** A gift made during life generally carries over the **donor's original cost basis** to the recipient (for computing gains). There is no step-up for lifetime gifts."},
  {"id":607,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"**Exam tip:** \"Should the client gift the highly appreciated stock now or leave it in the estate?\" Gifting carries over the low basis (built-in gain follows the asset); holding until death gives the heir a stepped-up basis that wipes out the gain. This is a classic estate-planning trade-off question.\n\n#### Qualified Dividends\n\nDividends are ordinary income by default, but **qualified dividends** are taxed at the lower **long-term capital gains rates (0/15/20%)**. To qualify:"},
  {"id":608,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"- The dividend is paid by a U.S. corporation or a qualified foreign corporation, **and**\n- The investor meets a **holding period**: the stock must be held **more than 60 days during the 121-day period beginning 60 days before the ex-dividend date**.\n\n**Non-qualified (ordinary) dividends** include REIT dividends (mostly), money-market \"dividends\" (really interest), and dividends on shares held too briefly.\n\n**Common trap:** Assuming all dividends get the low rate. REIT and most MLP distributions do **not** qualify. Also, a day-trader who buys a stock just to capture the dividend and sells immediately fails the holding period — the dividend is non-qualified.\n\n#### Alternative Minimum Tax (AMT)"},
  {"id":609,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"The **AMT** is a parallel tax system designed to ensure that higher-income taxpayers who use many deductions and preference items still pay a minimum amount. The taxpayer computes tax two ways — regular and AMT — and **pays the higher** of the two.\n\nAMT is calculated by starting with regular taxable income, **adding back certain \"preference items\"** to reach Alternative Minimum Taxable Income (AMTI), subtracting the AMT exemption, and applying the AMT rate.\n\nCommonly tested AMT preference/add-back items:"},
  {"id":610,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"- **Interest on private-activity (private-purpose) municipal bonds** — otherwise tax-free interest becomes taxable for AMT.\n- The **bargain element on incentive stock options (ISOs)** exercised and held.\n- **Accelerated depreciation**, **intangible drilling costs**, and **percentage depletion** (common in oil-and-gas and real estate limited partnerships).\n- Certain deductions such as **state and local taxes**."},
  {"id":611,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"**Exam tip:** The bond distinction is a favorite. Interest on a **public-purpose** general obligation or standard revenue muni is *not* an AMT item; interest on a **private-activity bond** *is* an AMT preference item. A client already subject to AMT should be steered away from private-activity municipals.\n\n**Common trap:** Thinking municipal bond interest is always completely tax-free. Public-purpose muni interest is free of regular federal tax, but private-activity muni interest can be pulled back into AMT.\n\n#### Retirement Plan Distributions and Required Minimum Distributions (RMDs)"},
  {"id":612,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"**Taxation of distributions.** Contributions and distributions are taxed depending on whether the account is traditional (pre-tax) or Roth (after-tax):"},
  {"id":613,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"- **Traditional IRA / 401(k):** contributions may be deductible (pre-tax); the account grows tax-deferred; **distributions are taxed as ordinary income** (never capital gains, even if the growth came from stocks).\n- **Roth IRA / Roth 401(k):** contributions are after-tax (no deduction); **qualified distributions are entirely tax-free** (account open 5 years *and* age 59½, death, or disability).\n- **Premature distributions** (generally before age **59½**) are subject to a **10% penalty** on top of ordinary income tax, unless an exception applies (death, disability, first-home up to $10,000, qualified education, certain medical, substantially equal periodic payments, etc.)."},
  {"id":614,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"**Common trap:** Answering \"capital gains\" for the tax on a traditional IRA withdrawal. All of it — original contributions deducted and all growth — comes out as **ordinary income**.\n\n**Required Minimum Distributions (RMDs).** The government eventually wants its tax, so traditional retirement accounts must begin paying out."},
  {"id":615,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"- RMDs must begin at age **73** (under the SECURE Act 2.0; scheduled to rise to 75 in 2033).\n- The **first** RMD may be delayed until **April 1 of the year *after*** the year the owner turns 73 (the \"required beginning date\"); every RMD after that is due by December 31.\n- **Roth IRAs have no RMDs during the owner's lifetime.** (SECURE 2.0 also eliminated lifetime RMDs from Roth 401(k)s beginning 2024.)\n- **Penalty for a missed RMD:** an excise tax of **25%** of the shortfall (reduced from the old 50%), dropping to **10%** if corrected promptly within the correction window."},
  {"id":616,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"RMD amount = Prior year-end account balance / IRS life-expectancy factor. Example: $500,000 balance and a life-expectancy factor of 25 → RMD = $500,000 / 25 = **$20,000** that must be withdrawn and taxed as ordinary income.\n\n**Exam tip:** Two dates to memorize — **59½** (penalty-free access begins) and **73** (RMDs begin). Delaying the *first* RMD to April 1 forces *two* taxable distributions into one year (that year's and the next), possibly pushing the client into a higher bracket — a suitability point.\n\n#### Government-Benefit Implications: IRMAA"},
  {"id":617,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"Retirement income does not exist in a vacuum; higher income can raise a client's **Medicare** costs. The **IRMAA (Income-Related Monthly Adjustment Amount)** is a surcharge added to **Medicare Part B and Part D** premiums for higher-income beneficiaries.\n\n- IRMAA is based on **Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI) from two years prior** (there is a lookback).\n- Because it is triggered by income *thresholds* (a \"cliff\"), a single extra dollar of income can bump a client into a higher IRMAA tier and raise premiums for the whole year."},
  {"id":618,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"**Exam tip:** Actions that spike a client's MAGI — a large Roth conversion, realizing big capital gains, or taking an oversized IRA distribution — can raise Medicare premiums two years later. A tax-aware adviser manages the *timing* of income around these thresholds. Roth distributions are especially valuable here because qualified Roth withdrawals do **not** count toward MAGI for IRMAA.\n\n**Related concept — taxation of Social Security benefits:** up to **85%** of Social Security benefits can become taxable once \"combined income\" (AGI + nontaxable interest + one-half of benefits) exceeds set thresholds — another reason tax-free Roth income is prized in retirement."},
  {"id":619,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"#### Corporate Taxation: C Corporation vs. S Corporation\n\nHow a business entity is taxed drives which securities and strategies suit its owners."},
  {"id":620,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"| Feature | C Corporation | S Corporation |\n|---|---|---|\n| Taxation | **Double taxation** — taxed at corporate level, then again on dividends to shareholders | **Pass-through** — no entity-level tax; income/loss flows to shareholders |\n| Corporate tax rate | Flat **21%** (post-TCJA) | None at federal level |\n| Shareholder limit | Unlimited | **100 or fewer** |\n| Classes of stock | Multiple allowed | **One class** of stock only |\n| Owner eligibility | Anyone | U.S. citizens/residents; generally no corporate or partnership owners |\n| Reports to owners | Form 1099-DIV for dividends | **Schedule K-1** |"},
  {"id":621,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"**Double taxation** is the defining C-corp trait: the corporation pays 21% on its profits, and shareholders pay tax again when those profits are distributed as dividends.\n\n**Dividends-received deduction (DRD):** to soften the layering of taxation when one corporation owns stock in another, a corporate shareholder can deduct **50%** of dividends received (65% if it owns 20% or more of the payer). This is why corporations sometimes prefer dividend-paying stock over bonds (interest is fully taxable to the corporation).\n\n**Common trap:** Applying the DRD to an individual investor. The dividends-received deduction is available only to **corporate** shareholders, not individuals.\n\n#### Trusts"},
  {"id":622,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"A **trust** is a separate taxable entity that holds assets for beneficiaries. For the exam:\n\n- **Simple trust:** must distribute **all** income annually and makes no principal distributions; the *beneficiaries* pay tax on the income.\n- **Complex trust:** may **accumulate** income and distribute principal; the trust pays tax on what it retains.\n- **Grantor trust:** the person who created it retains control, so **income is taxed to the grantor**, not the trust."},
  {"id":623,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"**Key exam point — compressed brackets:** Trusts (and estates) reach the **top marginal tax rate (37%) at a very low income level — around $15,000** — far faster than an individual, who does not hit the top bracket until income is in the hundreds of thousands. This creates a planning incentive to **distribute income to beneficiaries**, who are usually in lower brackets, rather than let it accumulate inside the trust.\n\n#### Pass-Through Entities: REITs, MLPs, and LLCs\n\nPass-through (or \"flow-through\") entities avoid entity-level tax; income flows to the owners, who are taxed once."},
  {"id":624,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"**REIT (Real Estate Investment Trust):**\n- Must distribute at least **90%** of its taxable income to shareholders to keep its pass-through status (and generally hold at least 75% of assets in real estate / derive at least 75% of income from real estate).\n- Distributions are **largely taxed as ordinary income to the shareholder — not qualified dividends** (though a portion may be return of capital or capital gain, and the ordinary-income portion of REIT dividends can qualify for the QBI/199A deduction)."},
  {"id":625,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"**MLP (Master Limited Partnership):**\n- A publicly traded **partnership** — a pass-through that reports on a **Schedule K-1**, not a 1099.\n- Distributions often exceed taxable income and are treated partly as **return of capital**, which **reduces the investor's basis** (deferring, not eliminating, tax until sale).\n- **Common trap:** Holding an MLP inside an IRA can generate **Unrelated Business Taxable Income (UBTI)**, which is taxable even inside the tax-deferred account — MLPs are generally poor IRA holdings."},
  {"id":626,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"**LLC (Limited Liability Company):**\n- Provides **limited liability** like a corporation but **flexible tax treatment**. By default it is a pass-through (single-member = disregarded entity; multi-member = partnership), but it may **elect** to be taxed as a C or S corporation.\n\n**Exam tip:** The recurring theme is *how the income is reported and taxed to the owner* — **K-1 (partnership/MLP/S corp)** vs. **1099**, and **ordinary income (REIT)** vs. **qualified dividend (typical C-corp stock)**.\n\n#### Wealth Transfer: Estate and Gift Tax Fundamentals"},
  {"id":627,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"The federal transfer taxes catch large transfers of wealth — during life (gift tax) and at death (estate tax). They share a **unified** framework."},
  {"id":628,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"**The unified credit and lifetime exemption.** Gift tax and estate tax are integrated under one **unified transfer tax** with a single **unified credit**. This credit shelters a large **basic exclusion amount (lifetime exemption)** from tax — indexed for inflation (**$15 million per individual in 2026** under the One Big Beautiful Bill Act, up from $13.99 million in 2025, and indexed for inflation thereafter). Transfers above the exemption are taxed at rates up to **40%**. Because the exemption is so high, most clients owe no federal estate or gift tax — but the *rules* are still tested."},
  {"id":629,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"**Annual gift tax exclusion (heavily tested).** Separate from the lifetime exemption, each donor may give up to the **annual exclusion amount per recipient, per year, with no gift tax and no filing** — **$19,000 per donee in 2026** (unchanged from 2025; indexed periodically). There is no limit on the *number* of recipients.\n\nWorked example: A grandmother gives $19,000 each to her four grandchildren in one year = $76,000 transferred out of her estate with **no** gift tax and no use of her lifetime exemption. Do it every year and the amounts compound out of the taxable estate."},
  {"id":630,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"**Gift splitting.** A married couple can elect to treat a gift as made half by each spouse, effectively **doubling** the annual exclusion to **$38,000 per recipient** (2026).\n\n**Annual vs. lifetime — how they interact:** Only the amount of a gift **above** the annual exclusion counts against the donor's lifetime exemption. Example: a $119,000 gift to one child in 2026 uses $19,000 of annual exclusion and $100,000 of lifetime exemption; no tax is actually due until the lifetime exemption is exhausted.\n\n**Other tested essentials:**"},
  {"id":631,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"- The **donor**, not the recipient, is liable for gift tax. Gifts received are **not** taxable income to the donee.\n- **Unlimited marital deduction:** transfers between U.S.-citizen spouses (during life or at death) are entirely tax-free.\n- **Unlimited exclusions** also apply to **direct payments of tuition and medical bills** made to the institution/provider, and to qualified charitable gifts.\n- **Basis reminder:** gifts carry **carryover basis**; bequests at death get a **stepped-up basis** (tie back to the Tax Basis section)."},
  {"id":632,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"**Portability.** If one spouse dies without using the full exemption, the **unused amount (the DSUE — Deceased Spousal Unused Exclusion) can be transferred to the surviving spouse**, who may add it to their own exemption. Portability is **not automatic** — the executor must **elect it by filing a federal estate tax return (Form 706)**, even if no tax is owed."},
  {"id":633,"part":"Part III","section":"E. Tax Considerations","anchor":"e-tax-considerations","text":"**Exam tip:** Remember the three tiers — **(1) annual exclusion** (per donee, per year), **(2) lifetime exemption/unified credit** (cumulative, shared by gift and estate tax), and **(3) unlimited marital deduction and portability** (spouse-to-spouse). The **annual gift tax exclusion** is the single most likely of these to appear on your exam.\n\n**Common trap:** Confusing the annual exclusion with the lifetime exemption. The **annual exclusion** ($19,000 in 2026) resets every year and is per recipient; the **lifetime exemption** ($15 million in 2026) is a one-time cumulative shelter that gift and estate tax both draw on."},
  {"id":634,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"Retirement plans are one of the most heavily tested topics in Part 3 because a Series 65 candidate is expected to recommend suitable savings vehicles to clients. The single most important organizing idea is the split between **qualified** and **nonqualified** plans and how each is taxed. Learn that framework first, then fit every specific plan (IRAs, 401(k)s, 403(b)s, etc.) into it."},
  {"id":635,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"> **Note on numbers:** Contribution limits are indexed for inflation and change most years. The figures below reflect widely tested current values. The exam rewards you for knowing the *concepts and relationships* (which plan lets you defer more, who bears investment risk, how distributions are taxed) far more than the exact dollar figure for any given tax year. Know the round numbers, but do not panic over a $500 change.\n\n#### The Big Picture: Qualified vs. Nonqualified Plans\n\nA **qualified plan** meets the requirements of the Internal Revenue Code and ERISA (the Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974) and therefore receives favorable tax treatment. Its defining features:"},
  {"id":636,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"- **Contributions are made with pre-tax dollars** (they are tax-deductible or made by salary reduction before tax).\n- **Earnings grow tax-deferred.**\n- **The entire distribution is taxed as ordinary income** at withdrawal — because none of the money was ever taxed.\n- The plan **must be nondiscriminatory** (it cannot favor highly compensated employees/owners), must follow vesting and coverage rules, and generally requires IRS approval and a trust.\n\nA **nonqualified plan** does **not** meet those Code/ERISA requirements. Its defining features:"},
  {"id":637,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"- **Contributions are made with after-tax dollars** (not deductible).\n- Earnings still grow **tax-deferred.**\n- At distribution, **only the earnings (growth) are taxed** as ordinary income; the participant's own after-tax contributions (the cost basis) come back tax-free because they were already taxed.\n- The plan **may discriminate** — this is exactly why employers use them to reward select executives."},
  {"id":638,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"**Exam tip:** The classic trap question asks how a distribution is taxed. In a **qualified** plan the *whole* payment is taxable ordinary income. In a **nonqualified** plan only the *earnings* are taxable; the basis is returned tax-free. Memorize that one sentence and you will answer several questions correctly.\n\n#### Traditional IRAs\n\nAn **Individual Retirement Arrangement (IRA)** is a personal, self-directed retirement account — it is *not* an employer plan and is *not* covered by ERISA."},
  {"id":639,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"**Who can contribute and how much.** You must have **earned income** (compensation from working — wages, salary, self-employment income, tips, and taxable alimony under pre-2019 divorce decrees). Investment income, Social Security, pensions, and rental income do **not** count. The annual contribution limit is the lesser of the indexed dollar cap or 100% of earned income:"},
  {"id":640,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"- Contribution limit: **$7,000** per year.\n- **Catch-up contribution** for those **age 50 and older: an extra $1,000**, for **$8,000** total.\n- Contributions may be made up until the **tax-filing deadline (April 15)** of the following year.\n- Thanks to the SECURE Act, there is **no upper age limit** on making contributions as long as you have earned income.\n\n**Spousal IRA.** A nonworking spouse may fund an IRA based on the working spouse's income, provided the couple files a **joint return** and has enough combined earned income to cover both contributions."},
  {"id":641,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"**Deductibility (the part people confuse).** The *contribution* is always allowed if you have earned income, but the *deduction* depends on whether you (or your spouse) are an **active participant** in an employer-sponsored plan and on your **modified adjusted gross income (MAGI)**:\n\n- If **neither** spouse is covered by an employer plan, the contribution is **fully deductible regardless of income.**\n- If you **are** an active participant, the deduction **phases out** over an income range (representative single range roughly $79,000–$89,000 MAGI; married filing jointly roughly $126,000–$146,000). Above the range, you get **no deduction** but may still make a **nondeductible contribution.**"},
  {"id":642,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"- Deductible contributions and all earnings are taxed as **ordinary income** when withdrawn (this is \"qualified-money\" treatment even though an IRA is technically not an ERISA \"qualified plan\").\n- **Early withdrawal penalty:** distributions before age **59½** face a **10% penalty** on top of ordinary income tax, unless an exception applies (death, disability, first-time home purchase up to $10,000, qualified higher-education expenses, certain medical expenses, health insurance while unemployed, or a series of substantially equal periodic payments).\n- **Required Minimum Distributions (RMDs):** the owner must begin taking RMDs at age **73** (rising to 75 in 2033 under SECURE 2.0). Failure to take an RMD triggers an excise tax of **25%** of the shortfall (reduced to 10% if corrected promptly)."},
  {"id":643,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"**Worked example — early withdrawal cost.** A 50-year-old in the 24% marginal bracket withdraws **$10,000** of fully deductible IRA money.\n\n- 10% penalty: 0.10 × $10,000 = **$1,000**\n- Ordinary income tax: 0.24 × $10,000 = **$2,400**\n- Total cost: $1,000 + $2,400 = **$3,400**; net received = **$6,600**\n\n**Worked example — RMD.** RMD = Prior Year-End Account Balance ÷ Distribution Period (from the IRS Uniform Lifetime Table). At age 73 the table factor is about **26.5**. With a **$500,000** balance:\n\n- RMD = $500,000 ÷ 26.5 = **$18,868** (approximately)\n\n#### Roth IRAs\n\nA **Roth IRA** flips the tax timing of a Traditional IRA: you pay tax now, not later."},
  {"id":644,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"- **Contributions are always after-tax** (never deductible).\n- The annual limit is the **same $7,000 / $8,000** cap, and it is a **combined** limit — total contributions to Traditional and Roth IRAs together cannot exceed it.\n- **Eligibility phases out at higher incomes** (representative single range roughly $150,000–$165,000 MAGI; married filing jointly roughly $236,000–$246,000). High earners can be barred from contributing directly to a Roth. (Contrast: there is no income limit to *contribute* to a Traditional IRA, only to *deduct* it.)\n- **No age limit** to contribute; earned income still required."},
  {"id":645,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"**Qualified (tax-free) distributions.** A Roth distribution is completely tax-free and penalty-free if **both** tests are met:\n\n1. The account has been open for at least **5 years** (the 5-year rule), **and**\n2. The owner is at least **59½**, **or** the distribution is due to **death**, **disability**, or a **first-time home purchase** ($10,000 lifetime cap).\n\n**Other key Roth features.**\n\n- **Contributions (basis) can be withdrawn at any time, tax- and penalty-free**, because that money was already taxed. Only *earnings* pulled out early are subject to tax and the 10% penalty.\n- **No RMDs during the owner's lifetime** — a major planning advantage over Traditional IRAs."},
  {"id":646,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"**Common trap:** Students confuse the two income tests. Roth IRAs have an income limit on the *ability to contribute at all*. Traditional IRAs have no contribution income limit — the income test only affects *deductibility*. Read the question stem carefully.\n\n#### Traditional vs. Roth IRA at a Glance"},
  {"id":647,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"| Feature | Traditional IRA | Roth IRA |\n|---|---|---|\n| Contribution deductible? | Sometimes (depends on active participation and MAGI) | Never (always after-tax) |\n| Income limit to contribute? | No | Yes (phase-out at higher MAGI) |\n| Growth | Tax-deferred | Tax-deferred |\n| Qualified distributions | Taxed as ordinary income | **Tax-free** (5-year rule + qualifying event) |\n| 10% early-withdrawal penalty? | Yes, before 59½ | On earnings only; basis always accessible |\n| Lifetime RMDs? | Yes, at age 73 | **None** |\n| Annual limit | $7,000 (+$1,000 age 50+) | Same $7,000 (+$1,000) cap, **combined** across both types |\n\n#### Solo 401(k): Traditional and Roth"},
  {"id":648,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"A **Solo 401(k)** (also called an Individual or One-Participant 401(k)) is designed for a **self-employed person with no employees** other than a spouse. Its power comes from letting the owner contribute in **two capacities at once**:\n\n1. **As the employee** — an elective salary deferral up to the standard 401(k) deferral limit of **$23,500** (plus the **$7,500** catch-up if age 50+). This portion can be made as **traditional (pre-tax)** or **Roth (after-tax).**\n2. **As the employer** — a profit-sharing contribution of up to **25% of compensation** (pre-tax).\n\nThe combined employee + employer total cannot exceed the overall defined-contribution limit of **$70,000** (plus any catch-up)."},
  {"id":649,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"**Worked example.** A 45-year-old sole proprietor with $120,000 of W-2-equivalent compensation:\n\n- Employee deferral: **$23,500**\n- Employer profit-sharing: 25% × $120,000 = **$30,000**\n- Total: $23,500 + $30,000 = **$53,500** (within the $70,000 cap)\n\n**Exam tip:** The Roth Solo 401(k) applies only to the **employee deferral** piece; the employer profit-sharing portion is always pre-tax. Also note the Solo 401(k) lets a self-employed saver contribute far more than a SEP or SIMPLE at modest income levels because of the flat employee deferral on top of the percentage-based employer piece.\n\n#### Qualified Employer Plans: Defined Benefit vs. Defined Contribution"},
  {"id":650,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"All employer-sponsored qualified plans fall into two structural families. Knowing **who bears the investment risk** is the key distinction.\n\n**Defined Benefit (DB) plan — a traditional pension.**\n\n- The plan **promises a specific benefit at retirement**, usually a formula based on salary and years of service (e.g., \"60% of final average salary\").\n- **The employer bears the investment risk**; required contributions are calculated by an **actuary** to fund the promised benefit.\n- Favors older, highly paid employees who have less time to accumulate.\n\n**Defined Contribution (DC) plan.**"},
  {"id":651,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"- The **contribution** is defined, but the **future benefit is not guaranteed** — it depends on investment performance.\n- **The employee bears the investment risk.**\n- Examples: **401(k)**, profit-sharing, money-purchase, and (in the broader sense) 403(b) plans.\n- Individual accounts; each participant's balance reflects contributions plus/minus investment results."},
  {"id":652,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"| | Defined Benefit | Defined Contribution |\n|---|---|---|\n| What's promised | A specific retirement benefit | A specific contribution amount |\n| Who bears investment risk | **Employer** | **Employee** |\n| Funding | Actuarially determined | Contributions to individual accounts |\n| Typical example | Traditional pension | 401(k), profit-sharing |\n\n**Vesting.** Employees are always **100% immediately vested in their own contributions.** **Employer** contributions may vest over time under a **cliff** (full vesting after a set number of years) or **graded** (gradual) schedule.\n\n#### Company 401(k)"},
  {"id":653,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"A **401(k)** is the dominant defined-contribution plan. Employees elect to defer part of their salary (a **cash-or-deferred arrangement**):\n\n- **Elective deferral limit: $23,500**, plus a **$7,500** catch-up at age 50+, and (under SECURE 2.0) an enhanced catch-up of **$11,250** for ages 60–63.\n- Employers frequently **match** contributions (e.g., 50% of the first 6% deferred) — commonly tested as \"free money\" the client should capture.\n- A **Roth 401(k)** option allows after-tax deferrals with tax-free qualified withdrawals; note that unlike a Roth IRA, a Roth 401(k) has **no income limit** and (under SECURE 2.0) **no lifetime RMDs** starting in 2024."},
  {"id":654,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"#### 403(b) Plans (Tax-Sheltered Annuities / TSAs)\n\nA **403(b)** is the nonprofit-sector cousin of the 401(k), available to employees of **public schools, tax-exempt 501(c)(3) organizations, and certain church organizations.**\n\n- Salary-reduction deferrals with the **same $23,500 limit** (plus catch-ups).\n- **Investment choices are restricted to annuities and mutual funds** (the latter held through custodial accounts). A 403(b) **cannot invest directly in individual stocks or bonds.**"},
  {"id":655,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"**Common trap:** If a question describes a public school teacher who wants to hold **individual stocks** inside a 403(b), the answer is that it is **not permitted** — 403(b) investments are limited to annuities and mutual funds.\n\n#### 457 Plans\n\nA **457(b)** is a deferred-compensation plan for **state and local government employees** and certain tax-exempt organizations."},
  {"id":656,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"- Same **$23,500** elective deferral limit; a special \"final-3-years\" catch-up can allow larger contributions as retirement nears.\n- **Key distinction:** a governmental 457(b) has **no 10% early-withdrawal penalty** on distributions after **separation from service**, even before age 59½. This is the most commonly tested 457 fact.\n- Technically, 457 plans are **nonqualified deferred-compensation** arrangements, but the exam groups them with employer retirement plans; they still receive salary-deferral, tax-deferred treatment.\n\n#### SIMPLE IRA\n\nThe **Savings Incentive Match Plan for Employees (SIMPLE) IRA** is for **small employers with 100 or fewer employees** that maintain no other plan."},
  {"id":657,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"- **Employee deferral limit: $16,500**, plus a **$3,500** catch-up at age 50+.\n- The employer **must** contribute, choosing either:\n  - a **dollar-for-dollar match up to 3%** of the employee's compensation, **or**\n  - a **2% nonelective** contribution for all eligible employees (whether or not they defer).\n- All contributions are **immediately 100% vested.**\n- **Special penalty:** withdrawals during the **first 2 years** of participation incur a **25% penalty** (versus the usual 10%).\n\n**Worked example — SIMPLE match.** An employee earning $60,000 defers 4% ($2,400). Under the 3% match, the employer matches only up to 3% of pay:"},
  {"id":658,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"- Employer match = 3% × $60,000 = **$1,800**\n- Total going into the account = $2,400 + $1,800 = **$4,200**\n\n#### SEP IRA\n\nThe **Simplified Employee Pension (SEP) IRA** is an easy-to-administer plan popular with small businesses and the self-employed.\n\n- **Funded entirely by the employer** — employees do not make their own salary deferrals.\n- The employer may contribute up to **25% of each eligible employee's compensation**, capped at **$70,000.**\n- Contributions are **discretionary** (the employer can vary the amount year to year) but must be a **uniform percentage** for all eligible employees, and are **immediately 100% vested.**"},
  {"id":659,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"**Worked example — SEP contribution.** An employer contributes the maximum for a common-law employee earning $100,000:\n\n- Contribution = 25% × $100,000 = **$25,000** (within the $70,000 cap)\n\n**Common trap (self-employed):** For a **common-law employee** the rate is **25% of compensation.** For a **self-employed** owner, the contribution is figured on net earnings *after* subtracting the contribution itself and half of self-employment tax, which works out to an effective **20% of net self-employment income.** If a question gives a sole proprietor's net earnings and asks the maximum SEP contribution rate, the answer is **20%**, not 25%.\n\n#### Nonqualified Retirement Plans"},
  {"id":660,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"**Nonqualified plans** intentionally sidestep ERISA's qualification rules so the employer can reward **selected executives or key employees** — discrimination is allowed. Common types include **deferred compensation plans**, **payroll-deduction plans**, **executive bonus plans**, and **split-dollar life insurance.**"},
  {"id":661,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"- **Contributions are after-tax / not deductible** to the participant (and the employer's deduction is generally deferred until the benefit is paid).\n- Earnings still grow **tax-deferred.**\n- At payout, **only the earnings are taxed**; the participant's after-tax basis is returned tax-free.\n- Because they are not ERISA-qualified, benefits may be at risk of the employer's creditors (the promise is often unfunded).\n\n**Worked example — nonqualified distribution taxation.** An executive contributed **$50,000** of after-tax dollars to a nonqualified plan that grew to **$80,000**, then took the full distribution:"},
  {"id":662,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"- Return of basis (already taxed): **$50,000 tax-free**\n- Taxable earnings: $80,000 − $50,000 = **$30,000** taxed as ordinary income\n\nContrast this with a **qualified plan**: if the entire $80,000 had been pre-tax money, the **full $80,000** would be taxable.\n\n#### Qualified vs. Nonqualified: The Taxation Distinction (Summary)\n\nThis comparison ties the whole section together and is the most exam-relevant takeaway:"},
  {"id":663,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"| Feature | Qualified Plan | Nonqualified Plan |\n|---|---|---|\n| Contributions | **Pre-tax** (deductible / salary reduction) | **After-tax** (not deductible) |\n| Must satisfy ERISA / be nondiscriminatory | **Yes** | **No** — may favor executives |\n| Earnings growth | Tax-deferred | Tax-deferred |\n| Cost basis at distribution | **Zero** (nothing was taxed yet) | Equal to after-tax contributions |\n| What is taxed at distribution | **Entire distribution** as ordinary income | **Only the earnings** as ordinary income |\n| IRS approval / trust required | Yes | No |\n| Examples | 401(k), 403(b), DB pension, profit-sharing, SEP, SIMPLE, IRAs | Deferred comp, executive bonus, split-dollar, payroll-deduction, 457 (technically) |"},
  {"id":664,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"**Exam tip:** When you see a distribution-taxation question, first classify the plan as qualified or nonqualified. Qualified → the entire payout is ordinary income. Nonqualified → subtract the after-tax basis and tax only the growth. Also remember that all retirement-plan distributions of previously untaxed money are taxed as **ordinary income**, never at capital-gains rates — a frequently tested point."},
  {"id":665,"part":"Part III","section":"F. Retirement Plans","anchor":"f-retirement-plans","text":"**Rollovers (brief but testable).** Money can move between retirement accounts without immediate tax. A **direct (trustee-to-trustee) transfer** moves funds between custodians with no withholding and no limit on frequency — the preferred method. A **60-day (indirect) rollover** pays the money to the client, who must redeposit it within **60 days**; on IRA-to-IRA rollovers this is limited to **one per 12-month period**, and employer-plan distributions paid to the client are subject to mandatory **20% withholding.** A **Roth conversion** moves Traditional IRA money to a Roth and is a **taxable event** in the year of conversion, but future qualified Roth withdrawals are tax-free."},
  {"id":666,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"The Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974 (ERISA) is the federal law governing how private-sector employer-sponsored retirement plans must be operated. On the Series 65, ERISA questions cluster around one central theme: the fiduciary standard. The people who run, advise, and invest a retirement plan owe demanding duties, and the exam tests whether you know who those fiduciaries are, what standard of care they owe, and what they are absolutely forbidden from doing. This subsection walks through the fiduciary rules (prudent investment choices, the qualified default investment alternative, and diversification), the investment policy statement that documents a prudent process, and the prohibited-transaction rules — all built on the foundational point that ERISA reaches private plans but not government plans."},
  {"id":667,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"#### What ERISA Covers — and What It Does Not\n\nERISA applies to **private-sector** employee benefit plans: corporate pension plans, profit-sharing plans, 401(k) plans, and similar arrangements sponsored by private employers. It does **not** apply to plans set up for government employees."},
  {"id":668,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"| Plan / arrangement | Covered by ERISA? |\n| --- | --- |\n| Corporate 401(k), profit-sharing, private pension | Yes |\n| Private employer defined benefit plan | Yes |\n| Federal, state, county, or municipal (public) employee plans — e.g., a state teachers' or city workers' plan, a 457(b) governmental plan | No |\n| Church plans (generally, unless they elect in) | No |\n| Individual IRAs the owner opens on their own | Not an ERISA plan (though the prohibited-transaction concepts have IRS parallels) |"},
  {"id":669,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"The exam loves the government-plan carve-out. If a question describes a pension for city firefighters, state university employees, or a public school district, ERISA's fiduciary provisions do **not** govern it — those plans answer to state and local law instead.\n\n**Exam tip:** The single most tested ERISA fact is the private-vs-government distinction. When you see \"municipal,\" \"state,\" \"county,\" \"public school,\" or \"federal\" employees, ERISA does **not** apply.\n\n**Common trap:** Candidates assume ERISA covers \"all retirement plans.\" It does not cover government plans, and a personal IRA is not itself an ERISA plan. Read the sponsor carefully."},
  {"id":670,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"#### Fiduciary Duties: The Prudent Expert Standard\n\nA **fiduciary** under ERISA is anyone who exercises discretionary authority or control over the management of the plan or its assets, or who renders investment advice for a fee. This sweeps in the plan sponsor's investment committee, trustees, and — importantly for you — an investment adviser hired to manage or advise on plan assets. Fiduciary status is based on function, not title: if you make decisions about the plan's money, you are a fiduciary.\n\nERISA imposes four core fiduciary duties:"},
  {"id":671,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"1. **Duty of loyalty (the \"exclusive benefit\" rule).** The fiduciary must act *solely* in the interest of plan participants and beneficiaries, for the exclusive purpose of providing benefits and defraying reasonable plan expenses. The fiduciary's own interests — and the employer's convenience — come second.\n2. **Duty of prudence (the \"prudent expert\" rule).** The fiduciary must act with the care, skill, prudence, and diligence that a prudent person \"familiar with such matters\" would use. That phrase — *familiar with such matters* — raises the bar above the ordinary \"prudent man\" standard to what is often called a **prudent expert** standard. It governs the *process*: it asks whether decision"},
  {"id":672,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"s were made carefully and with appropriate expertise, not whether every investment happened to earn a positive return.\n3. **Duty to diversify.** The fiduciary must diversify plan investments to minimize the risk of large losses, unless under the circumstances it is clearly prudent not to.\n4. **Duty to follow plan documents.** The fiduciary must operate the plan according to its governing documents, to the extent those documents are consistent with ERISA."},
  {"id":673,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"**Exam tip:** The prudent-expert standard is about **prudent process, not perfect results.** A fiduciary who followed a careful, well-documented process is not liable simply because an investment lost money. Conversely, a lucky gain does not excuse a reckless process.\n\n##### Diversification in more detail"},
  {"id":674,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"Diversification exists to reduce the chance of a catastrophic loss from concentration. A fiduciary who dumps most of the plan into a single stock or sector has breached the duty even if that bet happens to pay off. The classic red flag is over-concentration in **employer (company) stock**, which exposes participants to a double risk — if the company fails, they lose both their jobs and their retirement savings. ERISA allows certain individual-account plans (such as ESOPs and some profit-sharing plans) to hold employer securities, but a prudent fiduciary still watches concentration carefully.\n\n#### Participant-Directed Plans and ERISA Section 404(c)"},
  {"id":675,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"Many modern plans (like 401(k)s) let each participant choose how their own account is invested. **ERISA Section 404(c)** offers the plan fiduciaries relief from liability for the *investment results* of those participant-directed choices — but only if the plan gives participants genuine control. To qualify, the plan generally must:"},
  {"id":676,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"- Offer a **broad range of investment alternatives** — at least **three diversified options** with materially different risk-and-return characteristics.\n- Allow participants to change their allocations with reasonable frequency (at least **quarterly**, and more often for volatile investments).\n- Provide participants with sufficient information to make informed decisions.\n\nIf those conditions are met and a participant chooses poorly, the fiduciary is not liable for that participant's loss. The fiduciary remains responsible, however, for **prudently selecting and monitoring** the menu of options offered — 404(c) never excuses a bad lineup, only bad participant choices from a good lineup."},
  {"id":677,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"**Common trap:** 404(c) relief does not cover the *selection and monitoring* of the investment menu. The fiduciary is always on the hook for choosing prudent options and replacing ones that no longer belong.\n\n#### Qualified Default Investment Alternatives (QDIA)"},
  {"id":678,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"Participant-directed plans face a practical problem: what happens to the money of an auto-enrolled worker who never actually picks an investment? Parking their contributions in cash would be safe short-term but a poor long-term choice, and it would leave the fiduciary exposed. The **Qualified Default Investment Alternative (QDIA)** solves this. If the plan defaults a non-electing participant's money into a QDIA and meets the notice and transfer conditions, the fiduciary receives **404(c)-style relief** for that default investment — as if the participant had chosen it.\n\nTo be a QDIA, the default option generally must:"},
  {"id":679,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"- Be **diversified** to minimize the risk of large losses.\n- **Not invest primarily in employer securities.**\n- Be managed by a registered investment manager, or be a diversified investment company (mutual fund) or similar product.\n- Permit participants to **transfer out** to other plan options at least as often as other participants can move, with **no restrictions, fees, or penalties during the first 90 days**.\n- Be preceded by an **advance notice** to participants explaining the default and their right to opt out.\n\nAcceptable QDIA types typically include:"},
  {"id":680,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"| QDIA type | Nature |\n| --- | --- |\n| Target-date / lifecycle fund | Asset mix based on the participant's expected retirement date; grows more conservative over time |\n| Balanced fund | Blend of stocks and bonds set for the plan population as a whole |\n| Managed account | Professionally managed allocation tailored to the individual participant |\n| Capital-preservation product (money market / stable value) | Permitted **only as a short-term default**, generally limited to the first 120 days, not as a long-term QDIA |\n\n**Exam tip:** A **target-date fund is the textbook QDIA answer.** Its self-adjusting, diversified allocation is exactly what regulators intended for a default."},
  {"id":681,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"**Common trap:** A money market fund is **not** a valid long-term QDIA. It is permitted only for a short window (about 120 days). And a fund that invests primarily in employer stock can never be a QDIA.\n\n#### The Investment Policy Statement (IPS)\n\nAn **Investment Policy Statement (IPS)** is the written roadmap for how a plan's (or client's) assets will be managed. While ERISA does not literally command every plan to adopt one by statute, maintaining and following an IPS is treated as a hallmark of a **prudent process** — it is the primary evidence that fiduciaries made decisions deliberately rather than haphazardly.\n\nA well-constructed IPS typically documents:"},
  {"id":682,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"- **Objectives and risk tolerance** — the plan's return goals and how much risk it can bear.\n- **Asset allocation guidelines** — target allocations and permitted ranges among asset classes.\n- **Selection criteria** — the standards used to choose investment options or managers (e.g., track record, expenses, style consistency).\n- **Monitoring and review procedures** — how performance is measured against benchmarks and how often the menu is reviewed.\n- **Criteria for replacing** underperforming options and other governance procedures."},
  {"id":683,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"Because the fiduciary standard is process-based, an IPS is the fiduciary's best defense: it shows a reasoned framework was set in advance and followed consistently. Note that the IPS is a set of guidelines to be applied prudently — it is not a rigid contract, and fiduciaries must still exercise judgment as conditions change.\n\n**Exam tip:** If a question asks what document demonstrates a prudent, disciplined investment process for a plan (or an individual client), the answer is the **Investment Policy Statement.**\n\n#### Prohibited Transactions and Parties in Interest"},
  {"id":684,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"ERISA flatly bars fiduciaries from causing the plan to engage in certain self-dealing and conflict-of-interest transactions with a **\"party in interest\"** (called a \"disqualified person\" in the parallel IRS rules). A party in interest includes the people and entities close to the plan, such as:\n\n- Plan **fiduciaries**, trustees, and the plan's **service providers** (including the investment adviser, custodian, and accountant).\n- The **employer** (plan sponsor) and its employees, officers, and directors.\n- **Relatives** of the above and entities they substantially own.\n\nThe following dealings between the plan and a party in interest are **prohibited transactions**:"},
  {"id":685,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"| Prohibited transaction | Example |\n| --- | --- |\n| Sale, exchange, or lease of property | The employer sells its office building to the plan |\n| Lending money or extending credit | The plan loans money to the sponsoring company |\n| Furnishing goods, services, or facilities | A party in interest provides services to the plan for more than reasonable compensation |\n| Transferring plan assets to, or using them for the benefit of, a party in interest | Using plan cash to prop up the employer |\n| Acquiring or holding employer securities or real property beyond legal limits | Over-concentrating the plan in company stock |"},
  {"id":686,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"On top of those, ERISA specifically forbids fiduciary **self-dealing**. A fiduciary may not:\n\n- Deal with plan assets in the fiduciary's **own interest** or for the fiduciary's own account.\n- Act on behalf of a party whose interests are **adverse** to the plan or its participants.\n- Receive any **consideration (a kickback)** for their own account from a third party dealing with the plan.\n\n**Exam tip:** Think \"no self-dealing, no cozy insider deals.\" If the plan is transacting with the employer, a fiduciary, or an adviser to the plan — buying from them, lending to them, or paying them a kickback — it is almost certainly a prohibited transaction."},
  {"id":687,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"The rules are not absolute. The Department of Labor can grant **Prohibited Transaction Exemptions (PTEs)** — either individual or class exemptions — when a transaction is protective of and in the interest of participants (for example, exemptions allowing certain adviser compensation or the provision of routine services at reasonable cost). The default, however, is prohibition; the exemption is the exception."},
  {"id":688,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"**Common trap:** Fair terms do not cure a prohibited transaction. Even if the employer sells property to the plan at an honest market price, the sale between the plan and a party in interest is still prohibited unless a specific exemption applies. The prohibition targets the **relationship**, not just the price."},
  {"id":689,"part":"Part III","section":"G. ERISA Issues","anchor":"g-erisa-issues","text":"Putting it together for the exam: ERISA fiduciaries owe an exclusive-benefit duty of loyalty, a prudent-expert duty of care focused on process, and a duty to diversify. They can shift responsibility for participants' own investment choices through Section 404(c) and for defaulted participants through a proper QDIA (a target-date fund being the model example), they document their prudent process in an Investment Policy Statement, and they must scrupulously avoid prohibited transactions with parties in interest — all within the world of **private-sector** plans, never government plans."},
  {"id":690,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"Beyond ordinary individual and joint brokerage accounts, the Series 65 expects you to know a handful of *tax-advantaged* and *custodial* account types built for specific goals — mostly education and health care. For each, focus on the three things the exam repeatedly tests: (1) **who contributes and how much**, (2) **the tax treatment going in, growing, and coming out**, and (3) **who actually owns and controls the money**. Get those three right and most questions in this area answer themselves."},
  {"id":691,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"A recurring theme is the **\"after-tax in, tax-free out\"** structure shared by 529 plans, Coverdell ESAs, and Roth IRAs: you fund them with money that has already been taxed (no federal deduction), the account grows tax-deferred, and **qualified** withdrawals come out entirely tax-free. (The **HSA**, covered last, is the exception — its contributions are *tax-deductible* going in.) The traps almost always involve *non-qualified* withdrawals or confusion about *control* of the assets."},
  {"id":692,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"Note: several dollar figures below (the gift-tax annual exclusion, HSA limits, and kiddie-tax brackets) are **indexed to inflation and adjust annually**. The exam rewards knowing the *structure and the standard tested values*, not memorizing the current-year number to the dollar. Fixed figures (Coverdell's $2,000 limit and its income phase-outs) do **not** adjust and are fair game as exact numbers.\n\n#### 529 Plans (Qualified Tuition Programs)\n\nA **529 plan** is a state-sponsored, tax-advantaged education savings vehicle authorized under **Section 529 of the Internal Revenue Code**. There are two distinct kinds, and the exam wants you to tell them apart."},
  {"id":693,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"**1. College Savings Plan (the common type).** The account owner (usually a parent or grandparent) contributes after-tax dollars, which are invested in a menu of portfolios chosen by the plan — typically mutual funds or **age-based (target-date) portfolios** that automatically shift from equities toward fixed income as the beneficiary nears college age. The account value **fluctuates with the market**; there is investment risk and no guarantee. Funds can be used at virtually any eligible institution nationwide."},
  {"id":694,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"**2. Prepaid Tuition Plan.** The owner buys future tuition credits at **today's prices**, hedging against tuition inflation. These plans are usually limited to **in-state public colleges**, may be **state-guaranteed** (or backed by the participating institutions), and offer far less investment flexibility. Value is measured in tuition units rather than a market portfolio.\n\n**Key features common to both types:**"},
  {"id":695,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"- **Contributions:** After-tax (no federal deduction), but **many states offer a state income-tax deduction or credit** to residents who use their own state's plan. There are **no income limits** on who may contribute.\n- **High contribution ceilings:** Set by each state and very high — commonly an aggregate account balance cap of **$300,000+ per beneficiary**.\n- **Tax treatment:** Tax-deferred growth; **qualified withdrawals are federally tax-free**.\n- **Qualified expenses:** Tuition, fees, books, supplies, required equipment, and **room and board** (if enrolled at least half-time). Also included: up to **$10,000 per year** for **K–12 tuition**, and up to **$10,000 lifetime** for **student-loan repayment**.\n- **Control stays with the owner.** This is heavily tested — see the callout below. The owner may also **change the beneficiary** to another qualifying family member without penalty.\n- **Financial aid:** A parent-owned 529 is treated as the **parent's asset** on the FAFSA, which is favorable (assessed at a low rate) compared with a student-owned custodial account."},
  {"id":696,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"**Non-qualified withdrawals:** The **earnings** portion is taxed as **ordinary income to the recipient plus a 10% penalty**. (Only the earnings are hit — the original after-tax contributions come out tax- and penalty-free.) The penalty is waived in limited cases such as the beneficiary's death, disability, or receipt of a scholarship (up to the scholarship amount)."},
  {"id":697,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"**529-to-Roth IRA rollover (SECURE Act 2.0):** Leftover 529 funds may be rolled into the **beneficiary's Roth IRA**, subject to a **$35,000 lifetime cap**, provided the 529 has been open at least **15 years**. Each year's rollover is also limited to the annual IRA contribution limit. This provision softens the old \"what if my child doesn't go to college?\" objection."},
  {"id":698,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"> **Exam tip:** In a 529 plan, the **account owner — not the beneficiary — retains control** of the assets, can change the beneficiary, and can even reclaim the money (subject to tax and penalty on earnings). This is the single biggest contrast with UTMA/UGMA custodial accounts, where the assets **irrevocably belong to the minor**."},
  {"id":699,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"**Gift-tax treatment and 5-year \"superfunding.\"** A contribution to a 529 is treated as a **completed gift** to the beneficiary, so it qualifies for the annual gift-tax exclusion (a representative tested figure is **$19,000 per donor, per beneficiary, per year**; it is inflation-indexed). A special election unique to 529 plans lets a donor **front-load five years of exclusions in a single year** and treat it as if spread evenly over five years."},
  {"id":700,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"- Formula: **Maximum 5-year front-load = Annual Exclusion × 5**\n- Worked example: One grandparent contributes in a single year: **$19,000 × 5 = $95,000** free of gift tax. A married couple electing to **split the gift** can contribute **$95,000 × 2 = $190,000** at once for one beneficiary — with no gift tax and no use of the lifetime estate/gift exemption, as long as they make no further gifts to that beneficiary for five years."},
  {"id":701,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"> **Common trap:** Distractors will say 529 contributions are \"**federally tax-deductible**.\" They are **not** — contributions are made with after-tax dollars. The tax benefit is **tax-free growth and qualified withdrawals** (plus a possible *state* deduction), not a federal deduction.\n\n#### Coverdell Education Savings Account (ESA)\n\nA **Coverdell ESA** is another after-tax-in, tax-free-out education account, but it is much smaller and more restricted than a 529 — and, importantly, it is typically **self-directed**, meaning the owner can invest in almost any securities (individual stocks, bonds, funds) rather than a fixed plan menu."},
  {"id":702,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"**Key rules (memorize the fixed numbers — they do not inflation-adjust):**"},
  {"id":703,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"- **Contribution limit: $2,000 per year, per beneficiary** — an **aggregate** cap across *all* contributors, not $2,000 from each. If a parent and a grandparent both want to contribute, together they may put in no more than $2,000 for that child that year.\n- **Income limits on contributors (MAGI phase-outs):** **$95,000–$110,000 for single filers** and **$190,000–$220,000 for married filing jointly**. Above the top of the range, direct contributions are disallowed.\n- **Age restrictions:** Contributions must stop when the beneficiary turns **18** (special-needs beneficiaries excepted), and the funds must generally be used by age **30**. Any balance left at 30 is distributed (earnings taxed + 10% penalty) or **rolled to another family member's** Coverdell.\n- **Qualified expenses:** Both **higher education and K–12** (tuition plus a broad range of elementary/secondary costs) qualify.\n- **Tax treatment:** After-tax contributions, tax-deferred growth, tax-free qualified withdrawals; non-qualified withdrawal earnings are taxed plus a **10% penalty**."},
  {"id":704,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"> **Exam tip:** If a question stresses **wide investment choice / self-directed** for an education account, think **Coverdell**. If it stresses **very high contribution limits and no income cap**, think **529**.\n\n#### Contrasting 529 vs. Coverdell\n\nThis side-by-side comparison is among the most testable in the whole education-accounts topic."},
  {"id":705,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"| Feature | 529 Plan | Coverdell ESA |\n|---|---|---|\n| Annual contribution limit | No fixed annual cap; high aggregate balance limit (often $300,000+, state-set) | **$2,000/year per beneficiary** (aggregate across all contributors) |\n| Income limit on contributor | **None** | **Yes** — $95,000–$110,000 single; $190,000–$220,000 MFJ |\n| Investment control | Limited to plan's menu (age-based/portfolio options) | **Self-directed** — nearly any security |\n| Age restrictions | **None** — any age beneficiary; no use-by deadline | Contribute before **18**; use by **30** |\n| Qualified expenses | Higher ed; up to $10K/yr K–12 **tuition**; $10K lifetime student loans | Higher ed **and** broad K–12 expenses |\n| Tax treatment (federal) | After-tax in; tax-free qualified out | After-tax in; tax-free qualified out |\n| Who controls the assets | **Account owner** (can change beneficiary) | Responsible individual/owner (can change beneficiary) |\n| Special features | 5-year gift front-loading; 529→Roth rollover | Broad K–12 coverage; self-directed |"},
  {"id":706,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"> **Common trap:** Watch for a stem claiming you **cannot** hold both. You *can* contribute to a 529 **and** a Coverdell for the same beneficiary in the same year. Also watch the K–12 distinction: 529s cover K–12 **tuition** (up to $10,000/yr), while Coverdells cover a **broader set** of K–12 expenses.\n\n#### UTMA / UGMA Custodial Accounts\n\nCustodial accounts let an adult hold and manage assets on behalf of a **minor** who cannot legally hold securities in their own name. Two statutes govern them:"},
  {"id":707,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"- **UGMA — Uniform Gifts to Minors Act:** the older law; permits gifts of **financial assets** — cash, securities, and life-insurance contracts.\n- **UTMA — Uniform Transfers to Minors Act:** the broader, more common modern law; permits **UGMA assets plus other property** such as **real estate, art, patents, and royalties**, and often allows the custodian to hold assets until a **later age** (up to 21, or in some states 25).\n\n**How they work — and the ownership rule the exam loves:**"},
  {"id":708,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"- A **gift to a custodial account is an irrevocable, completed gift.** Once transferred, the assets **belong to the minor** — not to the custodian and not to the donor. The custodian merely manages them as a **fiduciary**, acting solely in the minor's best interest.\n- **Account registration:** Assets are held under the **minor's name and Social Security number**, so income is **taxed to the minor** (subject to the kiddie tax, below).\n- **One custodian, one minor** per account. The custodian may be the donor, a parent, or another responsible adult, but the roles cannot be split among several people, and one account cannot serve multiple minors.\n- **At the age of majority** (set by state law,"},
  {"id":709,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"commonly **18 or 21**), the account transfers outright to the (former) minor, who may then use the money for **any purpose whatsoever** — a key contrast with a 529, where the owner keeps control indefinitely.\n- **No contribution limits** (the gift-tax annual exclusion still applies), and **no restriction to education** — funds may be spent for the minor's benefit at any time.\n- **Death of the minor:** because the minor owns the assets, they pass to the **minor's estate**, not back to the donor."},
  {"id":710,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"> **Exam tip:** The custodian **cannot** use custodial assets for their own benefit, cannot pledge them for a personal loan, and generally should not buy on **margin, trade options, or sell short** — these are inconsistent with the prudent, conservative standard owed to a minor. The account is managed for the **minor's** benefit under a fiduciary duty."},
  {"id":711,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"**Kiddie tax.** To stop parents from shifting investment income to a low-bracket child, a child's **unearned (investment) income** above a threshold is taxed at the **parents' marginal rate**. The tested structure: a first slice of unearned income is tax-free (the child's standard deduction), the next slice is taxed at the **child's own rate**, and everything above that is taxed at the **parents' rate**."},
  {"id":712,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"- Illustrative (inflation-adjusted) figures: first **~$1,350** tax-free; next **~$1,350** at the child's rate; amounts over **~$2,700** at the **parents'** rate.\n- Worked example: A UTMA account generates **$3,000** of dividends and interest in a year. Roughly the first $1,350 is offset by the standard deduction, the next $1,350 is taxed at the child's (low) rate, and the remaining **$3,000 − $2,700 = $300** is taxed at the **parents' marginal rate**."},
  {"id":713,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"> **Common trap:** Answer choices often say the custodial account is owned by the **custodian** or the **donor**. It is **owned by the minor** — the donor cannot take the gift back, and the custodian cannot spend it on themselves. Contrast this squarely with the **529 owner's retained control**.\n\n#### Health Savings Account (HSA)\n\nAlthough not an education account, the HSA appears in this section as a **tax-advantaged special account**, and it carries the strongest tax benefit of all: a **triple tax advantage**."},
  {"id":714,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"- **Contributions are tax-deductible** (or made pre-tax through payroll), reducing current taxable income.\n- **Growth is tax-deferred** (invested balances compound tax-free).\n- **Qualified-medical withdrawals are tax-free.**\n\n**Eligibility.** To contribute, an individual must be covered by a **High-Deductible Health Plan (HDHP)**, must **not** be enrolled in **Medicare**, and must **not** be claimed as someone else's dependent. The HDHP must meet minimum-deductible and maximum-out-of-pocket thresholds set by the IRS (these adjust annually)."},
  {"id":715,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"**Contribution limits (indexed annually; representative tested values):** **$4,400 for self-only** coverage and **$8,750 for family** coverage, with an extra **$1,000 catch-up** for those **age 55+**. Unlike a Flexible Spending Account (FSA), **HSA balances roll over year to year** and are **portable** — the account belongs to the individual, not the employer, with no \"use it or lose it.\"\n\n- Worked example: A 57-year-old with **family** HDHP coverage may contribute **$8,750 + $1,000 = $9,750** for the year (using the representative figures above).\n\n**Withdrawals:**"},
  {"id":716,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"- **Qualified medical expenses:** always **tax-free**, at any age.\n- **Non-qualified withdrawals before age 65:** the amount is taxed as **ordinary income plus a 20% penalty** (note: **20%**, higher than the 10% on education and retirement accounts).\n- **After age 65:** non-medical withdrawals are taxed as **ordinary income with no penalty** — effectively functioning like a **Traditional IRA**, while medical withdrawals remain tax-free. This makes the HSA a powerful supplemental retirement vehicle."},
  {"id":717,"part":"Part III","section":"H. Special Types of Accounts","anchor":"h-special-types-of-accounts","text":"> **Exam tip:** Two HSA facts are disproportionately tested: (1) you must have an **HDHP** to contribute, and (2) the **penalty for non-qualified withdrawals is 20%** before age 65 — larger than the 10% penalty attached to 529s, Coverdells, and IRAs. After 65, the penalty disappears and only ordinary income tax applies to non-medical use.\n\n> **Common trap:** Don't confuse the **HSA** (paired with an HDHP, balances roll over, individually owned, investable) with an **FSA** (employer plan, \"use it or lose it,\" not investable). Only the HSA offers the triple tax advantage and long-term investment growth."},
  {"id":718,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"How an account is titled determines who controls the assets, who inherits them, whether they pass through probate, and how they are taxed at death. On the Series 65, this material appears as scenario questions (\"Two sisters own an account together and one dies — what happens to her share?\") and as comparison questions that hinge on a single distinguishing feature: right of survivorship, spousal-only status, creditor protection, or step-up in basis. Learn each form by its distinguishing feature and you can answer almost any question in this area.\n\n#### Forms of Property Ownership: An Overview"},
  {"id":719,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"Property held by more than one person is held in one of several \"concurrent ownership\" forms. The two questions the exam always cares about are:\n\n1. **Is there a right of survivorship?** Does a deceased owner's share pass automatically to the surviving owner(s), or does it fall into the deceased owner's estate?\n2. **Does the transfer avoid probate?** A right of survivorship generally means the asset passes automatically and bypasses probate.\n\nThe forms below are simply different ways those two questions get answered.\n\n#### Joint Tenants with Right of Survivorship (JTWROS)"},
  {"id":720,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"Under JTWROS, two or more people own the account together, each with an **equal, undivided interest** in the whole. The defining feature is the **right of survivorship**: when one owner dies, that person's share passes **automatically** to the surviving owner(s), **outside of probate**, regardless of what the deceased's will says.\n\n- Owners need **not** be married (a parent and child, or two business partners, may hold JTWROS).\n- Because shares are equal, a two-person JTWROS is always 50/50; a three-person JTWROS is one-third each.\n- Any owner can typically trade or withdraw the full account balance, so JTWROS requires mutual trust."},
  {"id":721,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"**Exam tip:** The phrase \"right of survivorship\" is your signal that the asset **avoids probate** and passes to the co-owner, not to heirs named in a will. A will cannot override survivorship.\n\n#### Tenants in Common (TIC)\n\nUnder Tenants in Common, two or more people own the account together, but there is **NO right of survivorship**, and ownership shares may be **unequal** (e.g., 70/30). When one owner dies, that person's fractional share passes to **their own estate** and is distributed by their will (or by state intestacy law) — meaning it **goes through probate**, not to the surviving co-owner."},
  {"id":722,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"- TIC is the common choice when co-owners want their share to go to their own heirs rather than to each other.\n- Because shares can be unequal, TIC also fits owners who contribute different amounts.\n\n**Common trap:** Students confuse JTWROS and TIC. Memorize the split: **JTWROS = equal shares + survivorship (avoids probate); TIC = can be unequal + no survivorship (share goes to the estate, through probate).**\n\n#### Tenancy by the Entirety (TBE)"},
  {"id":723,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"Tenancy by the Entirety is a special joint form available **only to married spouses** (and, in some states, only for real property). It includes a **right of survivorship**, so on the death of one spouse the property passes automatically to the survivor and avoids probate.\n\nTwo features distinguish TBE from ordinary JTWROS:\n\n- **Both spouses must consent** to sell, transfer, or encumber the property — one spouse acting alone cannot dispose of it.\n- **Creditor protection:** a creditor of only **one** spouse generally **cannot** attach or force the sale of TBE property, making TBE a modest asset-protection tool. (A joint creditor of both spouses can still reach it.)"},
  {"id":724,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"**Exam tip:** If a question stresses that neither spouse can act without the other, or that one spouse's creditors can't reach the property, the answer is **tenancy by the entirety**. TBE is **not** recognized in every state.\n\n#### Community Property (and CPWROS)\n\nIn the nine **community property states** — Arizona, California, Idaho, Louisiana, Nevada, New Mexico, Texas, Washington, and Wisconsin — most property **acquired during the marriage** is owned **50/50** by both spouses, regardless of who earned it. (Property owned before marriage, or received by gift or inheritance, is typically **separate property**.)"},
  {"id":725,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"Standard community property does **not** automatically include a right of survivorship; the deceased spouse's half passes by will and may go through probate. **Community Property with Right of Survivorship (CPWROS)** adds survivorship, so the deceased spouse's half passes automatically to the surviving spouse and **avoids probate**.\n\nThe heavily tested advantage of community property is the **full (double) step-up in cost basis** at the first spouse's death — see the worked example below.\n\n#### Cost Basis Step-Up at Death — Worked Example"},
  {"id":726,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"At death, inherited property generally receives a **step-up in basis** to its **fair market value (FMV) on the date of death**. How much of the property steps up differs by ownership form, and this is a favorite exam calculation.\n\nFormula (plain text):\n\n- New basis of survivor's holding = (stepped-up portion at FMV) + (any portion that keeps its original basis)\n\n**Facts:** A married couple owns stock with an original cost basis of **$100,000**. On the first spouse's death, the stock is worth **$500,000** (FMV). The survivor sells immediately."},
  {"id":727,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"**Case A — JTWROS in a common-law (non-community-property) state:** Only the **deceased spouse's half** steps up. Original basis was split 50/50 = $50,000 each.\n- Survivor's own half keeps its basis: **$50,000**\n- Deceased half steps up to FMV (half of $500,000): **$250,000**\n- New total basis = $50,000 + $250,000 = **$300,000**\n- Taxable gain if sold at $500,000 = $500,000 − $300,000 = **$200,000**\n\n**Case B — Community property (CPWROS):** The **entire** account receives a **full step-up** to FMV.\n- New total basis = **$500,000**\n- Taxable gain if sold at $500,000 = $500,000 − $500,000 = **$0**"},
  {"id":728,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"**Exam tip:** The **double step-up** is the key benefit of community property versus JTWROS. Same asset, same death: community property erased the entire $200,000 of built-in gain, while JTWROS stepped up only half.\n\n#### Comparison Table: Ownership Forms"},
  {"id":729,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"| Form | Who can hold it | Shares | Right of survivorship? | Probate on death? | Distinctive feature |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| JTWROS | Anyone (2+) | Equal | Yes | No — passes to co-owner | Any owner can act alone |\n| TIC | Anyone (2+) | May be unequal | No | Yes — passes to estate/heirs | Share goes to owner's own heirs |\n| TBE | Married spouses only | Equal | Yes | No — passes to spouse | Both must consent; creditor protection |\n| Community property (CPWROS) | Married spouses (9 states) | 50/50 | Only if \"WROS\" added | No if WROS | Full step-up in basis |\n\n#### Transfer-on-Death (TOD) / Pay-on-Death (POD)"},
  {"id":730,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"A **TOD** designation (used for **brokerage/securities accounts**) — and its bank-account equivalent, **POD** (\"pay on death\") — lets an owner name a beneficiary who receives the account **automatically at death, avoiding probate**.\n\n- During life, the owner keeps **complete control**: the beneficiary has **no rights, no access, and no ownership** while the owner is alive. The owner can spend it all, change the beneficiary, or revoke the designation.\n- TOD/POD is a simple, low-cost probate-avoidance tool that does **not** require a trust.\n- The designation controls who inherits — it **overrides the will** for that account."},
  {"id":731,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"**Common trap:** A TOD beneficiary is not a co-owner. Compare with JTWROS, where the co-owner has full ownership and access **during life**. With TOD, control stays 100% with the owner; the beneficiary only inherits later.\n\n#### Beneficiary Designations: Per Stirpes vs. Per Capita\n\nRetirement accounts (IRAs, 401(k)s), TOD/POD accounts, annuities, and life insurance pass by **beneficiary designation**, which **overrides the will**. A key testable detail is what happens when a named beneficiary **dies before** the account owner. That is governed by whether the designation is **per stirpes** or **per capita**."},
  {"id":732,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"- **Per stirpes** (\"by the branch/by the roots\"): a deceased beneficiary's share passes **down to that beneficiary's own descendants** (their children).\n- **Per capita** (\"by the head\"): the assets are divided **equally among the surviving named beneficiaries**; a deceased beneficiary's descendants get **nothing**.\n\n**Worked example.** An IRA owner names her **three children — A, B, and C — as equal beneficiaries (one-third each)**. Child **C dies before** the owner, leaving **two children** (grandchildren of the IRA owner)."},
  {"id":733,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"- **Per stirpes:** A = 1/3, B = 1/3, and **C's two children split C's 1/3**, so each grandchild gets **1/6**. C's branch is preserved.\n- **Per capita:** the account is split only among the **surviving named beneficiaries**, A and B, so **A = 1/2 and B = 1/2**, and C's children receive **nothing**.\n\n**Exam tip:** Remember **stirpes = descendants** (the deceased beneficiary's line inherits in their place). **Per capita = by head count of survivors**, so a predeceased beneficiary's children are cut out.\n\n#### Wills, Intestacy, and Probate"},
  {"id":734,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"A **will** is a legal document directing how a person's assets are distributed at death. It names an **executor** (personal representative) to carry out its terms and can name a **guardian** for minor children. Dying **without** a valid will is dying **intestate**; in that case, **state intestacy law** determines who inherits — the individual loses all say in the distribution.\n\n**Probate** is the court-supervised legal process that:\n\n1. Validates the will,\n2. Pays the deceased's debts and taxes, and\n3. Distributes the remaining assets to heirs."},
  {"id":735,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"Probate is generally **public, time-consuming, and costly**. That is why the probate-avoiding tools above — JTWROS/TBE survivorship, TOD/POD, beneficiary designations, and revocable trusts — are central to estate planning.\n\n**Exam tip:** Assets that pass by **survivorship, beneficiary designation, TOD/POD, or a funded living trust bypass probate**. Assets titled in the individual's name alone, or passing under a **will**, go **through** probate. A will controls only **probate** assets — it does **not** override survivorship or beneficiary designations.\n\n#### Trusts (Basic Concepts)"},
  {"id":736,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"A **trust** is a legal arrangement in which a **grantor** (also called *settlor* or *trustor*) transfers assets to a **trustee**, who holds and manages them for the benefit of one or more **beneficiaries** according to the trust document. The three parties to know are **grantor, trustee, and beneficiary** (one person can occupy more than one role).\n\nKey classifications:"},
  {"id":737,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"| Trust type | Can it be changed? | In the grantor's taxable estate? | Probate at death? | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Revocable (living) trust** | Yes — grantor can amend/revoke | **Yes** — still the grantor's assets | **No** — avoids probate | Grantor is usually trustee; primary purpose is probate avoidance and incapacity planning, **not** tax savings |\n| **Irrevocable trust** | No — generally cannot be changed | Generally **No** — removed from estate | No | Used for estate-tax reduction and **asset protection**; grantor gives up control |\n| **Testamentary trust** | Created by the will | Yes | **Yes** — created *through* probate | Comes into existence only at death, via the will |\n| **Living / inter vivos trust** | Created during life | Depends (revocable vs. irrevocable) | No | Any trust established while the grantor is alive |"},
  {"id":738,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"**Exam tips:**\n- A **revocable living trust avoids probate but does NOT reduce estate taxes** — the grantor still controls the assets, so they remain in the estate. Students often wrongly assume a revocable trust saves estate tax; it doesn't.\n- An **irrevocable trust** trades away control in exchange for removing assets from the estate and providing creditor/asset protection.\n- A **testamentary trust is created by the will**, so it **does not avoid probate** — it is born out of the probate process.\n\n#### Qualified Domestic Relations Orders (QDROs)"},
  {"id":739,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"A **QDRO** is a court order, issued in a **divorce or legal separation**, that divides the benefits of an **ERISA-qualified employer retirement plan** (such as a **401(k) or pension**) between the plan participant and an **alternate payee** (typically a former spouse, but it may also be a child or other dependent).\n\nCritical, frequently tested points:"},
  {"id":740,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"- A QDRO applies to **employer-sponsored qualified plans**, **not IRAs**. Dividing an IRA in a divorce is done through a \"**transfer incident to divorce**,\" which is not a QDRO.\n- When funds are paid to a **former spouse** under a QDRO, the **former spouse (alternate payee) is responsible for the income tax** on distributions they take — the tax burden shifts to the recipient.\n- A distribution made to an alternate payee under a QDRO is **exempt from the 10% early-withdrawal penalty**, even if the payee is under age 59½. This QDRO exception is a classic exam point."},
  {"id":741,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"**Exam tip:** If a question involves splitting a **401(k) or pension in a divorce**, the answer is **QDRO** — and remember the **10% penalty does not apply** to the QDRO distribution.\n\n**Common trap:** Do not apply QDRO rules to an **IRA** — IRAs are divided by a transfer incident to divorce, not a QDRO.\n\n#### Donor-Advised Funds (DAFs)"},
  {"id":742,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"A **donor-advised fund** is a charitable-giving vehicle sponsored by a public charity (often the charitable arm of a brokerage or fund company). The donor makes an **irrevocable contribution** to an account within the fund, receives an **immediate income-tax charitable deduction** in the year of the gift, and then retains **advisory privileges** — the donor can recommend how the assets are invested and which qualified charities receive grants, and when, over future years.\n\nWhy DAFs are attractive and how they're tested:"},
  {"id":743,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"- **Immediate deduction, deferred giving:** the donor deducts in the contribution year but can spread grants to charities across many later years.\n- **Appreciated securities:** contributing long-term appreciated stock lets the donor **avoid capital gains tax** on the appreciation and generally **deduct the full fair market value** — a common exam advantage.\n- **No annual payout requirement:** unlike a **private foundation** (which must distribute roughly 5% of assets annually), a DAF has **no mandated minimum annual distribution**.\n- **Lower cost and administrative burden** than establishing a private foundation, plus **anonymity** if the donor wants it."},
  {"id":744,"part":"Part III","section":"I. Ownership and Estate Planning Techniques","anchor":"i-ownership-and-estate-planning-techniques","text":"**Exam tip:** Key DAF facts are (1) the contribution is **irrevocable**, (2) the donor gets the **deduction now** but keeps only **advisory (not legal) control** over grants, and (3) it is **simpler and cheaper than a private foundation**, with **no 5% annual payout rule**."},
  {"id":745,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"Once an investment adviser has recommended a security or strategy, the trade has to be routed through the market and executed. The Series 65 expects you to understand the mechanics of how orders are priced, the different order types, the accounts trades settle in, who the players in the execution chain are, and what a trade costs. You are not expected to be a trading-desk professional, but you must know the vocabulary cold and be able to reason about the conflicts of interest and the fiduciary duty of best execution.\n\n#### The Quote: Bids, Offers/Asks, and the Spread"},
  {"id":746,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"Every actively traded security has a two-sided quote. Learn these definitions from the perspective of the dealer or market maker who is publishing the quote — this is the single most common source of confusion on the exam.\n\n- **Bid** — the price at which the dealer is willing to **buy** the security. Therefore the bid is the price at which the **public investor sells**.\n- **Offer** (also called the **ask**) — the price at which the dealer is willing to **sell** the security. Therefore the offer/ask is the price at which the **public investor buys**.\n\nThe bid is always lower than the ask. The difference between them is the **spread**.\n\nSpread = Ask − Bid"},
  {"id":747,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"**Worked example.** A market maker quotes ABC stock \"20.00 – 20.25.\" An investor who wants to *sell* receives the bid, 20.00. An investor who wants to *buy* pays the ask, 20.25. The spread is 20.25 − 20.00 = 0.25 (25 cents per share). If the investor bought at 20.25 and immediately sold at 20.00, the round-trip cost from the spread alone would be 0.25 per share.\n\n**Exam tip:** Remember the mnemonic \"buyer pays the ask, seller hits the bid.\" When a question says a customer \"buys at the market,\" they pay the **ask**; when they \"sell at the market,\" they receive the **bid**."},
  {"id":748,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"**Common trap:** Students reverse the definitions because they think of \"bid\" from their own point of view (as if *they* are bidding to buy). On the exam, the quote is always from the **dealer's** side. The dealer bids to buy from you and offers to sell to you.\n\nA **narrow** spread signals a liquid, actively traded security; a **wide** spread signals an illiquid security (thinly traded stock, some municipal or corporate bonds) and a higher implicit cost of trading.\n\n#### Types of Orders\n\nAn order tells the broker-dealer how and when to fill the trade. The four you must know are market, limit, stop, and stop-limit."},
  {"id":749,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"| Order type | What it guarantees | What it does NOT guarantee | Typical use |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Market | Immediate execution | Price | Get in/out right now |\n| Limit | Price (or better) | Execution | Control the price paid/received |\n| Stop | Triggers, then becomes a market order | Price after trigger | Protect a position / limit a loss |\n| Stop-limit | Triggers, then becomes a limit order | Execution after trigger | Trigger + price control |\n\n**Market order.** Executes immediately at the best available price. It guarantees that the trade *will* happen but not at what price. In a fast-moving or illiquid market the fill can be worse than the last quote."},
  {"id":750,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"**Limit order.** Sets the worst acceptable price. A **buy limit** executes at the limit price *or lower*; a **sell limit** executes at the limit price *or higher*. It guarantees the price will be as good as (or better than) specified but does not guarantee the order fills at all — the market may never reach the limit.\n\n- Example: A buy limit at 50 fills at 50 or less. A sell limit at 50 fills at 50 or more.\n\n**Stop order (stop-loss).** A resting order that is dormant until the market trades at or through the **stop (trigger) price**. Once triggered, it becomes a **market order** and executes at the next available price."},
  {"id":751,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"- A **sell stop** is placed *below* the current market. It protects a long position (or an unrealized gain) by getting the investor out if the price falls to the stop.\n- A **buy stop** is placed *above* the current market. It is most often used to protect a **short seller**, limiting the loss if the price rises.\n\n**Stop-limit order.** Identical trigger mechanics, but once the stop is hit the order becomes a **limit order** rather than a market order. This adds price protection but reintroduces the risk of non-execution — if the price gaps past the limit, the order may not fill."},
  {"id":752,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** The key contrast is \"limit guarantees price but not execution; market guarantees execution but not price.\" A stop *becomes* a market order when triggered; a stop-limit *becomes* a limit order when triggered.\n\n**Common trap:** A \"sell stop\" is placed **below** the market and a \"buy stop\" **above** the market — the opposite of where beginners expect. Also, a stop order is *not* the same as a limit order: the stop price is a *trigger*, not the execution price.\n\n#### Short Sales"},
  {"id":753,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"A **short sale** is the sale of a security the investor does not own. The mechanics: the investor **borrows** the shares (through the broker-dealer), sells them at the current price, and hopes to **buy them back later at a lower price** to return to the lender, keeping the difference. It is a bet that the price will **fall**.\n\nKey facts tested:"},
  {"id":754,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"- **Unlimited loss potential.** A stock's price can rise without limit, so the short seller's potential loss is theoretically infinite (unlike a long buyer, whose maximum loss is the amount invested).\n- **Must be done in a margin account.** Short selling requires borrowing securities and posting collateral, so it cannot be done in a cash account.\n- **Locate/borrow requirement.** Under Regulation SHO the broker-dealer must reasonably locate and arrange to borrow the shares before executing the short sale (this prevents \"naked\" short selling).\n- The short seller is responsible for any **dividends** paid on the borrowed stock while the position is open."},
  {"id":755,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** Contrast the risk profiles — a long position has *limited* loss (price can only fall to zero) and *unlimited* gain; a short position has *limited* gain (price can only fall to zero) and *unlimited* loss. Short selling is generally suitable only for sophisticated, risk-tolerant clients.\n\n#### Cash Accounts vs. Margin Accounts\n\nThe account type determines how a purchase is funded."},
  {"id":756,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"| Feature | Cash account | Margin account |\n|---|---|---|\n| Payment | Pay in full for all purchases | Borrow part of the purchase price from the BD |\n| Leverage | None | Yes (amplifies gains and losses) |\n| Short selling | Not permitted | Permitted |\n| Governing rule | — | Reg T initial requirement (currently 50%) |\n| Documents | Standard new-account form | Also requires a margin (credit) agreement and hypothecation agreement |\n\n**Cash account.** The customer pays the full purchase price. This is the default account and the only type suitable for many retail clients, retirement accounts, and fiduciary accounts."},
  {"id":757,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"**Margin account.** The customer deposits a portion of the price and **borrows the remainder** from the broker-dealer, pledging the securities as collateral. Margin creates **leverage**, magnifying both gains and losses, and the customer pays **interest** on the borrowed (debit) balance."},
  {"id":758,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"- The Federal Reserve's **Regulation T** sets the **initial margin** requirement — currently **50%** of the purchase price for marginable equity securities. (Reg T is a Federal Reserve rule; you need to know the 50% figure, not perform detailed margin math on the Series 65.)\n- A margin account exposes the client to a **margin call** — a demand to deposit additional funds if the account's equity falls below the maintenance level.\n\n**Exam tip:** Margin and short selling are frequently flagged as **unsuitable** for conservative clients, retirees living on the account, or fiduciary/trust accounts because of the leverage and open-ended loss potential."},
  {"id":759,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"#### Principal vs. Agency Trades, and the Costs of Trading\n\nHow a broker-dealer participates in a trade determines how it is compensated. This is one of the most heavily tested ideas in the trading section.\n\n**Agency (brokerage) trade.** The firm acts as a **broker** — an agent — matching the customer with the other side of the trade. It does not take ownership of the security. Its compensation is a **commission**, which is disclosed as a separate line item on the confirmation."},
  {"id":760,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"**Principal (dealer) trade.** The firm acts as a **dealer** — a principal — trading **from its own inventory**. When it sells to the customer out of inventory it adds a **markup**; when it buys from the customer into inventory it subtracts a **markdown**. The markup/markdown is built into the net price the customer pays or receives.\n\n| | Agency trade | Principal trade |\n|---|---|---|\n| Firm's role | Broker (agent) | Dealer (principal) |\n| Source of security | Another party | Firm's own inventory |\n| Compensation | Commission | Markup / markdown |\n| How shown on confirmation | Separately stated | Embedded in the net price |\n\n**The three costs of trading:**"},
  {"id":761,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"1. **Commission** — the agency charge, disclosed separately.\n2. **Markup / markdown** — the principal charge, embedded in the price.\n3. **Spread** — the difference between bid and ask; when a market maker fills a customer as principal, the spread is effectively the firm's revenue and the customer's implicit cost.\n\n**FINRA 5% Policy.** As a guideline, FINRA expects markups, markdowns, and commissions to be **fair and reasonable**. The \"5%\" is a **guideline, not a hard rule** — the reasonableness of a charge depends on the security's price, availability, the amount of work involved, and other factors. A charge above 5% can be fair, and one below 5% can be excessive."},
  {"id":762,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"**Worked markup example.** A dealer buys a security into its inventory at 20.00 and shortly after sells it to a customer as principal at 21.00. Because the dealer's contemporaneous cost (20.00) establishes the prevailing market price, the markup is:\n\nMarkup % = (Customer Price − Prevailing Market Price) / Prevailing Market Price × 100\nMarkup % = (21.00 − 20.00) / 20.00 × 100 = 5.0%\n\nA markup at 5% sits right at the FINRA guideline and would draw scrutiny; whether it is actually fair still depends on the security and the circumstances."},
  {"id":763,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"**Common trap:** A markup is **not** a commission. If the firm acts as **principal**, it charges a markup/markdown (buried in the price); if it acts as **agent**, it charges a commission (shown separately). The exam often gives you a fact pattern (from inventory vs. matching a buyer and seller) and asks which form of compensation applies.\n\n#### Market Participants: Introducing BDs, Clearing BDs/Custodians, Market Makers, and Exchanges\n\nAn order passes through several firms before it settles. Know each role and, crucially, **which firm holds the customer's cash and securities**."},
  {"id":764,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"- **Introducing broker-dealer.** The firm with the **customer relationship**. It opens accounts, takes orders, and makes recommendations, but it typically **does not hold customer funds or securities** — it \"introduces\" the business to a clearing firm. Introducing firms operate this way to avoid the capital and operational burden of custody.\n- **Clearing broker-dealer / custodian.** The firm that **carries the account**: it holds (has custody of) the customer's cash and securities, executes the clearance and **settlement** of trades, sends confirmations and statements, and handles margin lending. In advisory relationships, a **qualified custodian** (often a clearing broker-dealer or a bank)"},
  {"id":765,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"is the entity that physically holds client assets — a critical concept tied to the custody rule.\n- **Market maker.** A **dealer** that stands ready to buy and sell a particular security by continuously publishing a **two-sided quote** (bid and ask). Market makers provide **liquidity** and trade as **principal**, earning the **spread** (plus any markup/markdown). Nasdaq is a **dealer/quote-driven** market built around competing market makers.\n- **Exchange.** A centralized, regulated **marketplace** that brings buyers and sellers together (e.g., the NYSE, an auction/order-driven market). Exchanges provide price transparency, standardized rules, and a mechanism for continuous trading."},
  {"id":766,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** If a question hinges on \"who holds the customer's securities and settles the trade,\" the answer is the **clearing/carrying firm (custodian)**, not the introducing firm. This distinction also matters for the **custody rule** under state and federal adviser regulation: an adviser that uses an independent **qualified custodian** to hold client assets faces lighter requirements than one deemed to have custody itself.\n\n#### Payment for Order Flow"},
  {"id":767,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"**Payment for order flow (PFOF)** is compensation a broker-dealer receives for **routing its customers' orders to a particular market maker or trading venue** for execution. The market maker pays the broker for the order because it profits from executing the flow (chiefly from the spread).\n\nWhy the exam cares:"},
  {"id":768,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"- **Conflict of interest.** PFOF creates an incentive to route orders to whoever pays the broker the most, rather than to the venue offering the **best execution** for the customer. The two can conflict.\n- **Disclosure requirement.** PFOF must be **disclosed** to customers, and firms must periodically disclose their order-routing practices. Receiving PFOF does **not** relieve the firm of its **best-execution** obligation.\n\n**Common trap:** PFOF is not inherently prohibited, and it does not by itself mean the customer got a bad price. The tested point is that it is a **disclosed conflict of interest** that must be managed so it does not compromise best execution.\n\n#### Best Execution"},
  {"id":769,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"**Best execution** is the obligation to seek the **most favorable terms reasonably available** for a customer's order. It is a duty owed both by broker-dealers executing orders and, as a component of the **fiduciary duty**, by investment advisers who direct client trades.\n\n\"Best execution\" is broader than simply the lowest quoted price. Firms must regularly and rigorously evaluate factors such as:\n\n- the **price** obtained (net of costs),\n- the **speed** and likelihood of execution and settlement,\n- the **size** of the order and available liquidity,\n- **transaction costs** (commissions, markups, fees), and\n- the **overall value** delivered to the client."},
  {"id":770,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"For an **investment adviser**, best execution is inseparable from the **duty of loyalty and care**. When an adviser has **discretion** to select broker-dealers, it must periodically assess the quality of executions and cannot let its own interests — for example, **soft-dollar** arrangements or directed-brokerage benefits — override the client's interest in favorable execution. The adviser must be able to **document** that its broker-selection process seeks best execution over time."},
  {"id":771,"part":"Part III","section":"J. Trading Securities","anchor":"j-trading-securities","text":"**Exam tip:** Best execution ≠ lowest commission. An adviser that routes trades to a broker offering research (soft dollars) or that receives PFOF still owes best execution and must show that the *total* result serves the client's interest. Failing to periodically review execution quality is a compliance and fiduciary breach.\n\n**Common trap:** Best execution is an ongoing, **process-based** obligation evaluated across many trades — not a guarantee that every single order got the single best possible price. The exam rewards recognizing it as a **fiduciary/regulatory duty** rooted in acting in the client's best interest."},
  {"id":772,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"Once an adviser builds a portfolio, both the adviser and the client need a way to answer a simple-sounding question: \"How did we do?\" The Series 65 expects you to know that there is no single answer — a return can be measured many ways, and each is designed to isolate a different thing: the security's income, the manager's skill, the investor's actual dollar experience, the effect of taxes, the effect of inflation, or the amount of risk taken to earn the return. This subsection walks through each measure, gives the formulas you must know cold, and flags the comparisons the exam loves to test."},
  {"id":773,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"Keep one distinction in mind throughout: a **return** describes what actually happened over a period, while a **yield** describes an ongoing income rate at a point in time. The exam frequently pairs a return figure with a \"which is larger?\" or \"which measure is appropriate here?\" question rather than asking for a raw calculation.\n\n#### Total Return and Holding-Period Return (HPR)\n\n**Total return** captures *everything* an investment earned: income (dividends or interest) plus capital appreciation (or minus depreciation). It answers \"what was my total gain relative to what I put in?\"\n\nTotal Return = (Income + (Ending Value − Beginning Value)) / Beginning Value"},
  {"id":774,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"The **holding-period return (HPR)** applies that same idea over the entire time the investor actually held the position — whether one month, one year, or five years. The formula is identical:\n\nHolding-Period Return = (Income Received + (Ending Value − Beginning Value)) / Beginning Value\n\n**Worked example.** An investor buys a stock for $50, holds it for one year, collects a $2 dividend, and sells it for $55.\n\n- Income = $2\n- Capital gain = $55 − $50 = $5\n- HPR = ($2 + $5) / $50 = $7 / $50 = **14%**"},
  {"id":775,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"HPR blends income and price change into one number. Had the same stock paid no dividend and simply risen to $55, the return would be only $5 / $50 = 10% — the $2 dividend is what lifts the total to 14%.\n\n**Exam tip:** When a question gives you a purchase price, a sale price, *and* a dividend or coupon, it is almost always testing total/holding-period return — you must include the income, not just the price change. Candidates who compute only the capital gain get the trap answer."},
  {"id":776,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"**Common trap:** HPR is *not* annualized. A 14% holding-period return earned over three years is very different from 14% per year. To compare investments held for different lengths of time, you must annualize (next topic).\n\n#### Current Yield\n\n**Current yield** is not a total return — it measures only the income a security throws off *right now* relative to its current market price. It ignores capital gains, capital losses, and a bond's pull to par at maturity.\n\nCurrent Yield = Annual Income / Current Market Price\n\nFor a bond, \"annual income\" is the annual coupon in dollars; for a stock, it is the annual dividend."},
  {"id":777,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"**Worked example (bond).** A bond has a 6% coupon (paying $60 per year on its $1,000 par value) and currently trades at $900.\n\nCurrent Yield = $60 / $900 = **6.67%**\n\n**Worked example (stock).** A stock pays a $2.40 annual dividend and trades at $60.\n\nCurrent Yield = $2.40 / $60 = **4.0%**\n\nThe bond example reveals a relationship the exam tests constantly. Because the bond trades at a *discount* ($900 < $1,000 par), its current yield (6.67%) is higher than its coupon rate (6%). The full ranking for a discount bond is:\n\n**Nominal (coupon) yield < Current yield < Yield to maturity (YTM) < Yield to call (YTC)**\n\nFor a *premium* bond (price above par), the ranking reverses:"},
  {"id":778,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"**Nominal (coupon) yield > Current yield > Yield to maturity > Yield to call**\n\n**Exam tip:** Memorize these two \"yield ladders.\" A common question shows a premium bond and asks you to rank the four yields — for a premium bond the coupon is always highest and YTC lowest, because calling a premium bond forces the premium to be recovered faster and returns less than holding to maturity.\n\n**Common trap:** Current yield uses *current market price*, never par value and never original cost. Dividing the coupon by $1,000 par just gives you the coupon rate back, not the current yield.\n\n#### Annualized Return"},
  {"id":779,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"An **annualized return** restates a return as an equivalent per-year rate so that investments held for different periods can be compared on equal footing. On a compounded basis:\n\nAnnualized Return = (1 + Holding-Period Return)^(1 / n) − 1, where n = number of years\n\n**Worked example.** The 14% holding-period return above was earned over exactly one year, so it is already annualized at 14%. But suppose a different position produced a 33.1% total return over three years:\n\nAnnualized Return = (1 + 0.331)^(1/3) − 1 = (1.331)^(0.333) − 1 = 1.10 − 1 = **10% per year**"},
  {"id":780,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"For sub-annual periods you scale up: a 3% return over six months compounds to (1.03)^2 − 1 = 6.09% annualized (or roughly 6% using simple annualization).\n\n**Exam tip:** The point tested is usually conceptual — you cannot directly compare a 20% return earned over four years with a 12% return earned over one year until both are put on an annual basis. Annualizing makes the comparison apples-to-apples.\n\n#### Time-Weighted vs. Dollar-Weighted Return\n\nThis is one of the most heavily tested distinctions in the entire performance section. Both measure return, but they answer different questions."},
  {"id":781,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"- **Time-weighted return (TWR)** measures the performance of the *portfolio/manager* by removing the distorting effect of client cash flows (deposits and withdrawals). It breaks the total period into sub-periods at each cash flow, computes each sub-period return, and links them geometrically: TWR = (1 + r1) × (1 + r2) × ... − 1. Because a manager does not control *when* a client adds or pulls money, TWR is the fair way to judge manager skill. This is why the GIPS standards and mutual fund performance reporting use time-weighted returns."},
  {"id":782,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"- **Dollar-weighted return (DWR)** measures the *investor's actual experience* in dollar terms. It is the internal rate of return (IRR) of the portfolio's cash flows, so it is sensitive to *how much* was invested and *when*. If a client adds a large sum right before a downturn, the DWR is dragged down even if the manager performed well.\n\n**Worked example illustrating the difference.**\n\n- Year 1: The account starts at $10,000 and grows to $12,000 (a +20% year).\n- End of Year 1: The client contributes an additional $12,000, bringing the balance to $24,000.\n- Year 2: The market falls and the account declines to $21,600 (a −10% year, since $24,000 × 0.90 = $21,600)."},
  {"id":783,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"*Time-weighted return* links the two annual returns: (1.20) × (0.90) − 1 = 1.08 − 1 = **+8% cumulative** (about +3.9% annualized). The manager earned a positive result.\n\n*Dollar-weighted return* solves for the single rate that equates all cash flows (−$10,000 at the start, −$12,000 at the end of Year 1, +$21,600 at the end): the IRR works out to roughly **−1.3% per year**. The client's *dollar* result was negative because most of the money was invested right before the losing year."},
  {"id":784,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"| Feature | Time-Weighted Return | Dollar-Weighted Return |\n|---|---|---|\n| What it measures | Manager/portfolio performance | Investor's actual dollar experience |\n| Effect of cash-flow timing | Removed (neutralized) | Included (drives the result) |\n| Equivalent to | Geometric linking of sub-period returns | Internal rate of return (IRR) |\n| Best used to | Compare managers / funds | Judge whether *this investor* did well |\n| Required by GIPS / fund reporting | Yes | No |"},
  {"id":785,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"**Exam tip:** Remember the mnemonic — **Time-weighted = Talent** (measures the manager) and **Dollar-weighted = Dollars/Decisions** (measures the investor's contribution timing). If a question asks which method fairly evaluates a portfolio manager whose clients make large, irregular deposits, the answer is time-weighted.\n\n**Common trap:** Dollar-weighted return and IRR are the *same thing*; do not treat them as two separate measures on the exam.\n\n#### Internal Rate of Return (IRR)"},
  {"id":786,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"The **internal rate of return** is the discount rate that makes the net present value (NPV) of all of an investment's cash flows equal to zero — the single interest rate at which the present value of everything you put in equals the present value of everything you take out. As noted above, IRR *is* the dollar-weighted return.\n\n**Worked example.** An investor puts in $1,000 today and receives $1,210 two years later with no interim cash flows.\n\n$1,000 × (1 + r)^2 = $1,210\n(1 + r)^2 = 1.21\n1 + r = 1.10\nr = **10% (the IRR)**\n\nTwo applications the exam expects:"},
  {"id":787,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"- **A bond's yield to maturity (YTM) is its IRR** — the single rate that discounts all future coupons and the par repayment back to the bond's current price. YTM assumes coupons are reinvested at the YTM, an assumption that rarely holds perfectly (a commonly tested limitation).\n- **IRR assumes reinvestment of all interim cash flows at the IRR itself.** This reinvestment assumption is the classic criticism of IRR on the exam.\n\n**Exam tip:** If a question describes \"the rate that sets the present value of cash inflows equal to the present value of cash outflows,\" it is defining IRR — and, by extension, the dollar-weighted return.\n\n#### Expected Return"},
  {"id":788,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"**Expected return** is a forward-looking, probability-weighted average of an investment's possible outcomes. It is the number you *anticipate* earning, not a historical result.\n\nExpected Return = Sum of (Probability of each outcome × Return in that outcome)\n\n**Worked example.** An analyst estimates three scenarios for a stock:\n\n| Scenario | Probability | Return |\n|---|---|---|\n| Strong economy | 30% | +20% |\n| Normal economy | 40% | +10% |\n| Weak economy | 30% | −5% |\n\nExpected Return = (0.30 × 20%) + (0.40 × 10%) + (0.30 × −5%) = 6% + 4% − 1.5% = **8.5%**"},
  {"id":789,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"For a *portfolio*, the expected return is the weighted average of the expected returns of its holdings, weighted by each holding's percentage of the portfolio.\n\n**Exam tip:** Expected return is an input to risk models like CAPM and is contrasted with *actual* (realized) return. The difference between an investment's actual return and the return CAPM said to expect (given its beta) is its **alpha** (see below).\n\n#### Risk-Adjusted Return\n\nTwo portfolios can post the same 10% return, but if one took far more risk to get there, its *risk-adjusted* return is inferior. Risk-adjusted measures relate return to the amount of risk taken. Three appear on the Series 65:"},
  {"id":790,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"| Measure | Risk used | Formula (conceptual) | Reward per unit of… |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Sharpe ratio** | Total risk (standard deviation) | (Portfolio Return − Risk-Free Rate) / Standard Deviation | Total volatility |\n| **Treynor ratio** | Systematic risk (beta) | (Portfolio Return − Risk-Free Rate) / Beta | Market risk |\n| **Alpha (Jensen's)** | Systematic risk (beta) | Actual Return − CAPM Expected Return | Skill above/below the market |\n\nThe \"excess return\" numerator in Sharpe and Treynor is the return *above the risk-free rate* (commonly proxied by the 90-day Treasury bill)."},
  {"id":791,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"**Worked example (Sharpe ratio).** A portfolio returns 10%, the risk-free rate is 2%, and the portfolio's standard deviation is 12%.\n\nSharpe Ratio = (10% − 2%) / 12% = 8 / 12 = **0.67**\n\nA higher Sharpe ratio is better — more return earned per unit of total risk. Sharpe is the most commonly tested of the three.\n\n**Alpha** measures value added by the manager: a *positive* alpha means the portfolio beat the return its risk level (beta) predicted, and a *negative* alpha means it underperformed for the risk taken. Alpha is often described as the manager's contribution or \"skill.\""},
  {"id":792,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"**Exam tip:** Know the risk each ratio uses. **Sharpe uses standard deviation (total risk); Treynor and alpha use beta (systematic risk).** Use Sharpe when a portfolio is *not* well diversified (total risk matters); use Treynor/alpha when the portfolio is well diversified (only market risk remains).\n\n**Common trap:** Do not confuse standard deviation with beta. Standard deviation measures *total* variability of returns; beta measures only *systematic* (market-related) risk relative to a benchmark.\n\n#### Inflation-Adjusted (Real) Return"},
  {"id":793,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"The **real return** strips out inflation to reveal the true increase in purchasing power. A 6% return when prices are rising 4% leaves an investor only slightly better off in what they can actually buy.\n\nApproximate Real Return = Nominal Return − Inflation Rate\n\nThe exact (Fisher) formula compounds them:\n\nReal Return = ((1 + Nominal Return) / (1 + Inflation Rate)) − 1\n\n**Worked example.** A portfolio earns a nominal 8% while inflation runs 3%.\n\n- Approximate: 8% − 3% = **5%**\n- Exact: (1.08 / 1.03) − 1 = 1.0485 − 1 = **4.85%**\n\nFor exam purposes, the approximation (subtract inflation) is usually sufficient unless the question specifically asks for the precise figure."},
  {"id":794,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"**Exam tip:** Real return is central to retirement and purchasing-power discussions. Fixed-income investors face **purchasing-power (inflation) risk** because a fixed coupon buys less over time — a key reason TIPS and equities are recommended for long-horizon, inflation-sensitive goals.\n\n#### After-Tax Return\n\nThe **after-tax return** is what the investor actually keeps once federal (and, where relevant, state) taxes are paid. It is what matters most to a taxable client.\n\nAfter-Tax Return = Pretax Return × (1 − Tax Rate)\n\n**Worked example.** An investment produces a 10% pretax return, and the applicable tax rate is 25%.\n\nAfter-Tax Return = 10% × (1 − 0.25) = 10% × 0.75 = **7.5%**"},
  {"id":795,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"A closely related, frequently tested calculation is the **tax-equivalent yield**, used to compare a tax-free municipal bond with a taxable bond. It grosses the muni yield *up* to the taxable yield a client would need in order to earn the same after-tax income:\n\nTax-Equivalent Yield = Tax-Free (Municipal) Yield / (1 − Tax Rate)\n\n**Worked example.** A municipal bond yields 4% and the investor is in the 32% federal bracket.\n\nTax-Equivalent Yield = 4% / (1 − 0.32) = 4% / 0.68 = **5.88%**\n\nSo this investor should prefer the 4% muni over any comparable taxable bond yielding *less than* 5.88%."},
  {"id":796,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"**Exam tip:** The higher the investor's tax bracket, the more attractive tax-free municipal bonds become — the tax-equivalent yield rises with the bracket. Match the strategy to the client: tax-exempt bonds suit high-bracket clients in taxable accounts, and the *type* of return matters, because long-term capital gains and qualified dividends are taxed at lower rates than ordinary income and interest.\n\n**Common trap:** Tax-deferred or tax-exempt vehicles change the analysis. Municipal bond interest, or gains inside a Roth IRA or 529 plan, are not reduced by the ordinary after-tax formula — check the account type before applying a tax haircut.\n\n#### Relevant Benchmarks"},
  {"id":797,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"A return means little in isolation; it must be compared against an appropriate **benchmark** — a passive index representing the same asset class, style, and risk as the portfolio. Beating a fair benchmark shows the manager added value."},
  {"id":798,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"| Benchmark | Represents | Notable feature |\n|---|---|---|\n| **S&P 500** | Large-cap U.S. equities | Market-cap weighted; broad large-cap gauge |\n| **Dow Jones Industrial Average (DJIA)** | 30 large U.S. \"blue-chip\" stocks | Price-weighted (higher-priced stocks have more influence) |\n| **NASDAQ Composite** | Tech-heavy / growth stocks | Market-cap weighted |\n| **Russell 2000** | Small-cap U.S. equities | The standard small-cap benchmark |\n| **MSCI EAFE** | Developed international equities (Europe, Australasia, Far East) | Excludes U.S. and Canada |\n| **Bloomberg U.S. Aggregate Bond Index** | Investment-grade U.S. bonds | Standard broad fixed-income benchmark |"},
  {"id":799,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"**Exam tip:** The benchmark must *match the portfolio's mandate*. Judging a small-cap growth fund against the large-cap S&P 500, or a bond fund against a stock index, is inappropriate and produces misleading conclusions. If a question offers an \"inappropriate benchmark\" answer choice, it usually involves a style or asset-class mismatch.\n\n**Common trap:** The DJIA is **price-weighted**, not market-cap weighted — a distinction the exam likes to test. In a price-weighted index, a $300 stock moves the average more than a $30 stock regardless of company size, whereas the S&P 500 and most other indexes weight by market capitalization.\n\n#### Putting It Together"},
  {"id":800,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"| Measure | Answers the question… | Key formula / idea |\n|---|---|---|\n| Total / Holding-period return | What did I earn in total? | (Income + Price Change) / Beginning Value |\n| Current yield | What income does it pay now? | Annual Income / Current Price |\n| Annualized return | What is that per year? | (1 + HPR)^(1/n) − 1 |\n| Time-weighted return | How good is the manager? | Geometric link of sub-period returns |\n| Dollar-weighted return / IRR | How did *this investor* do? | Rate where NPV of cash flows = 0 |\n| Expected return | What do we anticipate? | Sum of (Probability × Return) |\n| Risk-adjusted (Sharpe/Treynor/Alpha) | Was the return worth the risk? | Excess return per unit of risk |\n| Real (inflation-adjusted) return | Did purchasing power grow? | Nominal − Inflation |\n| After-tax return | What did I keep? | Pretax × (1 − Tax Rate) |\n| Benchmark comparison | Compared to what? | Match index to portfolio style |"},
  {"id":801,"part":"Part III","section":"K. Portfolio Performance Measures","anchor":"k-portfolio-performance-measures","text":"**Exam tip:** Most performance questions are not brute calculations — they ask you to pick the *right measure for the situation* (manager evaluation → time-weighted; investor's dollar experience → dollar-weighted/IRR; concentrated vs. well-diversified → Sharpe vs. Treynor; high-bracket client → tax-equivalent yield) or to rank yields correctly for premium and discount bonds. Master the distinctions and the handful of formulas you can be asked to compute (HPR, current yield, Sharpe, tax-equivalent yield), and this subsection becomes a reliable source of points."},
  {"id":802,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"Investment adviser (IA) regulation sits at the center of the Series 65 and is the single most heavily tested cluster of material on the exam. To master it, keep two questions separate at all times: **(1) Is this person an investment adviser at all?** (the definition), and **(2) If so, who regulates them — the SEC or the states?** (the division of authority). This subsection walks through both, then covers how advisers register, stay registered, and supervise their people.\n\n#### The Two Governing Statutes and the Division of Authority\n\nTwo laws work in tandem:"},
  {"id":803,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"- The **Investment Advisers Act of 1940** — the federal statute. It defines an investment adviser at the federal level and governs advisers who register with the **SEC** (\"federal covered\" advisers).\n- The **Uniform Securities Act (USA)** — the model state law (as interpreted through NASAA model rules) adopted in some form by each state. It governs advisers who register with a **state Administrator** (\"state-registered\" advisers)."},
  {"id":804,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"The line between them was drawn by the **National Securities Markets Improvement Act of 1996 (NSMIA)**, which eliminated dual registration. For registration purposes, an adviser is regulated by the SEC **or** the state(s) — not both at once. States retain **antifraud authority** over everyone, but only the appropriate regulator handles registration.\n\n**Exam tip:** When a question describes a person doing something with securities, run the definition first. Only after you have decided the person *is* an IA do you ask which regulator applies.\n\n#### Defining an \"Investment Adviser\": The Three-Prong ABC Test"},
  {"id":805,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"A person is an investment adviser only if **all three** prongs are met. Memorize them as **A-B-C**:\n\n| Prong | Name | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A** | **Advice** | Gives advice, issues reports, or renders analyses about **securities** (their value or the advisability of buying/selling them). |\n| **B** | **Business** | Is **in the business** of doing so — holds itself out as an adviser, provides advice with regularity, or the advisory activity is a normal part of its operations. |\n| **C** | **Compensation** | Receives **compensation** — any economic benefit — for the advice. |\n\nKey nuances the exam loves:"},
  {"id":806,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"- **A (Advice about securities specifically.)** Advice about fixed annuities, rare coins, commodities, real estate, or life insurance is **not** securities advice. But advice about the *asset allocation* of a securities portfolio, or telling a client to sell securities to buy something else, generally **is** securities advice.\n- **B (Business.)** The advisory service does **not** have to be the person's *primary* business. Doing it regularly, or holding out as an adviser, satisfies this prong.\n- **C (Compensation.)** Compensation need **not** come directly from the client and need **not** be a separate fee. It can be a commission, a 12b-1 fee, or any economic benefit from any source. **Common trap:** \"The planner charged no fee for the plan.\" If the planner earns commissions on the products recommended, the compensation prong is still met."},
  {"id":807,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"**Exam tip:** If any **one** prong is missing, the person is not an IA. All three are required.\n\n#### Exclusions From the Definition (the \"LATE\" Professionals and Others)\n\nCertain persons are **excluded from the definition** even though they might seem to satisfy the ABC test. Under the Advisers Act, the main exclusions are:"},
  {"id":808,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"- **Banks** and bank holding companies (not savings and loans).\n- **L**awyers, **A**ccountants, **T**eachers, and **E**ngineers (**\"LATE\"**) whose advice is **solely incidental** to their profession and who receive no special compensation for it.\n- **Broker-dealers** whose advice is **solely incidental** to their brokerage business and who receive **no special compensation** for the advice. **Common trap:** the moment a BD charges a **separate advisory fee** (special compensation) or offers wrap-fee/managed accounts, this exclusion is lost and it must register as an IA.\n- **Publishers** of bona fide newspapers or magazines of **general and regular circulation** (not market letters timed to specific events or promotional in nature).\n- Persons whose advice relates **only to U.S. government securities**."},
  {"id":809,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"Under state law, the definition also **excludes** investment adviser representatives, federal covered advisers, and certain other persons, since they are regulated elsewhere.\n\n#### Exempt Reporting Advisers (ERAs)\n\nAn **Exempt Reporting Adviser** is an adviser that is **exempt from registration** with the SEC but is still required to **file reports** — specific items of **Form ADV Part 1A** through the IARD system. ERAs are \"reporting, not registering.\" Two categories qualify:"},
  {"id":810,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"1. The **Private Fund Adviser Exemption** — advisers who advise **solely private funds** and have **less than $150 million** in private-fund assets under management in the U.S.\n2. The **Venture Capital Fund Adviser Exemption** — advisers who advise **solely one or more venture capital funds**, regardless of AUM.\n\nERAs remain subject to the **antifraud provisions** and to SEC examination even though they are not fully registered.\n\n**Exam tip:** Watch the **$150 million** figure and the word \"**solely**\" (\"solely private funds\" or \"solely venture capital funds\"). Adding a single separate-account managed client can blow the exemption."},
  {"id":811,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"#### Federal Covered vs. State-Registered: The AUM Dividing Line\n\nFor a typical adviser, the split turns on **Regulatory Assets Under Management (RAUM)** — reported on Form ADV, calculated on a **gross** basis (leverage included) for securities portfolios that receive **continuous and regular** supervisory or management services. The commonly tested categories:"},
  {"id":812,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"| Size category | RAUM | Where to register |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Small adviser** | Under **$25 million** | **State** (register with the state Administrator). |\n| **Mid-sized adviser** | **$25M to $100M** | **State**, *unless* an exception forces the SEC (see below). |\n| **Buffer zone** | **$100M to $110M** | Adviser's **choice** — may register with SEC or remain state-registered. |\n| **Large adviser** | **$110 million or more** | **SEC** (federal covered) — **mandatory**. |\n\n**Mid-sized adviser rule.** A mid-sized adviser ($25M–$100M) generally must register with the **states** and is **prohibited** from SEC registration **unless**:"},
  {"id":813,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"- It is **not required to register** in the state where it maintains its **principal office** (e.g., that state has no IA registration requirement), **or**\n- Its home state does **not subject the adviser to examination** (New York is the classic tested example — it does not examine mid-sized advisers, so a mid-sized adviser headquartered in NY must register with the **SEC**), **or**\n- It would otherwise be required to register in **15 or more states** (the multi-state exemption), which makes it **eligible** for SEC registration.\n\n**The 90/100/110 buffers.** To spare advisers from flipping registration back and forth over small market swings:"},
  {"id":814,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"- A mid-sized adviser becomes **eligible** for the SEC at **$100 million** and is **required** at **$110 million**. Between **$100M and $110M**, registration is optional.\n- Once SEC-registered, an adviser need not withdraw and move to the states until its RAUM drops **below $90 million**.\n\n**Worked example.** An adviser reports RAUM of $103 million on its annual updating amendment.\n- Rule: If RAUM ≥ $110M → SEC required; if $100M ≤ RAUM < $110M → adviser's choice; if RAUM < $90M (once SEC-registered) → withdraw to states.\n- $103M falls in the **$100M–$110M buffer**, so the adviser **may** choose to register with the SEC or remain state-registered. Neither is forced."},
  {"id":815,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"**Other federal covered advisers (outside the standard RAUM thresholds).** Some advisers qualify for or must use SEC registration regardless of their own RAUM:"},
  {"id":816,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"- Advisers to a **registered investment company** (a mutual fund or other RIC) — SEC registration is **mandatory** regardless of size.\n- **Pension consultants** whose advisory services cover employee benefit plan assets of at least **$200 million** (eligible/optional). Note this test looks to the **plan assets advised**, not the adviser's own RAUM.\n- **Internet investment advisers** that provide advice exclusively through an interactive website.\n- Advisers **eligible for the multi-state (15+ states) exemption** (optional).\n- **Newly formed** advisers that reasonably expect to be eligible for SEC registration within **120 days** of registering."},
  {"id":817,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"**Common trap:** \"An adviser manages $40 million but advises a mutual fund.\" Do not be fooled by the small AUM — advising a **registered investment company** makes it **federal covered**.\n\n#### Notice Filing for Federal Covered Advisers\n\nA federal covered adviser does **not register** with the states, but a state may still require it to make a **notice filing**. Notice filing means the adviser:\n\n- Files **copies of the documents it filed with the SEC** (i.e., its Form ADV),\n- Pays the **state filing fees**, and\n- Files a **consent to service of process**."},
  {"id":818,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"Notice filing lets states collect revenue and monitor advisers doing business there **without** imposing substantive registration requirements (which NSMIA preempts). The state **retains antifraud jurisdiction** and can still bring enforcement actions.\n\n**Exam tip:** Federal covered advisers **notice file**; state-covered advisers **register**. If a question asks what a state can require of a federal covered adviser, the answer is a **notice filing (documents + fees + consent to service of process)** — not full registration.\n\n#### Registration and Form ADV (Parts 1, 2, and 3)"},
  {"id":819,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"Registration for both SEC and state advisers is filed electronically through the **IARD** (Investment Adviser Registration Depository). The vehicle is **Form ADV**, which has three parts:"},
  {"id":820,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"| Form ADV part | What it is | Contains |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Part 1** | Administrative/\"check-the-box\" information about the firm | Business form, ownership, AUM, types of clients, disciplinary history, custody. Part **1A** = SEC; Part **1B** = additional state-required items. |\n| **Part 2A** | The **firm brochure** (plain-English narrative) | Services, **fee schedule**, types of clients, methods of analysis, **conflicts of interest**, disciplinary disclosures. |\n| **Part 2B** | The **brochure supplement** | Information about the **specific IARs** who serve the client — education, business experience, disciplinary history. |\n| **Part 3** | **Form CRS** (Client Relationship Summary) | A short (max **2 pages** for a standalone adviser) relationship summary for **retail investors**; required of SEC-registered advisers. |"},
  {"id":821,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"**State registration effectiveness.** Under the Uniform Securities Act, a complete application becomes **effective at noon on the 30th day** after filing, absent a pending proceeding or a stop order.\n\n#### Post-Registration Requirements\n\n**Brochure delivery (the \"two-day free look\").** The adviser must deliver the **brochure (Part 2A) and supplement (Part 2B)**:"},
  {"id":822,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"- **State/NASAA rule:** Deliver **at least 48 hours before** entering into the advisory contract, **OR** at the time of entering the contract **if** the client is given a **5-business-day right to rescind** the contract without penalty. **Exam tip:** remember it as \"**48 hours in advance, or a 5-day free look.**\"\n- **Annual delivery:** Within **120 days** of the end of the adviser's fiscal year, deliver either the updated brochure **or** a **summary of material changes** with an **offer** to provide the full brochure, **free of charge**."},
  {"id":823,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"**Form ADV amendments.** File an **annual updating amendment within 90 days** of the fiscal year-end, and file **prompt** amendments whenever the information becomes **materially inaccurate**.\n\n**Books and records.** Both SEC (Rule 204-2) and the NASAA model require records be kept for **5 years**, with the most recent **2 years** in an **easily accessible** place (typically the principal office). Corporate/organizational documents (charter, articles, minute books) are kept for the **life of the firm** plus a period after dissolution. SEC-registered advisers follow SEC record rules, and states **cannot impose stricter** recordkeeping on them."},
  {"id":824,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"**Registration maintenance.** State registration is effective for **one year, expiring December 31**, and is **renewed annually** with a fee; the adviser must keep all filings current through amendments.\n\n#### Financial Requirements and Commonly Tested State Thresholds\n\nState-registered advisers face **net worth**, **bonding**, and **disclosure** thresholds that appear repeatedly on the exam:"},
  {"id":825,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"| Situation | Minimum net worth (state) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Adviser has **custody** of client funds/securities | **$35,000** | Also triggers qualified custodian, account statements, and an annual **surprise exam** by an independent accountant. |\n| Adviser has **discretionary authority** but **no custody** | **$10,000** | Discretion = adviser decides securities/amount/timing without prior client approval. |\n| Neither custody nor discretion | No net-worth minimum (may need a bond) | — |"},
  {"id":826,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"- **Net worth deficiency:** If net worth falls below the minimum, the adviser must **notify the Administrator by the close of the next business day** and then file a **financial report** (typically by the close of business the following day).\n- **Bonding:** A state may require a **surety bond** instead of (or in addition to) the net worth requirement; the adviser may deposit **cash or securities** in lieu of the bond.\n\n**Substantial prepayment of fees (balance sheet trigger).**"},
  {"id":827,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"- **State rule:** If an adviser collects **more than $500 in prepaid fees, 6 months or more in advance**, it must include an **audited balance sheet** in its brochure.\n- **Federal (SEC) rule:** The threshold is **more than $1,200, 6 months or more in advance**.\n\n**Worked example.** A state-registered adviser bills a client **$900 up front covering the next 12 months**. Is a balance sheet required? The prepayment ($900) is **more than $500** and covers a period of **6 months or more** (12 months). **Yes** — the adviser must include an **audited balance sheet** in Part 2A."},
  {"id":828,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"**Common trap:** Do not confuse the **$500 (state)** and **$1,200 (federal)** prepayment thresholds — the exam frequently swaps them.\n\n#### IAR Continuing Education\n\nAn **investment adviser representative (IAR)** is an **individual** associated with an IA who makes recommendations or gives advice, manages accounts, solicits or sells advisory services, or **supervises** those who do. IARs register at the **state** level where they have a **place of business** — this is true even for IARs of a **federal covered** adviser."},
  {"id":829,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"Under the **NASAA Model Rule on IAR Continuing Education** (states began adopting it effective January 1, 2022), a registered IAR must complete **12 CE credits per year**, split as:\n\n- **6 credits — Products and Practice**, and\n- **6 credits — Ethics and Professional Responsibility**.\n\n**Worked example.** 6 + 6 = **12 total credits annually**. An IAR who completes 6 Ethics credits but only 4 Products-and-Practice credits has **10 of 12** and is deficient."},
  {"id":830,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"Failing to complete CE renders the IAR's status **\"CE inactive\"** — the IAR **cannot renew** the registration until the credits are made up, though this does not by itself revoke the registration. **Exam tip:** CE is a **renewal** requirement; it does **not** waive the qualification exam, and it applies only in states that have **adopted** the model rule.\n\n#### IAR Supervision\n\nAn investment adviser must **establish, maintain, and enforce written supervisory procedures** reasonably designed to prevent and detect violations of the securities laws by the firm and its IARs. Core points tested:"},
  {"id":831,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"- The firm must designate **supervisors** with responsibility over the activities of IARs and other associated persons.\n- **Failure to supervise** is itself a prohibited business practice and a basis for **disciplinary action** against the firm, even where the underlying violation was committed by an employee.\n- Reasonable supervision includes review of recommendations, correspondence, advertising, and handling of client funds, plus follow-up on **red flags**."},
  {"id":832,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"**Exam tip:** The firm can be held liable for an IAR's misconduct if it **failed to supervise**, but it has a defense if it had **reasonable procedures in place and was following them** — supervision must be both written **and** actually enforced.\n\n#### Quick-Reference: Commonly Tested Thresholds"},
  {"id":833,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"| Item | Threshold |\n|---|---|\n| Small adviser (state) | Under **$25M** AUM |\n| Mid-sized adviser (state, with exceptions) | **$25M–$100M** AUM |\n| SEC registration optional (buffer) | **$100M–$110M** AUM |\n| SEC registration mandatory | **$110M+** AUM |\n| Withdraw from SEC back to states | RAUM below **$90M** |\n| Exempt Reporting Adviser (private fund) | Under **$150M** in private-fund AUM |\n| Multi-state SEC eligibility | Required to register in **15+ states** |\n| Pension consultant SEC eligibility | **$200M+** in plan assets advised |\n| Net worth — custody (state) | **$35,000** |\n| Net worth — discretion, no custody (state) | **$10,000** |\n| Substantial prepayment balance sheet — state |"},
  {"id":834,"part":"Part IV","section":"A. Regulation of Investment Advisers (State-Registered and Federal Covered)","anchor":"a-regulation-of-investment-advisers-state-registered-and-federal-covered","text":"Over **$500**, 6+ months in advance |\n| Substantial prepayment balance sheet — federal | Over **$1,200**, 6+ months in advance |\n| Brochure delivery | **48 hours** before contract, or with a **5-day** rescission right |\n| Annual brochure delivery | Within **120 days** of fiscal year-end |\n| Form ADV annual updating amendment | Within **90 days** of fiscal year-end |\n| Books and records retention | **5 years** (first **2** easily accessible) |\n| State registration effectiveness | Noon on the **30th day** after filing |\n| IAR continuing education | **12 credits/year** (6 Products & Practice + 6 Ethics) |"},
  {"id":835,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"An investment adviser (IA) is a **firm** — a legal entity. But the actual advice is delivered by **people**: the humans who sit across from clients, manage portfolios, and pitch the firm's services. Those individuals are **investment adviser representatives (IARs)**, and they are regulated separately from the firm that employs them. This subsection covers who is an IAR, when registration is required, who does the registering, and what an IAR must do to stay in good standing after registration."},
  {"id":836,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"**Exam tip:** Keep three roles cleanly separated in your mind — the **IA** (the firm/entity), the **IAR** (the individual giving advice), and the **BD agent** (an individual on the brokerage side). The exam constantly tests whether you can tell which rules attach to the entity versus the individual.\n\n#### What Is an Investment Adviser Representative?\n\nAn IAR is any **individual (a natural person)** who is a partner, officer, or director of — or is otherwise employed by or associated with — an investment adviser (or a federal covered adviser) and who does **one or more** of the following:"},
  {"id":837,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"- **Makes recommendations or renders advice** regarding securities;\n- **Manages the accounts or portfolios** of clients;\n- **Determines** which securities recommendation or advice should be given;\n- **Solicits, offers, or negotiates for the sale of, or sells, investment advisory services**; or\n- **Supervises** employees who perform any of the above."},
  {"id":838,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"Two words in that definition are load-bearing. First, **individual** — an IAR is always a natural person; a firm can never be an IAR (a firm is an IA). Second, the fifth bullet — **supervisors of advisory personnel are themselves IARs**, even if they never personally speak to a client. A branch manager who oversees ten advice-givers must register as an IAR.\n\nNotice that **soliciting or selling advisory services** counts. This means a salesperson who never actually gives investment advice — someone whose entire job is to bring in advisory clients — is still an IAR and must register."},
  {"id":839,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"**Common trap:** Students assume \"IAR\" only means someone who picks stocks. It also includes the person who *sells the advisory relationship* and the person who *supervises* the advice-givers. Both must register.\n\n#### Activities That Trigger IAR Registration\n\nBecause the five functions above define the role, doing **any one of them** on behalf of an adviser is the activity that triggers registration. The exam likes fact patterns where you must decide whether a given employee is \"in\" or \"out\":"},
  {"id":840,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"| Employee's activity | IAR? |\n|---|---|\n| Meets clients and recommends a portfolio allocation | Yes |\n| Cold-calls prospects to sell the firm's wrap-fee advisory program | Yes (solicits/sells advisory services) |\n| Decides which model portfolio a client is placed in | Yes (determines the advice) |\n| Manages a supervisory team of advice-givers | Yes (supervises IAR functions) |\n| Answers the phone, schedules appointments, files paperwork | No (purely clerical/ministerial) |\n| Processes trade confirmations and mails statements | No (ministerial) |\n\n#### Exclusions from the Definition\n\nNot everyone at an advisory firm is an IAR. The exclusions are the mirror image of the activities above."},
  {"id":841,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"**Clerical and ministerial personnel are excluded.** Receptionists, IT staff, back-office processors, and administrative assistants are not IARs because they render no advice and solicit no advisory business. If a person's function is purely secretarial or administrative, they do not register — no matter that they work at an advisory firm."},
  {"id":842,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"**Federal covered adviser personnel who fall below the federal thresholds.** For an individual who works for a **federal covered adviser** (an IA registered with the SEC), SEC Rule 203A-3 defines which employees the states may require to register as IARs. Under that rule, a *supervised person* of a federal covered adviser is an IAR (subject to state registration) **only if** the individual has a **place of business in the state** and:\n\n- has **more than five** clients who are natural persons (excluding certain high-net-worth \"qualified\" clients); **and**\n- **more than 10%** of the individual's clients are such natural persons."},
  {"id":843,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"Additionally, a supervised person is **not** an IAR if they either:\n\n- do **not**, on a regular basis, solicit, meet with, or otherwise communicate with clients; **or**\n- provide **only impersonal investment advice** (advice not tailored to individual clients — e.g., a general market newsletter or statistical/model output distributed to everyone).\n\n**Exam tip:** The \"more than 5 natural-person clients / more than 10%\" screen is a **federal** test used to decide which employees of a *federal covered adviser* must register at the state level. Do not apply it to employees of a *state-registered* adviser — for a state adviser, the five-function definition (plus the clerical exclusion) governs."},
  {"id":844,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"#### Where an IAR Must Register: Registration Authority, Place of Business, and the De Minimis Exemption\n\n**IARs register at the STATE level — always.** There is no such thing as SEC registration of an individual IAR. The **SEC registers firms** (advisers); the **state Administrator registers individuals** (IARs). This holds even when the IAR works for a federal covered adviser: the *firm* is SEC-registered, but its *representatives* still register with the states. The **registration authority for every IAR is the state Administrator.**"},
  {"id":845,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"The trigger for *which* states an IAR registers in turns on **place of business** and, secondarily, on client counts. A **\"place of business\"** is any office where the IAR regularly provides advisory services or meets with clients, or any location held out to the public as such a place.\n\n| The IAR works for a… | Must register in a state when… |\n|---|---|\n| **State-registered IA** | The IAR has a **place of business** in the state, **or** has clients in the state and does not qualify for the de minimis exemption |\n| **Federal covered adviser** | The IAR has a **place of business** in the state — this is the **only** trigger (client count in that state is irrelevant under NSMIA) |"},
  {"id":846,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"The key takeaway for federal covered adviser IARs: **no place of business in a state means no registration in that state, even if the IAR has many clients there.** The National Securities Markets Improvement Act (NSMIA) prohibits states from requiring registration of a federal covered adviser's representative absent a place of business in the state.\n\n**The IAR de minimis exemption.** An IAR who has **no place of business** in a state is **not required to register** in that state if, during the preceding 12 months, the IAR has had **no more than five (5) clients** who are residents of that state. In counting terms:"},
  {"id":847,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"- Clients in the state within 12 months = **5 or fewer**, **and** no place of business → **exempt** (no registration).\n- A **6th** resident client (with no place of business) → registration is now required.\n\n*Worked example.* An IAR maintains her only office in Illinois. Over the past 12 months she has picked up **4** advisory clients who reside in Indiana, where she has no office and never meets clients (all contact is by phone). Count = 4, which is 5 or fewer, and she has no place of business → she is **exempt** in Indiana. If her Indiana clientele later grows to **6** residents, she exceeds the de minimis of 5 and must register in Indiana."},
  {"id":848,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"**Common trap:** The de minimis counts **clients**, not solicitations or prospects, and the client cap only helps you when there is **no place of business** in the state. The moment the IAR opens or holds out an office in a state, she must register there even with a single client — the de minimis is off the table.\n\n**Exam tip:** An IAR is **not** required to meet any **financial/net-worth or surety-bond** requirement. Minimum net worth, net capital, and bonding obligations attach to the **adviser (the firm)** — typically when it has custody or discretion — never to the individual IAR. If a question asks whether an IAR must post a bond, the answer is no."},
  {"id":849,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"#### Registration Process and Effectiveness\n\nAn IAR registers by filing **Form U4** (Uniform Application for Securities Industry Registration or Transfer) electronically through the CRD/IARD system, together with the applicable fee. (The firm, by contrast, files **Form ADV**; do not confuse the two.)"},
  {"id":850,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"An IAR's registration **cannot stand alone** — it is effective only while the individual is **associated with a registered investment adviser or a federal covered adviser.** If the employing adviser's registration is suspended or revoked, the IAR cannot function. Absent a specified date or a request for more information, an application generally becomes effective at **noon on the 30th day** after a complete filing, consistent with the general registration-effectiveness rule of the Uniform Securities Act.\n\nBecause registration is tied to the employing firm, **moving between firms requires re-registration** — an IAR's registration does not automatically travel from one adviser to another."},
  {"id":851,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"**Termination and beginning of employment — who must notify.** This is a heavily tested distinction that hinges on whether the employer is state-registered or federal covered:\n\n| IAR's employer | Who notifies the Administrator when the IAR begins/terminates? |\n|---|---|\n| **State-registered IA** | The **investment adviser (the firm)** notifies |\n| **Federal covered adviser** | The **IAR (the individual)** notifies |\n| *(Compare: BD agent)* | **Both** the agent **and** the broker-dealer notify |"},
  {"id":852,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"The logic: for a federal covered adviser, the **firm is not registered with the state**, so the state can only look to the individual to report the change. Termination is documented on **Form U5**, filed promptly (generally within 30 days).\n\n**Common trap:** For **BD agents**, both the agent *and* the broker-dealer must notify. For **IARs of a state adviser**, only the **firm** notifies; for **IARs of a federal covered adviser**, only the **individual** notifies. Match the notifier to the fact pattern.\n\n#### Post-Registration Maintenance Requirements"},
  {"id":853,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"Registration is not \"set and forget.\" An IAR has three ongoing obligations: keep the uniform forms current, disclose reportable events, and complete continuing education.\n\n**1. Updating uniform forms.** The IAR must keep **Form U4 accurate and current** by filing **amendments promptly** whenever the information becomes materially inaccurate — generally **within 30 days** of the IAR learning of the change. Changes that require an amendment include name and residential/office address, employment and outside business activities, and any of the disclosure (reportable) events below."},
  {"id":854,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"**2. Disclosure of reportable events.** Form U4 contains disclosure questions that must be updated as events occur. Commonly tested reportable events include:"},
  {"id":855,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"- **Criminal** charges or convictions (felonies and certain misdemeanors, including those involving money, securities, or fraud);\n- **Regulatory or self-regulatory actions** (denials, suspensions, revocations, fines, bars, orders);\n- **Civil judicial** actions involving investment-related conduct;\n- **Customer complaints, arbitrations, and settlements** meeting reporting thresholds;\n- **Financial disclosures** — personal **bankruptcy** within the past 10 years, and unsatisfied **judgments or liens**;\n- **Termination for cause** (being discharged after allegations of violating rules, fraud, or failure to supervise)."},
  {"id":856,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"**Exam tip:** A disclosure event does not automatically bar or revoke registration, but **failing to update the U4 to disclose it is itself a violation** and grounds for administrative action. The obligation is affirmative and continuous, not something that waits for the annual renewal.\n\n**3. Continuing education (CE).** Under the **NASAA Model Rule on Investment Adviser Representative Continuing Education**, a registered IAR must complete **12 CE credits every year**, allocated as:\n\nCE credits per year = **6 credits \"Ethics and Professional Responsibility\" + 6 credits \"Products and Practice\" = 12 total**"},
  {"id":857,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"- **6 Ethics and Professional Responsibility** credits (at least 3 of which must cover ethics), **and**\n- **6 Products and Practice** credits (covering securities products, investment strategies, and applicable regulatory requirements)."},
  {"id":858,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"*Worked example.* An IAR completes 6 Products-and-Practice credits and only 4 Ethics-and-Professional-Responsibility credits in a given year: 6 + 4 = 10 credits, which is short of the required 12 and also short of the 6 required in the Ethics-and-Professional-Responsibility bucket. The IAR has **not** satisfied CE for the year, and her registration becomes **\"CE inactive.\"** A CE-inactive IAR remains registered but **cannot renew** the registration until the deficiency is cured; if the shortfall persists, the individual will be unable to continue acting as an IAR once the annual renewal period closes."},
  {"id":859,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"An individual who holds **both** IAR and BD-agent registrations and who satisfies FINRA's continuing education for the agent side may be credited toward the corresponding NASAA IAR CE requirement, avoiding fully duplicative coursework.\n\n**Common trap:** IAR CE is an **annual** requirement (12 credits every year), not a one-time or every-other-year obligation, and the credits are **split into two fixed buckets** — you cannot satisfy the requirement by loading up on one category. Also note that the consequence of noncompliance is a **CE-inactive status that blocks renewal**, not an immediate revocation."},
  {"id":860,"part":"Part IV","section":"B. Regulation of Investment Adviser Representatives","anchor":"b-regulation-of-investment-adviser-representatives","text":"**Renewal.** Like all registrations under the Uniform Securities Act, an IAR's registration **expires December 31** each year and must be renewed (with the renewal fee) to continue into the next calendar year — assuming CE and all disclosure obligations are current."},
  {"id":861,"part":"Part IV","section":"C. Regulation of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"c-regulation-of-broker-dealers","text":"Broker-dealers (BDs) are the firms that execute securities transactions — the \"plumbing\" through which investors buy and sell securities. Unlike investment advisers, where the National Securities Markets Improvement Act of 1996 (NSMIA) split regulatory authority between the SEC and the states, broker-dealers remain subject to **dual regulation**. A firm typically registers with the SEC, becomes a member of FINRA, *and* registers in each state where it does business — unless a specific exclusion applies. There is no \"federal covered broker-dealer\" that escapes state registration the way a federal covered adviser escapes state adviser registration. For the Series 65, this subsection tests four things precisely: what a BD *is*, who is *excluded*, *when* registration in a state is triggered, and the *snowbird* exemption for existing customers."},
  {"id":862,"part":"Part IV","section":"C. Regulation of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"c-regulation-of-broker-dealers","text":"#### Definition of a Broker-Dealer Under the Uniform Securities Act\n\nThe Uniform Securities Act (USA) defines a **broker-dealer** as *any person engaged in the business of effecting transactions in securities for the account of others or for its own account.* Break the definition into its parts:"},
  {"id":863,"part":"Part IV","section":"C. Regulation of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"c-regulation-of-broker-dealers","text":"- **\"Person\"** — under the USA this is broad: individuals, corporations, partnerships, associations, trusts, and other legal entities. (In practice a BD is an entity/firm, while the individuals working for it are \"agents.\")\n- **\"Engaged in the business\"** — the activity must be conducted as a business, i.e., with regularity and for compensation. A person who executes an occasional, isolated transaction for its own account is not \"in the business\" of effecting transactions.\n- **\"For the account of others\"** — acting as an agent/broker (executing trades on behalf of clients for a commission).\n- **\"For its own account\"** — acting as a principal/dealer (buying and selling from its own inventory, earning a markup or markdown)."},
  {"id":864,"part":"Part IV","section":"C. Regulation of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"c-regulation-of-broker-dealers","text":"Notice that the state term \"broker-dealer\" combines two roles that federal law (the Securities Exchange Act of 1934) defines separately — \"broker\" (agent) and \"dealer\" (principal). At the state level, both roles are captured under the single term **broker-dealer**.\n\n**Exam tip:** The two elements the exam keys on are \"engaged in the business\" and \"transactions in securities for the account of others or its own account.\" If a scenario describes someone regularly buying and selling securities for clients (or trading securities as a business from its own inventory), it is a broker-dealer unless an exclusion removes it.\n\n#### Exclusions From the Definition of Broker-Dealer"},
  {"id":865,"part":"Part IV","section":"C. Regulation of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"c-regulation-of-broker-dealers","text":"Certain persons are carved out of the definition — meaning they are **not** broker-dealers and therefore do not register as BDs in the state. The USA excludes the following:"},
  {"id":866,"part":"Part IV","section":"C. Regulation of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"c-regulation-of-broker-dealers","text":"1. **An agent.** An agent is an *individual* who represents a broker-dealer or an issuer in effecting securities transactions. The salesperson is not the firm; the individual registers as an \"agent,\" not as a \"broker-dealer.\"\n2. **An issuer.** A company selling its *own* securities is transacting in its own securities, not effecting transactions for others as a business.\n3. **A bank, savings institution, or trust company.** These institutions are excluded from the *state* definition of broker-dealer. (Federal treatment of banks under the '34 Act is more nuanced, but the Series 65 tests the state exclusion.)\n4. **A person with no place of business in the state who deals only with excluded (in"},
  {"id":867,"part":"Part IV","section":"C. Regulation of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"c-regulation-of-broker-dealers","text":"stitutional) clients.** The firm has no place of business in the state and effects transactions exclusively with or through: the *issuers* of the securities involved, *other broker-dealers*, and *institutional clients* — banks, savings institutions, trust companies, insurance companies, investment companies, pension or profit-sharing trusts, and other financial institutions or institutional buyers.\n5. **A person qualifying for the snowbird exemption** (existing out-of-state customers) — covered in detail below."},
  {"id":868,"part":"Part IV","section":"C. Regulation of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"c-regulation-of-broker-dealers","text":"| Person / situation | Excluded from BD definition? | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Individual sales rep at a firm | Yes — this is an **agent** | Individuals are agents, not BDs |\n| Company selling its own stock | Yes — this is an **issuer** | Transacting in its own securities |\n| Bank / savings institution / trust company | Yes | Statutory institutional exclusion |\n| Firm, no office in state, deals only with banks, insurers, investment companies, other BDs, issuers | Yes | No place of business + institutional-only clients |\n| Firm with an office in the state | **No** — must register | Place of business triggers registration |"},
  {"id":869,"part":"Part IV","section":"C. Regulation of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"c-regulation-of-broker-dealers","text":"**Common trap:** The exclusions in items 4 and 5 both depend on the firm having **no place of business** in the state. If the firm maintains a place of business in the state, it must register as a broker-dealer there **even if it deals only with institutions or only with out-of-state customers.** Test writers love to place an office in the state and then describe an otherwise \"exempt\" client list — the office defeats the exclusion.\n\n**Exam tip:** Memorize the three \"pure\" exclusions that apply regardless of place of business: **agent, issuer, and bank/savings institution/trust company.** The other two exclusions are conditional on having *no place of business* in the state."},
  {"id":870,"part":"Part IV","section":"C. Regulation of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"c-regulation-of-broker-dealers","text":"#### When a Broker-Dealer Must Register in a State\n\nA person that meets the definition of broker-dealer and does not qualify for an exclusion **must register in the state** before transacting business there. Registration is triggered by either of two things:"},
  {"id":871,"part":"Part IV","section":"C. Regulation of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"c-regulation-of-broker-dealers","text":"- **Having a place of business in the state.** A \"place of business\" is an office at which the firm conducts securities business or holds itself out as doing so. Any place of business in the state requires registration.\n- **Doing business with (non-exempt) persons in the state.** Even with no in-state office, a firm that solicits or transacts securities business with *retail* (non-institutional, resident) customers in the state must register. The state has jurisdiction where an offer is made *in* the state, *from* the state, or *accepted* in the state."},
  {"id":872,"part":"Part IV","section":"C. Regulation of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"c-regulation-of-broker-dealers","text":"Mechanically, a broker-dealer registers by filing **Form BD** through the **Central Registration Depository (CRD)** system operated by FINRA, which routes the filing to the SEC, FINRA, and the relevant states. Registration in a state generally becomes effective at **noon on the 30th day** after a complete application is filed, absent a stop order or a request for additional information. The Administrator may also require the BD to meet **minimum net capital** and post a **surety bond** (no bond may be required of a firm that meets the applicable net capital requirement), and may require a **qualification examination**."},
  {"id":873,"part":"Part IV","section":"C. Regulation of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"c-regulation-of-broker-dealers","text":"**Common trap:** Because broker-dealers are dually regulated, there is **no NSMIA-style preemption** of state registration for BDs. A firm registered with the SEC and FINRA still must register in each state where it has a place of business or non-exempt customers. Do not confuse the \"federal covered adviser\" preemption — which removes *state* registration for large investment advisers — with broker-dealers; no such preemption exists for BDs.\n\n#### The De Minimis / No-Place-of-Business \"Snowbird\" Exemption for Existing Customers"},
  {"id":874,"part":"Part IV","section":"C. Regulation of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"c-regulation-of-broker-dealers","text":"The most heavily tested exclusion is the **snowbird exemption**. It solves a practical problem: a customer who lives in one state but travels to another (classically, a retiree wintering in Florida — a \"snowbird\") should be able to keep dealing with their existing broker-dealer without forcing that firm to register in every state the customer happens to visit.\n\nA broker-dealer is **excluded** from the definition — and thus need **not** register in the visited state — when **all** of the following are true:"},
  {"id":875,"part":"Part IV","section":"C. Regulation of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"c-regulation-of-broker-dealers","text":"- The firm has **no place of business in that state**; and\n- Its only business in that state is with **existing customers** (an established relationship already exists); and\n- Those customers are **not residents of the state** — their principal place of residence is in another state; they are only temporarily present.\n\n(The firm is presumed to remain properly registered where it does maintain a place of business, typically the customer's home state.) Put simply: *no office in the state + existing customer + customer is a non-resident just passing through.* If those conditions hold, the firm can continue servicing that customer without registering in the state the customer is visiting."},
  {"id":876,"part":"Part IV","section":"C. Regulation of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"c-regulation-of-broker-dealers","text":"Because no dollar amounts or offer counts change the outcome once these conditions are met, there is **no calculation** to perform here — the exemption is a *status test*, not a *threshold test*. The label \"de minimis\" simply reflects that the firm's contact with the state is minimal (existing, transient, out-of-state customers); unlike the numeric de minimis test for investment advisers, it imposes no numeric client limit."},
  {"id":877,"part":"Part IV","section":"C. Regulation of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"c-regulation-of-broker-dealers","text":"**Common trap — new customers.** The exemption covers **existing** customers only. If the traveling snowbird refers a friend who lives in the visited state, or the firm otherwise **solicits a new customer** in that state, the exemption is lost and registration is required. The relationship must have pre-existed the customer's presence in the state.\n\n**Common trap — the customer becomes a resident.** If the snowbird stops being a visitor and **establishes residency** (principal place of residence) in the new state, the customer is now a resident and the exemption no longer applies — the broker-dealer must register in that state to continue serving them."},
  {"id":878,"part":"Part IV","section":"C. Regulation of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"c-regulation-of-broker-dealers","text":"**Exam tip:** Watch two facts in any snowbird question — (1) is there a **place of business** in the state, and (2) is the customer a **pre-existing, non-resident** customer? If the firm has any in-state office, or the customer is new or a resident, the exemption fails and registration is required."},
  {"id":879,"part":"Part IV","section":"C. Regulation of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"c-regulation-of-broker-dealers","text":"| Snowbird scenario (firm has NO in-state office) | Registration required in the visited state? |\n|---|---|\n| Existing customer of the firm, resides out of state, vacationing in the state | No — snowbird exemption applies |\n| New prospect solicited while in the state | Yes — not an existing customer |\n| Existing customer who moves and becomes a resident of the state | Yes — customer is now a resident |\n| Existing out-of-state customer, but firm opens an office in the state | Yes — place of business defeats the exemption |"},
  {"id":880,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"An \"agent\" is the human being on the front line of the brokerage business — the person who actually talks to customers and enters their buy and sell orders. The Uniform Securities Act (USA) defines who qualifies as an agent, dictates when that person must register with the state, and carves out the narrow situations in which an individual can effect securities transactions without registering. Because this material overlaps heavily with the Series 63/66 world, the Series 65 tests it through precise definitions and \"gotcha\" fact patterns rather than math. Master the exact wording and you will get every one of these points.\n\n#### What Is an Agent? (The Statutory Definition)"},
  {"id":881,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"Under the Uniform Securities Act, an **agent** means *any individual, other than a broker-dealer, who represents a broker-dealer or issuer in effecting or attempting to effect purchases or sales of securities.*\n\nBreak that definition into its load-bearing pieces:"},
  {"id":882,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"- **\"Any individual\"** — An agent is *always a natural person*. A corporation, partnership, LLC, or any other entity can never be an agent. Firms register as broker-dealers; the people who work for them register as agents.\n- **\"Other than a broker-dealer\"** — The statute deliberately separates the firm (broker-dealer) from the human (agent). The same actor is not both under a single transaction.\n- **\"Represents a broker-dealer or issuer\"** — An agent works on behalf of either a brokerage firm or an issuer of securities. Which of these two the individual represents is the single most important fact for deciding whether registration is required (covered below).\n- **\"In effecting or attempting to effect purchases or sales of securities\"** — The individual must be involved in the *securities transaction itself*: soliciting, taking, or executing orders. \"Attempting\" matters — merely trying to make a sale (cold-calling, soliciting) triggers agent status even if no trade ever closes."},
  {"id":883,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"**Exam tip:** If a question describes an entity (a firm, a bank, a corporation) and asks whether it is an \"agent,\" the answer is almost always **no** — an agent is by definition an individual. The classic distractor is calling a broker-dealer firm an \"agent.\"\n\n**Common trap:** Partners, officers, and directors of a broker-dealer or issuer are **agents only if they otherwise meet the definition** — that is, only if they personally effect or attempt to effect securities transactions. A CFO who signs regulatory filings but never solicits or takes customer orders is not acting as an agent.\n\n#### Agent vs. Broker-Dealer: A Critical Distinction"},
  {"id":884,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"Candidates constantly blur these two terms. Keep them cleanly separated:"},
  {"id":885,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"| Feature | Agent | Broker-Dealer |\n|---|---|---|\n| Legal nature | Always an **individual** (natural person) | A **\"person\"** — almost always a firm/entity (corporation, partnership, LLC); can be a sole proprietor |\n| Function | *Represents* a BD or issuer in effecting trades | Is *in the business* of effecting securities trades for others' accounts (broker) or its own account (dealer) |\n| Registration form | Form U4 | Form BD |\n| Relationship | Must be associated with a registered BD (or issuer) to be registered | Registers on its own behalf; employs and supervises agents |\n| Compensation clue | Transaction-based pay (commissions) for effecting trades | Earns commissions, markups, and markdowns as a firm |"},
  {"id":886,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"The mental model: the **broker-dealer is the firm**, and its **agents are the licensed individuals** who transact with the public on the firm's behalf. A single BD may have thousands of agents; each agent generally represents one BD at a time (dual registration with a second BD is possible only with the consent of the firms and the Administrator).\n\n**Exam tip:** Watch the word \"person.\" In securities law a **person includes entities**, but an **agent is specifically an *individual***. That narrowing is intentional and heavily tested.\n\n#### When Is Registration Required?\n\nThe governing rule from the USA is simple to state:"},
  {"id":887,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"> It is unlawful for any individual to **transact business as an agent** in a state unless the individual is **registered** in that state or is **exempt/excluded** from the definition.\n\nPractical consequences the exam tests:"},
  {"id":888,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"- **Register in every state where you do business.** An agent must be registered in each state where they have customers or solicit business — determined by where the *client* is located and where the offer is *directed and received*, not merely where the agent sits. Soliciting a customer in a state where the agent is not registered is a violation even if the customer declines.\n- **No registration link, no agent registration.** An agent's registration is *tethered* to their employing broker-dealer (or issuer). If the broker-dealer's registration is not effective — or is suspended or revoked — the agent's registration is **inoperative** for that period. An agent's registration is not effectiv"},
  {"id":889,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"e during any time the agent is not associated with a registered broker-dealer or issuer.\n- **Changing firms is a three-party notice event.** When an agent moves from one broker-dealer to another, **the agent, the former firm, and the new firm must all promptly notify the Administrator.** (Under the USA, whenever an agent begins or terminates a connection with a broker-dealer or issuer, the agent *and* the firm involved must notify the Administrator — so a move triggers notice by all three.)\n- **Mechanics.** Registration is filed on **Form U4** through the CRD system, generally becomes effective at **noon on the 30th day** after a complete filing (absent a stop order or a request for additional information), and requires **consent to service of process** appointing the Administrator as attorney to receive legal papers. The Administrator may also require exams, minimum standards, and fees."},
  {"id":890,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"**Common trap:** A newly hired individual **may not solicit or accept orders before their registration is effective**. \"The client called *me* first\" does not cure an unregistered agent's status — the unsolicited-order exemption is an *exempt transaction* concept that relieves the *firm/issuer* from certain obligations, not a license for an unregistered person to transact business.\n\n#### Exclusions: When an Individual Is NOT an Agent\n\nThe most heavily tested part of this subsection is the set of situations in which an individual effects securities transactions yet is **excluded from the definition of agent** and therefore **need not register**. Two ideas dominate."},
  {"id":891,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"**1. Purely clerical and ministerial employees.** An individual who performs only administrative, clerical, or back-office functions and who does **not** effect (or attempt to effect) securities transactions is not an agent — such a person simply never meets the definition. Answering phones, mailing statements, or processing paperwork is not \"effecting transactions.\"\n\n**2. Certain individuals who represent an *issuer*.** This is the heart of the topic and the source of most trick questions. An individual who represents an **issuer** is *excluded from the definition of agent* (no registration) when effecting transactions in the following categories:"},
  {"id":892,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"- Transactions in the specific enumerated **exempt securities** that trigger the exclusion (see list below);\n- **Exempt transactions** (see list below);\n- Transactions in certain **federal covered securities**; and\n- Transactions with the issuer's **own existing employees, partners, or directors** where **no commission or other remuneration is paid, directly or indirectly, for soliciting** any person in the state (e.g., an employee-stock-plan enrollment handled by an unpaid company officer).\n\nThe unifying principle: representing an *issuer* in one of these \"clean\" contexts is activity the states chose not to license."},
  {"id":893,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"##### The exclusion applies to ISSUER representation only — not broker-dealer representation\n\nThis is the point NASAA loves to test. The exempt-securities and exempt-transactions carve-outs **only** apply to an individual representing an **issuer**. There is **no** parallel carve-out for an individual representing a **broker-dealer**."},
  {"id":894,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"| The individual represents… | …in a transaction involving… | Agent? Register? |\n|---|---|---|\n| An **issuer** | A qualifying **exempt security** (e.g., U.S. Treasury bonds, muni bonds) | **No** — excluded, no registration |\n| An **issuer** | An **exempt transaction** | **No** — excluded, no registration |\n| An **issuer** | A **non-exempt** security in a **non-exempt** transaction | **Yes** — must register |\n| A **broker-dealer** | An **exempt security** (e.g., U.S. Treasury bonds) | **Yes** — must register |\n| A **broker-dealer** | **Anything** involving securities transactions | **Yes** — must register |"},
  {"id":895,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"**Common trap:** \"An individual sells **U.S. government securities** on behalf of a **broker-dealer**. Must the individual register as an agent?\" The tempting wrong answer is \"no, because government securities are exempt.\" The correct answer is **YES** — the exempt-security exclusion protects only *issuer* representatives. Because this person represents a *broker-dealer*, the exempt status of the product is irrelevant.\n\n**Exam tip:** Flip the same facts and the answer flips. \"An individual represents the **issuer** in selling that issuer's own **U.S. government securities**\" → **not an agent**, no registration required."},
  {"id":896,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"##### Reference: Which exempt securities trigger the issuer-agent exclusion\n\nThe issuer-agent exclusion does not reach every security the USA exempts; it is tied to specific enumerated categories. An individual representing an **issuer** is not an agent when effecting transactions in:"},
  {"id":897,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"- U.S. government and municipal (state/local) securities\n- Securities of foreign national governments with which the U.S. maintains diplomatic relations\n- Depository institution securities (banks, savings institutions, credit unions)\n- Certain short-term commercial paper (top-tier rated, $50,000 or greater denomination, maturity of nine months or less)\n- Investment contracts issued in connection with employee benefit (pension/profit-sharing) plans\n- Certain federal covered securities (e.g., exchange-listed and investment-company securities)"},
  {"id":898,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"**Note:** Other securities the USA generally exempts — for example, insurance company securities, regulated public utility and common carrier securities, and nonprofit (religious/educational/charitable) securities — do **not** by themselves extend the issuer-agent exclusion. And **variable annuities/variable life are not exempt at all** — they are securities requiring registration and a licensed agent.\n\n##### Reference: Exempt transactions (issuer's representative is not an agent)"},
  {"id":899,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"- **Isolated non-issuer transactions** (occasional, one-off resales)\n- **Unsolicited non-issuer orders** (the customer initiates; the BD did not solicit)\n- **Underwriter transactions** (between the issuer and an underwriter, or among underwriters)\n- **Institutional transactions** — sales to banks, insurance companies, investment companies, other broker-dealers, pension plans, and similar financial institutions\n- **Private placements / limited offerings** (offers to a limited number of non-institutional buyers, meeting the statutory conditions)\n- **Fiduciary transactions** by an executor, administrator, sheriff, marshal, receiver, trustee in bankruptcy, guardian, or conservator\n- **Transactions with existing security holders** (preemptive rights, warrants, conversions) where no commission is paid for soliciting"},
  {"id":900,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"**Exam tip:** Notice the built-in shortcut — the biggest categories of *institutional* and *unsolicited* business are exempt *transactions*. That is why an individual dealing only with those situations on behalf of an issuer often escapes agent registration, while a retail-facing individual almost never does.\n\n#### A Note on Snowbirds and No Place of Business"},
  {"id":901,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"An agent's registration obligation follows the broker-dealer's. If the BD is **not required to register** in a state — for example, it has **no place of business** there and deals only with an **existing customer who is merely temporarily present** in the state (a client on vacation) — then an agent representing that BD there is likewise not transacting business in a way that requires new registration. The relationship pre-existed and the customer's presence is transient. Do not over-extend this: soliciting *new* residents of the state, or maintaining any office there, defeats it."},
  {"id":902,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"**Common trap:** This relief depends on the client being *temporarily* in the state. If the customer **moves** and becomes a resident of the new state, the agent (and the firm) must become registered there to continue doing business with that person.\n\n#### Rapid-Fire Exam Summary"},
  {"id":903,"part":"Part IV","section":"D. Regulation of Agents of Broker-Dealers","anchor":"d-regulation-of-agents-of-broker-dealers","text":"- An **agent is always an individual**; a broker-dealer is (almost always) a **firm**.\n- \"Agent\" = an individual representing a **BD or issuer** in **effecting or attempting to effect** securities transactions.\n- **Purely clerical** staff are **not** agents.\n- The **exempt-security / exempt-transaction exclusions apply only to issuer representatives** — an individual representing a **BD** is an agent regardless of how exempt the product is.\n- An individual selling an issuer's securities to the issuer's **own employees for no commission** is **not** an agent.\n- Registration is required in **every state where the agent transacts business**, is filed on **Form U4**, and is **inoperative whenever the associated BD's registration is not in effect**.\n- When an agent **changes firms**, the **agent, the former BD, and the new BD** all notify the Administrator."},
  {"id":904,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"This subsection covers the \"product and issuer\" side of state securities law: what counts as a security in the first place, the three ways securities get registered at the state level, the long list of things that escape registration (either because they are exempt or because they were never securities at all), who an \"issuer\" is and when the people selling for an issuer must register, and finally the enforcement and antifraud powers the state Administrator holds over everyone. On the Series 65 this material is a heavy source of questions, and the traps are predictable once you understand the underlying logic.\n\n#### 1. What Is a \"Security\"? — The Definition and the Howey Test"},
  {"id":905,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"The Uniform Securities Act (USA) does not define a security with a single sentence; it gives a long **list**. If an instrument appears on that list, it is a security. The commonly tested items include:\n\n- Stock, preferred stock, and treasury stock\n- Bonds, debentures, notes, and other evidence of indebtedness\n- Transferable shares and certificates of interest in a profit-sharing agreement\n- **Investment contracts**\n- Certificates of interest in oil, gas, or mining title or lease\n- Warrants, rights, options on securities, and interests in a voting-trust certificate\n- Certificates of deposit **for a security** (a receipt for a security, not a bank CD)"},
  {"id":906,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"The catch-all is the **investment contract**, and that is where the **Howey test** comes in. In *SEC v. W.J. Howey Co.* (1946), the Supreme Court held that an investment contract — and therefore a security — exists when there is:\n\n1. An **investment of money**,\n2. In a **common enterprise**,\n3. With an **expectation of profits**,\n4. Resulting **solely (or primarily) from the efforts of others** — a promoter or a party other than the investor."},
  {"id":907,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"All four prongs must be met. The classic Howey facts: investors bought rows of orange trees and simultaneously signed a service contract letting Howey's company cultivate and sell the fruit and remit profits. The land sale alone was not a security, but bundled with the management contract it became an investment contract because the buyers expected profits from *someone else's* work."},
  {"id":908,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"**Exam tip:** The fourth prong is the decider. If the investor is expected to run the enterprise personally (e.g., a general partnership interest where every partner actively manages), it usually fails Howey and is not a security. A **limited** partnership interest, where limited partners are passive, generally *is* a security.\n\n**What is NOT a security (exclusions from the definition).** These never become securities, so registration questions do not even arise:"},
  {"id":909,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"- Insurance and fixed annuity contracts, whole life, term life, and endowment policies with a **guaranteed fixed benefit** (note: a **variable** annuity or variable life policy *is* a security because the investment risk is on the holder)\n- Commodities themselves and commodity **futures** contracts\n- Collectibles, precious metals, gold/silver bullion, real estate held for personal use, and condominiums (unless sold with a mandatory rental-pool/management arrangement, which can re-create a Howey investment contract)\n- Retirement plans and accounts as vehicles — an IRA or Keogh is *not itself* a security (although it holds securities)"},
  {"id":910,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"**Common trap:** A **fixed** annuity is not a security; a **variable** annuity is. A bank **CD** is not a security; a **certificate of deposit for a security** is. The exam swaps these constantly.\n\n#### 2. State Registration Methods\n\nAny security that is not exempt and is not a federal covered security must be **registered** in a state before it can be offered or sold there. The USA provides three methods. All three, once effective, are good for **one year**."},
  {"id":911,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"| Method | Who uses it | How it becomes effective | Key facts |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Notification / Filing** (Sec. 302) | Seasoned, established issuers with a track record (often already registered federally) | Automatically, on a short streamlined basis, absent a stop order | Fastest/least burdensome; reserved for issuers that meet operating-history and financial-condition tests |\n| **Coordination** (Sec. 303) | Issuers registering with the **SEC** under the Securities Act of 1933 **at the same time** (typical IPO) | Automatically, at the **same moment** the federal registration becomes effective, provided the documents have been on file with the Administrator for the required minimum period | Most **common** method for interstate offerings; state and federal registrations \"coordinate\" |\n| **Qualification** (Sec. 304) | Securities sold only within one state (**intrastate**) and not registered federally; anything that doesn't fit the other two | When the **Administrator so orders** — the Administrator has discretion over the effective date | Most **thorough/stringent**; requires the most disclosure |"},
  {"id":912,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"**Registration by Coordination** is the workhorse for companies going public across state lines: the issuer files essentially the same registration statement/prospectus it filed with the SEC, and state effectiveness is pegged to federal effectiveness (so long as the statement has sat on file with the Administrator for the minimum period — commonly cited as 10 days).\n\n**Registration by Qualification** is the only method available when there is no federal registration to coordinate with — the classic case is a purely **intrastate** offering. Because there is no SEC review to lean on, the Administrator demands the fullest disclosure and controls the effective date."},
  {"id":913,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"**Exam tip:** Remember the effective-date logic: coordination = effective *with the SEC*; qualification = effective *when the Administrator orders*. Any of the three methods can require the issuer to file a **consent to service of process** and to pay filing fees.\n\n**Note on federal covered securities:** Under the National Securities Markets Improvement Act (NSMIA), states may **not** require registration of federal covered securities (e.g., investment company/mutual fund shares, and securities listed on the NYSE, Nasdaq, or an exchange of equal standing). States may still require a **notice filing** (documents plus a fee) and always retain **antifraud** authority."},
  {"id":914,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"#### 3. Exemptions From Registration\n\nThe USA relieves many offerings from registration. Distinguish two very different categories:\n\n- **Exempt securities** — the *security itself* is exempt, no matter how many times or to whom it is sold. The exemption follows the security.\n- **Exempt transactions** — a *particular transaction* is exempt because of how or with whom it occurs. The exemption follows the deal, not the paper.\n\nIn **both** cases, the exemption is only from **registration** (and from filing of advertising/sales literature). It is **never** an exemption from the **antifraud** provisions.\n\n**Common exempt securities:**"},
  {"id":915,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"- U.S. government and **municipal** securities (any state, city, or political subdivision)\n- Securities of the Canadian government (and any Canadian province) and of a foreign national government the U.S. maintains diplomatic relations with\n- Securities of **banks, savings institutions, and trust companies**\n- Securities of **insurance companies** authorized to do business in the state\n- Securities of federally regulated common carriers and **public utilities**\n- Securities issued by nonprofit, religious, charitable, or fraternal organizations\n- Securities of federal covered issuers, including investment companies registered under the Investment Company Act of 1940\n- Securities issued in connection with an **employee benefit plan**\n- **Commercial paper** (promissory notes) with a maturity of **9 months (270 days) or less**, in denominations of at least **$50,000**, rated in one of the **top three** rating categories"},
  {"id":916,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"**Common exempt transactions** (these come up more often on the exam than exempt securities):"},
  {"id":917,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"- **Isolated non-issuer transactions** — an occasional, one-off resale not by the issuer\n- **Unsolicited non-issuer transactions** — a customer contacts the agent to buy or sell without solicitation. This is the **most common** exempt transaction; the Administrator may require the customer to acknowledge in writing that the order was unsolicited\n- Transactions between an issuer and an **underwriter**, or among underwriters\n- **Transactions with institutional/financial investors** — banks, insurance companies, investment companies, broker-dealers, pension/profit-sharing trusts, and other financial institutions\n- **Fiduciary transactions** — sales by an executor, administrator, sheriff, marsha"},
  {"id":918,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"l, receiver, trustee in bankruptcy, guardian, or conservator (note: a **custodian** or **ordinary trustee** is *not* on this list)\n- **Private placements** — offers to **no more than 10 non-institutional (retail) persons** in a **12-month** period, where the seller reasonably believes buyers purchase **for investment** (not resale), and **no commission** is paid for soliciting retail buyers. Offers to institutional buyers are **unlimited** and do not count toward the 10\n- **Preorganization certificates** — no more than **10 subscribers**, no commission paid, and no payment made by any subscriber\n- Transactions with **existing security holders** (rights, warrants, conversions), if no commission is paid for soliciting\n- **Manual exemption** — certain non-issuer transactions in securities listed in a recognized securities manual"},
  {"id":919,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"**Worked example — counting private-placement offers.** An issuer wants the private-placement exemption. In a 12-month window it makes offers to 8 pension funds, 3 banks, and 9 individual retail investors. Do the institutional offers count?\n\n- Institutional offers (8 pension funds + 3 banks = 11) are **unlimited** → they do not count.\n- Retail offers = **9**, which is **≤ 10** → still within the limit.\n- Result: the exemption is **preserved**. If the issuer had made offers to 11 retail investors, the exemption would be lost — and note it is the number of **offers**, not sales, that matters."},
  {"id":920,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"**Exam tip:** The private-placement ceiling is on **non-institutional offers made**, not on purchasers or dollars. **Common trap:** A security being *exempt* does not exempt the *seller* from registering as a broker-dealer or agent, and it never exempts anyone from antifraud liability.\n\n#### 4. Issuers, Issuer Agents, and Finders"},
  {"id":921,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"**Definition of an issuer.** An **issuer** is any person who **issues or proposes to issue a security**. The company raising capital is the issuer. A **non-issuer** transaction is one in which the proceeds do not go to the issuer — i.e., ordinary secondary-market trading among investors. (Special rule: for fractional undivided interests in oil, gas, or mining, there is deemed to be *no* issuer.)\n\n**Registration of an issuer's agents.** An **agent** is an *individual* (a natural person) who represents a broker-dealer or an issuer in effecting or attempting to effect securities transactions. Two rules matter:"},
  {"id":922,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"- An individual representing a **broker-dealer** in securities transactions is **always** an agent and must register (unless the role is purely **clerical/ministerial**).\n- An individual representing an **issuer** is **excluded** from the definition of agent (and need **not** register) when the transactions are in specified **exempt securities** (such as U.S. government, municipal, and certain financial-institution securities), or are **exempt transactions**, or involve certain employee-benefit-plan sales with no commission. Representing an issuer in the sale of **non-exempt** securities to the **public** *does* require registration as an agent."},
  {"id":923,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"**Common trap:** \"An individual sells only U.S. Treasury securities for the issuing entity, so she must register.\" False — representing an issuer in exempt securities places her *outside* the agent definition. But note that a person representing a **broker-dealer** in those very same Treasuries **is** an agent and must register. The exclusion is for **issuer** representatives, not broker-dealer representatives."},
  {"id":924,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"**Finders.** A **finder** is a person who, for compensation, brings together parties to a securities transaction — for example, introducing an issuer to potential investors or a firm to potential clients. When a finder receives **transaction-based compensation** tied to securities business, states increasingly require the finder to **register** (as an agent or broker-dealer). Many states have adopted specific finder-registration requirements. On the exam, treat referral-for-a-fee arrangements that touch securities transactions as generally triggering a registration requirement.\n\n#### 5. State Enforcement and Antifraud Authority"},
  {"id":925,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"**Jurisdiction — when does the Administrator have authority?** An offer or sale is subject to a state Administrator if the offer **originates in** that state, is **directed into** that state, or is **accepted in** that state. This means a single transaction can fall under more than one Administrator's jurisdiction. Two exceptions: offers made through a **bona fide newspaper** published out of state, or via a **radio/TV broadcast** originating outside the state, are **not** considered offers made in the state.\n\n**Powers of the Administrator.** The Administrator may:"},
  {"id":926,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"- Make, amend, and rescind **rules and orders**, and require **registration**\n- Conduct **investigations** (inside or outside the state), **administer oaths**, and **subpoena** people and records\n- Issue **cease-and-desist orders** — with or without a prior hearing\n- **Deny, suspend, revoke, cancel, or withdraw** registrations (for cause, generally after notice and opportunity for a hearing)\n- Require the filing of advertising and sales literature for non-exempt securities\n\nThe Administrator **cannot**:"},
  {"id":927,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"- **Issue an injunction** directly — the Administrator must **ask a court** to do so\n- **Arrest** anyone or bring criminal charges — those are referred to the **attorney general / district attorney**\n- **Award damages** to injured investors — that is for the **civil courts**\n\n**Antifraud authority reaches everyone.** This is the single most tested point in the subsection: the antifraud provisions apply to **all securities and all persons**, including **exempt securities**, **exempt transactions**, and **federal covered securities**. A security can be exempt from *registration* yet its seller can still be prosecuted for fraud. The Administrator never loses antifraud jurisdiction."},
  {"id":928,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"**Criminal penalties (the \"5 and 3\" and \"5-year\" rules).** A **willful** violation of the USA is a **criminal offense**. The maximum criminal penalty is a fine of up to **$5,000** and/or imprisonment of up to **3 years**. The **statute of limitations for criminal** prosecution is **5 years** from the alleged violation. (A defendant who proves he had no knowledge of the rule or order violated cannot be imprisoned.)"},
  {"id":929,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"**Civil liability and rescission (the \"2 and 3\" rule).** A purchaser who bought a security sold in violation of the Act may sue to recover. The **statute of limitations** is the **sooner of 2 years after discovery or 3 years after the sale/purchase**. The buyer recovers via **rescission** (tendering the security back) or, if it has already been sold, damages.\n\nThe recovery formula is:\n\n**Recovery = Purchase Price + Interest (at the state rate) − Income Received on the security + Reasonable Attorney's Fees and Court Costs**"},
  {"id":930,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"**Worked example — civil rescission.** An investor paid **$10,000** for a security sold in violation of the Act. Two years later she still holds it. The state legal interest rate is **6%** per year, she has collected **$400** in dividends over the two years, and reasonable attorney's fees and court costs total **$500**. She sues for rescission.\n\n- Purchase price = **$10,000**\n- Interest = 6% × $10,000 × 2 years = **$1,200**\n- Less income received (dividends) = **−$400**\n- Plus attorney's fees and court costs = **+$500**\n- **Recovery = $10,000 + $1,200 − $400 + $500 = $11,300**, and the investor tenders the security back to the seller."},
  {"id":931,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"Notice that **income the buyer received is subtracted** (she cannot keep both the dividends and full rescission), while interest at the state rate is **added**.\n\n**The rescission offer.** Rather than face a lawsuit, a seller who made a violative sale may send the buyer a **letter of rescission**, offering to buy the security back for the amount above. The buyer generally has **30 days** to accept or reject. If the buyer **does not respond within 30 days**, or accepts the offer, the buyer **loses the right to later sue** over that transaction."},
  {"id":932,"part":"Part IV","section":"E. Regulation of Securities and Issuers","anchor":"e-regulation-of-securities-and-issuers","text":"**Exam tip:** Memorize the two limitation pairs — **criminal: 5-year statute, 5,000 dollar fine, 3 years prison (\"5/5/3\")**; **civil: sooner of 2 years after discovery or 3 years after sale (\"2 and 3\")**. **Common trap:** The Administrator does not put anyone in jail, does not issue injunctions on its own, and does not hand investors a check for damages — courts do those. The Administrator investigates, orders, and refers."},
  {"id":933,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"This subsection covers what happens when the securities laws are broken: who enforces them, what tools that enforcer has, and what penalties a violator faces. On the Series 65 these questions reward precise knowledge of who can do what (the *Administrator* versus the *courts*), the exact standards that must be met before a registration is denied or revoked, and a small set of memorizable numbers (criminal penalties and statutes of limitations). Master the vocabulary and the numbers and this becomes one of the more predictable topics on the exam."},
  {"id":934,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"Throughout, \"the Act\" means the **Uniform Securities Act (USA)** — the model state law each state adapts — and \"the Administrator\" means the state securities official (or agency) charged with enforcing it.\n\n#### The Administrator's Authority: Rulemaking, Orders, Investigations, and Subpoenas\n\nThe Administrator is an *administrative* official, not a legislator and not a judge. Understanding those boundaries is the key to the whole subsection.\n\n**Rulemaking and orders.** The state legislature passes the statute (the Act itself). The Administrator may then **make, amend, and rescind rules, forms, and orders** necessary to carry the Act out. The distinction matters:"},
  {"id":935,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"| Instrument | Who issues it | Scope |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Statute (the Act)** | State legislature | The law itself |\n| **Rule** | Administrator | General application to everyone (like a mini-law) |\n| **Order** | Administrator | Applies to a specific person or specific situation |\n\nRules and orders carry the force of law, but they may **not conflict with the Act** and may not conflict with federal securities law. The Administrator cannot rewrite the statute — only the legislature can do that."},
  {"id":936,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"**Exam tip:** A *rule* applies to all persons; an *order* is directed at a specific applicant, registrant, or transaction. If a question describes something that affects everyone (e.g., a new recordkeeping requirement for all advisers), that is a rule.\n\n**Good-faith reliance protection.** No liability provision of the Act applies to anyone who acted in **good-faith reliance** on a rule, form, or order — even if that rule or order is later amended, rescinded, or held invalid by a court. This protects registrants who follow the Administrator's guidance."},
  {"id":937,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"**Investigations.** The Administrator may conduct investigations — **public or private, inside or outside the state** — to determine whether any person **has violated, is violating, or is about to violate** the Act. Note the breadth: the Administrator does **not** have to wait for an actual violation, and does not have to prove one before investigating. In the course of an investigation the Administrator may require written statements under oath."},
  {"id":938,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"**Subpoena power.** To conduct investigations, the Administrator may:\n- Administer oaths and affirmations,\n- **Subpoena witnesses** and compel their attendance,\n- Take evidence, and\n- Require the **production of books, records, correspondence, and other documents.**\n\nThis subpoena power reaches across state lines: an Administrator can compel testimony and records from persons located in other states, and one state's Administrator will assist another's."},
  {"id":939,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"**Common trap:** The Administrator **cannot** issue an injunction and **cannot** imprison anyone — those are judicial (court) powers, and criminal fines and imprisonment are imposed only by the courts. If a subpoenaed person refuses to cooperate (contumacy), the Administrator must go to **court**, and the court can hold that person in **contempt**. Likewise, to stop ongoing conduct with an order enforceable by contempt, the Administrator must obtain an **injunction from a court.** The Administrator investigates and issues administrative orders; the courts punish and enjoin.\n\n#### Administrative Actions Against Registrations: Deny, Suspend, Revoke, Cancel, Bar"},
  {"id":940,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"These actions concern a person's *registration* as an investment adviser (IA), investment adviser representative (IAR), broker-dealer, or agent — or the registration of a security. Distinguish carefully between **punitive** actions (which require wrongdoing) and **non-punitive** actions (which do not).\n\n**The two-part test for punitive action.** Before the Administrator may **deny, suspend, or revoke** a registration (or **bar/censure** a person), *both* of the following must be true:\n\n1. The action is **in the public interest**, **AND**\n2. A **specific cause** enumerated in the Act exists."},
  {"id":941,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"**Common trap:** \"Public interest\" **alone is never sufficient.** The exam loves an answer choice that says the Administrator revoked a registration \"because it was in the public interest.\" That is wrong — a stated statutory cause must also exist. Both prongs are always required."},
  {"id":942,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"**Enumerated causes** include that the applicant or registrant:\n- Filed an application that was **false or misleading** in a material way;\n- **Willfully violated** the Act (or another securities law);\n- Was **convicted of a securities-related misdemeanor, or any felony, within the past 10 years**;\n- Is **enjoined by a court** from engaging in the securities business;\n- Is the subject of an order by another state's Administrator or the SEC (within the past 10 years);\n- Engaged in **dishonest or unethical practices** in the securities business;\n- Is **insolvent**;\n- Is **not qualified** on the basis of training, experience, or knowledge; or\n- **Failed to pay** the required filing fee (a fee-based denial is cured when the fee is paid)."},
  {"id":943,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"**Exam tip:** Memorize the **10-year window** for convictions and prior orders. A felony conviction 11 years ago is *not*, by itself, a cause. Also note the qualification standard: the Administrator **may not** base an order **solely on an IAR's lack of experience** — lack of experience must be combined with lack of knowledge or training. And the Administrator may not use a fact already known at the time registration was granted as grounds to later revoke."},
  {"id":944,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"**Cancellation — the non-punitive termination.** The Administrator **cancels** (not \"revokes\") a registration when a registrant:\n- **No longer exists**,\n- **Cannot be located** (e.g., mail is returned undeliverable),\n- Has **ceased doing business**, or\n- Is **declared mentally incompetent.**\n\nCancellation involves **no finding of wrongdoing** and requires no public-interest/cause test. It is purely administrative housekeeping."},
  {"id":945,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"**Common trap:** Distinguish *cancel* from *revoke*. Revocation is **punitive** (a penalty for cause). Cancellation is **non-punitive** (the registrant simply cannot be located, no longer exists, or is incompetent). A firm whose owner dies is *canceled*, not *revoked*.\n\n**Withdrawal.** A registrant may voluntarily **withdraw** its registration. Withdrawal becomes effective **30 days** after filing (unless a proceeding is pending). Importantly, the Administrator retains **jurisdiction for one year** after a withdrawal becomes effective — a registrant cannot escape a pending or forthcoming enforcement action simply by withdrawing."},
  {"id":946,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"| Action | Punitive? | Initiated by | Requires cause + public interest? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Deny / Suspend / Revoke / Bar | Yes | Administrator | Yes (both prongs) |\n| Cease-and-desist | Yes (enforcement) | Administrator | Cause; may issue with or without hearing |\n| Cancel | No | Administrator | No (person gone/incompetent) |\n| Withdraw | No | Registrant | No (voluntary; 1-yr retained jurisdiction) |\n\n#### Cease-and-Desist Orders and Injunctions"},
  {"id":947,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"A **cease-and-desist (C&D) order** is the Administrator's tool to tell a person to **stop** a violation. Key features:\n- The Administrator may issue a C&D **with or without a prior hearing** (it can be summary — issued immediately when needed).\n- A C&D **carries no fine or penalty by itself** and the Administrator **cannot itself enforce it.** It is simply a directive to stop.\n- If the person **ignores** the C&D, the Administrator must apply to a **court for an injunction.** Violating the court's injunction can then result in contempt, fines, or imprisonment imposed by the **court** — not the Administrator."},
  {"id":948,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"**Exam tip:** A cease-and-desist order is administrative and unenforceable on its own; an **injunction** is judicial and enforceable through contempt. The Administrator issues the first; only a **court** issues the second.\n\n#### Hearings, Summary Orders, and Appeals\n\nDue process runs through all of this. A person facing an adverse order is generally entitled to **notice** and an **opportunity for a hearing**, with the Administrator issuing **written findings of fact and conclusions of law.**"},
  {"id":949,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"- **Right to a hearing:** If a person requests a hearing in writing, the Administrator must hold it within **15 days** of receiving the request.\n- **Summary (interim) orders:** The Administrator may, **by order**, **summarily postpone or suspend** a registration *pending final determination* of a proceeding — i.e., before a full hearing — but must **promptly notify** the affected person and give an opportunity to be heard.\n- **Appeal to the courts:** A person aggrieved by a final order may seek judicial review by filing a petition in the appropriate court **within 60 days** of the order. Filing an appeal does **not automatically stay** (halt) the order unless the court so directs."},
  {"id":950,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"**Exam tip:** Two numbers to keep straight — **15 days** for the Administrator to hold a requested hearing, and **60 days** to appeal a final order to court.\n\n#### Criminal Penalties\n\nCriminal liability under the Act is reserved for **willful** violations. The standard Uniform Securities Act criminal penalty — the number the Series 65 expects — is:\n\n> **Fine of up to $5,000 and/or imprisonment for up to 3 years** per willful violation.\n\nA memory aid: **\"3 / 5 / 5\"** — up to **3** years' imprisonment, up to a **$5,000** fine, and a **5**-year criminal statute of limitations (covered below)."},
  {"id":951,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"Three important qualifiers:\n- **\"Willful\" does not require intent to break the law.** It only means the person intended to do the act; the person need not have known the act was illegal.\n- **No-knowledge defense for rules/orders:** A person may **not be imprisoned** for violating a *rule or order* if they prove they had **no knowledge** of that rule or order. (This defense applies to rules and orders, not to violating the Act's core provisions.)\n- Criminal cases are brought and penalties are imposed by the **courts** (typically referred to the state Attorney General for prosecution), never by the Administrator directly."},
  {"id":952,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"**Comparison for context** (state vs. federal acts — the state USA figure is the one most heavily tested on a state-law exam):\n\n| Law | Max criminal fine | Max imprisonment |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Uniform Securities Act (state)** | **$5,000** | **3 years** |\n| Investment Advisers Act of 1940 | $10,000 | 5 years |\n| Securities Act of 1933 | $10,000 | 5 years |\n\n**Common trap:** Don't confuse the state USA numbers ($5,000 / 3 years) with the federal Investment Advisers Act numbers ($10,000 / 5 years). The question stem will tell you which law applies.\n\n#### Civil Liability and Rescission"},
  {"id":953,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"Civil liability is the **private remedy**: a wronged **purchaser sues the seller** (or a client sues the adviser) to get their money back. It arises when a security or advisory service is sold/rendered in **violation** of the Act — for example, sale of an **unregistered non-exempt security**, sale by an **unregistered agent**, or a sale made through a **material misstatement or omission.**\n\n**What the buyer can recover (rescission).** If the buyer **still owns** the security, they may **tender it back** and recover:\n\n> **Recovery = Consideration paid + Interest at the state's legal rate − Income already received (dividends/interest) + Court costs + Reasonable attorney's fees**"},
  {"id":954,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"**Worked example:**\n- An investor buys a security for **$20,000** in a transaction that violated the Act.\n- She holds it for **2 years** and receives **$1,000** in total dividends.\n- The state's legal interest rate is **8%.**\n\nStep 1 — Interest: $20,000 × 8% × 2 years = **$3,200**\nStep 2 — Subtract income received: −**$1,000**\nStep 3 — Recovery = $20,000 + $3,200 − $1,000 = **$22,200**, plus court costs and reasonable attorney's fees, **upon tendering the security back** to the seller."},
  {"id":955,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"**If the buyer has already sold** the security (no longer owns it), they recover **damages** instead of full rescission: essentially the amount they would have recovered on tender **minus** what they received when they disposed of the security (so both income received and the sale proceeds reduce the claim, while interest is still added).\n\n**Advisory clients** have a parallel remedy: a client who received services in violation may recover the **advisory fees paid plus any loss caused by the advice, plus interest, minus any income received,** together with costs and attorney's fees."},
  {"id":956,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"**Right (offer) of rescission.** A seller who discovers they made a sale in violation may head off a lawsuit by sending a **written offer of rescission** — an offer to buy the security back for the price paid plus interest, less income received. The buyer has **30 days** to accept. **If the buyer does not accept a valid offer within 30 days, the buyer loses the right to sue.**\n\n**Exam tip:** In a rescission calculation, you **add interest** and **subtract income (dividends/interest) received.** A common wrong answer forgets to subtract the income or forgets to add interest. Also remember: the buyer must **tender the security back** to collect full rescission.\n\n#### Statutes of Limitations"},
  {"id":957,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"A statute of limitations sets the deadline for bringing an action. The USA sets separate clocks for criminal and civil matters — memorize both.\n\n| Type of action | Statute of limitations |\n|---|---|\n| **Criminal** prosecution | Within **5 years** of the alleged violation |\n| **Civil** action (rescission/damages) | The **sooner** of **2 years after discovery** of the violation **or 3 years after** the sale/contract |\n\nFor the civil clock, think **\"2 and 3, whichever comes first.\"** If the fraud is discovered quickly, the 2-year-from-discovery clock usually controls; if it stays hidden, the 3-year-from-sale cap can bar the claim even before discovery."},
  {"id":958,"part":"Part IV","section":"F. Remedies and Administrative Provisions","anchor":"f-remedies-and-administrative-provisions","text":"**Worked example (civil SOL):** A security is sold in violation on **January 1, 2024.** If the buyer discovers the violation on **June 1, 2025**, they have until **June 1, 2027** (2 years after discovery) — *but* the 3-year outer limit expires **January 1, 2027**, so the **earlier** date, **January 1, 2027**, is the true deadline.\n\n**Exam tip:** The single most tested numbers in this subsection are **criminal SOL = 5 years**, **civil SOL = sooner of 2 years (discovery) or 3 years (sale)**, and **criminal penalty = $5,000 / 3 years.** Lock these in and you'll capture most of the points this topic offers."},
  {"id":959,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"Everything an investment adviser (IA) says to a client or prospect — from the disclosure documents it hands over, to the contract it signs, to a tweet or a homepage banner — is regulated. The unifying idea is the adviser's **fiduciary duty**: the obligation to act in the client's best interest, which requires **full and fair disclosure of all material facts**, especially conflicts of interest. This subsection walks through the required disclosures, the things an adviser may never say, the rules governing advisory contracts, and the standards for advertising and electronic communications.\n\n#### Required Disclosures: The Brochure and Brochure Delivery"},
  {"id":960,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"The core disclosure document is the adviser's **brochure**, which is **Form ADV Part 2**. It must be written in **plain English** and delivered to clients. Part 2 has two pieces:\n\n| Document | Common name | What it discloses |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Form ADV Part 2A** | The \"firm brochure\" | Services, fee schedule, types of clients, methods of analysis, conflicts of interest, disciplinary history, business practices |\n| **Form ADV Part 2B** | The \"brochure supplement\" | Information about the specific **individuals** (supervised persons) who actually give advice to that client — their education, business background, and disciplinary events |"},
  {"id":961,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"**The brochure delivery rule (the \"48-hour rule\").** The adviser must deliver the brochure to a client or prospective client under one of two timing options:\n\n- **At least 48 hours before** entering into the advisory contract — this 48-hour gap serves as the \"cooling-off\" period, so **no separate termination right is required**; **or**\n- **At the time of entering into** the contract, **but only if** the client is given the right to **terminate without penalty within 5 business days**.\n\nThink of it as \"48 hours before, or at signing with a 5-day free look.\""},
  {"id":962,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"**Annual delivery.** Each year, within **120 days of the end of the adviser's fiscal year**, the adviser must deliver, free of charge, **either** (1) an updated brochure that includes a **summary of material changes**, **or** (2) just the summary of material changes together with an **offer** to provide the full updated brochure."},
  {"id":963,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"- **Worked example (annual delivery deadline):** If the adviser's fiscal year ends December 31, the deadline is 120 days later. Counting the days after December 31: 31 (Jan) + 28 (Feb) + 31 (Mar) + 30 (Apr) = 120 days → **April 30**. The exam loves this \"120 days\" figure. (Do not confuse it with the **90-day** deadline for *filing* the annual updating amendment to Form ADV — that is a separate requirement.)\n\n**Exam tip:** The brochure need **not** be delivered to clients who are **registered investment companies** (mutual funds) or to clients receiving only **impersonal advice costing less than $500 per year**. Watch for those two carve-outs."},
  {"id":964,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"**Common trap:** Candidates confuse \"48 hours\" with \"5 days.\" Remember: **48 hours before** signing = no withdrawal right needed; **at signing** = client gets the **5-business-day** penalty-free termination window.\n\n#### Disclosure of Material Conflicts of Interest\n\nBecause the adviser is a fiduciary, it must disclose — clearly and in advance — any **material conflict of interest** that could affect its advice or that might cause it to render advice that is not disinterested. Disclosure lets the client give **informed consent**; hiding the conflict is a violation regardless of whether the client was actually harmed.\n\nCommonly tested examples of conflicts that must be disclosed:"},
  {"id":965,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"- The adviser or its reps earn **commissions** on products they recommend (dual registration as an agent/IAR).\n- **Soft-dollar** arrangements or use of an affiliated broker-dealer.\n- Acting as **principal or agent** in a transaction with a client (which also requires written disclosure **and** client consent **before** the completion of that specific trade).\n- **Compensation for referrals**, or a financial interest in a recommended security.\n- Charging **higher-than-typical fees**, or having **custody** of client assets."},
  {"id":966,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"**Exam tip:** The tested standard is \"disclose, then obtain informed consent.\" A conflict is **not** automatically prohibited — an **undisclosed** conflict is what gets an adviser in trouble.\n\n#### Unlawful Representations Concerning Registration\n\nIt is a prohibited and unethical practice to state or imply that registration means the government has **approved, endorsed, or passed on the merits** of the adviser or its services. The correct, legally required understanding is:\n\n> **Registration does not imply approval.** Being registered with the SEC or a state means the required forms were filed — it says **nothing** about the quality, honesty, or ability of the adviser."},
  {"id":967,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"Related unlawful statements:\n\n- Claiming the SEC or a state administrator has **\"approved\"** or **\"sponsored\"** the firm.\n- Misrepresenting the meaning of designations, or of terms like **\"Registered Investment Adviser (RIA)\"** as if it were a badge of merit.\n- Misstating **qualifications** (education, experience, credentials).\n\n**Common trap:** An adviser may **truthfully state that it is registered** — that is permitted. The violation is suggesting registration equals a government **seal of approval**. The exam phrase to memorize is \"registration does not imply approval or endorsement.\"\n\n#### Prohibition on Performance Guarantees"},
  {"id":968,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"An adviser may **not guarantee** a client that a specific result will be achieved — for example, promising that a security will not lose value, that gains are assured, or that the account will hit a set return. Markets carry risk, and any guarantee against loss or of a specific profit is inherently misleading. This is a straightforward prohibited practice."},
  {"id":969,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"**Exam tip:** Distinguish this from lawful, factual statements. Describing a bond's **stated (fixed) coupon**, or noting that an **FDIC-insured** instrument protects principal up to limits, is not a prohibited \"guarantee\" — those are contractual/statutory features. The prohibition targets the **adviser guaranteeing investment performance or against loss**.\n\n**Common trap:** \"You can't lose money in this account\" from an IAR is a guarantee-against-loss violation, even if said informally or verbally.\n\n#### Investment Advisory Contracts"},
  {"id":970,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"Under the NASAA Model Rule, a state-registered adviser's contract must be **in writing** and must address several specific points. Federal covered advisers are governed by parallel provisions of the Investment Advisers Act of 1940 (Section 205), which impose the same substantive terms but do not themselves require a written contract. Required/prohibited contract terms include:"},
  {"id":971,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"| Contract requirement | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| **No assignment without consent** | The contract may not be **assigned** to another party without the **client's consent**. |\n| **Notice of partnership change** | If the adviser is a **partnership**, the contract must provide that the adviser will **notify the client of any change in the membership** of the partnership within a **reasonable time**. |\n| **No performance-based fees** | Compensation may **not** be based on a share of capital gains or capital appreciation, **except as permitted** (see below). |\n| **Written terms** (state level) | The contract discloses services, term, the **fee or fee formula**, and the amount to be **refunded** on prepaid fees if the contract is terminated. |"},
  {"id":972,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"**Assignment explained.** \"Assignment\" includes an outright transfer of the contract and also a change in **controlling ownership** of the adviser. For a **partnership**, assignment is triggered by a change involving a **majority** of the partners (a \"material\" change in membership). A **minority** change in partners does **not** count as an assignment — but the client must still be **notified** of it within a reasonable time. Because advisory services are personal, the client's consent is required so they are never silently handed to a different firm.\n\n**Refund of prepaid fees.** If a client prepays and later terminates, the adviser refunds the **unearned** portion."},
  {"id":973,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"- **Worked example (prepaid fee refund):** A client prepays a **$2,400** annual fee on January 1. The client terminates at the end of March, having received **3 of 12 months** of service.\n  - Refund = Total prepaid × (Unused months ÷ Total months)\n  - Refund = $2,400 × (9 ÷ 12) = **$1,800**\n  - The adviser keeps $600 (the 3 months earned) and refunds $1,800.\n\n**Exam tip:** Requiring **substantial prepayment** of fees (for a **state-registered** adviser: **more than $500, six or more months in advance**; the SEC threshold is **$1,200 or more**, six or more months in advance) triggers a **balance-sheet** disclosure requirement in Form ADV Part 2 — a favorite detail."},
  {"id":974,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"##### The Performance-Fee Exception (Qualified Clients)\n\nThe default rule bans fees calculated as a share of gains, because they can tempt an adviser to take excessive risk (heads the adviser wins, tails the client loses). The main exception allows performance fees **only** when the client is a **qualified client** — a client who is financially sophisticated enough to fend for themselves. Under SEC Rule 205-3, a qualified client is one who has **at least $1.1 million in assets under management with the adviser**, **or** a **net worth of more than $2.2 million** (these dollar figures are inflation-adjusted; older exam materials may cite $1 million / $2 million)."},
  {"id":975,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"- Net worth for a natural person **excludes** the value of the **primary residence**.\n- The fee arrangement must still be **fair** and disclosed.\n\n**Exam tip:** Another permitted structure is a **fulcrum fee**, which adjusts symmetrically up **and** down relative to a benchmark (used with registered investment companies). Straight \"we keep 20% of the profits\" is only allowed with **qualified clients**.\n\n**Common trap:** Even for a qualified client, the adviser must still meet all other fiduciary and disclosure duties — qualifying for the exception does not waive suitability or conflict disclosure.\n\n#### Correspondence and Advertising"},
  {"id":976,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"All communications to the public — **letters, emails, texts, digital messaging, websites, blogs, and social media** — are treated as advertising when they promote advisory services, and they may **not be false or misleading**. The federal standard is the **SEC Marketing Rule (Rule 206(4)-1)**, which consolidated the old advertising and cash-solicitation rules. Under it, an advertisement may **not**:"},
  {"id":977,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"- Make an **untrue statement** of material fact or omit a material fact.\n- Make a **material statement the adviser cannot substantiate** on demand.\n- Include **untrue or misleading implications or inferences**.\n- Discuss **benefits without fair and balanced treatment** of associated **material risks or limitations**.\n- Reference **specific investment advice** in a way that is not fair and balanced (**cherry-picking** winners).\n- Present **performance results** in a misleading way."},
  {"id":978,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"**Performance advertising specifics** the exam may touch: net-of-fee performance generally must accompany gross performance, and certain time-period and disclosure requirements apply. The overarching theme is **fair, balanced, and not misleading**.\n\n##### Social Media, Email/Digital Messaging, and Websites"},
  {"id":979,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"- **Static vs. interactive content.** A firm's **website content, a fixed \"profile,\" or a pinned bio** is generally treated as **static** content the firm creates and must supervise/approve. Real-time, back-and-forth **interactive** posts are treated more like conversation but are still subject to recordkeeping and anti-fraud rules.\n- **Entanglement and adoption.** A firm can become responsible for **third-party** content if it was **entangled** in creating it or later **adopts/endorses** it (e.g., \"liking,\" sharing, or linking to a favorable third-party post can make that content the firm's own advertising).\n- **Recordkeeping.** Business-related electronic communications — including texts and social-media messages — must be **retained** as required records. Advisers commonly prohibit using unmonitored personal channels for business."},
  {"id":980,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"##### Testimonials and Endorsements\n\nHistorically, testimonials were **flatly prohibited** in adviser advertising. The **SEC Marketing Rule now permits testimonials and endorsements**, but only with strict conditions. Know both the old default (still a tempting wrong answer) and the modern conditions.\n\n| Old rule (pre-2021) | Current Marketing Rule |\n|---|---|\n| Testimonials **prohibited** in adviser ads | Testimonials/endorsements **permitted with conditions** |\n\nConditions for a permitted testimonial/endorsement:"},
  {"id":981,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"- **Clear and prominent disclosure** of (1) whether the speaker is a **client** or non-client, (2) whether the person is **compensated**, and (3) any **material conflicts of interest**.\n- **Adviser oversight** — the adviser must supervise the compliance of the testimonial/endorsement.\n- **Written agreement** with the promoter if compensation exceeds a **de minimis** amount (**more than $1,000 in a 12-month period**).\n- **Disqualification** — certain **\"bad actors\"** (persons with disqualifying regulatory or criminal events) may not be paid more than the de minimis amount to give endorsements."},
  {"id":982,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"**Worked example (de minimis threshold):** A firm pays an influencer **$75 per referral**. After the influencer sends 20 referrals in a year, total compensation is 20 × $75 = **$1,500**, which **exceeds $1,000** — so a **written promoter agreement** and the full disclosure conditions are required. Had it been a **$40 gift card** (under $1,000/year), the written-agreement condition would not be triggered, though disclosure and oversight still apply."},
  {"id":983,"part":"Part IV","section":"G. Communication with Clients and Prospects","anchor":"g-communication-with-clients-and-prospects","text":"**Exam tip:** If a question is framed around the **traditional NASAA/Uniform Securities Act** unethical-practices list, \"using **testimonials**\" may be presented as **prohibited**. If the question references the **current SEC Marketing Rule**, testimonials are **allowed with disclosure and oversight**. Read which framework the question invokes.\n\n**Common trap:** A **paid** endorsement that is not disclosed as paid is a violation under either framework — the compensation and conflict **must** be disclosed clearly and prominently. Silence about payment is the classic wrong practice."},
  {"id":984,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"This subsection ties together the ethical backbone of the entire exam. Nearly every \"prohibited practices\" question flows from one root principle: an investment adviser (IA) is a **fiduciary**. Master the fiduciary standard and the specific rules below become logical applications of it rather than a list to memorize.\n\n#### The Fiduciary Duty of an Investment Adviser"},
  {"id":985,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"A fiduciary must place the client's interests ahead of its own at all times. This standard arises under the Investment Advisers Act of 1940 and was reinforced by the Supreme Court in *SEC v. Capital Gains Research Bureau* (1963), which held that Section 206 imposes a duty to disclose all material conflicts of interest. The fiduciary duty has two components:"},
  {"id":986,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"- **Duty of loyalty** — Act in the client's best interest, avoid conflicts of interest, and where a conflict cannot be avoided, **fully and fairly disclose** it so the client can give informed consent. Loyalty means not favoring the adviser's own compensation, affiliates, or proprietary products.\n- **Duty of care** — Provide advice that is suitable and in the client's best interest, seek best execution of trades, and monitor the account over the course of the relationship."},
  {"id":987,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"Contrast this with the standard historically applied to broker-dealer agents. The IA fiduciary standard is higher than the older \"suitability only\" floor, and disclosure plus informed consent is the mechanism that legitimizes an otherwise conflicted transaction. (Broker-dealers now operate under a \"best interest\" standard of their own, but the adviser's fiduciary duty remains the more demanding benchmark.)"},
  {"id":988,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Exam tip:** The single most tested fiduciary concept is **disclosure**. When a question describes a conflict (the adviser earns a commission on a recommended product, has a financial interest in a security, or acts as principal), the correct answer is almost always \"disclose the conflict in writing and obtain the client's consent,\" not \"the practice is automatically prohibited.\" Disclosure cures most conflicts; concealment is the violation.\n\n**Common trap:** A fiduciary cannot disclaim its fiduciary duty by contract. A \"hedge clause\" or waiver purporting to limit the adviser's liability for its own misconduct is prohibited, even if the client signs it."},
  {"id":989,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"#### Compensation: Fees, Commissions, and Performance-Based Fees\n\nAn adviser's compensation is a built-in conflict, so its form and disclosure are heavily regulated.\n\n**Permitted fee structures.** IAs may charge a percentage of assets under management (AUM), a flat/fixed fee, an hourly fee, or a subscription/retainer. All fees must be **reasonable** relative to services rendered. Fees need not match the industry average, but grossly excessive fees violate the fiduciary duty. The fee schedule and how fees are calculated and billed must be disclosed in **Form ADV Part 2A (the brochure)**."},
  {"id":990,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Commissions and dual registration.** An adviser (or its associated persons) who also acts as a broker-dealer agent may earn commissions on securities it recommends. This creates a conflict, permitted only if **disclosed**. If the adviser wants to act as **principal** (selling a security out of its own inventory to a client) or as **agent for the other side of a client trade**, Section 206(3) requires **written disclosure to, and consent obtained from, the client before completion of each such transaction** — transaction-by-transaction consent, not a blanket authorization."},
  {"id":991,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Prepayment of fees.** Under NASAA rules, an adviser that collects **more than $500 in fees, six or more months in advance**, is deemed to have a \"substantial prepayment\" and must include an audited **balance sheet** in its brochure. This threshold is a favorite exam number."},
  {"id":992,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Performance-based fees.** A performance-based fee is compensation based on capital gains or appreciation of the client's account (for example, \"20% of profits\"). These are **generally prohibited** under Section 205 of the Advisers Act because they tempt the adviser to take excessive risk with the client's money. Rule 205-3 provides an exception — a performance fee is permitted only for a **\"qualified client,\"** defined as a client who meets one of these tests:"},
  {"id":993,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"| Qualified client test | Threshold (current SEC-adjusted figure) |\n|---|---|\n| Assets under management with the adviser | at least **$1,100,000** |\n| Net worth (individual, excluding primary residence) | more than **$2,200,000** |\n| Alternatively | a \"qualified purchaser\" (≥ $5 million in investments) or a knowledgeable employee of the adviser |"},
  {"id":994,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Worked example (performance-fee eligibility):** A prospective client has $600,000 invested with the adviser and a net worth of $2.4 million (excluding home). She fails the AUM test ($600,000 < $1.1 million) but **passes** the net worth test ($2.4 million > $2.2 million). Because the tests are alternatives, she **is** a qualified client and a performance fee is permitted."},
  {"id":995,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Exam tip:** Some older materials list $1,000,000 AUM / $2,100,000 net worth. The SEC adjusts these for inflation; the current values are **$1.1 million / $2.2 million**. Either way, the exam concept is that performance fees are barred for ordinary retail clients and allowed only for sophisticated, wealthy investors after disclosure.\n\n**Common trap:** A normal AUM fee (the same percentage whether the account rises or falls) is **not** a performance fee and is allowed for any client. Only a fee that shares in gains or appreciation triggers the qualified-client rule.\n\n#### Soft Dollars and the Section 28(e) Safe Harbor"},
  {"id":996,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"\"Soft dollars\" arise when an adviser directs client brokerage (commissions) to a broker-dealer and, in return, receives **research or brokerage services** rather than cash. Because the client pays the commission but the adviser receives the benefit, this is a conflict of interest. **Section 28(e)** of the Securities Exchange Act creates a **safe harbor**: an adviser does not breach its fiduciary duty by paying more than the lowest available commission, provided the products or services received provide **lawful and appropriate assistance in the investment decision-making process** and the adviser makes a good-faith determination that the commissions are reasonable relative to the value received."},
  {"id":997,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"| Inside the safe harbor (permitted) | Outside the safe harbor (prohibited use of soft dollars) |\n|---|---|\n| Research reports, financial analysis | Rent, office furniture, and overhead |\n| Software that analyzes securities/portfolios | Travel, entertainment, meals |\n| Attendance at research seminars | Salaries and personal expenses |\n| Trade execution and clearance services | Computer hardware for general office use |"},
  {"id":998,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Exam tip:** The dividing line is \"does it help me make investment decisions or execute trades?\" If yes, it likely qualifies. Anything that primarily benefits the **adviser's own operations** (overhead, personal items) falls outside 28(e) and must be paid with the adviser's hard dollars. Soft-dollar arrangements must also be **disclosed** in Form ADV.\n\n#### Custody of Client Funds and Securities\n\n**Custody** means holding, directly or indirectly, client funds or securities, or having **any authority to obtain possession** of them. An adviser has custody if it:"},
  {"id":999,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"- Physically holds client cash or certificates;\n- Has authority to **withdraw funds or securities** from the client's account (including the ability to **deduct advisory fees directly** — this alone is deemed custody);\n- Acts as **general partner** of a limited partnership, **trustee** of a trust, or in a comparable capacity giving it access to client assets;\n- Receives client checks made payable to the adviser (as opposed to third-party checks it forwards promptly).\n\nUnder the NASAA custody rule (mirroring Advisers Act Rule 206(4)-2), an adviser with custody must:"},
  {"id":1000,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"1. **Use a qualified custodian** — a bank or a registered broker-dealer that holds the assets in a separate account under the client's name (or in the adviser's name as agent or trustee for clients).\n2. **Notify the client in writing** of the qualified custodian's name and address and how the assets are held.\n3. Have a **reasonable belief that the qualified custodian sends account statements at least quarterly** directly to the client.\n4. Undergo an **annual surprise examination** by an **independent public accountant** who verifies the client funds and securities without advance notice.\n5. If the adviser (or an affiliate) is itself the custodian, obtain an annual **internal control report** (e.g., SOC-1/SAS 70) from an independent accountant."},
  {"id":1001,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Minimum financial requirements (NASAA, state-registered IAs):**\n\n| Situation | Minimum net worth (or bonding alternative) |\n|---|---|\n| IA has **custody** of client funds/securities | **$35,000** |\n| IA has **discretion** but no custody | **$10,000** |\n| Net worth falls below the required minimum | Notify the Administrator **by the close of the next business day**, then file a financial report by the close of business the following day |"},
  {"id":1002,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Exam tip:** Deducting advisory fees straight from the client account **is custody** under the rule. However, many states grant relief from the **surprise examination** requirement when custody arises *solely* from fee deduction and safeguards are met (fees billed with client authorization, statements sent by the qualified custodian). Watch for questions distinguishing \"custody exists\" (yes, fee deduction counts) from \"surprise exam required\" (often excused for fee-deduction-only custody)."},
  {"id":1003,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Common trap:** Simply having **discretionary authority** to place trades is **not** custody — the adviser decides trades but never possesses the assets. Custody requires the ability to *withdraw or possess* funds or securities. Distinguish the $10,000 (discretion) versus $35,000 (custody) net-worth figures.\n\n#### Discretion and Trading Authorization"},
  {"id":1004,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Discretionary authority** is the power to decide, without contacting the client first, at least one of the **three A's**: the **Asset** (which security), the **Action** (buy or sell), or the **Amount** (how many shares). Deciding only the **time or price** of a trade the client has already ordered (\"buy 100 XYZ, you pick a good moment today\") is **not** discretion."},
  {"id":1005,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"- **State (NASAA) rule:** The IA must have **written** discretionary authorization. It may exercise discretion on **oral authorization for up to the first 10 business days** following the initial discretionary transaction, provided the written authority is obtained within that period.\n- **Trading authorization** may also be granted via a **limited power of attorney** (trading only) versus a **full power of attorney** (which permits withdrawals and generally creates custody)."},
  {"id":1006,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Common trap:** Time-and-price-only discretion does **not** require written authorization and does not make the account \"discretionary.\" Only choosing the security, the action, or the amount triggers the written-authorization requirement.\n\n#### Standard of Care, Suitability, and Anti-Money Laundering (AML)\n\n**Suitability and best interest.** Consistent with the duty of care, every recommendation must fit the client's financial situation, objectives, risk tolerance, time horizon, and needs — gathered through a documented client profile. For advisers, suitability is elevated to a **best-interest** obligation rather than a mere \"not unsuitable\" floor."},
  {"id":1007,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Anti-Money Laundering (AML).** AML obligations flow from the **Bank Secrecy Act** and the **USA PATRIOT Act**, administered by **FinCEN**. Key components tested:"},
  {"id":1008,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"- **Customer Identification Program (CIP)** — Verify the identity of each customer (name, date of birth, address, taxpayer ID) and check names against government (OFAC) lists.\n- **Currency Transaction Report (CTR)** — Filed for cash transactions **exceeding $10,000** in a single business day.\n- **Suspicious Activity Report (SAR)** — Filed for suspicious transactions (generally **$5,000 or more** for broker-dealers) where money laundering or fraud is suspected; the firm may **not** \"tip off\" the customer that a SAR was filed.\n- **Structuring** — Deliberately breaking a large cash transaction into smaller amounts (e.g., several $9,500 deposits) to evade the CTR threshold is itself a crime."},
  {"id":1009,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Exam tip:** Memorize the two numbers together — **CTR = cash over $10,000**, **SAR = suspicious activity (BD threshold $5,000)**. Also remember that structuring to avoid reporting is illegal regardless of the source of funds.\n\n#### Conflicts of Interest and Prohibited Business Practices\n\nThe following practices are core \"unethical business practices\" that appear repeatedly. Each is an application of the fiduciary duty of loyalty or of the anti-fraud provisions (Section 206 and the NASAA Model Rule on Unethical Business Practices)."},
  {"id":1010,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Loans to or from clients.** An IA generally may **not borrow money or securities from, or lend to, a client.** Exception: the client is in the business of lending (a bank, broker-dealer, or affiliate) or is an affiliate of the adviser. A broker-dealer agent likewise may not lend to or borrow from a customer unless the customer is a lending institution or an immediate family member (firm-specific rules apply)."},
  {"id":1011,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Sharing in profits/losses.** An IA may **not** share directly in the profits or losses of a client's account (other than a permitted performance fee for a qualified client). For **broker-dealer agents**, sharing is permitted only if the agent receives **written approval from the firm**, shares **in direct proportion to the agent's own financial contribution**, and the account is a **joint account** — but for an IA the sharing arrangement is barred outright.\n\n**Client confidentiality.** Nonpublic client information may not be disclosed to third parties without the client's consent, except as required by law (subpoena, regulatory request). This connects to the privacy rules below."},
  {"id":1012,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Insider trading.** Trading on, or tipping, **material nonpublic information** is prohibited under the **Insider Trading and Securities Fraud Enforcement Act of 1988 (ITSFEA)**. Penalties (as amended by later securities legislation):\n- **Civil penalty of up to three times (treble) the profit gained or loss avoided**, plus disgorgement.\n- **Criminal fines up to $5 million** for individuals ($25 million for entities) and **up to 20 years imprisonment**.\n- **Controlling persons** (the firm) can be liable up to the **greater of $1 million or three times** the profit or loss from the controlled person's violation if they failed to maintain adequate supervisory procedures."},
  {"id":1013,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Worked example (treble damages):** An analyst learns of an unannounced merger and buys stock, making a **$50,000** profit. The SEC may seek disgorgement of the $50,000 **plus** a civil penalty of up to **3 × $50,000 = $150,000**, for total exposure of **$200,000**, in addition to possible criminal prosecution.\n\n**Selling away.** An agent effecting **private securities transactions outside the firm** without the firm's prior written knowledge and approval. Prohibited because the firm cannot supervise it."},
  {"id":1014,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Market manipulation.** Creating a false or misleading appearance of trading activity — examples include **matched orders**, **wash trades** (buying and selling to create fake volume), **painting the tape**, and **pump-and-dump** schemes. All are prohibited.\n\n**Excessive trading / churning.** Trading in a client's account primarily to generate commissions rather than to benefit the client. In a fee-based advisory account the analog is **reverse churning** — charging an ongoing wrap/advisory fee on an account that rarely trades, so the client would be better off in a commission account. Both violate the best-interest duty."},
  {"id":1015,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Front running, misrepresentation, and unauthorized trading** round out the anti-fraud list: trading ahead of a client's large order, making false statements about qualifications or returns, and executing trades the client never authorized.\n\n**Common trap:** Guaranteeing a client against loss, or promising a specific return, is **always prohibited** for both IAs and agents. Only certain securities (e.g., a bond guaranteed by a third party as to principal and interest) may use the word \"guaranteed,\" and even then it refers to the issuer's or guarantor's obligation, not a promise from the adviser.\n\n#### Personal Securities Transactions and Holdings Reports"},
  {"id":1016,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"Because an adviser's employees can front-run or trade against clients, Advisers Act **Rule 204A-1** requires a written **Code of Ethics**. **\"Access persons\"** (employees with access to nonpublic recommendations or client holdings) must report their personal and outside securities accounts and holdings:\n\n| Report | Deadline |\n|---|---|\n| **Initial holdings report** | Within **10 days** of becoming an access person; information current as of a date **no more than 45 days** before the report |\n| **Annual holdings report** | At least once every **12 months**; information current within **45 days** |\n| **Quarterly transaction report** | Within **30 days** after the end of each calendar quarter |"},
  {"id":1017,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Outside securities accounts.** An associated person who opens a brokerage account away from the firm must give **written notice to the employing firm**, and the executing firm must, on request, provide **duplicate confirmations and statements** to the employer so the personal trading can be supervised.\n\n**Exam tip:** Anchor the numbers — **10 days initial, 45 days current, 30 days quarterly, annual**. Reportable securities generally **exclude** direct-obligation U.S. Treasuries, money market funds, and open-end mutual funds not advised by the firm, because those pose little front-running risk.\n\n#### Political Contributions and Pay-to-Play"},
  {"id":1018,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"To stop advisers from \"buying\" government advisory business through campaign donations, Advisers Act **Rule 206(4)-5** imposes a **two-year \"time out.\"** If the adviser (or a covered associate) makes a political contribution to an official who can **influence the award of advisory business** to a government client (e.g., a public pension fund), the adviser is **barred from receiving compensation** for advisory services to that government entity for **two years**.\n\n**De minimis exceptions** (contributions small enough to be exempt):"},
  {"id":1019,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"| Contribution | De minimis limit per election |\n|---|---|\n| To a candidate the person **is entitled to vote for** | **$350** |\n| To a candidate the person **cannot vote for** | **$150** |\n\n**Worked example:** A portfolio manager contributes **$400** to a state treasurer for whom she is eligible to vote. Because $400 exceeds the **$350** limit, the exception does not apply and the firm faces a **two-year ban** on compensated advisory work for that state's pension plan."},
  {"id":1020,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Common trap:** The pay-to-play penalty is a **ban on compensation**, not a ban on providing the service and not a fine paid to the client. Note also that the limits are \"per election,\" so a primary and a general election count separately.\n\n#### Exploitation of Vulnerable Adults"},
  {"id":1021,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"The **NASAA Model Act to Protect Vulnerable Adults from Financial Exploitation** addresses suspected exploitation of **\"eligible adults\"** — clients **age 65 or older**, or adults who qualify for protective services under state law. When a **\"qualified individual\"** (an agent, IA representative, or supervisor) reasonably believes financial exploitation is occurring or has been attempted, the model act allows the firm to:"},
  {"id":1022,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"- **Notify** the state securities regulator and **Adult Protective Services (APS)**;\n- **Delay a disbursement or transaction** for up to **15 business days**, extendable by an additional **10 business days** if requested by a regulator or agency, or as ordered by a court;\n- **Notify** third parties previously designated by the client;\n- Receive **immunity** from liability for good-faith reporting and delays.\n\n**Exam tip:** Remember the age (**65+**) and the initial hold period (**15 business days**). The purpose is protective — the firm is permitted, not required, to pause a suspicious transaction while it investigates.\n\n#### Cybersecurity, Privacy, and Data Protection"},
  {"id":1023,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"Protecting client data is both a fiduciary and a regulatory duty."},
  {"id":1024,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"- **Regulation S-P (Privacy Rule)** requires delivery of a **privacy notice at account opening (initial)** and **annually** thereafter, describing what nonpublic personal information is collected and shared. Before sharing information with **nonaffiliated third parties**, the firm must give clients an **opt-out** opportunity. (Sharing with affiliates and sharing to service the account are generally exempt from the opt-out.) The **Safeguards Rule** requires written policies to protect client records and information.\n- **Regulation S-ID (Identity Theft Red Flags Rule)** requires a written **identity theft prevention program** to detect, prevent, and respond to \"red flags.\"\n- **Cybersecurity programs** should address access controls, encryption, employee training, vendor/third-party risk, and an **incident response plan** for breaches. Firms are expected to conduct risk assessments and to maintain procedures for detecting and reporting intrusions."},
  {"id":1025,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Exam tip:** Two triggers for a privacy notice — **initial (account opening)** and **annual** — plus the **opt-out** right for sharing with **nonaffiliated** third parties. Reg S-P = **privacy**; Reg S-ID = **identity theft red flags**. Don't confuse the two.\n\n#### Business Continuity and Succession Planning\n\nThe **NASAA Model Rule on Business Continuity and Succession Planning** requires every state-registered adviser to maintain a written **Business Continuity Plan (BCP)** proportionate to its size and complexity. Because clients rely on the adviser as a fiduciary, an interruption in service is itself a client-protection issue. A BCP should address:"},
  {"id":1026,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"- **Protection, backup, and recovery of books, records, and data** (including electronic/cloud data);\n- **Alternate means of communication** with clients, employees, custodians, and regulators;\n- **Office relocation** in the event of a disaster (disaster recovery);\n- **Assignment of duties** to qualified personnel during a business interruption;\n- **Succession planning** — designating who will manage or wind down the business if a key person (often a sole proprietor) dies, becomes disabled, or otherwise cannot continue, so client accounts are not orphaned."},
  {"id":1027,"part":"Part IV","section":"H. Ethical Practices and Fiduciary Obligations","anchor":"h-ethical-practices-and-fiduciary-obligations","text":"**Exam tip:** Distinguish the two prongs. **Disaster recovery** keeps the firm operating through short-term disruptions (fire, storm, system outage). **Succession planning** addresses the long-term loss of a key individual. A common question tests why a **sole proprietor adviser** especially needs a succession plan — so clients retain access to their assets and advice if that one person is suddenly unavailable."},
  {"id":1028,"part":"","section":"Appendix A — Key Formulas","anchor":"appendix-a-key-formulas","text":"This appendix collects every calculation the Series 65 exam is likely to test. Each entry gives the formula in plain text, defines each variable, and closes with a one-line worked example. Formulas are grouped by theme.\n\n#### Yields & Returns\n\n**Current Yield**\n\nCurrent Yield = Annual Coupon Interest / Current Market Price\n\n- *Annual Coupon Interest* = the dollar interest a bond pays per year (coupon rate x par value)\n- *Current Market Price* = the bond's present market price\n\nExample: An 8% bond ($80 annual coupon) trading at $1,000 has a current yield of 80 / 1,000 = 8.0%.\n\n**Tax-Equivalent Yield (TEY)**\n\nTax-Equivalent Yield = Municipal (Tax-Free) Yield / (1 - Marginal Tax Rate)"},
  {"id":1029,"part":"","section":"Appendix A — Key Formulas","anchor":"appendix-a-key-formulas","text":"- *Municipal Yield* = the tax-free yield offered by a municipal bond\n- *Marginal Tax Rate* = the investor's highest bracket, expressed as a decimal\n\nExample: A 4% muni for an investor in the 25% bracket has a TEY of 0.04 / (1 - 0.25) = 5.33%.\n\n**Holding-Period Return / Total Return**\n\nTotal Return = (Ending Value - Beginning Value + Income Received) / Beginning Value\n\n- *Ending Value* = the investment's value at the end of the period\n- *Beginning Value* = the value at the start (typically purchase price)\n- *Income Received* = dividends or interest collected during the period\n\nExample: Buy at $50, sell at $55, collect $2 in dividends: (55 - 50 + 2) / 50 = 14%."},
  {"id":1030,"part":"","section":"Appendix A — Key Formulas","anchor":"appendix-a-key-formulas","text":"**Real (Inflation-Adjusted) Return**\n\nReal Return = Nominal Return - Inflation Rate\n\n- *Nominal Return* = the stated (unadjusted) return on the investment\n- *Inflation Rate* = the rate at which prices rose over the period (often the CPI)\n\nExample: A 7% nominal return during 3% inflation gives a real return of 7% - 3% = 4%.\n\n**After-Tax Return**\n\nAfter-Tax Return = Pre-Tax Return x (1 - Tax Rate)\n\n- *Pre-Tax Return* = the return before taxes are applied\n- *Tax Rate* = the applicable tax rate on the gain or income, as a decimal\n\nExample: A 10% taxable return for an investor in the 30% bracket nets 0.10 x (1 - 0.30) = 7%.\n\n**Net Asset Value (NAV)**"},
  {"id":1031,"part":"","section":"Appendix A — Key Formulas","anchor":"appendix-a-key-formulas","text":"NAV per Share = (Total Assets - Total Liabilities) / Shares Outstanding\n\n- *Total Assets* = market value of all securities and cash the fund holds\n- *Total Liabilities* = amounts the fund owes\n- *Shares Outstanding* = number of fund shares held by investors\n\nExample: A fund with $100M assets, $10M liabilities, and 9M shares has an NAV of (100M - 10M) / 9M = $10.00.\n\n**Dividend Yield**\n\nDividend Yield = Annual Dividends per Share / Price per Share\n\n- *Annual Dividends per Share* = total dividends paid on one share over a year\n- *Price per Share* = the stock's current market price\n\nExample: A stock paying $1.50 in dividends at a $30 price yields 1.50 / 30 = 5.0%.\n\n#### Ratios & Valuation"},
  {"id":1032,"part":"","section":"Appendix A — Key Formulas","anchor":"appendix-a-key-formulas","text":"**Current Ratio**\n\nCurrent Ratio = Current Assets / Current Liabilities\n\n- *Current Assets* = cash and assets convertible to cash within one year\n- *Current Liabilities* = obligations due within one year\n\nExample: $600,000 current assets against $300,000 current liabilities gives a current ratio of 2.0.\n\n**Quick Ratio (Acid-Test)**\n\nQuick Ratio = (Current Assets - Inventory) / Current Liabilities\n\n- *Current Assets* = current assets as above\n- *Inventory* = goods held for sale (excluded because it is the least liquid current asset)\n- *Current Liabilities* = obligations due within one year\n\nExample: ($600,000 - $200,000 inventory) / $300,000 = a quick ratio of 1.33.\n\n**Debt-to-Equity Ratio**"},
  {"id":1033,"part":"","section":"Appendix A — Key Formulas","anchor":"appendix-a-key-formulas","text":"Debt-to-Equity = Total Debt / Total Shareholders' Equity\n\n- *Total Debt* = the company's interest-bearing borrowings (often long-term debt)\n- *Total Shareholders' Equity* = assets minus liabilities; the owners' stake\n\nExample: $4M in debt against $8M in equity gives a debt-to-equity ratio of 0.5.\n\n**Price-to-Earnings (P/E) Ratio**\n\nP/E = Market Price per Share / Earnings per Share (EPS)\n\n- *Market Price per Share* = the stock's current price\n- *Earnings per Share* = net income available to common shareholders divided by shares outstanding\n\nExample: A $40 stock with $2 EPS has a P/E of 40 / 2 = 20.\n\n**Price-to-Book (P/B) Ratio**\n\nP/B = Market Price per Share / Book Value per Share"},
  {"id":1034,"part":"","section":"Appendix A — Key Formulas","anchor":"appendix-a-key-formulas","text":"- *Market Price per Share* = the stock's current price\n- *Book Value per Share* = (Total Assets - Total Liabilities - Preferred Equity) / Common Shares Outstanding\n\nExample: A $30 stock with a $15 book value per share has a P/B of 30 / 15 = 2.0.\n\n**Market Capitalization**\n\nMarket Capitalization = Current Share Price x Shares Outstanding\n\n- *Current Share Price* = the stock's market price\n- *Shares Outstanding* = total common shares held by investors\n\nExample: A $50 stock with 20 million shares outstanding has a market cap of 50 x 20M = $1 billion.\n\n#### Risk & Statistics\n\n**CAPM Expected Return**\n\nExpected Return = Risk-Free Rate + Beta x (Market Return - Risk-Free Rate)"},
  {"id":1035,"part":"","section":"Appendix A — Key Formulas","anchor":"appendix-a-key-formulas","text":"- *Risk-Free Rate* = yield on a risk-free asset, typically short-term Treasuries\n- *Beta* = the asset's sensitivity to market movements (systematic risk)\n- *Market Return* = the expected return of the overall market; the term in parentheses is the market risk premium\n\nExample: With a 2% risk-free rate, beta of 1.2, and 10% market return: 2% + 1.2 x (10% - 2%) = 11.6%.\n\n**Alpha**\n\nAlpha = Actual Return - CAPM Expected Return\n\n- *Actual Return* = the return the portfolio actually earned\n- *CAPM Expected Return* = the return predicted by CAPM for the portfolio's beta\n\nExample: A portfolio that returned 13% when CAPM predicted 11.6% has an alpha of +1.4% (outperformance).\n\n**Beta**"},
  {"id":1036,"part":"","section":"Appendix A — Key Formulas","anchor":"appendix-a-key-formulas","text":"Beta = Covariance(Asset Return, Market Return) / Variance(Market Return)\n\n- *Covariance* = how the asset's returns move together with the market's\n- *Variance(Market Return)* = the dispersion of the market's returns\n- The market itself has a beta of 1.0; beta above 1 is more volatile than the market, below 1 is less\n\nExample: A stock with a beta of 1.5 tends to rise (or fall) about 15% when the market moves 10%.\n\n**Standard Deviation (Concept)**\n\nStandard Deviation = the square root of the average squared deviation of returns from their mean"},
  {"id":1037,"part":"","section":"Appendix A — Key Formulas","anchor":"appendix-a-key-formulas","text":"- *Concept* = a measure of total risk (volatility); the dispersion of an investment's returns around its average return\n- Larger standard deviation means wider swings and greater uncertainty; roughly 68% of returns fall within one standard deviation of the mean\n\nExample: A fund averaging 8% with a standard deviation of 10% typically returns between -2% and 18% in a given year.\n\n**Sharpe Ratio**\n\nSharpe Ratio = (Portfolio Return - Risk-Free Rate) / Standard Deviation\n\n- *Portfolio Return* = the return the portfolio earned\n- *Risk-Free Rate* = return on a risk-free asset\n- *Standard Deviation* = the portfolio's total risk (volatility)"},
  {"id":1038,"part":"","section":"Appendix A — Key Formulas","anchor":"appendix-a-key-formulas","text":"Example: A portfolio returning 12% with a 2% risk-free rate and 10% standard deviation has a Sharpe ratio of (12 - 2) / 10 = 1.0.\n\n#### Time Value of Money\n\n**Future Value (FV)**\n\nFuture Value = Present Value x (1 + r)^n\n\n- *Present Value* = the amount invested today\n- *r* = the periodic interest (growth) rate, as a decimal\n- *n* = the number of compounding periods\n\nExample: $1,000 growing at 6% for 2 years: 1,000 x (1.06)^2 = $1,123.60.\n\n**Present Value (PV)**\n\nPresent Value = Future Value / (1 + r)^n\n\n- *Future Value* = the amount to be received in the future\n- *r* = the discount rate per period, as a decimal\n- *n* = the number of periods until the amount is received"},
  {"id":1039,"part":"","section":"Appendix A — Key Formulas","anchor":"appendix-a-key-formulas","text":"Example: $1,000 needed in 2 years, discounted at 6%: 1,000 / (1.06)^2 = $890.00.\n\n**Net Present Value (NPV)**\n\nNPV = (Sum of Present Values of Future Cash Flows) - Initial Investment\n\n- *Present Values of Future Cash Flows* = each expected cash inflow discounted back to today\n- *Initial Investment* = the up-front cost paid today\n- A positive NPV means the investment's discounted return exceeds its cost; NPV > 0 implies a yield above the discount rate\n\nExample: An investment costing $900 that returns a single $1,000 in one year, discounted at 6%, has NPV = (1,000 / 1.06) - 900 = +$43.40.\n\n**Rule of 72**\n\nYears to Double = 72 / Interest Rate (in whole-number percent)"},
  {"id":1040,"part":"","section":"Appendix A — Key Formulas","anchor":"appendix-a-key-formulas","text":"- *72* = a fixed approximation constant\n- *Interest Rate* = the annual compounding rate expressed as a whole number (e.g., 8, not 0.08)\n- Rearranged, Rate to Double = 72 / Years, giving the return needed to double money in a set time\n\nExample: At an 8% annual return, money doubles in about 72 / 8 = 9 years."},
  {"id":1041,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**Accredited investor** — Under Regulation D, an individual with $200,000 income ($300,000 joint) for the past two years or $1 million net worth excluding primary residence, or specified institutions; such investors may buy unregistered private-placement securities.\n\n**Accretion** — The annual upward adjustment of a bond's cost basis from its discount purchase price toward par; on a taxable original-issue-discount bond the accreted amount is taxed as interest each year."},
  {"id":1042,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**ADR (American Depositary Receipt)** — A negotiable receipt issued by a U.S. bank representing shares of a foreign company, letting Americans trade foreign equity in dollars on U.S. markets; holders bear currency risk and, while they receive dividends and can generally vote the underlying shares, lack preemptive rights.\n\n**Agent** — Under the Uniform Securities Act, an individual who represents a broker-dealer or issuer in effecting securities transactions; agents must register in each state where they solicit or transact."},
  {"id":1043,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**Alpha** — The portion of a portfolio's return above or below what its beta (market risk) would predict; positive alpha indicates value added by the manager relative to expected risk-adjusted return.\n\n**AML (Anti-Money Laundering)** — Programs required under the Bank Secrecy Act and USA PATRIOT Act to detect and prevent laundering, including customer identification, SAR filings, and CTRs for cash transactions over $10,000.\n\n**Annuity** — An insurance contract that accumulates on a tax-deferred basis and can pay out income for a set period or life; fixed annuities pay guaranteed amounts while variable annuities fluctuate with subaccount performance."},
  {"id":1044,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**Basis point** — One one-hundredth of one percent (0.01%); 100 basis points equal 1%, a standard unit for quoting yield and interest-rate changes.\n\n**Beta** — A measure of a security's volatility relative to the overall market; a beta of 1.0 moves with the market, above 1.0 is more volatile, and below 1.0 is less volatile.\n\n**Breakpoint** — A dollar level of mutual fund purchase at which the sales charge is reduced; selling just below a breakpoint to earn a higher commission (\"breakpoint sale\") is a prohibited practice."},
  {"id":1045,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**Brochure Rule** — SEC Rule 204-3 requiring a registered investment adviser to deliver its Form ADV Part 2 (or equivalent brochure) and brochure supplement to clients at or before entering an advisory contract, with annual updates.\n\n**Callable bond** — A bond the issuer may redeem before maturity, usually when rates fall; the call feature benefits the issuer and exposes the holder to reinvestment risk, so such bonds carry higher yields.\n\n**Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM)** — A model that estimates an asset's required return as the risk-free rate plus beta times the market risk premium; it prices only systematic (non-diversifiable) risk."},
  {"id":1046,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**Convertible bond** — A bond that the holder may exchange for a fixed number of the issuer's common shares; the conversion feature lets bondholders participate in equity upside, so these bonds typically pay lower coupons.\n\n**Correlation** — A statistic from -1 to +1 measuring how two assets' returns move together; combining assets with low or negative correlation reduces portfolio risk through diversification.\n\n**Coupon (nominal yield)** — The fixed annual interest rate a bond pays on its par value, stated as a percentage; a 5% coupon on a $1,000 bond pays $50 per year regardless of market price."},
  {"id":1047,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**Current yield** — A bond's annual coupon interest divided by its current market price; it rises when the bond trades at a discount and falls when it trades at a premium.\n\n**Custody** — Holding or having access to a client's funds or securities, or authority to withdraw them; advisers with custody face extra requirements such as surprise audits, qualified custodians, and account statements.\n\n**De minimis exemption** — A provision letting an adviser or agent avoid state registration if it has no place of business in the state and serves few clients—generally five or fewer clients in any 12-month period for advisers."},
  {"id":1048,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**Discount rate** — The interest rate the Federal Reserve charges member banks for short-term loans through the discount window; also, more broadly, the rate used to compute the present value of future cash flows.\n\n**Discretion** — Authority granted to an adviser or agent to decide the security, quantity, or whether to buy or sell without prior client approval for each trade; it requires prior written authorization (though time and price alone are not discretion).\n\n**Diversification** — Spreading investments across different securities, sectors, or asset classes to reduce unsystematic (company-specific) risk; it cannot eliminate systematic market risk."},
  {"id":1049,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**Dollar cost averaging** — Investing a fixed dollar amount at regular intervals regardless of price, which buys more shares when prices are low and fewer when high, lowering average cost per share over time.\n\n**Duration** — A measure of a bond's price sensitivity to interest-rate changes, expressed in years; the higher the duration, the more the bond's price moves for a given change in rates.\n\n**Efficient Market Hypothesis (EMH)** — The theory that security prices already reflect available information, so consistently beating the market is difficult; its weak, semi-strong, and strong forms differ on which information is already priced in."},
  {"id":1050,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**ERISA (Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974)** — Federal law setting fiduciary, funding, vesting, and reporting standards for private-sector employer retirement plans; Section 404(c) shifts investment liability to participants who direct their own accounts.\n\n**ETF (Exchange-Traded Fund)** — An investment fund, usually index-based, whose shares trade intraday on an exchange like a stock; ETFs typically offer low expenses and tax efficiency and can be bought on margin or sold short.\n\n**Expected return** — The probability-weighted average of an investment's possible returns; it represents the mean outcome an investor anticipates, not a guaranteed result."},
  {"id":1051,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**Federal covered adviser** — An investment adviser registered with the SEC rather than the states—generally those managing $110 million or more, advising registered investment companies, or otherwise exempt from state registration; states may still require notice filing.\n\n**Fiduciary** — A person legally obligated to act in a client's best interest, placing the client's interests above their own; investment advisers owe clients a fiduciary duty of care and loyalty."},
  {"id":1052,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**Form ADV** — The uniform registration form investment advisers file; Part 1 covers business and disciplinary information, Part 2 is the client-facing brochure, and Part 3 (Form CRS) is a relationship summary for retail clients.\n\n**Form U4** — The Uniform Application for Securities Industry Registration used to register agents and investment adviser representatives, disclosing employment, disciplinary, and financial background.\n\n**529 plan** — A state-sponsored, tax-advantaged education savings plan whose earnings grow tax-deferred and are tax-free when used for qualified education expenses; contributions may qualify for state tax benefits."},
  {"id":1053,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**GDP (Gross Domestic Product)** — The total market value of all final goods and services produced within a country in a period; two consecutive quarters of declining real GDP signal a recession.\n\n**GO bond (General Obligation bond)** — A municipal bond backed by the full faith, credit, and taxing power of the issuer; because repayment relies on taxes, issuing GO debt often requires voter approval.\n\n**Hedge fund** — A lightly regulated private pooled fund open mainly to accredited and institutional investors that uses leverage, short selling, and derivatives to pursue high returns, typically charging a performance-based fee."},
  {"id":1054,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**IAR (Investment Adviser Representative)** — An individual associated with an investment adviser who gives advice, manages accounts, solicits clients, or supervises those who do; IARs register at the state level.\n\n**Index** — A statistical benchmark tracking the performance of a group of securities, such as the S&P 500; indexes are used to gauge markets and as the basis for index funds and ETFs.\n\n**Inflation** — A sustained rise in the general price level that erodes purchasing power; it is commonly measured by the Consumer Price Index (CPI) and is a key form of purchasing-power risk for bondholders."},
  {"id":1055,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**Investment adviser** — Any person who, for compensation, is in the business of advising others about securities or issuing analyses; the definition turns on the three-part \"advice, business, compensation\" test.\n\n**Investment company** — A firm that pools investor money to invest in securities, classified as a face-amount certificate company, unit investment trust, or management company (open-end mutual funds and closed-end funds).\n\n**Leading indicators** — Economic data series such as building permits, new orders, and stock prices that tend to change before the overall economy does, used to forecast turns in the business cycle."},
  {"id":1056,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**Liquidity** — The ease with which an asset can be converted to cash at a fair price without significant loss; money market instruments are highly liquid, while real estate and thin securities are not.\n\n**Margin** — Borrowing from a broker-dealer to buy securities, using the account as collateral under Regulation T; margin magnifies both gains and losses and requires maintaining minimum equity.\n\n**Modern Portfolio Theory (MPT)** — A framework holding that investors should evaluate securities by their contribution to a portfolio's overall risk and return, building an \"efficient frontier\" that maximizes return for each level of risk."},
  {"id":1057,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**Money market** — The market for short-term, high-quality debt instruments maturing in one year or less, such as T-bills, commercial paper, and CDs, valued for safety and liquidity.\n\n**Municipal bond** — Debt issued by state or local governments whose interest is generally exempt from federal income tax (and often state tax for residents), making it attractive to investors in high tax brackets.\n\n**NAV (Net Asset Value)** — The per-share value of a mutual fund, equal to total assets minus liabilities divided by shares outstanding, calculated at least once daily; it is the price at which open-end fund shares are redeemed."},
  {"id":1058,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**Nonsystematic (unsystematic) risk** — Risk specific to a single company or industry—such as business, financial, or regulatory risk—that can be reduced through diversification.\n\n**Open-end company (mutual fund)** — A management investment company that continuously issues and redeems shares at NAV; investors buy from and sell back to the fund rather than trading on an exchange.\n\n**Preferred stock** — An equity security paying a fixed dividend with priority over common stock in dividends and liquidation but usually no voting rights; its price behaves much like a bond, moving inversely with interest rates."},
  {"id":1059,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**Present value** — The current worth of a future sum discounted at a given interest rate; it reflects the time value of money, the principle that a dollar today is worth more than a dollar later.\n\n**Prospectus** — The disclosure document delivered to investors in a registered public offering, summarizing the issuer, the securities, risks, and use of proceeds; it is the abbreviated form of the registration statement's Part I."},
  {"id":1060,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**QDIA (Qualified Default Investment Alternative)** — A diversified default investment—such as a target-date or balanced fund—into which a participant's 401(k) contributions are placed when no election is made, giving plan fiduciaries safe-harbor protection under ERISA.\n\n**Qualified plan** — An employer retirement plan meeting ERISA and IRS rules, offering pre-tax contributions, tax-deferred growth, and taxation upon withdrawal; examples include 401(k)s and defined-benefit pensions."},
  {"id":1061,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**REIT (Real Estate Investment Trust)** — A company that owns or finances income-producing real estate and trades like a stock; it avoids corporate tax by distributing at least 90% of taxable income to shareholders as dividends.\n\n**Revenue bond** — A municipal bond repaid solely from the income of a specific project or facility, such as a toll road or airport, rather than from general taxes; it carries no voter approval requirement and higher risk than a GO bond."},
  {"id":1062,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**Roth IRA** — An individual retirement account funded with after-tax dollars whose qualified withdrawals, including earnings, are tax-free; it has income eligibility limits and no required minimum distributions during the owner's life.\n\n**Rule 144** — The SEC rule governing the resale of restricted and control (affiliate) securities into the public market, imposing holding periods, volume limits, and Form 144 filing to allow sales without full registration.\n\n**Sharpe ratio** — A measure of risk-adjusted return equal to a portfolio's return above the risk-free rate divided by its standard deviation; a higher ratio indicates better reward per unit of total risk."},
  {"id":1063,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**Soft dollars** — Research or brokerage services an adviser receives from a broker-dealer in exchange for directing client trades there; the Section 28(e) safe harbor permits this only if the benefits are eligible research or execution services.\n\n**Standard deviation** — A statistic measuring how widely an investment's returns vary around their average, used as a proxy for total risk; a higher standard deviation means greater volatility.\n\n**Systematic risk** — Market-wide risk—such as interest-rate, inflation, and market risk—that affects all securities and cannot be diversified away; it is the risk measured by beta."},
  {"id":1064,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**TIPS (Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities)** — U.S. Treasury bonds whose principal adjusts with the CPI, so both interest payments and the amount repaid at maturity keep pace with inflation, protecting purchasing power.\n\n**Total return** — An investment's complete return combining income (interest or dividends) and capital appreciation, expressed as a percentage of the amount invested; it gives a fuller performance picture than yield alone.\n\n**Uniform Securities Act (USA)** — The model state securities law (blue-sky law) that states adapt to register securities, advisers, agents, and IARs, and to police fraud; it is the foundation of the NASAA-administered state regulatory framework."},
  {"id":1065,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**Variable annuity** — An annuity whose value and payout depend on the performance of investor-selected subaccounts; because it carries investment risk, it is a security requiring both a securities and an insurance license to sell.\n\n**Wrap fee (wrap account)** — An arrangement bundling advisory services, execution, and administrative costs into a single asset-based fee; sponsors must deliver a specialized wrap-fee brochure (Form ADV Part 2A, Appendix 1).\n\n**Yield curve** — A graph plotting yields of similar bonds across maturities; a normal upward-sloping curve reflects higher long-term yields, while an inverted curve (long-term below short-term) often signals a coming recession."},
  {"id":1066,"part":"","section":"Appendix B — Glossary","anchor":"appendix-b-glossary","text":"**Yield to maturity (YTM)** — The total annualized return an investor earns holding a bond to maturity, accounting for coupon income plus any gain or loss between purchase price and par; it is the market's true measure of a bond's yield.\n\n**Zero-coupon bond** — A bond issued at a deep discount that pays no periodic interest, returning full par value at maturity; the accreted discount is taxed annually as \"phantom income\" even though no cash is received."}
];
